  "@graph": [
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that before the proclamation was made to them, all the rulers of the aeons, of fate and of the sphere were already bound in their bonds, their spheres and their seals, as the overseer of the light had bound them from the beginning; that each remained in his own order; and that each travelled his course as the overseer had established it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaIeuVincula",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Herrscher waren von Anfang an vom Aufseher des Lichts gebunden",
        "en": "The rulers were bound from the beginning by the overseer of the light"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also settles what the seals are here, and it is not what the later handbooks mean by the word. In this passage a seal is part of what binds the ruler, not an instrument the practitioner carries; the seals of traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl are held by the one who passes. Two uses of one term in one circle of writings, and they point in opposite directions.",
          "The consequence is the one this material is usually read against. The rulers are not autonomous adversaries whom an ascending soul must outwit: they are held in an arrangement made by someone else, and their courses were set for them. A formula addressed to such a ruler is not a negotiation with a power but an appeal to the order that constituted him — and the same work says two chapters earlier that a third of that power was taken away, which is an act inside the same arrangement rather than a victory over it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Pronouncing the orations of the art on the prescribed days of the moon, at the prescribed hours and with the prescribed number of repetitions, alone, secretly and in a voice barely audible to the speaker, having fasted on the first day and living chastely."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sprechen der Orationen",
        "en": "The saying of the orations"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The procedure the text prescribes is larger than this node: the orations accompany the notae, and the witness read carries none of them. What is entered is the part the witness gives."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 14-15 and pp. 13-14, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A divination performed with grains of maize and the seeds of the tzite tree, carried out by the diviner pair Xpiyacoc and Xmucane at the request of the makers, in order to learn whether the men to be carved of wood would turn out. The seeds are addressed in the second person and told to come together, to take hold of one another and to speak, so that the answer is given by things spoken to and not by things merely read."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Tzite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Losen mit Maiskörnern und Tzite-Samen",
        "en": "Casting the lot with maize grains and tzite seeds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same procedure survives in the highlands as the office of the ajq'ij, the daykeeper who counts tzite seeds. The colonial spellings tzite and tzité and the modern tz'ite' stand for one word and one seed of the coral tree; a search under only one of them misses most of the literature."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapter 2",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that whoever pronounces the orations at the appointed times, setting all other occasions aside, profits in all the sciences within one month. It is examinable because the claim names a regimen, a term and an outcome that a third party can measure."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NotoryArtOneMonthExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Wissenschaften binnen des genannten Monats eintreffen",
        "en": "Whether the sciences arrive within the month the art names"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case has been collected. The obstacle is named rather than left to be inferred, and it is not the difficulty of the regimen: the witness in print omits the notae, so nobody working from it can keep the procedure the text prescribes. A negative result obtained from this witness alone would therefore not tell against the art, only against half of it. That is a property of the transmission and belongs in the protocol, because it is exactly the kind of gap a later reader would otherwise supply on his own.",
          "The clause that would absorb every outcome is present in the corpus but not in this claim. Elsewhere the text bars the man impeded by crime from saying the orations at all; here nothing says that a failure shows the speaker was unworthy. Whether a fuller recension closes that gap is unread, and it would change the state of this protocol rather than its subject.",
          "The measurement is the weak point and is stated so: profiting in all the sciences is not a quantity, and any protocol has to choose one art and one instrument before the month begins. Choosing them afterwards would let the outcome pick its own measure, which is the failure mode pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate records after the fact and which is cheaper to prevent here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One person who keeps the full regimen for a lunation — the orations on the fourth, eighth, twelfth, sixteenth, twenty-fourth, twenty-eighth and thirtieth day of the moon, at the four hours, alone and in the prescribed manner — and who at the end of the month scores no higher on a test of a liberal art set before the month began than a person matched for prior knowledge who did not keep it. The claim is universal in form and admits no degree, so a single such case refutes it as stated.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-05",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsFacundia"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has another speak or act through the practitioner, who is described as receiving, carrying or being ridden rather than as doing."
      },
      "id": "pm:cededAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgetretene Trägerschaft",
        "en": "Ceded agency"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The axis records the account the source gives, not a verdict on it. A tradition that credits a spirit and one that credits a trained faculty differ in what they claim, and that difference is worth holding whatever either claim is worth."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AgencyHolding",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A condition in which no object at all is apprehended, whether reached in sleep or in a cessation described by a handbook of absorption."
      },
      "id": "pm:objectlessDepth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegenstandslose Tiefe",
        "en": "Objectless depth"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Māṇḍūkya calls this suṣupti. A source that has something exercised here is worth a second look before it is entered, because most traditions place their capacities short of it."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaCaksus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama auf die Sonne bringt Weltkenntnis hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama on the sun yields knowledge of the world"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative Vedic corpus: spells for healing, protection, harm, love and kingship, with the ritual manual that tells how each is employed."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Atharvavedic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atharvavedische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Atharvavedic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The oldest Indian material the project would hold that is a working corpus rather than a doctrine."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā (Śaunaka recension); Kauśikasūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "One party ascribes the capacity to another, who does not report it. Common for capacities said to be lost, since no bearer remains to be asked."
      },
      "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fremdzuschreibung",
        "en": "Third-party ascription"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source instructs that one comes before the other, without saying what follows from disregarding it."
      },
      "id": "pm:prescribedOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Angeordnete Reihenfolge",
        "en": "Prescribed order"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the stars; the two recensions differ on whether it is the constellations or the signs of the stars."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kokabel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel",
        "en": "Kokabel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Kôkabêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the naming tradition takes the bearer to be, in that tradition's own term: watcher, angel, demon, daimon, god, spirit, ancestor."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Naming",
      "id": "pm:nameRole",
      "label": {
        "de": "Rolle des Benannten",
        "en": "name role"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately a literal and not a controlled scale. A fixed vocabulary would have to decide whether a Greek daimon is an angel or a demon, and that decision belongs to no tradition in the record: it is the harmonisation this project exists to avoid. Where two traditions call a shared name differently, the difference is the finding, and a scale would have deleted it."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Chinese texts and practices concerned with the cultivation and circulation of qi."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Daoist",
      "label": {
        "de": "Daoistische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Daoist tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately not documented. From the arrival of Buddhism in China the route is plain and the file's material lies mostly after it; for the earlier strata it is not, and the whole independence value of the Chinese material hangs on where that line falls.",
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citations of the jade inscription name the work they were read in. Three passages stood without an edition because it was no longer recoverable, and the file said so rather than guessing. Two of the three have since been reopened: Zhuangzi 4 was read at the wording in Legge's translation of eighteen ninety-one and now carries it, and the reopening turned up three renderings of the term the passage ends on; and Baopuzi neipian 8 was read at the wording in the Daozang text on 2026-09-09, which corrected one reading and added ten nodes below. Chen Zhixu's Jindan dayao is still unopened, and for it the juan division is itself edition-dependent.",
          "The file is no longer thin only by necessity. The eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, opened on 2026-09-09, gave what the earlier runs had gone looking for elsewhere: a list of what the circulation of the qi is said to accomplish, an external sign of the breathing, a condition under which the practice is said to have no benefit at all, two prohibitions with a stated consequence, and a passage in which the author sets his own tradition against the Zhuangzi this file also holds. What stays out is what stayed out before: the absorption of solar and stellar qi and the abstention from grain are at prima-materia#17, not here.",
          "Thin on purpose, and the thinness is the finding. Of the material this tradition is credited with, two places could be run down to a text that names its own words: the jade inscription, which is the earliest datable witness located for a deliberate circulation of qi through the body, and the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, which states the manner of the breathing in terms an examination could take hold of. How early the inscription is remains contested, and the dispute node holds that. What is widely reported and was not run down to a citable passage stays out rather than being approximated: the absorption of solar, lunar and stellar qi (fuqi) and the abstention from grain (bigu) are recorded at prima-materia#17, not here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A man who had owned a sacred stone for many years but had not been able to command it as the medicine men do attributed the failure to himself, saying that he does not place his faith wholly upon it but believes in the help of many other agencies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FailureOfTheStonesAttributedToTheOwner",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Stein, der nicht gehorcht, liegt am Besitzer",
        "en": "A stone that will not obey is the owner's fault"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also the reason the two printed cases at pmc:TunkanDistantKnowledge cannot be counted as a run of successes: the corpus records the failures under a heading that removes them from the count.",
          "The counterpart of the eight causes at pmc:BengeFailureCauses in the Azande file: an instrument that fails is not thereby discredited, because the transmission has a place to put the failure. Here the place is the owner's divided faith, which cannot be inspected from outside and which no outcome can clear. It is entered beside the capacity it protects and not away from it, because the two only mean anything together."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 207",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office of the speaker who gave out the oracles to those of the locality. The witness reports that the respect shown them was so great that they did not ordinarily leave their houses except carried on litters."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Chilan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chilan",
        "en": "Chilan"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same section gives the office of the priest beside it: to teach the sciences, to indicate calamities and the means of remedying them, to preach at the festivals and to celebrate the sacrifices. Indicating a calamity and naming its remedy are one office in this transmission, which is why the remedy for a foretold year below is not a separate institution."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "chilam"
        },
        {
          "@language": "es",
          "@value": "chilanes"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXVII, pp. 46-47",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The raising of the body hair, counted among the eight involuntary signs and treated as a mark of an inner state, not as something to be produced for its own sake."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Romanca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haaraufrichtung",
        "en": "Horripilation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Attested outside the dramaturgical treatise in the epic corpus, where trembling and horripilation are named together as signs of an overwhelming state."
      },
      "source": [
        "Bhagavadgītā 1.29",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen",
        "en": "Cultivation of the divine abidings"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The being named more often than any other in the Meux roll, against whose evil eye the first, second, third, sixth, seventh and eighth prayers ask deliverance, and against whom the diseases enumerated in them are directed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianBarya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Barja",
        "en": "Barya"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The edition read prints Barya and Borya for what is plainly one name and does not say which spelling stands in the parchment. Held as one naming with the second form as an alternate label rather than split into two, because nothing in the volume supports two bearers."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Barya",
      "pm:nameRole": "the bringer of the evil eye against which the prayers are said",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Borya"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I, II, III, VI, VII, VIII, pp. 216-220",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second set of seven in the same diagram, each with a name and an animal form: Michael the lion-like, Suriel the bull-like, Raphael the serpent-like, Gabriel the eagle-like, Thauthabaoth the bear-like, Erataoth the dog-like, and Onoel or Thartharaoth, somewhat asinine in appearance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteHebdomasTheriomorpha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sieben Herrscher in Tiergestalt",
        "en": "The seven ruling demons in animal shape"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Origen states the division of labour between his source and Celsus outright: Celsus gave the shapes and not the names, and the names come from the diagram Origen had. The first four are the four living creatures of the merkabah under angel names, the last three are formed like the names of the other heptad."
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus",
        "en": "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourthJhana"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The guard the transmission puts against its own condition of failure. A master of song usually gathered two or more of his fellows to edit and to memorise the lines, and genealogies in particular had to be memorised by more than one reciter."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HawaiianRedundantMemorising",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Auswendiglernen eines Gesangs durch mehrere Rezitatoren",
        "en": "Memorising a chant with more than one reciter"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Worth holding separately from the rule it answers, and for a reason that goes beyond this tradition. A transmission that names a failure condition and stops has stated a threat; one that names the failure condition and then builds a redundancy against it has treated its own rule as a real risk and acted on it. The second is evidence about the practice that the first is not, and the graph has very few instances of it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A falsifying condition is stated and no case has yet been recorded against it."
      },
      "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verfahren ohne Fälle",
        "en": "Procedure without cases"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The seeker strips and wears only a robe, a breechclout and moccasins, takes a pipe, smoking material and a knife, and goes to the top of a high place where others are not likely to intrude. There he clears a space of every living or growing thing large enough to sit or lie on, and on entering it invokes the Four Winds that they may not bring inclement weather upon him. He may stand, sit or lie, awake or asleep, and may smoke as often as he wishes, but he must not leave the space he has prepared and must neither eat nor drink while the quest lasts."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Hanblapi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hanblapi, das Suchen einer Mitteilung",
        "en": "Hanblapi, seeking a communication"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The second account in the same volume, given as the origin of the Buffalo ceremony, prescribes four passages through the vitalizing lodge first and sends the seeker up naked with a robe, a pipe and willow bark, and has him invoke four named gods and then call the spirit of the buffalo. The two accounts differ in the clothing and in the number of purifications and agree on the solitude, the high place, the fasting and the waiting. Both are printed in the same paper and neither is preferred here."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConfessionYieldsAverting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Beichte wendet den Zorn ab",
        "en": "The confession turns aside the anger"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."
      },
      "id": "pm:agencyNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Trägerschaft nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Agency not stated"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AgencyHolding",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A search returned work that contradicts the evidence, fails to replicate it, or narrows its reach. What was found belongs in the note and, where it bears on the finding, in the protocol's own notes."
      },
      "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegensuche mit Fund",
        "en": "Counter-search found opposition"
      },
      "type": "pm:CounterSearchState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When a man struck by lightning is left alive, injured but living, he is held to be as if divinely chosen for the service of the huacas. The earlier report gives the claim in the first person of the transmission: those called camascas said that the grace and virtue they had was received from the Thunder — that when lightning fell and left a man in terror, he said on coming to himself that the Thunder had shown him that art, whether curing with herbs or giving answers to what was asked; and the same was said of a man who escaped a river or a great danger."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightningStrikeElection",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vom Blitz Getroffene gilt als erwählt",
        "en": "The lightning-struck is taken as elected"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The two do not say the same thing about what is received. The later gives a status — he is as if elected. The earlier gives a content — the Thunder showed him an art, and names two arts. The difference is recorded rather than merged, because a status can be conferred by the community after the fact and a taught art cannot.",
          "Two recorders, forty-six years apart, in different regions, on different sides of the colonial administration, one writing in Cuzco and one in the archdiocese of Lima, give the same claim. Neither cites the other. This is the best-attested single claim in the file, and the only one for which the internal date spread is wide enough to be worth stating."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fols. 6v-7r, on the camascas",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim that the tummo practice produces inner heat, taken up because this is the one pairing in the collection whose capacity has an outward side that instruments reach."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TummoHeatExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfung der Tummo-Hitze-Behauptung",
        "en": "Examination of the tummo heat claim"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386.",
          "What the measurements bear on is the somatic claim and nothing beyond it. Reported temperature rises are consistent with the practice producing heat; they say nothing about the cosmology the practice is embedded in, and the node must not be read as though they did. The reported effect also depends on the forceful-breath component together with the visualisation, which means the claim that is actually supported is narrower than the one the tradition makes."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:protocolledPractice"
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "Temperature change under comparable breathing effort without the practice, in matched participants.",
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not documented. This node predates the requirement; whether a counter-search was carried is not recoverable from it, and an undocumented search is treated as uncarried for the same reason pm:independenceGround reverses its burden."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "H. Benson et al., Body temperature changes during the practice of g-tummo yoga, Nature 295 (1982), 234-236",
        "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "H. Benson and colleagues; M. Kozhevnikov and colleagues",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "Practitioners of the forceful-breath tummo method show no rise in measured body temperature beyond that produced by the breathing alone in matched non-practitioners.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-28",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PenemueTeachesBitterAndSweet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue offenbart bitter und süß",
        "en": "Penemue discloses bitter and sweet"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Penemue"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Breath or spirit, named as the first of the ten emanations, from which air issues, from air water, and from water fire."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ruach",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ruach",
        "en": "Ruaḥ"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The citation was extended from 1:9-10 to 1:9-12 when the passage was reopened: in this numbering the first emanation is breath, the second breath from breath, and only the third and fourth carry water from breath and fire from water. The earlier range stopped two verses short of the sequence the definition states. The numbering is recension-dependent throughout, and the long and Saadia versions divide the same material otherwise.",
          "The text also has the patriarch stirring the letters with breath, which is the closest the source comes to breath as an operative medium; it remains an act of formation, not a conduction through the body."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
        "Sefer Yetzirah 6:7 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SeferYetzirah"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An ordering of four states held to be possible for a person: sleep; the state ordinarily called waking, which the source holds to be a second form of sleep in which people walk the streets, write books and kill one another; self-consciousness, which is self-remembering; and an objective state in which things are seen as they are. The source holds that ordinary people live in the two lowest only, that flashes of the third occur but cannot be produced by wish or decision, and that the fourth is reached solely through the third."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vier Bewusstseinszustände",
        "en": "The four states of consciousness"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The claim carries its own account of why it is resisted: the chief obstacle is said to be that people believe they already possess the third state, together with a permanent I, will and the ability to do. A claim of this shape cannot be tested by asking people whether they have the state, because it predicts the affirmative answer.",
          "The ordering is fourfold and is not the Indian one. It does not count waking, dream and deep sleep with a fourth beyond them; it counts degrees of presence within waking, and it puts the ordinary waking state on the lower half of its own scale. Two of its four terms therefore have no counterpart on pm:SensoryAnchoring, whose values were drawn from the Māṇḍūkya. See prima-materia#653.",
          "The source names no antecedent for this ordering, and that silence is itself worth recording. The same book gives the fourfold division of bodies under four sets of names and asserts that comparable tables stand in most systems, which is held at pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies. For the states it names nobody. Whether a route to the theosophical or Vedantic material can be shown is open; the work does not supply one."
        ]
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the talk on the four states of consciousness (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; the scan does not carry its imprint, so the edition is left unstated rather than guessed)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteAloaeus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Aloaeus, Herrscher des zweiten Tores",
        "en": "Aloaeus, governor of the second gate"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Aloaeus",
      "pm:nameRole": "governor of the second gate, to whom the speaker brings the symbol of his mother, a grace hidden by the powers of the realms",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "de",
          "@value": "Ailoaios"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Eloaeus"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The finding that the diagram holds two distinct groups of seven, given in two separate chapters and never equated: the ruling demons in animal shape with angelic and barbarous names, and the named rulers of the gates to whom the speeches are addressed. No name occurs in both lists."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteDuaeHebdomades",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zwei Siebenheiten in einem Diagramm, nicht eine",
        "en": "Two heptads in one diagram, not one"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The single sentence that connects them is the remark that the star Phaenon is in sympathy with the lion-like ruler, placed directly after the speech to Ialdabaoth. It joins one member of each list and leaves the other six pairs unstated. A table that runs the two heptads together into one column of seven is doing what the source does not do."
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30-31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission divides dreams into witchcraft-dreams and oracular dreams, a nightmare being of the first kind and a pleasant dream of the second. A bad dream is held to be not a symbol of witchcraft but an actual undergoing of it: in waking life a man learns that he was bewitched only from a subsequent misfortune or from an oracle, but in a dream he sees the witches themselves and may speak with them. The soul is held to leave the body in sleep, and the witch to send the soul of his witchcraft to eat the soul of his victim while both sleep."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WitchcraftDreamIsExperienceNotSymbol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein böser Traum ist eine Erfahrung der Hexerei, kein Zeichen für sie",
        "en": "A bad dream is an experience of witchcraft, not a sign of it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it stands squarely against a claim this graph holds open elsewhere, that the figures met at the threshold of sleep are the signature of the sleeper's own state and not visitors. Here the transmission asserts the opposite in as many words. It is one side and not a refutation; the other side is not in this file, so no dispute node is written."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix II, pp. 230-231",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earlier step the later one is said to require."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:priorStep",
      "label": {
        "de": "vorausgesetzter Schritt",
        "en": "prior step"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office is not sought but suffered. The one marked for it is seized in infancy by an illness in which he is haunted by apparitions, and from these he learns, according to his years, what belongs to the art. Those seized a second time see more visions and gain greater knowledge. Those seized a third time — which is seldom without great torment or the utmost danger of life — arrive at the perfection of the art and become so knowing that without the drum they can see things at the greatest distances."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThreefoldSeizure",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die dreifache Heimsuchung",
        "en": "The threefold seizure"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A graded scale with a stated cost at each step and a stated end state, which is the material an examination is made of. The recorder's own explanation of the seizure is that the devil takes a liking to the person in infancy; that explanation is his and is not entered as the transmission's."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhakaHoldsOwnAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten behält die eigene Trägerschaft",
        "en": "Retention alone holds its own agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:ownAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The verse has the practitioner able to hold the breath at his own pleasure, and what follows is credited to that holding. Nobody else acts in these verses: there is no descent, no possession and no conferral, only a capacity said to be at the practitioner's disposal once the practice has taken."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office named first in the earlier report: those who see the fortune and outcome of what is asked of them. For this they killed birds, lambs and llamas, and blowing through a certain vein into the lungs found in them signs by which they said what was to happen."
      },
      "id": "pmp:LungReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Lesen an der Lunge des getöteten Tieres",
        "en": "Reading the lungs of the killed animal"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one procedure in this file that reads a variable natural object rather than a count. It is the Andean counterpart of the guinea-pig inspection that the later account gives under two other names, and the two together make entrail reading the best-witnessed technique of the transmission."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the calparigu",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The inhabitants of a town club together to fee a practitioner of the luck-giving order. A post of a named wood is cut, a hole made in its top and filled with the charm, a hole dug beside the road the women take to the water, a goat killed and its head laid in the hole under the post, the earth rammed round it and the goat's blood poured over the charm. No sacrifice is then made on a schedule; the charm is refreshed with more blood when a misfortune befalls the town."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NkindaAEvata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nkind' a evata, das Setzen des Ortszaubers",
        "en": "Nkind' a evata, the setting up of a town charm"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only.",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 220-221",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A bundle or a carved figure into which ingredients have been put, held thereby to bring a named spirit under the control of whoever owns it and to perform one named office: to make a thief invisible or to detect him, to give a child or to withhold one, to make a gun shoot straight or another gun shoot crooked. The corpus states that there is no good wish and no evil desire for which one cannot be bought, and that a new complaint brings a new cult with a new charged thing to control the power responsible for it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Nkisi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nkisi, das geladene Ding",
        "en": "Nkisi, the charged object"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered as a container and not as an object of worship because the corpus says so in as many words: there is held to be no idea of worship in the sacrifice made to one, and no prayers are offered with it. The Vili witness makes the same separation in his own terms, dividing the people who serve the charged figures from the priests of the sacred groves and holding that the old Portuguese took the first for the whole.",
          "The name is written nkisi by Weeks, nkixi in Bentley's dictionary of eighteen eighty-seven, and nkici with the plural zinkici by Dennett, who sets out his own orthography and warns that his x stands for a different sound than Bentley's. The identifier follows the form of the Lower Congo works."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 234-235",
        "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 92"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The knowledge of what the seed-star is made of, how heavy it is and how it moves, said in the account to have been unavailable to men while the star's existence was known but its characteristics unrevealed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis der Merkmale des Same-Sterns",
        "en": "Knowledge of the characteristics of the seed-star"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282 and 284",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The middle-way school of Buddhist philosophy founded on the verses of Nāgārjuna and its commentarial literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Madhyamaka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Madhyamaka",
        "en": "Madhyamaka"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: to the Śaiva and Śākta material a route exists and is not in dispute, so a correspondence with it would be reception and not a finding; towards the Greek or the Western esoteric material none is documented. Edition: the verses are read in Stcherbatsky's translation of 1927, named on the node, which carries the commentary of Candrakīrti alongside them; the Sanskrit has not been consulted. Number of citable passages: one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transfer of power from one who has it to one who does not, graded in the Kashmirian exegesis into nine degrees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Saktipata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Herabkunft der Kraft",
        "en": "Descent of power"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sources give transfer by glance, by touch and by word. The specifically manual modality of the modern draft is not attested here."
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SituatingArsNotoriaPrognosisAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Prognose wird bei abgetretener Handlung verortet",
        "en": "The prognosis is placed at ceded agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The text states the coordinate itself rather than describing a condition from which it has to be inferred: know that this miracle proceeds not from your own nature, but from the nature and virtues of the holy angels, it being a part of their office to reveal these things to you. The operator says the words and asks the question; what is said to act is named as someone else.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a person is not born with the bodies beyond the physical, that these can only be cultivated artificially and only where favourable conditions, inner and outer, are present, and that immortality therefore is not a property one is born with but something acquired."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FinerBodiesNotInnate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die feineren Körper sind nicht angeboren, sondern gezüchtet",
        "en": "The finer bodies are not innate but cultivated"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also fixes what the paths in pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal are for. If the goal were innate, a way would be a matter of recognising it; here it is a matter of building something, which is why the classification falls entirely on how much of a person is worked on. See prima-materia#65.",
          "The claim is stated against the traditions it borrows its terms from: most teachings that repeat the divisions are said to have forgotten or omitted precisely this feature. That makes it a correction offered to a corpus rather than a report of it, and it is the load-bearing move of the whole system — everything about ways, effort and time follows from a possession that must be made and can fail to be made."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The most important feature of an ancient teaching, lost by the systems that preserved its divisions, rather than a claim first published in this form in 1949.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the four bodies of man",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fourth quarter of the self, given by negation throughout: not inwardly cognitive, not outwardly cognitive, not both, not a cognition-mass, neither cognitive nor non-cognitive; unseen, ungraspable, without distinguishing mark, unthinkable, not to be designated. Its positive terms are the cessation of development, tranquil, benign, without a second. The passage closes by identifying it with the self and saying it should be discerned."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Turiya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Vierte",
        "en": "The fourth"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Nothing in the verse gives a criterion by which a reader could decide of a described condition that it is this one. Every mark is a denial, and the one positive phrase, without a second, is a claim about reality rather than about what the practitioner meets. A coordinate has to be decidable from a text; this is not, and that is the second reason it is a claim here and not a value there.",
          "The separation of verse from commentary is drawn more sharply above than the transmission can bear, and the correction belongs in the node rather than only in a review. The Upaniṣad is transmitted inside the Kārikā and the question of an independent transmission is open; whether that changes what the verse alone supports is what prima-materia#443 has to settle at the wording. Until it does, the sentence above states what the printed verse says and not that verse and commentary reached us apart.",
          "What the verse does not say is as important as what it says, and it is why this node exists rather than a coordinate on an axis. The text enumerates the fourth alongside the other three as a fourth quarter, and does not say there that it underlies them or that the other three are its appearances. The substrate reading is carried by Gauḍapāda's Kārikā and by Śaṅkara after him and is not asserted here on the strength of this passage."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 7, in Robert Ernest Hume (trans.), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads (London: Humphrey Milford, Oxford University Press, 1921), p. 392",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Upanisadic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of a body of practice concerning attention, dreaming and the assemblage point, presented as the teaching of a Yaqui man of knowledge."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CastanedaToltec",
      "label": {
        "de": "Castanedas toltekische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Castaneda's Toltec transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The fieldwork is not credible and the informant is not attestable, and the practices are nonetheless transmitted today by people who follow them. Both facts belong in the graph, which is why the tradition is registered rather than dismissed."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
