    {
      "id": "pmc:MayaIxYearRemedyAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Abwehr der Übel wird dem Gott zugeschrieben, nicht den Feiernden",
        "en": "The averting of the foretold ills is credited to the god, not to the celebrants"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The witness does not say that the offerings work, but that they were held to be very acceptable to the idols and remedial through that acceptance. The efficacy is placed with the receiving party; the celebrants supply the occasion.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The narrator relates that her father, called a great shaman, understood hidden things and perceived that the children she wanted to run out and play with were the souls of her dead brothers and sisters, whereupon his helping spirits set a barrier between her and them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FatherPerceivesHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dem Vater der Erzählerin wird zugeschrieben, die verborgenen Dinge verstanden zu haben",
        "en": "The narrator's father is said to have understood hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The case is worth the node because of its shape rather than its content. The perception is not reported as a vision or a journey but as a correction of an ordinary judgement: the child saw children, the shaman saw who they were. A claim in that form is answerable in a way that a claim about an inner state is not."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "The narrator Takornâq's father, named in the narrative as a great shaman",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 23-24",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The insertion of a needle at a named place on the body to a stated depth, held to move the qi of a depot or a vessel either towards fullness or towards emptiness. The corpus treats it throughout as an operation that can go wrong, and states the going wrong in the same register as the going right."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Cizhen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nadelung",
        "en": "Needling"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FourteenfoldMastery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen",
        "en": "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Marathi devotional transmission of the pilgrimage to Paṇḍharpūr, carried in the songs of its poet-saints rather than in a treatise."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Varkari",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vārkarī-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Vārkarī transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Jñāneśvarī; the abhaṅga of Tukārām, Nāmdev and Eknāth",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded order of ways of practice in which each is declared superior to the one before: the Veda above all others, the Vaiṣṇava above the Veda, the Śaiva above the Vaiṣṇava, the Dakṣiṇa above the Śaiva, the Vāma above the Dakṣiṇa, the Siddhānta above the Vāma, the Kaula above the Siddhānta, and nothing above the Kaula."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AcaraLadder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die aufsteigende Ordnung der ācāras",
        "en": "The ascending order of the ācāras"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chapter and verse are not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running structure the scan does not carry reliably. Work, section and edition are named instead, which meets the standard of findability; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different numbering. See prima-materia#45 and prima-materia#56.",
          "This is the passage Wilson translates in 1832 without naming a place in the work, and on which pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature rests. It is now located in an edition, so the order is a claim of the tantra and no longer only a claim of its reporter.",
          "Two things follow that the Western pair of names cannot carry. The order has seven steps and not two, so a scheme with two is not a simplification of it but a replacement. And within the order the left stands above the right, so the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses its valuation. Whoever explains the modern left-hand path out of this passage explains it out of a text that ranks the left higher than the right and both below two further steps."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Kulārṇava Tantra, in the section on the superiority of the Kula-dharma, at the verses giving the order of superiority of the ācāras (text with English translation by Ram Kumar Rai, Varanasi 1999)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A capacity claimed in the performance and not outside it: the shamans, though not all of them, may say what the persons present are thinking or doing, and in almost all performances they divine in some form."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusKnowledgeOfTheHidden",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wissen um Gedanken und Taten der Anwesenden",
        "en": "Knowing what those present are thinking or doing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Both explanations are the recorder's and neither was tested. The claim is entered because it is the one capacity in this file that could be examined against a base rate, and the passage is the address at which such an examination would start.",
          "The recorder gives two cases and a counter-explanation for each. In the first a shaman accused a young man of having eaten the kidney of a sacrificial animal and ordered it given back, and the man vomited into the drum; the recorder believes the shaman could not have seen the act and offers instead that he could infer which of the young men present was likely to have done it. In the second a shaman took a coin from a sceptic and the coin was afterwards found in the sceptic's own hand; the recorder proposes that it was handed back unnoticed. He states in the same passage that in a great number of cases he did not find the shamans conscious of tricking their audience."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 121, pp. 331-332",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsParakayaPravesa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama bringt den Eintritt in einen anderen Körper hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama yields entry into another body"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The root class of all ongoing acts modelled by prima-materia. Every substantive class in the ontology is a subclass of pm:Process."
      },
      "id": "pm:Process",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prozess",
        "en": "Process"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Three places in the same canon give different intervals for the same strike, and a fourth witness gives a fourth set. The needling-prohibition chapter has the kidney at six days, the lung at three and the spleen at ten. The chapter on the essentials of examination has the heart dying within one circuit rather than in one day, the spleen at five days, the kidney at seven, the lung at five, and adds a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover. The Zhenjiu jiayi jing has the kidney at three days, the spleen at fifteen, and gives the liver's sign as a different character from the one the received Suwen prints. The Song editors record in their collation notes that the older recension of Quan Yuanqi agreed with the Jiayi jing on both numbers, and that Wang Bing changed the liver's sign."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CizhongIntervalDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wie lange nach dem Treffer ins Depot der Tod eintreten soll",
        "en": "How long after a depot is struck death is said to follow"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The commentators do not treat the numbers as observations. The note beside the spleen argues from ten being the completion number of earth, and other commentators have wanted to correct liver and lung to the generation numbers of wood and metal. A number defended from a numerology is not thereby false, but it is not evidence of a counted interval either, and a protocol on this claim would have to begin there.",
          "The consequence for this graph is narrower than the dispute. Nothing here says that the intervals are wrong; what it says is that a citation naming one chapter conceals that the same canon carries three answers, and that the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of the six places.",
          "The dispute is entered because the transmitted text states it about itself and states a verdict on it. The collation note printed with the chapter on the essentials of examination says that these three discourses are all the words of Qi Bo and that their differing is an error of transmission. That is one party's account of the divergence and not a neutral finding, and the graph keeps it as such."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:CizhongWuzang"
      },
      "source": [
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 16 (Zhen yao jing zhong lun), with the Xin jiao zheng collation notes, in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
        "Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing, juan 5 (Zhen jiu jin ji), in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect the knotting is performed for: that the fits, counted by the charm as seventy-sevenfold, leave the body at the place the crosses are made."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung",
      "label": {
