      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published zagovory collections; the Latvian dainas collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rate against which observed outcomes are compared, with the population it was taken from."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:baseRate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Basisrate",
        "en": "base rate"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim itself, stated in the abstract and worded so that a result could contradict it."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Generalizing",
      "id": "pm:generalizedStatement",
      "label": {
        "de": "verallgemeinerte Aussage",
        "en": "generalized statement"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A wording that covers an outcome and its opposite alike is the failure pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate records after the fact. Here the cost is lower: it can be caught while the sentence is being written."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An attempt to fail the claim that a misrecited ritual utterance does not obtain its effect. The claim is unusually well placed for this, because two of the three traditions name an outward event that follows a lapse, and one of them names who notices it and when."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfprotokoll zur Genauigkeitsregel",
        "en": "Protocol on the exactness rule"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The Navajo witness supplies the sharpest form of the test and it needs no laboratory: at the public ceremony many present know the songs, an error is caught, and the ceremony is broken off as fruitless. The condition can therefore be failed by the tradition's own reporting rather than by an outside measurement, which is what the falsifier below states.",
          "The examination is entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised, and the difference is the whole point of the scale. A condition has been stated here; nobody has yet collected the cases against it."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the three witnesses; no cases collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case, from within one of these transmissions, of a ritual utterance known to have been misrecited and nonetheless reported by that transmission as having obtained its effect; or, on the Navajo side, a recorded public ceremony in which a detected error did not break off the proceeding.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-08-30",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsSexusInfantis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Oration offenbart das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes",
        "en": "The oration reveals the sex of the unborn child"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaSexusInfantis"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that because the goddess takes for herself the share offered to others, worship performed in the heterodox method never results in clearing the debt to ancestors, sages, gods and the human race; and that one who becomes an expert in the tripurā practice attains liberation only after a very long time and is reborn on this earth again and again, having been visited by the sin arising from the uncleared debt."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HeterodoxWorshipNeverClearsTheDebt",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die heterodoxe Methode tilgt die Schuld nie",
        "en": "The heterodox method never clears the debt"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A failure condition of the kind this project looks for, and an unusually exact one: the source states the price of its own path, names the mechanism, and says what remains unattained. It does not warn against the rite. It says what the rite costs, which is the harder thing for a tradition to write down about itself.",
          "It also settles what the two methods are weighed on in this text. Not purity, not moral standing, not the direction of the will: the debt. The orthodox method discharges it and is rewarded in due course; the heterodox method leaves it standing and pays immediately instead. Whoever takes the left hand path here for the faster route has read half of it — it is the faster route to power and the slower one to liberation, and the source says both in the same breath."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:VamaWorship"
      },
      "source": "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses stating that heterodox worship never clears the debt (132-133a) and that liberation comes only after a very long time with repeated rebirth (133b-135a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MayaOfficePresupposesZuyuaNaat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Matte und Thron setzen das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua voraus",
        "en": "The mat and the throne presuppose understanding the language of Zuyua"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the text sets no lesser outcome beside it. There is no passage naming a second attempt, a number of chiefs to be examined, or a remedy for failure."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The source states the consequence for the whole procedure and states it as death, not as exclusion: those who die are those who do not understand, those who live will understand it. The punishments are named in a sequence. The hands of those who fail are bound before them to a wooden collar and they are pulled along with the cord; they are taken to the house of the ruler; they shall be hung by the neck, the tips of their tongues shall be cut off, their eyes shall be torn out. The chapter adds an omen for the case that the chiefs of the towns do not understand: ill-omened is the star adorning the night.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PopDzam"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuyuaNaat"
      },
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 91-93",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A tradition attesting the correspondence for which no route of contact is documented, so that its testimony is not the same statement counted a second time."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:independentAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "unabhängige Bezeugung",
        "en": "independent attestation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earlier definition called this the only kind of attestation that makes a correspondence a finding. It is not: it is the kind that makes the count honest. A correspondence carried by reception is still a finding about what was carried; what it may not be is a tally of independent witnesses."
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SarielTeachesSignsOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel offenbart die Zeichen des Mondes",
        "en": "Sariel discloses the signs of the moon"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Given under the name Sahriel; stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the course of the moon."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheMoon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Sariel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of a final state in which the mother returns within the fullness and receives the saviour as her spouse; those who call themselves spiritual are stripped of their souls, made purely intellectual spirits, and become brides of spiritual angels; the creator, being merely animal, moves into the mother's place; and the souls of the righteous rest in an intermediate place."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ValentinianConsummation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vollendung als Hochzeit, nicht als Verschmelzung",
        "en": "The consummation as a marriage rather than a merging"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Placed on the telic axis as pm:conjugalTelos. The placement is a reading and carries the same burden as the claim it sits on: it rests on the bridal state reported in the passage, which has two parties and therefore neither one term nor an absolute. It is stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted.",
          "Recorded because it does not fit either end of the axis it is regularly assigned to. The spiritual ones are not dissolved: they are stripped and transformed, but a bridal state presupposes two, so what is transformed persists as a party to it. Nor are they made into absolutes of their own: they are married, not set apart. The end is a union that keeps what it unites, and that is a third case, distinct from the one in pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution, where the creature stays with itself against dissolution rather than entering a twoness. See prima-materia#70 and prima-materia#52.",
          "The passage is a report by an opponent and is attested as such. What can be said on this source is that the doctrine was ascribed in this form in the second century, not that its holders stated it so. See the caution on pmt:ValentinianGnosis.",
          "Two further things in the same passage that a summary loses. The demiurge is not destroyed but relocated, which makes the account less anticosmic than its modern reception; and the souls of the righteous, who are not of the spiritual class, are neither saved into the fullness nor lost, but given a place between. A scheme with two outcomes cannot hold either detail."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:telicOrientation": {
        "id": "pm:conjugalTelos"
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I, in the account of the Valentinian consummation, at the passage on the mother's return, the brides of the angels and the removal of the creator (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ValentinianGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of first putting forward a claim that no transmission carries, in a work that can be named and dated, where the claim afterwards circulates as though it were old."
      },
      "id": "pm:Originating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Urheben",
        "en": "Originating"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which arranges material that was already there and names what it arranges. An origination arranges nothing: the claim begins with it. Holding the two apart is what keeps the record from reporting an invention as an ordering of something ancient, which is the commonest way a modern construction acquires a warrant.",
          "The circulating ascription is the point of the class, not an addendum to it. A node that names only the author and the work is a bibliography; a node that also names what the claim is taken for is the finding, and it is the kind of finding this project exists to make."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The north Indian transmission of an inner sound held to be audible under instruction, and of union without image or rite."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SantNada",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sant- und Nāda-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Sant and inner-sound transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Kabīr granthāvalī; Ādi Granth; Nāda-bindu Upaniṣad",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Gabonese transmission of an initiation carried by a root preparation, in which the initiate is expected to meet the dead and to report the meeting."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Bwiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bwiti",
        "en": "Bwiti"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The expected content of the experience is stated in advance, which makes the tradition unusually well suited to an examination of what such expectation determines."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Fernandez, Bwiti: An Ethnography of the Religious Imagination in Africa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Abulafia's transmission of letter permutation, breath and head movement as a technique for reaching a state he calls prophecy."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PropheticKabbalah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prophetische Kabbala",
        "en": "Prophetic Kabbalah"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered apart from Kabbalah because it is a manual and not a doctrine. On the level of stated procedure it is the closest western counterpart to the Yogasūtra that exists, including its warnings about what befalls the unprepared."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Abulafia, Or ha-Sekhel; Abulafia, Ḥayyei ha-Olam ha-Ba",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Nāṭyaśāstra and the theory of aesthetic states that follows from it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sanskritische Dramaturgie",
        "en": "Sanskrit dramaturgy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both citations the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter division of the Nāṭyaśāstra differs between the recensions, so the number seven does not by itself locate the passage on the bodily signs."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Nāṭyaśāstra VII",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArmarosTeachesLoosingOfSpells",
      "label": {
        "de": "Armaros offenbart das Lösen von Beschwörungen",
        "en": "Armaros discloses the loosing of spells"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:LoosingOfSpells"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Armaros"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Norse transmission of runic and sung operations and of a divinatory practice performed from a raised seat with a stated arrangement of singers and questioners."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NorseSeidr",
      "label": {
        "de": "Altnordische Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Old Norse ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Hávamál names eighteen effects in list form, which is a capacity catalogue in the same shape as the siddhi lists. Written down by Christians centuries after the practice, so the contact question is a question about the recording hand."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hávamál; Grímnismál; Snorri, Ynglinga saga; Eiríks saga rauða",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AhwalAreTransient",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zustände vergehen",
        "en": "The states pass"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:transientState"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Ahwal"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The same passage has them pass as well as descend, and the two are separate claims: a gift may be kept. The Sufi material answers both questions the same way, which is why one axis sufficed until a tradition answered them differently."
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, printed after the charm against powder and ball, that whoever carries this booklet is safe from all his enemies, visible and invisible; that whoever has it with him cannot die without the whole corpus Christi, cannot drown in any water, cannot burn in any fire, and that no unjust sentence can be spoken over him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Schutz des getragenen Büchleins",
        "en": "The protection of the carried booklet"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The claim stands word for word in the Romanus-Büchlein of seventeen eighty-eight and again in the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, and in both it says this booklet. The referent therefore moves with the reprinting: the sentence that guarantees the bearer of one book is carried over unchanged into another and guarantees the bearer of that one. Nothing in either printing marks the transfer.",
          "This is the passage the transmission is most reliably misreported on. It is regularly given as a Pennsylvania German claim of eighteen twenty; it was in print in Silesia thirty-two years earlier. The title page of the Reading printing does not in fact claim otherwise: it says the greater part is still little known and appears in print in America for the very first time, which is a claim about America and not about first publication. The English title page of eighteen fifty drops in America from the first half of that sentence and thereby makes the wider claim the German never made."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 66, S. 29, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An Iranian transmission teaching successive bodily lives as a purification, carried in Gorani verse."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yarsan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yarsanische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yarsan transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kalām-e Saranjām",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Irish and Welsh transmission of poetic knowledge as an acquired faculty, with named divinatory procedures and the ecclesiastical prohibitions attached to them."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InsularCeltic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Inselkeltische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Insular Celtic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prohibitions are in the same manuscripts as the procedures, so procedure and counter-testimony arrive together."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The legal and glossarial material on imbas forosnai, teinm laída and díchetal do chennaib; the immrama; the Book of Taliesin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A documented route of historical contact between the traditions in which the correspondence appears."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:transmissionPath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übertragungsweg",
        "en": "transmission path"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The earlier definition here said that the presence of a route downgrades the correspondence from convergence to reception. That was a judgement wearing the clothes of a definition, and it is withdrawn. A route says how a claim travelled, not whether anything was in it. Survival through many hands is selection, because most claims do not survive it, and a record that treats transmission as noise throws away the only evidence it has about what kept being worth copying.",
          "What the route does decide is narrower and stays in force: an agreement reached along it may not be counted as independent attestation, because the same statement would be counted twice. Both may stand on one node, and on several they already do. The route bars a particular way of counting; it does not devalue the correspondence."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practice a yielding claim proceeds from."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Yielding",
      "id": "pm:byPractice",
      "label": {
        "de": "durch Praxis",
        "en": "by practice"
      },
      "range": "pm:Practicing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from lightning, given in place of astrology in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfLightning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Blitze",
        "en": "Signs of the lightning flashes"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Standing before a sick person who lies languishing and saying the oration of the physical art three times with great reverence, and then asking him how he feels himself."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Oration der Heilkunst, vor dem Kranken gesprochen",
        "en": "The oration of the physical art said before the sick"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The asking is part of the procedure and not an afterthought. Without it the oration yields nothing that could be read, and with it the reading is fixed in advance: the text sets out the answers and what each of them decides."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26-30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prohibition attached to a named place: garments of new caribou skin may not be sewn on the island of Oqshoriaq."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verbot, auf Oqshoriaq neues Karibufell zu nähen",
        "en": "The prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on Oqshoriaq"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prohibition is local and specific, which distinguishes it from the general taboo system the later chapters of the volume set out. Whether it is a case of that system or a rule of its own cannot be decided from the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Islamic mystical path, systematised in the handbooks of the tenth and eleventh centuries."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Sufism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sufismus",
        "en": "Sufism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For both works the edition or translation the passages were read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. Neither citation carries a page or section number, and the printed editions of the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ and the Risāla divide their material differently, so the passages are to be reopened before anything in this file is cited by place."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operation the founder offers as the means of that distinction, and he claims for it both exclusivity and certainty in one sentence: this means is the only one, but it is infallible, because there is no bad communication that can withstand a rigorous critique. The marks it works with are given as a numbered list, among them that good spirits never command but advise; that they do not flatter, while bad ones praise extravagantly and stimulate vanity while preaching humility; that an elevated spirit does not contradict itself across time and place; and that good spirits say only what they know and are silent or confess their ignorance, while bad ones speak of everything with a false assurance."
      },
      "id": "pmp:CritiqueRigoureuse",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die strenge Kritik einer Mitteilung",
        "en": "Rigorous critique of a communication"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim of infallibility is the strongest single sentence this transmission makes and the one most open to examination, because it asserts that no false communication can survive the procedure. It is entered in the graph as it stands, without softening, since the value of holding it is that it can be held against cases."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 266-267",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The combined application of dhāraṇā, dhyāna and samādhi to a single object, presented as the general operator from which the capacities of the third chapter follow."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Samyama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama",
        "en": "Saṃyama"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-3",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source only presents the material in a sequence. Any necessity is the reader's, and belongs to whoever read it that way."
      },
      "id": "pm:presentationOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Darstellungsreihenfolge",
        "en": "Presentation order"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome attached to the ordeals in the same report: after passing through all of them in succession the candidate at last receives the highest initiation if he is still alive, and an honourable burial if he has succumbed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicProbatioLetalis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übersteht er es, die höchste Weihe; erliegt er, ein Begräbnis",
        "en": "If he survives, the highest initiation; if he succumbs, a burial"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A stated condition of failure, and the scarcest kind in this graph: not a sign to watch for but an outcome that excludes the other. Almost everything else the ascent material holds tells the practitioner what he will see; this tells him what happens if it does not work, and the answer is that he dies. That it comes from a late and hostile commentator is the reason it is held here with the caution and not as an established practice — but a Christian polemicist has no incentive to invent an ordeal his opponents survive.",
          "The Augustan History cuts the other way and is worth holding beside it: the rites, it says, normally involved saying or feigning something that would produce an impression of terror, and Commodus defiled them by killing for real. If that is right, the ordeals were mimed and the deaths were not expected — and the eighty-grade report has mistaken the mime for the thing."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:MithraicProbationes"
      },
      "source": "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen, with Historia Augusta, Life of Commodus 9 (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission of divination by counting stalks down to a remainder, of a parallel counting on a different modulus, and of the siting of graves on the doctrine that the bones of the parents receive the qi of the ground and that the descendants are shaded by it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChineseDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chinesische Divinations- und Siedlungslehre",
        "en": "Chinese divinatory and siting transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second reason the old value could not stand. The works read span the first century before the common era to a redaction of the fourteenth: the burial book as read is the recension Wu Cheng made from the eight sections Cai Yuanding had left of twenty, and it is first attested in the Song bibliography. Absent asks whether a route was available at the time the corpus was fixed, and for that end of the corpus the answer is not the same as for the Xici.",
          "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance, and the reading bore the registration out on both halves. Fristen and stated outcomes are dense here: the burial book says that where the configuration is inauspicious the misfortune does not turn a day, and it names what a given shape of ground brings to the descendants, down to the extinction of the line. What the registration did not say is that the same book carries six adversities under which a favourable site produces nothing, and that the commentary on the sixth enumerates the delays, disputes, poverty and wars through which a burial fails to happen at all. Every non-occurrence has a place to go.",
          "Edition note. The Changes were read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; that text names no printing, and the citations say so rather than inventing one. The Taixuanjing and the Lunheng were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, the Zangshu and the Jiu Tangshu in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text.",
          "Read in this run at the wording, in the Chinese: Zhouyi, Xici A, sections on the great expansion and on what the Changes are without; Zhouyi, hexagram 4; Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh; Zangshu, the whole of the single juan of the Siku recension, inner, outer and miscellaneous sections with its interlinear commentary; Lunheng 24, the treatise on divining; Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial on the burial books. The Taixuanjing outside the counting chapter, and the rest of the Lunheng, were searched by term and not read continuously.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the dispute is in the literature rather than in the sources. The graph already holds three transmissions that read a figure off an ungoverned remainder — the Ifá casting, the Fa casting and the geomantic tradition — and the counting of the Changes has the same form on a different modulus. One side derives the Arabic sand art from Chinese material through Needham's transmission routes; the other reconstructs it inside Abbasid Mesopotamia and states that the Chinese derivation was tried and dropped. Neither side was read at the wording in this run, and nothing here decides it; what the value records is that the question is contested and open, which is the opposite of what absent asserted."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhouyi with the Xici commentary; Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing; Zangshu, in the recension of Wu Cheng; Wang Chong, Lunheng; the memorials of Lü Cai in Jiu Tangshu 79",
      "source": "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rack set at the foot of the candidate's bed of sage is of cedar because the cedar is favoured by the Winged God, who will not visit one protected by it nor cause such a one to act foolishly. Cedar leaves and twigs are also sprinkled over the altar in the dance lodge, where they are said to ward against the contrary conduct of the Winged God and of the heyoka."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CedarWardsTheWingedGod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zedernholz hält den Geflügelten Gott fern",
        "en": "Cedar keeps the Winged God away"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is worth holding because it is the counter-move to the obligation on the neighbouring node: the same transmission that says a communication can bind a man for life also names a material that prevents it. A third means is named in the Hunka ceremony, where the waved wands are said to shield a man from seeing the person of the Winged God and so from being made heyoka. Three protections against one outcome, from three different rites, all in the same paper."
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 70 and p. 110 and p. 133",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1975",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the Egyptian term for becoming, coming into being, personified in the scarab god and signifying self-generation and the dawn, names the transformation and evolution of the will, and that it is uttered as the word of a new age succeeding the age of indulgence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Xeper als das Wort der Selbsthervorbringung",
        "en": "Xeper as the word of self-generation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination because of the shape of the claim rather than its content. The word is Egyptian and its lexical sense is not in dispute; what begins in 1975 is its use as the formula of a path, in a sequence of ages with named predecessors. The Egyptological reference is present in the source and does not make the doctrine ancient.",
          "This is the end state the milieu sets against dissolution, and it is worth recording in the author's own terms rather than in the summaries: not the preservation of an ego as ordinarily meant, but a self-generation modelled on a god who brings himself forth. A record that reports it as mere ego-preservation reports the criticism and not the claim."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A doctrine recovered from Egyptian antiquity, reported as the teaching the word is drawn from rather than as a formula first uttered in 1975.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Michael A. Aquino",
      "source": "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An ascent in thirty steps, running from renunciation and exile through the active virtues to stillness, prayer, freedom from the passions and love, and passing in its course from human labour to what is given."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Leiter der dreißig Stufen",
        "en": "The ladder of thirty steps"
      },
      "source": "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An experiential account given in the first person by the one who had it, recorded once."
      },
      "id": "pm:firstPersonReport",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erfahrungsbericht",
        "en": "First-person report"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteSabaoth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sabaoth, Herrscher des fünften Reiches",
        "en": "Sabaoth, governor of the fifth realm"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Sabaoth",
      "pm:nameRole": "powerful governor of the fifth realm, defender of the law of his own creatures, who are liberated by his grace through a more powerful pentad",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of ritual handbooks for the constraint of spirits, carrying preparation, circle, seal, adjuration and dismissal as a fixed sequence."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SolomonicGrimoire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Salomonische Grimoire-Tradition",
        "en": "Solomonic grimoire transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
