      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Clavicula Salomonis; Lemegeton; Heptameron; the Book of Abramelin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What a path says becomes of the bounded self at its end: whether the self is to be dissolved into what exceeds it, strengthened and preserved as its own absolute, or whether the path declines the question. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."
      },
      "id": "pm:TelicOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung des Zieles",
        "en": "Telic Orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The values name what a source claims, not what happens. Nothing on this axis is examined by holding it; where somebody takes up the examination, that is a pm:Testing node pointing at the claim, and the examination states of the two values are not symmetric.",
          "This is the axis the Western terms were built to carry, and the axis they carry badly, because they carry the other one at the same time. A record that wants to state where a path claims to end has to be able to state that without also stating how it proceeds. See prima-materia#52."
        ]
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LimbPracticeYieldsLightOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Übung der Glieder bringt das Leuchten der Erkenntnis hervor",
        "en": "Practice of the limbs yields the light of knowledge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sūtra makes the arising conditional on the wearing away of impurity, which is itself said to come from the practice. The condition is part of the claim and is not to be dropped when the claim is quoted."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:LightOfKnowledge"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of consciousness-only doctrine together with the most detailed Indian description of meditative signs and the stages at which they appear."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yogacara",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yogācāra",
        "en": "Yogācāra"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Asaṅga, Yogācārabhūmi, particularly the Śrāvakabhūmi; Vasubandhu, Triṃśikā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment of the path, held here to record that no procedure for failing it has been devised."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SeparativeEndStateExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der absondernde Endzustand überhaupt geprüft wurde",
        "en": "Whether the separative end state has been examined at all"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No pm:falsifiedBy is given, and the omission is the permitted one: the state says outright that no procedure has been devised. Everywhere else its absence would let a claim drift from unexamined to examined-and-unsupported.",
          "The asymmetry is worth naming because it is easy to mistake for evidence. States of dissolution have measurable correlates and a literature; the end state claimed on the other side has neither instrument nor operationalisation. That is a fact about the instruments. Whoever infers from it that one path is better attested than the other has read the reach of the measuring apparatus as a finding about the paths. See prima-materia#73.",
          "This node exists to prevent a confusion, not to report a result. Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted, and that is a statement about the means of examination and not about the claim. A record that left the protocol out entirely would say the same thing by silence; a record that reported the claim as unsupported would say something else and something false."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-29",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment Celsus reports gate by gate: the leaden gate to Saturn for the slowness of that star, the tin one to Venus for brightness and softness, the bronze one to Jupiter as firm-based and solid, the iron one to Mercury because iron and Mercury alike endure every work, make money and are laborious, the alloyed one to Mars because a mixture is uneven and varied, the silver one to the Moon and the golden one to the Sun, after the colours of the two."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jedes Tor einem Planeten zugeordnet, mit einer Begründung aus seinem Metall",
        "en": "Each gate assigned to one planet, with a reason from its metal"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Celsus gave the reason for the sequence and the witness withheld it. Origen reports that he went on to examine why the stars stand in this order, that musical reasons out of Persian theology were adduced, and that a second musical explanation followed — and then declines to quote any of it as beneath him. What is lost is exactly the part that would say whether the order is arbitrary, harmonic or observational. The project's own criterion names this gate: an order held back is a gate, and here it was held back by the transmitter rather than by the tradition.",
          "The order is the test, not the resemblance. Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, Moon, Sun is neither the received planetary sequence by distance nor the order in which the ophite speaker meets his gates. A transfer from here to there would have had to change the order while keeping the idea, which is a stronger claim than a borrowing and is not made by anyone yet.",
          "This is the only place in the whole ascent material of this graph where a late antique witness distributes planets over seven gates one at a time. The ophite diagram does not: it counts by gates and realms, and the single heavenly body named anywhere in it is Phainon, in sympathy with the lion-shaped ruler of the other, differently named hebdomad. Irenaeus I.30.9 equates the hebdomad with the planets collectively and never pairs a name with a star. Every modern table that seats Ialdabaoth on Saturn is therefore filling one witness from another, and this is the nearest candidate for where the filling came from — nine chapters earlier in the same book, in a passage whose author expressly invites the comparison of the two diagrams. See prima-materia#595 and prima-materia#641."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition that an act fall on a named weekday, before sunrise, and in a named quarter of the moon: Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the written slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism, Thursday morning before sunrise and in the open air for the binding of a thief."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond",
        "en": "The window of weekday and moon"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27, 29, 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32, 41"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the Aramaic-Greek teaching list as the one who disclosed the signs of the shooting stars. The position he occupies is held by Ezeqeel in the Ethiopic; no identification of the two is asserted here."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ziqel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziqel",
        "en": "Ziqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Ziqel",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The book opens with the sentence that burying is riding the living qi. The five qi run within the earth and issue forth to generate the ten thousand things; the qi scatters when it rides the wind and halts where it is bounded by water, and because the ancients gathered it so that it would not scatter and moved it so that it would halt, the thing is called wind and water. The living is a gathering of qi which congeals into bone and remains alone after death, so that burying is the method of returning the qi into the bone in order to shade what it generated."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Zang",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bestattung, die das lebendige Qi reitet",
        "en": "Burial that rides the living qi"
      },
      "source": "Zangshu, inner section, opening, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cutting three chips at the place the thief went out, going to a wagon unbeschrauen, taking off a wheel, putting the chips into the hub and driving the wheel backwards while the compelling words are spoken."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drey Spänlein in der Rad-Nabe",
        "en": "The three chips in the wheel hub"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 83-84",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 52-53"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Among the Tungus of Transbaikalia the shaman's paraphernalia are laid with the body or hung on a tree standing nearby. It is not recommended to touch them: those who do may get mental diseases, they may lose their minds."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTouchingTheBurialGear",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was am Grab bleibt, nicht anrühren",
        "en": "Do not touch what is left at the grave"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusParaphernalia"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The interpretation the living Jesus gives of sending the earth up to heaven: that he who hears the word of gnosis has ceased to have the understanding mind of a man of earth and has become a man of heaven, his understanding mind having ceased to be earthly and become heavenly; and that because of this he is saved from the ruler of this aeon, who becomes the Midst, because it is nothing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuHomoCaelestis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der irdische Sinn hört auf, und ein himmlischer steht an seiner Stelle",
        "en": "The earthly mind ceases and a heavenly one stands in its place"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A change of what someone is, stated as such and not as an achievement or a permission. The text is asked how heaven is brought down and answers by describing a state rather than an operation, twice in the same passage.",
          "The last clause resists paraphrase and is left as it stands: the ruler becomes the Midst, because it is nothing. Whether the Midst is a place, a rank or a privation is not decidable from what was read, and the corpus uses the term elsewhere in chapters that were not opened."
        ]
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 3 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of what was put into the bodies of John and of the disciples: into Elizabeth a power taken from the little Jao of the midst, and in place of the soul the soul of the prophet Elijah, brought by the light-virgin and her receivers and cast into her womb; into the disciples the twelve powers of the twelve saviours of the treasury, in place of souls of the rulers, so that they were born without souls of the rulers in them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaDuaeVires",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Kraft anstelle einer Seele",
        "en": "A power in place of a soul"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is that what the disciples have instead of a soul of the archons is a power from the treasury, and that this is why they are not of the world. It is the same reading the Books of Jeu give at their own second chapter — the ruler finds nothing of his own in the one he faces — arrived at from the other end, by construction rather than by knowing."
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 7-8 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether a strong affect shifts attention, readiness and the threshold for risk, and of what remains of the claim once it does."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AffectCouplingProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Verschiebung eintritt, und ob die Behauptung etwas über sie sagt",
        "en": "Whether the shift occurs, and whether the claim says anything about it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A convergence worth following separately. Several transmissions warn against exactly this coupling on the ground that it deceives the practitioner about his own position, and traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl already carries pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle in that shape. If the direction of the shift is unknowable in advance, the warning and the finding say something close to the same thing from opposite ends, and that is a candidate for a convergence node rather than a remark here.",
          "Counter-search, carried and not favourable to either side. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, while the association between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The opposition of directions survives; its symmetry does not.",
          "On the state. This protocol is the case that made pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate necessary, and it is deliberately not recorded as cases without deviation. There the claim says something and the cases bear it out; here they bear it out because the claim does not say enough to be borne out against anything. Filing it as an ordinary confirmation would have added a success to a record that had in fact found the claim empty.",
          "That the shift occurs is not in doubt and the claim is borne out in that narrow sense. Strong affect measurably moves risk judgement and the choices that follow from it, in induced and in naturally occurring form alike.",
          "The consequence for anyone acting on the claim, which is the reason to enter this node at all. Whoever couples an intended state to a strong affect in order to shift his own threshold does not know from the claim whether he has raised or lowered it. Confirmation at this level of generality is not usable guidance, and the node says so rather than recording a success.",
          "What the evidence takes away in the same motion. The direction of the shift is set by the particular affect and not by its strength. Fear and anger are both strong and both unpleasant, and they move the threshold for risk in opposite directions: the fearful judge risk pessimistically and choose cautiously, the angry judge it optimistically and choose boldly, with the angry closer to the cheerful than to the fearful. A claim that names only the strength of the affect therefore cannot say which way anything moves, and is compatible with every outcome."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Carried. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, the one between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The finding is in the notes above. Not searched: later meta-analytic work on the framework as a whole."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Beyond valence: toward a model of emotion-specific influences on judgement and choice, Cognition and Emotion 14 (2000), no. 4, pp. 473-493",
        "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Fear, anger, and risk, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 81 (2001), no. 1, pp. 146-159, doi:10.1037/0022-3514.81.1.146"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off experimental work on specific emotions and their appraisal tendencies, and off a registered replication of it",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Strong affect, induced or occurring, leaves risk judgement and the choices following from it unmoved against a neutral condition."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier"
      },
      "pm:undividedOutcomes": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A threshold for risk that has been raised, and one that has been lowered. The claim covers both alike, so acting on it cannot tell the practitioner which of the two he has brought about."
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SpiritismPrinciplePresupposesControl",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Grundsatz gehört zur Lehre erst nach der allgemeinen Kontrolle",
        "en": "A principle enters the doctrine only after the control of generality"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the passage on which the registration's judgement rested, and it holds: a transmission that writes down the condition under which it would change is doing something almost nothing else in this collection does. What the next section of this file records is what the same author did with that opening elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The consequence is stated as a disqualification and is quotable: any principle that has not received the consecration of the control of generality cannot be considered an integral part of the doctrine. The same article adds the other half, that if new discoveries showed spiritism to be in error on a point, it would modify itself on that point.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ControleUniversel"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, article 55",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether the effect measured in the practice survives the removal of its forceful-breath component."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HeatWithoutBreathProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die sanftere Form dieselbe Hitze erzeugt",
        "en": "Whether the gentler form produces the same heat"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No new evidence was gathered for this protocol and none was needed. The measurements already held at pmc:TummoHeatExamination separate the two components and report the temperature rise as depending on the forceful breath together with the visualisation, with the visualisation alone yielding the lesser effect. The claim that the breath may be dropped is therefore addressed by evidence that has stood in this file since the twenty-eighth of August.",
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The finding matters more than its size. Where a draft and its source transmission differ on a component, and the difference is exactly the component the measurements isolate, the draft is testable at that seam and almost nowhere else. Removing a prescribed element and claiming the effect survives is the one kind of modification that instruments can settle.",
          "What is not settled. That the gentler form yields less heat is not that it yields none, and the studies were not designed to find the floor. The deviation recorded here is from the claim of sameness and not from the claim that the gentler form does anything at all."
        ]
      },
      "pm:baseRate": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Temperature change under the visualisation alone, against the change under visualisation together with the forceful breath, in the same participants."
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not carried. The component separation was read off a single study and no search was made for work that failed to reproduce it."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "M. Kozhevnikov et al., Neurocognitive and somatic components of temperature increases during g-tummo meditation, PLoS ONE 8(3) (2013), e58244, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0058244",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The separation of the two components is the design of the second study already cited in this file",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Practitioners of the gentler form show the same rise in measured body temperature as practitioners of the form with the forceful breath."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-02",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CommunicationMustBeInterpretedByAShaman",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Mitteilung setzt einen Deuter voraus",
        "en": "The communication presupposes an interpreter"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says that the seeker ought to consult a shaman even where the communication is apparently intelligible and easily understood, and that a candidate for the Sun dance must consult his mentor and be guided by him, but it names no consequence for the man who interprets his own. What it does name is one possible content of the interpretation, and that is entered in the definition because it is a stop rule.",
          "The stop rule: the communication may prohibit the candidate from dancing the Sun dance, and if it does he is to proceed no further in the matter. A ceremony that can be called off by the very thing it is undertaken to obtain is unusual enough in this graph to be recorded where it stands."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Wowihanble"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:ShamanicInterpretation"
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 68-69",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteGabriel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gabriel, der Adlergestaltige",
        "en": "Gabriel the eagle-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Gabriel",
      "pm:nameRole": "fourth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, eagle-like",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The circulation is to be done in the hours of living qi and not in the hours of dead qi. The day and night hold twelve hours; the six from midnight to noon are living qi and the six from noon to midnight are dead qi, and in the hours of dead qi, the chapter says, circulating the qi is of no benefit."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ShengqiSiqi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die lebendigen und die toten Stunden",
        "en": "The living hours and the dead hours"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also, read the other way, the cheapest possible defence: any session without result can be assigned to the hour. Both readings are true of the same sentence, and which of them the transmission intends is not decided here.",
          "This is the sentence for which the chapter was worth opening. It states a condition under which the practice does nothing, it binds the condition to the clock rather than to the practitioner, and it therefore names an outcome the practice excludes: a benefit obtained in the afternoon. Almost nothing else in this file does that."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Geez transmission of protective prayers written on strips of parchment and worn on the body, in which lists of divine and angelic names are said to keep off named harms, and in which the name of the person the roll was written for stands in every prayer."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Äthiopisch-orthodoxe Zaubertradition",
        "en": "Ethiopian Orthodox magical transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Contact route kept at documented, and now carried twice over. The registration argued it from the Enochic book, whose fullest witness is the Ethiopic one and which this graph already holds. The reading adds a second and closer route: the story that grounds the seventh prayer, in which Susenyos on horseback kills the child-killer Werzelya and takes an oath from her, is the Ethiopian station of a legend transmitted in Greek, Coptic, Syriac, Armenian and Slavonic. That the parallels exist was established in the landscape stage of this run and not read at the wording; the comparative works are named in an issue rather than asserted here.",
          "Read for the first time in one edited volume: the nine prayers of Lady Meux MS No. 5, written for the woman Aheta Mikael, and the three supplementary rolls printed with them in the same volume. The English translations were read at the wording, page by page, from p. 216 to p. 220 for the Meux roll and from p. LIV to p. LXV for the three supplementary rolls, the third of which is a litany that runs on past the point where the summaries of the other two end. What was not read: the Geez texts printed at the end of the volume, because the reading is of the translation and the edition is named accordingly; and the greater part of the volume, which carries the Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary and the Life of Hanna and is a different corpus.",
          "The remaining works named in the principal corpus were not opened in this run. The Lefafa Sedeq is the chief absence and it is a large one: Budge printed it separately in 1929 and that volume is in full view on HathiTrust under the identifiers of the Indiana and Michigan copies, so it is reachable in the sense of SPEC 15 and its absence here is a property of this run and not of the work."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Lefafa Sedeq; the asmat scroll corpus; the Geez canon",
      "source": "E. A. Wallis Budge (ed.), The Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Life of Hanna (Saint Anne), and the Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (Lady Meux Manuscripts Nos. 2-5; London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, pp. 216-220",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A python of one particular kind, held to be a god and kept in a temple at Whydah with free exit through holes in the walls, so that the animals move about the town and the bush. They are carried back only when they have entered a profane place, such as the house of a European, and then by a priest who purifies himself by rubbing fresh green leaves between his palms and prostrates himself before the animal before taking it up. Every python of this kind is to be treated with veneration wherever met, and a man of Whydah who meets one in his path prostrates himself, rubs his forehead in the earth and addresses it as master, father and mother."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DanhGbi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Danh-gbi, die heilige Python",
        "en": "Danh-gbi, the python held sacred"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held apart from the boa said to be the messenger of the rainbow god, of which the same source states that only certain individuals are to be respected. The distinction matters for the prohibition on the neighbouring node: it applies to a kind and not to a single animal."
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 57-58",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."
      },
      "id": "pm:acquisitionNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erwerb nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Acquisition not stated"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateAcquisition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Greek and Latin historiographical and doxographical reports of extraordinary capacities."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GraecoRoman",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechisch-römisches Zeugnis",
        "en": "Graeco-Roman testimony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The book and chapter numbers are stable across the modern critical editions, which is why the loss is smaller here than elsewhere; it is still a loss, because it is unknown whether the passages were read in the original or in translation.",
          "The file records that a route to Indian material has been proposed for the older Greek transmission and not shown. Disputed is therefore the state of the question, not a hedge."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to see what is to come, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the pictographic chart by lines drawn upward from the eyes."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideForesight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Blick in das Künftige",
        "en": "Looking into futurity"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PotencyPresupposesTheSongsThatBelongToIt",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gebieten über eine Potenz setzt ihre Lieder voraus",
        "en": "Commanding a potency presupposes its songs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: two informants in the same volume make obedience conditional on the songs and not merely customary, and the second witness reports the same rule attached to a particular stone, whose song was transferred with it and was to be sung whenever a request was made of it. The consequence is stated as a condition on the obedience rather than as a punishment, which is the strongest form this corpus uses."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The chosen potency must do as it is directed by the one who chose it, but the chooser must know the songs that belong to it; a man should know the songs, and if he sings them his potency will do as he wishes."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sicun"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SingingTheSongsOfAPotency"
      },
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 211",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 158 and pp. 158-159"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EvocationAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Operation verortet die Handlungsträgerschaft beim Geist",
        "en": "The operation places the agency with the spirit"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:Evocation"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The medium supplies the hand or the voice and the content is the spirit's. The transmission makes the point itself at the place where it matters: the spirits decide whether to answer, they withdraw from a question they dislike, and they give proof only when it suits them. What the sitter controls is the form of the question, not the reply.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement among editors about the age of the inscription on circulating the qi and about how its opening is to be construed. One dating places it in the late fourth or early third century before the common era, another in the middle of the sixth; one reading takes the opening characters as a title and begins the text at the second line, another counts and construes differently."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiInscriptionDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Datierung und Lesung der Jadeinschrift",
        "en": "The dating and reading of the jade inscription"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node rather than as a remark, because the dating decides whether this is the earliest datable witness to deliberate circulation of the qi or one witness among several, and because a citation that names only the inscription conceals that anything is at issue."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:Xingqi"
      },
      "source": [
        "D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim against what the transmissions that report the journey say about its duration."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WilledDisplacementProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Ortswechsel so unmittelbar ist, wie die Behauptung verlangt",
        "en": "Whether the displacement is instantaneous in the way the claim requires"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A transmission in this holding says the opposite, and it says it in the one form that can be compared. pmc:DurationTracksDistance records that the time the practitioner lies still is stated to depend on how near or far the place is, an hour or two for a near one, with an upper bound of a day however great the distance. A journey whose duration follows the distance is not a displacement by willing; it is a movement with a speed.",
          "How much weight this bears, stated plainly. It is a seventeenth-century report of what practitioners said, not a measurement, and it is one transmission among several. It does not refute the posit. What it does is show that the draft's account is not the one its sources give, at a point where the sources happen to be specific — and specificity of that kind is rare enough in this field that it should not pass unremarked.",
          "No procedure devised, for the same reason as at the neighbouring node: the duration of the journey is only reachable through the traveller's report, and the claim is about what that report describes. The falsifying condition would require an independent fix on when the traveller arrived, which is the whole question."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Nobody. The comparison with the transmission is recorded in the notes and is not an examination; no protocol has been devised that could be run",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:IntentionReplacesMusculature"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The consultation in which a shaman renders what a communication means. The doctrine holds that shamans are the vicars of the gods, that communications from them are usually in mystic form, and that the shaman's interpretation is authoritative."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ShamanicInterpretation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Deuten einer Mitteilung durch einen Schamanen",
        "en": "Having a communication interpreted by a shaman"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 68-69 and p. 135",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The correspondence between the Mithraic ladder of metal gates and the ophite gate speeches: both divide the ascent of the soul into gates passed one at a time in a fixed order, and both are preserved in the sixth book of one refutation, nine chapters apart. Only the Mithraic account assigns a planet to each gate; only the ophite account gives the words spoken there."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConvergingGatedAscentInOneWitness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dieselbe Sieben-Tor-Architektur, zweimal in einem Buch, und nur einmal mit Planeten",
        "en": "The same seven-gate architecture, twice in one book, and only once with planets"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The order is what a transfer would have to explain. Lead-Saturn, tin-Venus, bronze-Jupiter, iron-Mercury, alloy-Mars, silver-Moon, gold-Sun is not the sequence in which the ophite speaker meets his gates, and it is not the received order by distance either. A borrowing that reversed or reshuffled the order while keeping the pairing is a larger claim than a borrowing, and nobody has made it.",
          "There is no independence here of any kind. One author set the two side by side to make a polemical point, and a second author preserved both while calling the pairing tendentious. The whole correspondence therefore lies inside a single channel, and it counts as one occurrence for any purpose that counts witnesses.",
          "This node exists to keep the two apart, not to join them. The architecture is shared and the halves are complementary in a way that invites exactly the merger this graph refuses: one account has the planets and no speeches, the other the speeches and no planets, and a table that seats each ophite ruler on a planet is the merger, performed silently. See prima-materia#595."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Both accounts stand in Origen, Contra Celsum VI, at chapters 22 and 31, and both are quoted from the same lost work of Celsus, who adduces the Mithraic ladder expressly so that the Christian rites may be examined alongside it. No transmission between the two cults is claimed; what is documented is that the pairing itself is the work of the reporting author.",
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of several thousand readings given in a sleep-like state, most of them addressed to a named person about a stated condition, and taken down stenographically."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CayceReadings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Cayce-Readings",
        "en": "The Cayce readings"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one corpus in this block built for examination without intending to be: individual readings, individual recipients, dated, indexed, and with outcomes that were sometimes followed up. Whatever it yields, it yields countably."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The indexed Cayce readings held by the Association for Research and Enlightenment; Sugrue, There Is a River",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MideDegreesPresupposeOneAnother",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jeder Grad setzt den darunter liegenden voraus",
        "en": "Each degree presupposes the one below it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passages read rather than of the society. The report sets the sequence, names the payment and the feasts due before each step, and says nothing about what happens to a man who takes a degree out of turn or without the preparation. A consequence would raise this to a necessity; none was found in the pages read. See prima-materia#119."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-170",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Cuban transmission of Kongo derivation, working through a charged vessel and a pact with a named spirit of the dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PaloMonte",
      "label": {
        "de": "Palo Monte",
        "en": "Palo Monte"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Cabrera, Reglas de Congo",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol the tradition states for itself. The claim is that the questions separate men of the ruling lineage from men who are not, and the tradition acts on the claim to the point of execution. The outcome is binary and is applied on the day."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuyuaExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zuyua-Verhör als Abstammungsprobe",
        "en": "The Zuyua interrogation as a test of descent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A route by which a European examination genre could have reached this material exists and is documented: a Yucatec translation of the Spanish question-and-answer book of the emperor Adrian and the boy Epitus stands in a colonial Yucatec manuscript. Nobody has set the Zuyua catalogue against that literature. See prima-materia#460.",
          "What the protocol cannot survive is the document that preserves it. The manuscript that reports the examination also prints the answers, so from the moment of that writing the procedure separates those who have read this book from those who have not, and no longer those of the lineage from the rest. A test whose key is published measures access to the key. This is a property of the record and not a modern objection to the tradition, and it is the reason the state stands at procedure without cases rather than higher."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case in which a man of the ruling lineage failed the questions, or in which a man not of that lineage answered them. The tradition claims the questions decide descent, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuyuaNaat"
      },
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The repeating unit of the account: each Jeu has a name, a character, three watchers, and twelve emanations; there are twelve ranks in each treasury, six heads on one side and six on the other turned towards each other, twelve heads in each rank and twelve in each emanation, and the ranks stand one outside the other endlessly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuThesauriStructura",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was jeder Rang der Schatzkammern trägt",
        "en": "What each rank of the treasuries carries"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The count that circulates for this corpus, sixty treasuries, does not come from anything read here. It is reported from a hymn later in the text and is not entered.",
          "The unit repeats without variation through the chapters that were read, and the excerpt states that it goes on repeating for the twenty-four chapters that were not. Whatever else this text is, it is an inventory, and an inventory of this shape presupposes that someone was meant to hold the entries apart."
        ]
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 5-10 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practice, at the public night ceremony, that many of those present know the songs well enough to catch a deviation, whereupon the ceremony is broken off as fruitless and the gathering disperses."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoPublicErrorDetection",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zuhörerschaft als Fehlerinstanz",
        "en": "The audience as the detector of the error"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The part of the claim that makes it examinable rather than merely asserted. The tradition does not only say that an error destroys the effect; it names who notices, when, and what visibly follows. Neither of the two parallel witnesses in this bundle carries that."
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The statement the text makes about its own procedure: that this miracle proceeds not from the operator's nature but from the nature and virtues of the holy angels, it being a part of their office wonderfully to reveal these things to him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaVirtusNonExNatura",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wunder geht nicht aus deiner eigenen Natur hervor",
        "en": "The miracle proceeds not from your own nature"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A transmission that says outright where it locates the acting is uncommon, and this one says it in the middle of an instruction rather than in a prologue. It is why the placement on the agency axis beside this file rests on the wording and not on a reading of it."
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A warning against treating the given states as a further ladder: they descend and pass, and no course of effort produces them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:StatesAreNotOrdered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zustände werden nicht der Reihe nach erlangt",
        "en": "The states are not attained in order"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Ahwal"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Fon and Ewe transmission of powers received into a person and of bound objects that act, together with the Fa oracle, in which sixteen palm nuts are cast until a figure is read off the count that remains in the hand."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Vodun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fon-Vodun-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Fon vodun transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds: the eastern Ewe are said to divine after the manner of the Yoruba god of divination, and the Fa practitioner is identified by a second and independent witness with the Egba babalawo. An agreement between the Fa figures and the Ifá figures is therefore reception and may not be counted twice.",
          "Read in this run at the wording: Ellis 1890, printed pages fifty-seven to fifty-nine, ninety-one to ninety-eight; Skertchly 1874, printed pages three hundred and seventy-nine to three hundred and eighty and four hundred and seventy-three to four hundred and seventy-six; Burton 1864, volume two, printed pages one hundred and forty-three to one hundred and forty-five. The remainder of each work was searched by term and not read continuously.",
          "The recorder problem. Two of the three are visitors of weeks or months who wrote for a European public and did not command the language; the third is a colonial officer and linguist who corrects the other two on terminology and who was himself in the country. Where they agree the agreement is worth something, because they did not work from one another on these points; where they differ, as they do on the width of the divining board and on the reading of the marks, the difference is recorded rather than smoothed.",
          "Two works that carry the centre of this transmission were not reachable in this run and the corpus names them all the same. The chapter in which Burton describes the casting in detail stands in volume one, and neither reachable digitisation of that volume carries it; the volume-two text read here refers to it. The systematic work on the oracle, Maupoil 1943, has no free full text at all, and the Fa chapter of Herskovits 1938 stands in a volume two that is available only on loan. The file therefore rests on two travellers and one missionary-linguist, which is a thinner base than the Kongo file has, and says so."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa; Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome; Skertchly, Dahomey as it is; Spieth, Die Ewe-Stämme, and Die Religion der Eweer in Süd-Togo; Le Hérissé, L'ancien royaume du Dahomey; Maupoil, La géomancie à l'ancienne Côte des Esclaves; Herskovits, Dahomey: An Ancient West African Kingdom",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity that an attribution ascribes."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Attributing",
      "id": "pm:ascribedCapacity",
      "label": {
        "de": "zugeschriebenes Vermögen",
        "en": "ascribed capacity"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of bracelets and ornaments, of antimony and the painting of the eyelids, of precious stones and colouring tinctures."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schmuck und Kosmetik",
        "en": "Adornment and cosmetics"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whoever comes into contact with the poison abstains from sexual relations, from the flesh of the elephant, from fish, from two glutinous vegetables named in the source, and from smoking hemp. Sexual abstinence is set at five or six days and abstinence from the forbidden foods at three or four, though the source records that the period is not fixed and that men give different estimates."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeTaboos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Tabus vor der Berührung des Orakelgifts",
        "en": "The taboos kept before touching the oracle poison"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A man asked to operate who has not kept the sexual taboo says that he has eaten the first of the two vegetables, and everyone understands the euphemism. The source adds that he may use the same words when he simply does not want the work, which makes the phrase a refusal as well as a confession."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 132-133",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The named compiler who performed the ordering act. Without it, an order made in the present is indistinguishable from an order found in the source."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Systematizing",
      "id": "pm:compiledBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "geordnet von",
        "en": "compiled by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NineYearsForTheSuccessor",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Nachfolger binnen neun Jahren",
        "en": "The successor within nine years"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at stated necessity: a period is named and the outcome of letting it pass is named with it. A dated deadline with a stated consequence is the material a condition of failure is made of, and this graph holds few of them.",
          "The observed cost of an unfilled interval is reported separately in the same section: the free clan spirits try one person after another and make them abnormal, sometimes for years. The recorder watched such a case daily for nearly two months among the Khingan Tungus."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The new shaman who gathers the spirits left free must appear not later than nine years after the shaman's death; otherwise there will be many people suffering from the spirits."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusFormalRecognition"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, pp. 382-383",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the moon's path, the calendrical rather than divinatory form of lunar knowledge."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CourseOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Lauf des Mondes",
        "en": "The course of the moon"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it: it sets out a practice and its results without saying what becomes of the bounded self at the end."
      },
      "id": "pm:telosNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziel nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Telic orientation not stated"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required on every subject axis by SPEC §17, and the case it covers is the ordinary one. A handbook of technique may name a dozen capacities and never state an end state at all; recording that as absence would put it in the same bucket as every text nobody has opened yet."
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a piece of blue vitriol placed in a hollow tooth drives out the severest toothache, held against the fact that toothache remits of itself. The count the source gives is the tradition's own and is treated here as the claim, not as the examination."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BlueVitriolToothacheExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das gezählte Zahnschmerzmittel die Spontanbesserung übertrifft",
        "en": "Whether the counted toothache remedy beats remission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No modern work has been opened for this protocol, and none is cited. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases: naming a state that reports cases would claim a reading that has not been done, and the dental and toxicological literature on copper sulphate is the obvious place to begin and belongs to whoever takes it up.",
          "The author states that he does not know whether the remedy works on teeth that are not hollow. That limit is part of the claim and narrows the protocol rather than weakening it: a series run on sound teeth would test something the source never asserted.",
          "The population the count runs over is unsettled between the two printings and is entered as a dispute beside the claim. Until that is decided the ratio cannot be compared with anything, because a man counting his own toothaches and a healer counting other people's are not measuring the same thing."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A series of applications on hollow teeth in which the pain is recorded before the application and at a fixed interval after it, with the failures kept alongside, showing no larger fall than in teeth left untreated over the same interval. The source's own ratio of one failure in more than sixty is the figure to beat and is not itself such a series, because it carries no untreated arm and no interval.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-05",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesMondfenster",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Knüpfen setzt das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond voraus",
        "en": "The knotting presupposes the window of weekday and moon"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaHoldsOwnAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama behält die eigene Trägerschaft",
        "en": "Saṃyama holds its own agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:ownAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The base text has the practitioner bind his own mind and hold it, and the capacities of the third chapter are said to follow from his doing so. Nothing in these sūtras has another speak or act through him; what is claimed is credited to the practice."
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of quiet sitting and of the investigation of things as a discipline of attention that claims no transcendent object."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NeoConfucianCultivation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neokonfuzianische Selbstkultivierung",
        "en": "Neo-Confucian self-cultivation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a control condition. A contemplative practice with the same postures and much of the same phenomenology, and without the metaphysical claim, is what any examination of the others needs to have alongside them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhu Xi, Zhuzi yulei on jingzuo; Wang Yangming, Chuanxilu; Mencius 2A.2 on the flood-like qi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteHoraeus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Horaeus, der über den Feuerwall sprang",
        "en": "Horaeus, who overleapt the rampart of fire"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Horaeus",
      "pm:nameRole": "ruler of the first gate, who fearlessly overleapt the rampart of fire, and who beholds the symbol sculptured on the figure of the tree of life",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "de",
        "@value": "Horaios"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vierte Grad bringt das Gedankenlesen hervor",
        "en": "The fourth degree yields the reading of thoughts"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol against the claim that a man publicly named at the felling of a muamba tree dies within ten days, and that this never fails."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TenDayTermProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokoll: die Zehntagesfrist des Gerufenen",
        "en": "Protocol: the ten-day term of the named man"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case is entered and none is claimed. The work read supplies no list of namings and no follow-up, and the practice was already being suppressed by the colonial administrations at the time the work was written. What a later run would need is a source that names an occasion and a person rather than the rule, and the candidates are the Laman notebooks and the Mayombe literature, neither of which was opened here.",
          "Taken up because the claim states its own falsifying condition, which is rare enough in this holding to be worth a protocol even where no case can yet be entered. The condition is not the death but the term and the completeness: a single named man alive after ten days refutes the claim as the corpus states it, and the corpus states it as exceptionless."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia compiler, from the wording of Dennett 1906, p. 93",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A man publicly named at the felling of a muamba tree who is alive on the eleventh day, in a source that names the occasion and the person rather than the rule."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-08",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TheNamedManDiesWithinTenDays"
      },
      "source": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A practitioner who appeared with a new charged thing at the towns round San Salvador and held a rite in the form of a communion, with slices of cassava and peanuts in palm wine. The recipient paid first, one string of beads for a child and five for an adult, then confessed every evil desire he had had for the sickness or death of another, then received the cassava, the peanut and a drop of the wine, and with them the promise that he should never die."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NkisiAKiniambe",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nkisi a kiniambe, der Kult, der den Tod aufhob",
        "en": "Nkisi a kiniambe, the cult that promised no death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The name is analysed by the recorder himself: the word for God on the Lower Congo is Nzambi and the form kiniambe is an upper river name for God, so a new charged thing was made under a borrowed divine name.",
          "The same recorder gives two different outcomes in two works, and the discrepancy is the finding. In nineteen hundred and nine he writes that when the people died the practitioner said it was because they had not made a full confession, and that he and his accomplices made a large sum out of the fear of death. In nineteen fourteen he writes that when the clients died like others the bubble burst and that cult and its charged thing fell into disfavour and neglect. Immunisation and collapse are the two possible responses to a failed promise, and here one witness reports both about one cult; the file records the discrepancy rather than choosing between them."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 234",
        "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), p. 57"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is said to arise, on the wearing away of impurity, and to continue up to discriminative discernment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Leuchten der Erkenntnis",
        "en": "The light of knowledge"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that one who bears his virginity and his garment without understanding and knowing him, and who blasphemes his name, is given over to destruction, and has become an earthly son because he has not known the word with certainty."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuKautelIgnorantiae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gewand tragen, ohne ihn zu erkennen",
        "en": "Bearing the garment without knowing him"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also answers a question the ophite material leaves open. There the passer shows a symbol and the gate opens; here having the garment and lacking the knowledge is an explicit case, and it goes badly. Whether the two corpora would agree about it is not asserted.",
          "The nearest thing to a condition of failure that the whole ascent material of this graph has produced so far, and it is worth being exact about how near. It names an outcome for a specific case — the bearer who does not know — and thereby says that bearing the garment is not sufficient. It does not name a sign by which the bearer or anyone else would recognise the case, so it still cannot be missed. The passage is damaged at this point in the excerpt read."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere"
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 4 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The bridge at which the accounting falls due. In the account of the journey under mang it is called very wide and strong, and its width for the traveller who has passed becomes nine javelin-lengths."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CinwadPuhl",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Činwad-Brücke",
        "en": "The Chinvat bridge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The measure is stated in a unit and is therefore comparable, which most of what the transmissions in this graph say about such passages is not. What the chapters read do not give is the counter-measure, the width for the one who fails; that stands in another book and was not opened here."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Chinvat"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Cinvat"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Cinwad puhl"
        }
      ],
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 4.7 and 5.1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the breath is drawn in over the tongue, protruded past the lips, and released through the nostrils."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sitali",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śītalī",
        "en": "Śītalī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An ongoing attempt to fail a claim under a stated condition. A protocol that names no condition under which the claim would be refuted is not a test of it."
      },
      "id": "pm:Testing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfen",
        "en": "Testing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "An instance exists only where somebody has taken the examination up, and its absence is therefore informative rather than empty: it says that no procedure has been taken up, not that the claim has been examined and found wanting. A stub for every claim in the collection would double the graph and say nothing the absence does not already say."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the source says happens if the prior step is passed over."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:consequenceOfSkipping",
      "label": {
        "de": "Folge des Überspringens",
        "en": "consequence of skipping"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required wherever the strength is pm:statedNecessity. Claiming that a text sets a necessity obliges the claimant to say what the text says goes wrong without it; a necessity nobody can quote a consequence for is an order read as a necessity."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second utterance, said after silence and before the disk expands: that the speaker is a star whose course runs with the course of those addressed, shining anew out of the depth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvIdentitasAstri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ich bin ein Stern, dessen Bahn eure Bahn ist",
        "en": "I am a star whose course is as your course"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A bare statement of what the speaker is, addressed to powers, with no request attached. It is the same shape as the ophite speeches without their closing petition, and it stands in a corpus with a documented route to that one rather than at a distance from it — so it is a second occurrence and not a second witness.",
          "The claim is of kinship in motion and not in rank: the course is the same course. Whether that is what the ophite speaker means when he names the symbol he already bears is exactly the question this file cannot settle, and it is why the two are held apart."
        ]
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, second utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NoaidiJourneyAnchoringIsNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise gibt keine Sinnesbindung an",
        "en": "The journey states no sensory anchoring"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:anchoringNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The passage was read for this question. It describes the practitioner from outside — he casts himself down, grows black in the face, lies as if dead, sweats — and says nothing about what he perceives while he lies there. An outside description of immobility is not a statement about the sensory channel, and reading one into the other would put a coordinate in the record that the source does not carry."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ReturnPresupposesUnbrokenSinging",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rückkehr setzt voraus, dass der Gesang nicht abbricht",
        "en": "The return presupposes that the singing does not stop"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and twice over: the singing must not cease and no one may stir him, and no fly or living creature is suffered to touch him. The recorder adds that the watching is for this reason and not, as another writer held, out of fear that the devil would take the body away; he names that writer and rejects him. The correction is his and is recorded as his."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "He would never recover out of the trance if they either ceased singing, or any one stirred him with hand or foot."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 56-57",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The correspondence between the hermetic account of the descent, in which each of the seven rulers gives Man a share of his own ordering, and the ophite account in which the ascending soul must give back or show what belongs to each ruler's realm."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConvergingDescentAcquires",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was die Seele trägt, wurde beim Abstieg aufgenommen",
        "en": "What the soul carries was taken on during the descent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Recorded because the asymmetry is the finding. A reader who meets the ophite speeches alone cannot tell whether the symbols are acquired, innate or conferred by a rite, and the received accounts settle that question with a doctrine borrowed from the other side of this node.",
          "This is the assumption that makes the seven-station ascent intelligible at all, and only one of the two witnesses states it. The hermetic treatise says outright that the rulers gave shares and that Man became a sharer in their nature; the ophite speeches presuppose that something is owed or shown at each gate and never say where it came from. The correspondence is therefore asymmetrical in evidence, not only in wording."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:HermeticDescensusPerHarmoniam"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Same milieu as above: Graeco-Egyptian, second to third century. No priority is asserted between the two accounts, and the dating of the Poimandres is itself disputed.",
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what becomes of the bounded self at the end of the path, carried on inside the modern occult literature with the same term on both sides. One party holds that a self which is not surrendered ossifies and is consumed by time, and calls that the second death; the other holds that a self brought forth and made to endure is the attainment, and names it with a word of self-generation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhetherTheSelfIsKeptOrSurrendered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Bewahren des Selbst das Ziel des Weges ist oder sein Scheitern",
        "en": "Whether keeping the self is the goal of the path or its failure"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One asymmetry the record should not smooth. The earlier side values the matter from a unitive end and treats the surviving self as a remainder that time consumes; the later values it from a separative end and treats the same survival as the work itself. Neither is a misreading of the other. A continuity story running from the one to the other as a single tradition with a changing mood reports a break as a development.",
          "That is why the disagreement is worth a node. Read as a quarrel about practices it dissolves into nothing, because neither side defines the path by practices. Read as a quarrel about the end state it is sharp and not decidable from inside either work, because each describes the same movement and calls it by the opposite name. See prima-materia#61 and prima-materia#68.",
          "The sides do not differ over means, and both say so. The one states that the signpost of a right-hand and a left-hand path is barely legible, that the two look alike for a long way, and that the question is at which point they diverge; he puts the divergence at the crossing of the Abyss and separates the sorcerer from the brother of the left-hand path as sharply as a thief from a tyrant. The other derives his word from an Egyptian term for coming into being and applies it to the transformation of the will, not to any particular practice. Whatever is at issue between them, it is not method."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:XeperAsSelfGeneration"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. Crowley, Magick Without Tears, chapter XII, on the left-hand path and the black brothers (electronic text of the collected letters)",
        "M. A. Aquino, The Temple of Set (Temple of Set, San Francisco), in the account of the Word Xeper and its Egyptian derivation"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The opening stage of the inscription: the breath is taken deeply, whereupon it gathers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiDepth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Tiefe als erste Stufe",
        "en": "Depth as the first stage"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A movement of about eighteen hundred and seventy-two, brought through the country by men from Luanda, which induced town after town to destroy every charged thing on a stated argument: since sickness and death come by the black art, there would be no more suffering and death if every charm were destroyed and none made again. Houses were searched and hiding places ransacked, and the movement proclaimed a code of law beside the burning."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kiyoka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kiyoka, das Verbrennen aller geladenen Dinge",
        "en": "Kiyoka, the burning of all the charged things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A prediction about the world, made inside the transmission, acted on at the scale of whole districts, and refuted by the event. The corpus states the outcome and the response in one movement: the prevalence of sickness and death as before convinced the people that their hopes were not realised, and they concluded that many had kept their charms in spite of everything, so that the correctness of the theory was unquestioned and the failure was put down to the treachery of those who had hidden them.",
          "The account was first printed in the compiler's dictionary appendix of eighteen ninety-five under the entry kiyoka and is repeated in the narrative of nineteen hundred; the second recorder quotes it from the appendix rather than reporting it himself, so there is one witness here and not two."
        ]
      },
      "source": "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 290-292",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Repeating the names of the fifth section of the roll at the moment when a man wishes to thrust a spear into the speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatSpokenAgainstTheSpear",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die beim Speerstoß wiederholten Namen",
        "en": "The names repeated at the thrust of the spear"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect an invocation is held to obtain, which the closing petition asks the invoked ancestor to grant."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Karakia",
        "en": "The efficacy of the karakia"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 11-12",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The canonical Chinese transmission of needling, of the qi and the vessels, and of the spirits held to reside in the depots, set out as dialogues between the Yellow Lord and his interlocutors and carrying, alongside the doctrine, its own prohibitions, its own intervals to death and its own signs by which an operation is said to have taken."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon",
        "en": "Chinese medical canon"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The received text is not the Han-period text but the recension Wang Bing completed in seven hundred and sixty-two as the editors of ten sixty-seven passed it on, and that recension is constituted by its commentary: the commentary printed with the opening chapter quotes the Laozi twice and names the Heshanggong commentary on it, and the commentary elsewhere in the same juan quotes the Zhuangzi. The Laozi and the Zhuangzi are the corpus the Daoist file in this graph rests part of its material on. An agreement between this file and that one is therefore not two witnesses at the level of the transmitted text, whatever may hold of the stratum beneath it.",
          "Edition note. Both classics were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, which is the received text and not a critical edition: for the Suwen the Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen with Wang Bing's commentary and the Song collation notes, for the Lingshu the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing. The digitisation used names the collection and not the printing within it, and the citations say so by naming the collection. Where a reading is contested the contest is entered as a node and not smoothed into the citation.",
          "Read in this run at the wording, in the Chinese: Suwen 1, 14, 16, 26, 52 and 54, and Lingshu 8, 9 and 73, each in the juan named in the citations below. The Song editors' collation notes printed with Suwen 16 and Suwen 52 were read with them and carry the dispute node in this file. The parallel in the Zhenjiu jiayi jing was opened for the same passage and is cited as the second witness there. The remainder of both classics, and the whole of the Nanjing, were searched by term and not read continuously; what that turned up and did not verify stands in the issues named in the harvest note, not here.",
          "The correction is narrower than the value can express, and the difference matters for any convergence built on this file. What is documented is a route at the point where the corpus was fixed in the form read, in the eighth century and again in the eleventh. Whether the first-century material that the compilation rests on had such a route is a separate question, and it is not decided here; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers. The corresponding question for the Indian and the Greek material was searched and stands as its own issue."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing; Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing; Yang Shangshan, Huangdi neijing taisu",
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from meteors, given in place of the knowledge of the clouds in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfShootingStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sternschnuppen",
        "en": "Signs of the shooting stars"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The restoration of a withered hand and of sight, ascribed to the touch of a ruler in Alexandria."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HealingByTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Heilung durch Berührung",
        "en": "Healing by touch"
      },
      "source": [
        "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A procedure for sending a soul out of a living body and getting a report back. The transmission states it as a sequence with numbers. From the whole assembly seven men without doubt are set apart, from the seven three, from the three one; the lot is cast three times, under the names good thought, good word and good deed, and all three fall to the same man. He prays for the departed, eats, makes his testament, washes head and body, puts on new clothes, perfumes himself, has a clean carpet spread on a prepared couch, consecrates the dron and remembers the souls, and eats again. Then three golden cups of wine and the mang of Wishtasp are given him, one spoken over with each of the three names."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MangIWistaspan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Trunk aus Wein und dem Mang des Wischtasp",
        "en": "The drinking of wine and the mang of Wishtasp"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order in which the preparation is given differs slightly between the verses as numbered and the sequence a reader reconstructs; the testament stands close to the prayer, and the second meal after the consecration. What is fixed and not reconstructed is the count: seven, three, one, three lots, three cups."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "bang"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "mang"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "mang i Wishtaspan"
        }
      ],
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 1.28-43 and 2.18-31",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When a man or woman is struck by a sudden illness, loses judgement and is left as if mad, the senior ministers say that this has come because the huacas want him for their villac and priest; and on his coming to himself they make him fast and learn the office. The ground given is that they themselves lose judgement when they speak with the huacas. From this all who fall in fits are counted as chosen for these ministries."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SeizureTakenAsCall",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein plötzlicher Anfall gilt als Ruf ins Amt",
        "en": "A sudden seizure is taken as a call to the office"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transmission gives its own explanation of the sign, and the explanation is an inference from the practitioner's state back to the novice's: the office is recognised by the symptom that the office produces. The recorder adds his own competing explanation, that the loss of judgement comes from the devil or from the strength of the chicha they drink before speaking with the huaca. Both are recorded; neither is the graph's."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A limit named as the end of twelve, at which the mind or the risen power comes to rest. The verses give the number and leave the unit unstated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dvadasanta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ende der Zwölf",
        "en": "The end of the twelve"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The count itself fluctuates. What is verse 50 in the numbering used here appears as 51 in other editions, and the following verse shifts with it. A reader working from a different edition should expect the two verses cited here to sit one number further on.",
          "Two things this node deliberately does not say. It does not fix the unit: the base text says end-of-twelve, and the reading that makes this twelve finger-breadths from the tip of the nose belongs to the commentarial layer, not to the verse. And it does not fix the place: the exegesis of Lakṣmaṇjoo counts several such limits in the body, at the crown, the brows, the throat, the base of the heart and the navel, and the verse that follows says outright that it may be reached in any manner and in any place. Nothing here yields a fixed radius. See prima-materia#38."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 50 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 51 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PranayamaPresupposesAsana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemregulierung setzt Festigkeit in der Haltung voraus",
        "en": "Breath regulation presupposes firmness in posture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The verse opens the breath chapter by requiring that the practitioner first be established in posture and in a measured diet, and that the breath practice then follow the instruction of a teacher. The condition is in the base text, not in a commentary."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Asana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.1 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Shield, breastplate and the sword for slaughter, disclosed as instruments of killing rather than as craft."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WeaponsOfDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Waffen des Todes",
        "en": "The weapons of death"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ManomayakayaIsDisplaced",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der geistgemachte Körper ist ein verlagerter Ort",
        "en": "The mind-made body is a displaced locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:displacedLocus"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The source has a second body, complete in its faculties, produced from this one and going where this one does not. That is the criterion for the displaced value; whether anything of the sort occurs is a separate question and not this one."
      },
      "source": "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stated condition under which the claim counts as refuted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:falsifiedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "widerlegt durch",
        "en": "falsified by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account in which the same seven names appear not as gatekeepers but as a descent: the first offspring of the mother is Ialdabaoth, from him Iao, from him Sabaoth, the fourth Adoneus, the fifth Eloeus, the sixth Oreus, and the seventh and last Astanphaeus. They are said to sit in heaven in the order of their generation and to rule invisibly over things celestial and terrestrial; the mother holds the eighth place."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteGenerationesIrenaei",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Sieben als Zeugungskette",
        "en": "The seven as a chain of generations"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The witness carries no speeches and no gates. What the first witness gives as a liturgy of passage, this one gives as a genealogy, and the only thing common to both is the set of names. That is the finding: the names are attested twice, the practice once."
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.5 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about a claim, carried on within a tradition, between traditions, or among the scholars who edit their texts."
      },
      "id": "pm:Disputing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bestreiten",
        "en": "Disputing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Without this class a disagreement can only be recorded by silently preferring one side, or by leaving the claim out because it is contested. Both losses are worse than the disagreement. A dispute node names what is at issue and cites each side, and a dispute with only one source cited is not a dispute."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1900",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account given in the introduction to the volume, in which the amulets are explained by a single principle: that wherever the name of a good or evil spirit, or of a man, existed, there also was the presence of that spirit or man, so that the presence of a name is equivalent to the presence of the being; and that the Ethiopians, having become Christians outwardly, retained their fear of demons and could resist one only by obtaining the protection of another of greater power."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BudgeOrderingOfEthiopianAmulets",
      "label": {
        "de": "Budges Ordnung der äthiopischen Amulette unter der Macht der Namen",
        "en": "Budge's ordering of the Ethiopian amulets under the power of names"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered as an ordering act and not as a claim of the transmission because none of the rolls printed in the volume says any of it. The formula about the presence of the name is the editor's, it is the sentence most often repeated about this material, and it is regularly given as an Ethiopian doctrine.",
          "The same introduction summarises the sixth section of the roll written for Za-Walda Hawaryat as declaring that the man who wears this amulet on his breast shall be delivered from the spear of any who would attack him. The translation printed sixty pages later gives two forms, the spoken one first, and attaches the reservation by the will of God to the worn one only. A reader of the summary gets the guarantee without the reservation and without the alternative, which is the part of the sentence a protocol turns on."
        ]
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "E. A. Wallis Budge",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianAsmat"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Life of Hanna (Saint Anne), and the Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), introduction, pp. L-LIII",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The same structure with every term reversed: at the end of the third night a foul-scented wind from the north, and four steps that lead to the hell of evil thought, of evil word, of evil deed, and to endless darkness, against the four that lead to the paradise of good thought, good word, good deed, and endless lights."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DaenaCounterpart",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gegenstück, das die Seele des Bösen trifft",
        "en": "The counterpart met by the soul of the wicked"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the symmetry is the point. A transmission that gives both outcomes in the same form, with the same number of steps and the same sequence, is making a claim with two branches rather than a promise; the passages read state the second as fully as the first."
      },
      "source": "Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2.15 and 3.25-33, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether the state depends on the practitioner holding the expectation of physical stillness, or arises without it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:StillnessExpectationProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Erwartung das Wirkende ist",
        "en": "Whether the expectation is what does the work"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No procedure devised, and the obstacle is worth naming exactly because the condition itself is easy to write. It would read: practitioners who do not hold the expectation reach the state as often as those who do. Every term in that sentence can be arranged except the last. There is no way to establish that the state has been reached other than the practitioner's report, and his report is made by the same person whose expectation is the variable. The design collapses at its measuring end, not at its manipulating end.",
          "What would open it is an outward mark of the state that does not depend on the report — the kind of mark that made the inner-heat claim reachable, where a thermometer settled what testimony could not. Whether such a mark exists for this state is itself unexamined."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Nobody. The state records that no falsifying condition has been devised that could be run, which is a fact about the means and not about the claim",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ExpectationOfStillnessIsTheOperator"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:RecognitionPresupposesMasteredSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Anerkennung setzt eine Mindestzahl gemeisterter Geister voraus",
        "en": "Recognition presupposes a minimum of mastered spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source states both that a candidate must have a certain minimum before being recognised and what follows where he has not, and it adds that the loss of recognition on this ground is met with among all Tungus groups. That the condition is reported as sometimes failing after the fact, and not only before it, is what makes it a condition and not a form of words."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "He would not be recognized as a real shaman by his clansmen and outsiders, and it is very likely that he would give up his practice of shamanism."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusFormalRecognition"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusListOfSpirits"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 B, pp. 271-272",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that moving air is a source from which the practitioner draws, beyond what breathing it supplies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind",
      "label": {
        "de": "Energie lässt sich aus Wind aufnehmen",
        "en": "Energy can be taken in from wind"
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#134",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten wird durch Anstrengung erlangt",
        "en": "Retention alone is attained by effort"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:byEffort"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The verse directs that the accompanied retention be practised for as long as it takes until the unaccompanied one succeeds, and the next verse names the condition by the success: it is called retention alone once it can be done without the emptying and the filling. The manual gives no other way to it, and the counted regimen of the same chapter sets a number of retentions to be performed at four times of the day."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72-73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A fast kept by the chiefs, the priests and the devout before the festival of the first month. The witness gives a floor and not an average: none for less than thirteen days. Some began three months before, some two, others as they chose. To continence was added the giving up of salt and of chili in the food, which the witness says was counted a grave penitential act."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MayaNewYearFast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fasten vor dem Neujahr",
        "en": "The fast before the new year"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same section of the same work records a second and separate fast, kept on receiving duties for the festivals and on undertaking duties of state, which it says at times lasted as long as three years. That is a different practice with a different occasion and is not entered here; it stands in prima-materia#462."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsSoulSeizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der vierte Grad bringt das Ergreifen des Schattens hervor",
        "en": "The fourth degree yields the seizing of the shadow"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the search for work opposing the cited evidence has been carried."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:counterSearch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegensuche",
        "en": "counter-search"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required wherever pm:evidenceFrom is set, enforced by pm:CounterSearchRequiredShape. A protocol that records no state here is not neutral about the question; it has not asked it."
      },
      "range": "pm:CounterSearchState",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite by which a person is admitted to the practice, set out in the tantra and reproduced with commentary in the Kashmirian exegesis."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Diksa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Initiation",
        "en": "Initiation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol the transmission states for itself and states completely: a plant in one place is held to report the death or survival of a person in another, and both readings are fixed before the event. The claim is about a correspondence between two separated states of affairs, so it is decidable in a way that a claim about an inner state is not."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MayaAjSignExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das gepflanzte Rohr als Meldung eines Todes andernorts",
        "en": "The planted cane as a report of a death elsewhere"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a protocol rather than as a remark because the tradition supplies every part a protocol needs and supplies it in advance: the observable, the two readings, the reader, and the moment of reading. What it does not supply is a case in which the reading was checked against the death."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A case in which the cane dried while the person who planted it lived, or sprouted again while he was dead, with the state of the plant recorded before the news arrived.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:MayaAjSign"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 7",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second member of the observed minimum. Its stated function is not signalling and not accompaniment but the shaman's own self-excitation, without which he cannot bring himself into the state the performance requires."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusShamanDrum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Trommel als Werkzeug der Selbsterregung",
        "en": "The drum as the instrument of self-excitation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stated as a function and not as a symbol, which is what makes it usable. The same passage names the toli as a placing for spirits and the drum as the means of self-excitation, so the two indispensable objects have two different offices and are not interchangeable."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, p. 272",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tibetan transmission of a primordially free awareness, with a practice whose stages are counted by visions appearing under stated conditions of posture, light and darkness."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Dzogchen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dzogchen",
        "en": "Dzogchen"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Nyingthig cycles; Longchenpa, Seven Treasuries",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MaqamatAreAbiding",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Stationen bleiben",
        "en": "The stations abide"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:abidingState"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Maqamat"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The handbooks have the station held by the one who reaches it, which is what makes the enumeration an ascent rather than a list of episodes."
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The ascription of the invocations used by men to the angels who transgressed, who are said to have brought them down and taught them for the completion of lawless deeds in the mystery of their sorcery."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaAngeliTransgressi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zaubereien wurden von den übertretenden Engeln herabgebracht",
        "en": "The sorceries were brought down by the angels who transgressed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same derivation the enochic material makes for the taught arts, in a work of another century and another circle, and worth holding apart from it rather than merging: here it is stated in passing, to explain what the taking of the third was aimed at, and the arts are not enumerated."
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 15 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The two ranks met after the gates open: seven maidens in linen with serpent faces and golden sceptres, called the Fates of heaven and guardians of the four pillars, and seven gods with the faces of black bulls, in linen aprons and with seven golden diadems, called the pole-rulers. Each rank is greeted first as a body and then member by member, and the text requires that each be addressed with its own name, given in order from first to seventh."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvSalutatioNominatim",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vierzehn Mächte, jede mit ihrem eigenen Namen begrüßt",
        "en": "Fourteen powers, each greeted by its own name"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The greeting is not a request. The maidens are saluted as holy and good; the pole-rulers are told what they do — they turn the axle of heaven at a single command and hurl thunder, lightning, earthquakes and bolts at the tribes of the impious — and are then asked for health, bodily wholeness, keenness of hearing and sight, and freedom from disturbance for the good hours of this day. A passage is not asked for and no passage is granted.",
          "This is the nearest thing in the corpus to a gate formula that names its keeper. The requirement is explicit — each is to be greeted with the name proper to it — and the order is fixed, so what is transmitted is a sequence of fourteen names in two ranks, not an invocation that could be paraphrased. In the translation read first, all fourteen stand as obeli; they are in the edition.",
          "Whether the names belong to the rite is the question the edition itself raises and does not close. Its editor holds that names must have stood in exactly these two places, which is a reconstruction from the shape of the passage rather than a mark in the manuscript, and he holds most other barbarous names in the piece to be interchangeable stock. One of the fourteen is read as Egyptian and as certainly belonging to the received text; that is the only one of them with an argument attached."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-723, the ninth and tenth utterances (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that what passes from one person to another passes through the palms, and not through some other part indifferently."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PalmsAreTheSendingOrgan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Handflächen sind das Sendeorgan",
        "en": "The palms are the organ of sending"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The force of the claim is in the exclusion. That the hands are used in transmission is reported widely and is not at issue; that they are the organ, so that another part would not serve, is the draft's addition and the part that can fail."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#314",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A set of capacities named as the fruit of the rite: happiness and prosperity, standing everywhere, a body resembling Madana, the subduing of kings together with their kingdoms and subjects, the drawing of all women, the subduing of lions, tigers, hyenas, bhūtas, pretas and piśācas, and movement everywhere like the wind."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WorldlyPowersOfTheLeftHandRite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die weltlichen Vermögen, die der heterodoxe Ritus verleihen soll",
        "en": "The worldly powers the heterodox rite is said to confer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as one set and not split into eight nodes, because the source gives them as a single list in one place and attaches no separate condition to any of them. Splitting would make the graph report eight claims where the text makes one.",
          "They are worldly and immediate, and that is the contrast the same passage draws. The orthodox side is not said to confer them; it is said to clear the debts and to be rewarded in due course. The difference between the two methods is stated here as a difference of what is obtained and when, not as a difference of moral standing. That is a fourth criterion beside rank, competence and deity, and it comes closer than any of them to what the Western pair was later made to carry — without ever carrying it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the fruits of the rite (135b-138a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Prana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prāṇa",
        "en": "Prāṇa"
      },
      "source": [
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5",
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Upanisadic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the goddesses Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā may be worshipped at will following either the orthodox or the heterodox method, and that the doer attains siddhi doing it either way."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BothMethodsReachSiddhiForKamakhya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Für Kāmeśvarī und Kāmākhyā erreicht jede der beiden Methoden die Kraft",
        "en": "For Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā either method attains the power"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The passage that most directly rules out reading the pair as two camps. The choice is left to the worshipper, both methods reach the same attainment, and the text sees no need to rank them. Whatever the division does in this work, it does not sort people into parties and it does not make one method the true one."
      },
      "source": "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on worshipping Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā by either method (139b-140a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim stands in a received work, at a passage that can be cited and read independently of this graph."
      },
      "id": "pm:textualAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Textzeugnis",
        "en": "Textual attestation"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Vitriol vertreibt die Zahnschmerzen",
        "en": "The vitriol drives out the toothache"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The author also states outright that he does not know whether the remedy works on teeth that are not hollow. The limit is his and is kept here rather than widened.",
          "The rare part of this claim is not the remedy but the number attached to it. The author states more than sixty applications and says that on one of them it did not help. A transmission that keeps its own failure in the count is uncommon in this graph, and it is what makes the claim examinable rather than merely asserted; what the number does not carry is a base rate, since toothache remits without treatment."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:firstPersonReport"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgePresupposesContemplation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gotteserkenntnis setzt Naturschau voraus",
        "en": "Knowledge of God presupposes contemplation of nature"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Theologike"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Physike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1912",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that every mental state is a rate of vibration, that states fall into innumerable classes each of which has two opposite poles, that the positive pole of a class stands at a higher degree than its negative, and that a state may be moved along its own scale but never across into another class."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticPolarityOfMentalStates",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Polarität der Gemütszustände",
        "en": "The polarity of mental states"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Opened for prima-materia#12, to date the step between the theosophical colour key of nineteen hundred and two and the numbered scale of nineteen ninety-five. Read at the wording, chapter ten.",
          "The finding is a denial, and it is sharper than a confirmation would have been. The vibration vocabulary is fully present and every mental state carries a rate. But the text states outright that the states belong to innumerable classes, each with its own two poles, and that movement is possible only within a class: love and hate are transmutable into one another, so are fear and courage, and fear cannot be turned into love. That is many two-ended scales and expressly not one scale carrying all feelings. A ranking of unlike feelings against each other is therefore not merely absent here, it is ruled out.",
          "What the later scales do inherit is the direction. The positive pole is said to stand at a higher degree than the negative and to dominate it, and a practitioner is described as raising his own vibration and inducing the change in another. Sixty years separate that from a numbered ladder, and the step is still undated."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A study of the Hermetic philosophy of ancient Egypt and Greece, so described on its own title page and taken by its readers for a transmission from Hermes Trismegistus rather than for a work of its own decade.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Three Initiates, the pseudonymous authorship under which the work appeared",
      "source": "Three Initiates, The Kybalion (Chicago: Yogi Publication Society, 1912), chapter X, Polarity",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity for which the office exists: to cure people affected by the spirits, to relieve sick people also where the disease is not of that order, and by his presence to give assurance to the people that they will not suffer."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusReliefOfSpiritAffliction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befreiung der von Geistern Befallenen",
        "en": "Relief of those afflicted by spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder adds his own explanation, that this is the result of an empiric experimentation of the Tungus on themselves reached through a long adaptation, and that the shaman functions as a safety valve of the clan. That reading is his. What is reported is the claim that the office relieves and the fact that people seek it."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 381",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The clearing of impurity from the channels, at which the breath practice is said to aim, and whose accomplishment the text says shows outwardly in the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NadiPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reinigung der Kanäle",
        "en": "Purification of the channels"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "No route is available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. Agreement with traditions already held is a candidate for independent attestation."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kein Kontaktweg",
        "en": "Contact route absent"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The delivery a master of song is held to owe: no breath taken before the close of a phrase, no mistake, and no hesitation in pronouncing a word. The three are named together and on the same footing, so that pausing counts as failing rather than as taking care. The manner reported beside it is a tone almost on one note with every word enunciated distinctly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HawaiianChantExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Gesang ohne Atemzug, Fehler und Zögern",
        "en": "The chant rendered without breath, mistake or hesitation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Beckwith gives the rule in her own voice and names her authorities for it: Kamakau for the manner of delivery, and Pukui's article of 1949 for the practice. Neither was opened in this run; the article is behind a paywall and the newspaper series was not reachable. The node therefore stands on Beckwith and says so."
      },
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PracticePresupposesInitiation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Praxis setzt die Initiation voraus",
        "en": "The practice presupposes initiation"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SaktipataTransmission"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Diksa"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XV",
        "Mālinīvijayottaratantra VIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seal made by pressing the perineum with the heel and contracting the anus, by which the downward wind is drawn upward."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Mulabandha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wurzelsiegel",
        "en": "The root seal"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.61-63 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim states that animals bearing marks named in advance by the candidate will be sent and found. A condition of refutation exists and can be stated; no case has been recorded under it, because the source that carries the claim reports no case and no count."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TestingTheMarkedAnimals",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfprotokoll: die an ihren Zeichen benannten Tiere",
        "en": "Protocol: the animals named by their marks"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The claim as the source words it is close to failing to discriminate, and the reason is inside it: where the named animals are not found, the spirit may be asked to send another. The falsifying condition below therefore has to be stated against the first designation and against the substitution separately, or the claim covers the finding of a marked animal and the finding of any animal alike. Whether the substitution is used often or rarely is exactly what the source does not say.",
          "The state is procedure without cases and not cases without deviation, although the source says the animals are always found. What it reports is a summary judgement without a number, and a protocol that entered it as a case series would be counting the recorder's confidence."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol devised from the wording of Shirokogoroff 1935, sect. 130 I",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A recorded search in which the marks named by the candidate before the hunt are not found on any animal taken within the stated period, and the designation is neither withdrawn nor replaced by a second one. Where the substitution is admitted, the claim is refuted only if the marks of the second designation also fail."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-03",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusMarkedAnimals"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a process instance with the tradition in which it is situated."
      },
      "id": "pm:withinTradition",
      "label": {
        "en": "within tradition"
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The western alchemical transmission, in which the stages of the work are described at once as operations and as states of the operator."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LatinAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lateinische Alchemie",
        "en": "Latin alchemy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The double reading is not a modern imposition and not a settled fact either. It is a dispute the corpus carries in itself and the graph should record as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Turba philosophorum; Rosarium philosophorum; Aurora consurgens",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first instruction of the rite: to take breath from the sun's rays, inhaling three times as deeply as the operator is able. Later stages prescribe the counter-movement — a prolonged bellowing like a horn-note, expelling the whole breath with pressure on the ribs, and a second bellowing with pressure on the belly to set the senses going all at once."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atem aus den Strahlen ziehen, dreimal so tief wie möglich",
        "en": "Drawing breath from the rays, three times as deeply as one can"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The most concrete bodily instruction in the whole of this graph's ascent material. It names the direction of the breath, its depth, its count and, at the exhalation, where the pressure is applied. Nothing in the ophite, hermetic or Jeu material comes near this, and it is the reason the register singled this corpus out."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction and fifth and eighth utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the fixed groupings of stars."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Constellations",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Sternbilder",
        "en": "The constellations"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A memorial preserved in the dynastic history sets seven numbered objections against the burial books, on an imperial commission to revise the divinatory literature. The rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so the burial has a set time and no year or month is to be chosen; the books call one day of the sexagenary cycle the most inauspicious of all for a burial, and a check of the classical annals turns up more than twenty burials on that day, so the day is not chosen either; the hour is fixed by what each dynasty honoured, so the hour is not chosen; one house kept its succession and another lost its sacrifices without either turning on a burial; the ancients buried north of their capitals in one fixed place, without the surname doctrine, and their descendants were not the poorer for it; a grave once made is never altered while rank rises and falls without rest, so office is not what a burial confers; and the practice makes mourners choose ground in hope of an office and a season in hope of money, so that the books corrupt the customs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZangshuDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Wahl von Grab und Tag das Geschick der Nachkommen entscheidet",
        "en": "Whether the choice of grave and day decides the fortune of the descendants"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The defending side is the book itself and the commentary printed with it, which cites two cases the other way: a reader of graves who saw a general's tomb and said the clan would be wiped out, and it was; and a duke of the annals who moved his capital against a prognostication that it would benefit the people and not the ruler, and died in the fifth month. Both are anecdotes reported by the defence and neither was traced to the works they name.",
          "The sixth objection is the one worth naming, because it is an argument of a form the graph can reuse: the cause is invariant and the effect fluctuates, therefore the cause is not that cause. The second is of another form again and is treated as evidence rather than as testimony, in the protocol beside this file."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:YitiShouyin"
      },
      "source": [
        "Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial of Lü Cai on the burial books, juan 79, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
        "Zangshu, inner and miscellaneous sections, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the Ethiopic teaching list as the one who disclosed the knowledge of the clouds."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ezeqeel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ezeqeel",
        "en": "Ezeqeel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Ezêqêêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that the naming of two Śākta branches as a right-hand and a left-hand ritual reports a division the Indian material carries, rather than imposing one it does not."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BinaryPredatesItsReportersExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Zweiteilung älter ist als ihre Berichterstatter",
        "en": "Whether the two-way division is older than the reports of it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The state moved from procedure-without-cases past cases-without-deviation and to non-discrimination, and the middle step was deliberately not taken. A case has now been read, so the first state no longer fits; but reporting it as a case without deviation would say the claim survived a test, when what happened is that the test could not tell the two possible worlds apart. The finding is about the wording of the claim and not about the Indian material.",
          "Three divisions now stand in the graph and no two of them agree: seven ascending ācāras with the left above the right (pmc:AcaraLadder), three dispositions deciding competence (pmc:BhavaClassification), and two methods assigned by the deity worshipped (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods). That is the shape of the actual answer, and it is more interesting than either yes or no: the material carries divisions in abundance, and none of them is the one the West received.",
          "What is still not established. The corpus has not been searched exhaustively, the tantras of the Kaula stream have not been opened for this question, and the modern scholarship on the construction of the category has been used only through the one work cited at pm:evidenceFrom. A reformulated claim is owed before this protocol can return anything but non-discrimination. See prima-materia#71."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Searched for the opposite of the claim: a pre-colonial Indian source carrying the two-way division. Opposition was found and is now in the graph. Urban's account of the Kālikāpurāṇa led to the passages, which were then read in the Shastri translation rather than taken from him; the purāṇa announces the orthodox and heterodox methods as a pair, prescribes the left hand method for particular deities, and closes by saying both have now been stated. Urban also reports that across most Tantric traditions the pair marks a literal against a symbolic reading of the same prohibited substances — a second division, again without moral polarity. What was not found in either is a criterion of union against self-deification, or a division of practitioners into camps."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "H. B. Urban, The Power of Tantra: Religion, Sexuality and the Politics of South Asian Studies (I.B. Tauris, London 2010), in the chapter on the sacrifice of desire, at the section on the origins of sexual rites and the right and left-hand paths",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl and from the Kālikāpurāṇa in the Shastri translation",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A pre-colonial Indian source that presents dakṣiṇa and vāma as a clean two-way division with a moral polarity, using union with the divine against self-deification as the criterion that separates them. One such passage refutes the claim that the division was imposed rather than found.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-09",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature"
      },
      "pm:undividedOutcomes": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The claim treats two findings alike that are not alike. In the one, no pre-colonial text divides at all, and the naming invents its object. In the other, a pre-colonial text does announce a two-way division of methods in these very terms, and the naming keeps the pair while attaching a moral charge the text does not carry. The falsification condition below is met by neither, so the protocol returned the same verdict for both — and the second is now the established case (pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods)."
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two informants in the same volume give the count differently. The one whose written Lakota underlies the doctrinal texts gives sixteen names in four groups of four and says that the term of the shamans differs from the common term only in considering all the benevolent gods, four of each of four classes, as one whole. The other, questioned directly, names eight, then says there is but one, then reduces the eight to four by pairing them, saying that the Sun and the Moon are the same, the Sky and the Wind the same, the Rock and the Winged the same, and the Earth and the Beautiful Woman the same. On a second point the same informant contradicts the people rather than the other informant: many of the people hold that the Sun and the Moving are one and the same, and he says that the shamans know it is not so, because the Sun is in the sky only half the time and the Moving is there all the time."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HowManyTheGreatMysteriousAre",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wie viele die Große Geheimnisvolle sind, und ob die Sonne der Große Geist ist",
        "en": "How many the Great Mysterious are, and whether the Sun is the Great Spirit"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The argument the second informant gives for his denial is worth keeping separate from the denial: it is an argument from the observed availability of the two, not from authority or from lineage. That form is rare in this corpus.",
          "The dispute is entered because the graph would otherwise hold the scheme of the first informant as the doctrine of the transmission, which the second informant does not confirm and the compiler himself declines to claim. It is a disagreement between two witnesses of the same transmission and not between two traditions, which is the commoner and less visible kind."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:WalkerSystematizationOfTheGods"
      },
      "source": [
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 153",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 154-155"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AnswersFromAnUncontrolledOutcome",
      "label": {
        "de": "Antworten am Ausgang, den der Betreiber nicht beherrscht",
        "en": "Answers fixed to an outcome the operator does not control"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A third case was considered and not used. The Sámi drum answers by the travel of a ring that the operator does not steer, and the reading rule is attested twice ninety years apart (pmc:SunCourseRule). It is not entered here because that node states a rule of reading and not a claim that the reading comes true, and pm:generalizedFrom must point at claims and not at descriptions. Seen and left out, with the reason.",
          "The Zande transmission has a feature that makes this generalization examinable rather than merely stateable: it prescribes a control, the second consultation that must reverse the first. It also names eight causes to which a failure of that control is attributed, so the condition never falls against the oracle itself. Both stand in traditions/azande.ttl, and both belong in any protocol devised against this node.",
          "The comparison class is the whole of the claim and is the part to attack. An oracle that answers yes or no is measured against one half; a system of two hundred and fifty-six figures against one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth. A protocol that does not fix the admissible set before the consultation measures nothing, because the set can always be narrowed afterwards to whatever came true.",
          "Two attested claims from two transmissions with no route between them that this holding has established: the Zande poison oracle and the casting of the Ifá figures. The mechanism is the same in both and is the reason they are taken together — the answer is not read off the operator's judgement but off an outcome he does not steer."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:generalizedFrom": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge"
        }
      ],
      "pm:generalizedStatement": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Procedures that fix an answer to the outcome of a physical process the operator cannot control name the state of affairs later established more often than an answer drawn at random from the same set of admissible answers."
      },
      "type": "pm:Generalizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tradition holds that the distinction this axis draws does not apply to what it teaches: nothing is to be withdrawn from and no state is to be entered, because whatever arises is taken as the way."
      },
      "id": "pm:anchoringDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sinnesbindung als Unterscheidung abgelehnt",
        "en": "Anchoring distinction declined"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A positive claim about a tradition and not a gap in the record. It needs a passage like any other claim; the same shape as pm:normDistinctionDeclined and pm:selfBoundaryDeclined on the two orientation axes."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The twentieth century Burmese reform transmission of insight practice as a lay technique detachable from monastic life, taught in fixed courses."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ModernVipassana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Moderne Vipassanā-Bewegung",
        "en": "Modern vipassanā movement"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered separately from the canon and the commentaries because it is a distinct transmission with its own founders and manuals, and because its relation to the older material, in particular its treatment of absorption, is contested inside the tradition itself."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Mahāsi Sayādaw, Manual of Insight; Ledi Sayādaw, the Manuals of Buddhism",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The being named in the seventh prayer as the man whose child was killed, who mounted his horse Cherubim, took a spear in his right hand, was granted power from a voice out of heaven, and slew the killer."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianSusenyos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Susenjos",
        "en": "Susenyos"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Susenyus",
      "pm:nameRole": "the saint on horseback whose prayer is written down and worn",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Socinius"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded on Claude Vorilhon's reported meeting of 1973, holding that humanity was made by a non-terrestrial people and reading scripture as the record of that making."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Raelism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Raëlismus",
        "en": "Raëlism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered because it performs the characteristic move of the block in its clearest form: it rereads received texts as accounts of contact. That reinterpretation is a claim about the older corpora and belongs in the graph beside them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Vorilhon, Le livre qui dit la vérité; Vorilhon, Les extra-terrestres m'ont emmené sur leur planète",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what the source text says, not about how to interpret it. The manuscript reads the possessive of the second person, which makes the hau that of the giver and keeps the giver attached to the thing after it has changed hands. The printed version of 1909 has the definite article in both key sentences, which makes the hau that of the object. Every later reading of the passage rests on one of the two."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGiftWordingDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wessen Hau: der Wortlaut des Ranapiri-Briefes",
        "en": "Whose hau: the wording of the Ranapiri letter"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a dispute over the text rather than over its meaning, because that is the part a later reader can check and because the substitution was made silently. Both sides were read in the same study that reports the substitution, so the collation itself has not been verified against the manuscript or against the 1909 printing in this run."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGift"
      },
      "source": [
        "Elsdon Best, Maori Forest Lore, part III, Transactions and Proceedings of the New Zealand Institute 42 (1909), p. 439",
        "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271, as collated in Hēnare, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the earth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Araqiel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Araqiel",
        "en": "Araqiel"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Charles brackets the clause naming Araqiel and Shamsiel as absent from part of the Ethiopic tradition; the bracketing belongs to the text as printed and is recorded here rather than resolved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Araqiêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement among editors about what belongs to the manual and how its verses are numbered. The widely circulated editions descend from single manuscripts and differ in the count and extent of their chapters; a critical edition drawn from more than two hundred manuscripts, with the oldest dated 1496, settles neither question in favour of the numbering that most citations use."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Textbestand und Verszählung des Handbuchs hängen von der Ausgabe ab",
        "en": "The text and the verse numbering of the manual are edition-dependent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node because every citation in this file carries a verse number, and a number is only as stable as the recension it comes from. The numbering used throughout this file follows the widely circulated vulgate text; a reader working from the critical edition may find the same passage under a different number. Naming the manual without naming the edition conceals that anything is at issue."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmt:Hathayoga"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, Kaivalyadhama S.M.Y.M. Samiti, Lonavla",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā, critical edition, ed. J. Mallinson, J. Birch, J. Hanneder, M. Demoto and N.-J. Liersch (2024)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā, ed. and tr. P. Sinh (1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The office of a town chief, named in the source by the two objects handed over with it, together with the first wand of office. The candidate who has understood comes before his lord on bended knees and receives them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PopDzam",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Matte und der Thron",
        "en": "The mat and the throne"
      },
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 92-93",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A coordinate on which a tradition, a practice, a capacity or a claim is placed. The axis belongs to no tradition; sources describe a condition and do not name a coordinate, so every placement is an act of the compiler and carries pm:compilerInference."
      },
      "id": "pm:subjectAxis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sachachse",
        "en": "Subject axis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two values are owed on every subject axis beyond the substantive ones. A declining value, for a tradition that rejects the distinction the axis draws: without it a rejection is recorded as an omission, which turns a doctrine into a gap. And a not-stated value, for a source that was read for the question and does not answer it: without it a node nobody tagged cannot be told from a source that was asked and stayed silent, and every query of the form 'only in X' reports the state of the tagging instead of the state of the traditions."
      },
      "type": "pm:ScaleFamily"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SituatingEthiopianAsmatAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Schutz der Namen wird bei abgetretener Handlung verortet",
        "en": "The protection of the names is placed at ceded agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianAsmatYieldsRestraintOfDevils"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The roll assigns the acting to others throughout: the angels protect and keep in safety, the names are the names of their God, the deliverance of the worn form comes by the will of God, and the seventh prayer rests the whole effect on an oath sworn to Christ by the adversary. What the person does is say or wear; what acts is named as someone else.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaLocusNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama gibt keinen Ort an",
        "en": "Saṃyama does not state a locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:locusNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The four sūtras were read for this question and are silent on it: they place the attention on an object and say nothing about where the awareness itself is held to be. That the collection has one capacity of displaced locus at III.38 does not settle the operator, which is applied to near and distant objects alike."
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Auge",
        "en": "Divine eye"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How firmly a source sets a prerequisite: as a necessity, as an instruction, or merely by the order in which it presents its material."
      },
      "id": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stärke der Voraussetzung",
        "en": "Prerequisite Strength"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The three must not collapse. A list of eight limbs in the order given is not the same claim as a sentence saying that the eighth is unreachable without the seventh, and reading the first as the second is the commonest way a modern system acquires an ancient warrant it never had."
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:sourceModalityScale"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An attested claim the generalization was formed from. At least two are required, and each must itself name a work."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Generalizing",
      "id": "pm:generalizedFrom",
      "label": {
        "de": "verallgemeinert aus",
        "en": "generalized from"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two is a floor and not a target. How many attested claims a generalization rests on, and how far apart their traditions stand, are the numbers that make one worth examining before another — the same distinction pm:Converging draws between many witnesses on one cable and many on three."
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning, ascribed in the text to the angel who declared the oration, that the holder presume neither to give it to another nor to expound anything out of it, neither himself, nor any one by him, nor any one after him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaSecretumKautel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das unentschuldbare Verbot der Weitergabe",
        "en": "The inexcusable prohibition on passing it on"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the gate the project's criterion is directed at, stated by the transmission in its own words and attributed to an angel so that no holder can lift it. It stands here as a claim of the tradition and not as an instruction; the text it guards has been in print since sixteen fifty-seven."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 17, p. 16, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Indian alchemical transmission of mercurial preparations held to make the body proof against decay, with named substances, processes and durations."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Rasashastra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Rasaśāstra",
        "en": "Rasaśāstra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Among the most testable claims available anywhere in the corpus, and testable in two directions at once: the promised outcome and the toxicity of the means."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasārṇava; Govindabhagavatpāda, Rasahṛdayatantra; Vāgbhaṭa, Rasaratnasamuccaya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."
      },
      "id": "pm:durationNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dauer nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Duration not stated"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateDuration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ClearLightIsAtTheSleepThreshold",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das klare Licht liegt an der Schlafschwelle",
        "en": "The clear light is placed at the sleep threshold"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:sleepThreshold"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The treatise places the luminosity at the moment of falling asleep and trains the practitioner to hold it rather than pass through it unconscious. The file's own note already separates this from a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; both fall on this coordinate, and the difference between them belongs in the nodes, not in the axis."
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operation by which a figure is obtained: sixteen palm nuts are held loosely in one hand and thrown through the half-closed fingers into the other. One nut left behind is marked with a double stroke and two nuts left behind with a single stroke, and the throw is repeated until two columns of four marks stand on the whitened board."
      },
      "id": "pmp:IfaCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse",
        "en": "Casting the sixteen palm nuts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The mark is set by what remains in the hand and not by what the diviner judges, so the figure is not chosen. The source describes the mechanism and does not draw that consequence itself; the observation is recorded here and not asserted as the source's."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ProphesyingPresupposesMedicineEating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Weissagen setzt das Essen der Medizinen voraus",
        "en": "Prophesying presupposes the eating of medicines"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source states a disqualification and not only an order. A novice who has eaten only a few times dances at the seance but does not divine, and the quantity eaten is what the source names as the reason."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A man who has eaten the medicines only a few times is not qualified to take a prominent part in a seance or to prophesy at it."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 96",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An amulet belonging to a god, of which every god has his own kind. Its power is said not to rest on an indwelling spirit but on its consecration: it lets the god recognise and protect his own. Neither prayer nor sacrifice is offered to it. Only the priests of a god know how to make the amulets of that god, and they sell them at a high price. The word is derived from the verb to bind, which is used only of amulets, because the priest binds them on the person or the property of the buyer."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VoSesa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vo-sesa, das gebundene Amulett",
        "en": "Vo-sesa, the bound amulet"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transmission attaches a public and checkable claim to these: crops standing in the bush and goods left for sale beside a frequented path are put under an amulet on a stick, with a few cowries on each article to show the price, and are said to be quite safe from pillage. Whether that held is a question about a distribution in the world, and it is the kind of claim this graph is short of."
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 92-93",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vier Vertiefungen",
        "en": "The four absorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The teaching that discretion is the mother of the virtues, directed against the practitioner who is undone not by laxity but by an excess undertaken in good faith."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExcessiveAsceticismCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung gegen das Übermaß",
        "en": "Discretion against excess"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": "John Cassian, Collationes II (De discretione)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteThauthabaoth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Thauthabaoth, der Bärengestaltige",
        "en": "Thauthabaoth the bear-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Thauthabaoth",
      "pm:nameRole": "fifth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, bear-like",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission received by Darryl Anka since 1984, teaching that acting on the strongest available excitement is the means by which one comes to occupy a different reality."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BasharTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bashar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Bashar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Unusually operative for a channelled corpus: the teaching is given as steps with stated conditions of failure, in front of audiences, on record."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Anka, Bashar: Blueprint for Change; the recorded session transcripts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The placement of a claim, a practice or a capacity at a value on an awareness axis. The node records the compiler's identification of a described condition with a coordinate, not the description itself."
      },
      "id": "pm:Situating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verorten",
        "en": "Situating"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Normally attested by pm:compilerInference. Textual attestation is available only where the source itself draws the distinction the axis draws — the Sufi division of what descends from what is attained is the clear case — and even then the wording that carries it belongs in pm:situationGround."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The angelic power under whose name the Law is said to be obtained. The transmission gives its own reason for needing him: Israel's understanding was obscured by the exile and the words of the Law became hard as brass and iron, and what the world lacks is this secret and this mystery which is not in it. The remedy is offered as a means of loftiness by which the people are to raise themselves."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SarTorah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sar ha-Tora, der Fürst der Tora",
        "en": "Sar ha-Torah, the Prince of the Torah"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sar Torah"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The graduation rite of the hula school, named after the brain of the victim that is eaten in it. The offering is a black suckling without spot or blemish; the parts consumed are the snout, the ear-tips, the tail, the feet and portions of the vital organs, above all the brain. Two foods are shut out of the feast, the cooked taro leaf and the drupe of the pandanus. In the days before it no pupil may leave the house except under the most stringent necessity, then only with the head muffled so as not to be recognised, and may hold no conversation whatever outside."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Ailolo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ailolo",
        "en": "Ailolo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The rite decides admission: the glossary of the same work defines ailolo as a critical ceremonial sacrifice whose conditions must be met before a novice can be admitted as a practitioner. What the passages read do not give is a way of failing it that is stated in advance, which is why no protocol node stands beside this one."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, pp. 31-35, and the glossary, s.v. ai-lolo",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A star named after the fonio grain, said to be white where Sirius is seen red, to be the first thing created, to be the egg of the world out of which everything visible and invisible developed, and to consist of a metal called sagala. It is called at once the smallest of all things and the heaviest, weighed in the account at four hundred and eighty donkey loads, so that all earthly beings together could not lift it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PoTolo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pô tolo, der Digitaria-Stern",
        "en": "Pô tolo, the Digitaria star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The number four hundred and eighty is glossed in the article itself as the product of the base eighty and the count of tens in the older base sixty, and is said to be used as the symbol of the greatest of numbers. A reader who takes it for a measured mass is reading against the note printed beneath it."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 284 and 287",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that the exercise fails without the practitioner noticing: the recorder reports holding the divided attention along a street, losing it at a single ordinary thought, and coming to two hours later in another part of the city, having in the meantime left a shop, made a telephone call and written two letters, and only then remembering that he had forgotten to remember himself."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LapseInSelfRememberingGoesUnnoticed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Aussetzer wird nicht bemerkt, solange er dauert",
        "en": "The lapse is not noticed while it lasts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "This is the shape of failure condition the project looks for, and it is unusually strong: the failure is not merely predicted but predicted to be invisible from inside, and the report performs it. It rules out the obvious procedure. Asking practitioners how long they sustained the exercise measures what they remember of it, and the claim says exactly that this is the faculty which fails.",
          "What it does not yield on its own is a duration. The account gives one lapse and no figure for how long the state can be held, so nothing here fixes a threshold a later examination could measure against. See prima-materia#654."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:firstPersonReport"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter seven, at the walk along the Nevsky and the waking two hours later (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesaIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Eintritt in einen anderen Körper geschieht in abgezogener Vertiefung",
        "en": "Entering another body is performed in withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The sūtra makes the entry follow from saṃyama, and the state saṃyama names is fixed by III.1-4. The placement is inherited from the operator the source itself binds the capacity to, and not read off the capacity."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The graffito scratched beside the cult niche of the same mithraeum: born at first light, in the consulship of the two Augusti Severus and Antoninus, on the twelfth day before the kalends of December, the day of Saturn, the eighteenth of the moon. It records an initiation, dated as a birth, on 20 November 202."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicNatalisInitiationis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geboren beim ersten Licht, am Saturntag, der Mond achtzehn Tage alt",
        "en": "Born at first light, on Saturn's day, the moon eighteen days old"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The most concrete single thing this tradition has left in its own hand, and three of its properties are worth keeping. It calls the initiation a birth without qualifying the word. It records the moment to the hour, with the consular year, the weekday and the age of the moon — that is the apparatus of a horoscope, cast for the second birth rather than the first. And it does not give a name.",
          "The omission of the name is what makes it more than a date. A man who writes his birth on a wall and leaves himself out of it is recording the event and not himself, and that is consistent with everything else this cult wrote down: the wall greets the fathers, not a father.",
          "The reckoning is exact enough to be checked against an ephemeris, which is unusual in this graph. Whether the eighteenth of the moon actually fell on that Saturday in 202 has not been checked here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, graffito beside the cult niche (CIMRM 476, after Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two prohibitions with their stated consequences. One is not to eat much, and not to eat raw vegetables or fat and fresh things, because they make a man's qi strong and hard to close off. And anger is forbidden: much anger disorders the qi, and since it then cannot flow over, it may set a man coughing. The chapter adds that few can do the thing at all, because men are by nature restless and few can be still enough to cultivate the way."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiProhibitions",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nicht viel essen, und nicht zürnen",
        "en": "Not much food, and no anger"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Both consequences are physical and immediate and neither is a misfortune sent from outside, which distinguishes these from most of the prohibitions this graph holds. The second also names a mechanism: anger does not spoil the practice directly, it makes the qi unable to overflow, and the cough is what follows from that."
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:XingqiBaopuzi"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The name under which the power is addressed at the moment of asking. When they invoke the huaca they call it Runapcamac, maker of men, and ask it for life, health and food, and they ask it nothing for any other life. Each lineage names its own pacarina, the place it says it came out of, and calls that Camac, maker; and each says it has its own maker, one a hill, one a spring."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Camac",
      "label": {
        "de": "Camac, der angerufene Macher",
        "en": "Camac, the maker that is invoked"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The term is entered at the strength the three passages give it and no higher. All three are the same recorder, and all three show him using the word as evidence that the Indians give to a stone what is due to God alone. What can be taken from them is that camac was the word used in address and that it was understood as making rather than as owning or protecting. What cannot be taken from them is a doctrine of camac as a substance or a force, which is a reading of the Quechua of Huarochirí and stands on a document this run could not open.",
          "The third of the three places is a question of the interrogatory the visitor was to put in every village. That the word had to be asked for by name shows that it was the recorder's own diagnostic and not an incidental report, which strengthens the evidence that it was in use and weakens the evidence about what it meant."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the invocation of the huaca; cap. VII, the pacarina of each lineage; cap. XIV, the third question of the visitor's interrogatory",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has the condition attained by practice directed at attaining it."
      },
      "id": "pm:byEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Durch Anstrengung",
        "en": "By effort"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the end of the axis where prerequisite chains are to be expected, and where their absence is worth a question. The Sufi handbooks call these maqāmāt and enumerate them in a set order."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateAcquisition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of an animal magnetism held to pass between bodies and to be directed by passes of the hand, together with the induced states its practitioners described."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Mesmerism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mesmerismus",
        "en": "Mesmerism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a tradition because it transmits by testimony and lineage. Its examination by the royal commission is evidence and belongs at an examination node, not here. The pair is the cleanest illustration the project has of the two predicates."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Mesmer, Mémoire sur la découverte du magnétisme animal; Puységur, Mémoires pour servir à l'histoire du magnétisme animal",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An efficacy that belongs to someone. In the passages read it is always somebody's: a father's, which may inspire the son so that he becomes the living representative of that mana and of the karakia with it; or an opponent's, which is asked for by name in an invocation before a stroke is made."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Mana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mana",
        "en": "Mana"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the witness does not show is the thing the term is famous for elsewhere. In these passages mana is never a quantity of impersonal force at large in the world; it is held, inherited, represented, and asked for from a named holder. The reading that made it an impersonal power belongs to a later literature and to a different archipelago, and it is not entered here because that literature has not yet been opened at the wording. See prima-materia#103 for the origination and prima-materia#105 for the philological dispute over the word itself."
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 54 and 62",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative means keep the norm of the order the path is transmitted in: what is prohibited there stays prohibited here."
      },
      "id": "pm:conformingMethod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ordnungstreue Methode",
        "en": "Conforming method"
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The count of the signs as the bishop's extract gives it: behind each of the sixteen representative palm nuts stand sixteen subordinate powers, and each of the whole number is itself called an Odu, which is to say a head; this gives two hundred and fifty-six. A further sixteen are counted behind each of those, which gives four thousand and ninety-six."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaOduEnumeration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zählung der Zeichen",
        "en": "The enumeration of the signs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two accounts read in this run do not generate the count the same way. The account of eighteen ninety-four compounds the further figures from the columns of two of the sixteen; the extract used here subordinates sixteen to each of the sixteen. Both arrive at a system built on sixteen, and neither states the other's derivation. The difference is recorded rather than smoothed, because which derivation a source gives is one of the things that would distinguish a locally grown scheme from a borrowed one."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Roman emperor to whom the Alexandrian healings are ascribed by two independent historians."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Vespasian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vespasian",
        "en": "Vespasian"
      },
      "source": "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The requirement, set on the parts of the ceremony chanted aloud and in unison, that the words be rendered letter perfect."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe",
        "en": "Letter-perfect delivery"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where a treatment does not take, the corpus states that the failure is put at the door of a broken prohibition or a slighted custom, and that the distinction between a trouble coming from a broken prohibition and a trouble coming from witchcraft, difficult as it is to trace, is decided by the practitioner himself. A recorded case of nineteen hundred and nine has a practitioner locked in a house with his charged thing, voices of young and old heard in conversation, and the verdict that the sickness came because the patient's brother had given no palm wine to his wife's family at his marriage."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FailureAttributedToABrokenProhibition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine ausbleibende Heilung wird einem gebrochenen Verbot angelastet",
        "en": "A cure that does not take is charged to a broken prohibition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held beside pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower and not merged with it. The first assigns a failure to the instrument, the second to the patient's conduct, and the second is the more expensive of the two, because the patient cannot show that he kept a prohibition he was never told he had.",
          "The recorder states his own explanation beside the material, and it is his and not the transmission's: he holds that the practitioners are themselves responsible for the prohibitions, because a prohibition leaves a reasonable excuse for a failure to cure, and the patient is satisfied with the reason and willing to pay the fee to have the matter set right. Entered here as the recorder's reading, not as a claim of the transmission."
        ]
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 231 and pp. 250-251",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InitiationPresupposesSaktipata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Initiation setzt die Herabkunft der Kraft voraus",
        "en": "Initiation presupposes the descent of power"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The exegesis asks who is qualified for initiation and answers by taking up the descent of power, which places the descent before the rite and before the path. Later and other traditions have read the descent as the fruit of practice instead; the direction recorded here is the one Abhinavagupta sets."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Diksa"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Saktipata"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The belly of a dead man is opened and the substance looked for. A post-mortem in which none is found clears his paternal kin of the suspicion, and cases so decided are quoted afterwards by that kin in their defence."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PostMortemForWitchcraftSubstance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Leichenschau auf die Hexereisubstanz",
        "en": "The post-mortem for witchcraft substance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A procedure inside the transmission with an outcome that could have fallen otherwise, applied to a claim about a class of persons, and reported as having been used to settle actual accusations. It is the second reason this tradition was opened."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 3-4",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MayaSafetyPresupposesUnbrokenFast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unversehrtheit setzt voraus, ein begonnenes Fasten nicht zu brechen",
        "en": "Remaining unharmed presupposes not breaking a fast once begun"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The evil is not specified, which is why this prerequisite is examinable only as far as its own wording reaches: a rule whose penalty has no described shape cannot be found to have failed. That is a property of the passage and is worth having on the record beside the passages that do name a shape."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The witness states it as the reason the fasts were not broken: those who began these fasts did not dare to break them, for they believed it would bring evil upon them or upon their houses. The consequence attaches to the breaking of a fast begun and not to the omission of the fast.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:MayaHouseholdUnharmed"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:MayaNewYearFast"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The diviner by dreams. Someone comes to ask whether he will recover or die, or whether a lost horse will be found. If the questioner is a man the diviner asks for the band from his head, or his bag, or his cloak, or some other part of his clothing; if a woman, for her sash or the like. He takes it home, sleeps on it, and answers according to what he dreams. Where the question is about love, he asks for the hair or the clothing of the person he is to divine about."
      },
      "id": "pmp:AndeanDreamDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagen durch Schlafen auf dem Kleidungsstück des Fragenden",
        "en": "Divining by sleeping on the questioner's garment"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The identifier carries the name of the tradition because the plain one was already taken by a practice of another transmission in this graph. That is a property of a flat identifier space and not of the practice; see prima-materia#275.",
          "The instrument is a possession of the person the question is about, not of the questioner, wherever the two differ. That distinction is stated in the source and is the only place in this file where the procedure names what it is a procedure on."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the moscoc",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the contents of another consciousness, said to follow from saṃyama on the presented idea."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins",
        "en": "Knowledge of another mind"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the man and his animal are one body for the purpose of injury: after a threatened person had a fight in the river with a caiman or in the open with some other animal, the man who had uttered the threat was afterwards found bearing the same wounds the animal had taken from the fight, although he had been elsewhere while it happened."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WoundsSharedWithTheAnimal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Mensch trägt die Wunden, die das Tier davontrug",
        "en": "The man is found with the wounds the animal took"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the most examinable claim in what was read, and it is examinable in the ordinary way: it asserts a coincidence of injuries between two bodies at a distance, with a stated time relation. Nothing about it requires an opinion on whether the transformation occurs. No protocol stands on it yet; see prima-materia#231."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The East Syriac transmission of solitary prayer, of the stages of stillness and of the wonder held to interrupt prayer rather than complete it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SyriacAsceticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Syrische Askese",
        "en": "Syriac ascetic transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Isaac of Nineveh, Ascetical Homilies; John of Dalyatha, Letters; Evagrius, Kephalaia Gnostica in the Syriac recension",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of a set of orations, said on prescribed days of the moon and at prescribed hours, held to confer the liberal arts, eloquence, memory and understanding within a stated term, and bound to conditions of solitude, silence, fasting and freedom from crime."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ArsNotoria",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ars Notoria",
        "en": "Ars Notoria"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read for the first time in Robert Turner's English translation, London 1657, in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson, which carries the printed pagination and the Latin of the Agrippa text beside it; the wording of every passage cited here was additionally checked against the optical text of the 1657 printing itself. Liber Juratus Honorii was not opened, and neither was the critical Latin edition of Véronèse.",
          "The version read is incomplete in a way that bears on every claim in this file. Both Turner's translation and the Latin of the Agrippa printing omit the notae, the figures which the text itself calls the centre of the art; the orations are given and the drawings they accompany are not. What was read is therefore the verbal half of a procedure whose other half is missing, and no claim entered here can be carried out from this witness alone."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ars Notoria in the medieval recensions; Liber Juratus Honorii",
      "source": "Ars Notoria: The Notory Art of Solomon, trans. Robert Turner (London: J. Cottrel, 1657), in the digital edition of Joseph H. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The witness states the failure case in its sharpest form and reports the response as a rule: if the amulet fails to defend the wearer from the one danger it was made to avert, so that an amulet consecrated to the fire god fails to protect its wearer from a disaster by fire, they are never at a loss for some excuse to account for its inefficacy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AmuletFailureIsAlwaysExcused",
      "label": {
        "de": "Für ein versagendes Amulett findet sich immer eine Ausrede",
        "en": "A failed amulet is never at a loss for an excuse"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also the recorder's summary rather than a quoted informant, and it is entered as such. What would settle it is a named excuse in a named case, and none was found in the works opened.",
          "The passage names the response and not its content, so the graph can hold the shape of the move and not the move itself. That is worth having, because it is the third transmission in this holding of which the same shape is recorded: the Kongo corpus assigns a failed object to neglect or exhaustion, the Lakota corpus assigns a stone that will not obey to the owner's divided faith, and here the excuse is reported without being specified."
        ]
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 92",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rule the text lays down for reading the answer: if he says he is at good ease or begins to mend, judge without doubt that he shall recover; if he says he is grievously ill or worse and worse, conclude that he will die; if he says he does not know whether he is better or worse, he will either die or the disease will change for the worse. Where the sick man is a child too young to answer, or too ill to answer, the oration is said three times and what first arises in the speaker's mind is the judgement."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaInterrogatioAegroti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entscheidungsregel an der Antwort des Kranken",
        "en": "The rule of decision on the sick man's answer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The fallback for the silent patient moves the sign from the sick man to the speaker, and with it the whole character of the procedure: what is read is then the operator's own first thought and not anything the patient did.",
          "Three answers, and only two of them decide. The third covers dying and getting worse together, so no fate the sick man can meet falls outside it, and that branch alone cannot fail. The first two are not like that: each names one outcome and excludes the other."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus",
        "en": "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FiveHindrances"
      },
      "source": "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that the names worn upon the breast shall deliver the wearer from his enemy and that he shall escape by the will of God. The verdict is reached from the wording and not from cases: the closing clause covers the outcome and its opposite."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianWornNamesSpearExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die getragenen Namen etwas ausschließen",
        "en": "Whether the worn names exclude anything"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A falsifying condition had to be entered here saying that there is none. pm:FalsifierRequiredShape exempts only pm:noProcedureDevised, and pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate is the one verdict whose content is that no falsifier exists; the shape therefore demands of it exactly what it reports as absent. Recorded rather than worked around, and filed as its own finding.",
          "The state is not a milder version of the one beside it and does not report a weaker claim. The spoken form says something and no cases have been gathered; the worn form gathers nothing because there is nothing that would count. The difference is three words at the end of one sentence."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "None is available from the wording as it stands, and that is the finding rather than an omission of this protocol. Were the deliverance stated without the closing clause, one wearer struck while carrying the names would refute it, exactly as in the spoken form beside it. With the clause in place there is no outcome to look for.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-07",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianWornAsmatYieldsDeliverance"
      },
      "pm:undividedOutcomes": "A wearer of the names who escapes his enemy, and a wearer of the names who is struck down by him. The first is deliverance by the will of God; the second is a deliverance that was not willed. The sentence covers both and forbids neither, so no observation of a wearer's fate can bear on it.",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The correspondence between the Indian channel-and-heat material and its Tibetan form, held as a node in order to be counted out: the route is documented, so the agreement is reception and carries no weight as evidence of an independently arrived-at structure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IndoTibetanReception",
      "label": {
        "de": "Indo-tibetische Rezeption, keine Konvergenz",
        "en": "Indo-Tibetan reception, not convergence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because a graph that only holds findings cannot show its own exclusions. Any later count of independent attestations must skip this pair."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Kundalini"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Rtsa"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The six yogas reach Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator; the transmission is named in the Tibetan sources themselves.",
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A dream held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and distinguished from the ordinary dream not by its content but by the state in which it arrives."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Theopemptos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum",
        "en": "The god-sent dream"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The state is described as the one in which sleep is departing and waking beginning, or as a condition between the two, accompanied by a voice, by a light, and by an inability to move the eyes. This is the closest description in the collection to what modern accounts call the hypnopompic state, and the immobility is named as part of the sign rather than as an obstacle. What arrives in it is a communication received, not a faculty exercised."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that was taken over, as it stands in the tradition performing the reworking."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:reworkedClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "bearbeitete Behauptung",
        "en": "reworked claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "2004",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that emotions form a graded ladder that indicates how near a person stands to a desired state, with joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love and appreciation at its top rung."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HicksEmotionalGuidanceScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Skala der emotionalen Führung",
        "en": "The emotional guidance scale"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The diagnostic marker is the top rung and its sequence: joy, knowledge, empowerment, freedom, love, appreciation. Six terms, and the fourth of them is the one that gives the sequence away, because a list built from an older stock has no reason to place freedom between empowerment and love. A later list carrying this block, in this order, carries it from here.",
          "The upper block of this scale reappears almost word for word in later lists that present themselves as older. Recorded so that the wording has a dated first appearance to be checked against."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A law of the emotions, presented as a description of how feeling works rather than as one author's arrangement.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Esther Hicks and Jerry Hicks",
      "source": "E. Hicks and J. Hicks, Ask and It Is Given (2004), ch. 22",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesUnbeschrauen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Knüpfen setzt voraus, dass es unbeschrauen geschieht",
        "en": "The knotting presupposes that it is done unbeschrauen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, because the passage gives the condition as part of the instruction and says nothing about what follows if it is broken. The book has consequences elsewhere and none here, and the difference is kept rather than filled in."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LovePresupposesApatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Liebe setzt Apatheia voraus, Erkenntnis die Liebe",
        "en": "Love presupposes apatheia, and knowledge presupposes love"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The chain is stated as generation and not as sequence: each term is the child or the flower of the one before it. That is why the strength here is necessity, and why a consequence can be quoted at all."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "Love is called the offspring of apatheia and is said to keep the door to the contemplation of created things, so that without it the door is not passed.",
      "pm:dependentStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Physike"
        }
      ],
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Agape"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Apatheia"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The final object of the ascent, seen only by one who has climbed by way of the intervening rungs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BeautyItself",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schönheit selbst",
        "en": "Beauty itself"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 211c-212a",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The date on which the count was last carried forward. A protocol without this date claims a scrutiny it does not document."
      },
