    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SelfRememberingAnchoring",
      "label": {
        "de": "Selbsterinnern wird im äußeren Wachen ausgeführt",
        "en": "Self-remembering is performed in outward waking"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:outwardWaking"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The source sets the exercise in the middle of ordinary activity and reports it while walking a street, entering a shop and telephoning; withdrawal is refused rather than unmentioned, since the whole classification of ways in this teaching turns on not leaving life."
      },
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The axis on which a source's account of where the awareness is located during the act is placed: with the body, or apart from it."
      },
      "id": "pm:AwarenessLocus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ort des Bewusstseins",
        "en": "Awareness Locus"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When the oracle predicts evil the enquirer must perform the remedy. Ground is cleared near the house or in the bush, a mat is spread and a short thick peg driven through it, and the enquirer with his practitioner, who taps a small cymbal with an iron rod, pours on the wood first water and then the blood of a fowl. The body of the fowl falls to the practitioner. The witness states that there are many different forms of the remedy."
      },
      "id": "pmp:VoSisa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vo-sisa, was bei schlechtem Spruch zu tun ist",
        "en": "Vo-sisa, what is done when the oracle answers badly"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The remedy is what turns a bad prediction into an action, and it is the point at which the oracle stops being falsifiable by the event: a predicted evil that does not occur is then the remedy working, and one that occurs is the prediction. The same witness reports the saying that the practitioner who is most cunning takes to the oracle, because it pays best."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Fon informants and interpreters at Abomey, unnamed in this passage and following an order the recorder says his interpreters dictated; recorded by Richard F. Burton on the mission of 1863 to 1864; given in Fon, of which the work prints the terms, the account itself in English.",
      "source": "Richard F. Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome (2nd ed., London: Tinsley Brothers, 1864), vol. II, pp. 143-144",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:VamaWorshipYieldsWorldlyPowers",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der heterodoxe Ritus bringt die weltlichen Vermögen hervor",
        "en": "The heterodox rite brings about the worldly powers"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered on the strength of the passage alone. Whether anything of the kind occurs is a separate question and would need a pm:Testing node; the claim here is that the source says so, and for that the place is the whole of the evidence."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:VamaWorship"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:WorldlyPowersOfTheLeftHandRite"
      },
      "source": "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the fruits of the rite (135b-138a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the breath is drawn in through the mouth with the tongue between the lips, producing a hissing sound, and is not let out the same way."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sitkari",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sītkārī",
        "en": "Sītkārī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.54-56 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianSucklingProtectionPresupposesTheWearing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bewahrung setzt das Tragen über die Frist voraus",
        "en": "The preserving presupposes the wearing through the term"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The prayer says the writing down and the wearing must be done and gives the term, and it names no consequence of breaking off, so nothing here carries a citable consequence of skipping."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianWearingUntilChildbearingEnds"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The great purification, stated throughout in numbers. Six holes are dug for the bull's urine and three more for the water, a pace apart; twelve furrows are drawn around them; the liquid is poured with a spoon of iron or of lead, so that the purifier does not touch. At the first water hole there is one washing, at the second two, at the third three. The whole runs nine nights, in three periods of three, with a washing of body and clothes after each. The fee is graded by station: for a priest a blessing of the just, for a lord of a province a camel of high value, for a master of a house a cow of three years."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Barasnum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Barašnūm der neun Nächte",
        "en": "The Barashnum of the nine nights"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Barasnum i no shab"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "barasnum"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "baresnum"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Vendidad, fargard 9.7-14, 9.31-38, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase",
        "en": "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it.",
          "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:Daoist"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practitioner beats the drum and sings, others present singing with him in higher and lower notes. He then casts himself on the ground, grows black in the face and lies as if dead, the drum laid as near as possible on his head, sweating. Those present do not leave off singing for the whole time. When he awakes he relates all the circumstances of the business enquired after."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise bei stillliegendem Leib",
        "en": "The journey made while the body lies still"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The continuous inward prayer that gives the tradition its practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herzensgebet",
        "en": "The prayer of the heart"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Inuit transmission of an office whose holder is said to perceive what is hidden, to call up helping spirits and to act through them, acquired through solitude and privation and exercised for a household rather than for an institution."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq",
      "label": {
        "de": "Inuitische Angakkuq-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Inuit angakkuq transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Contact route, stated rather than assumed. No route is proposed by anyone between the Iglulik Inuit and the literate transmissions this graph already holds, and none is available for the doctrinal content. The corpus was nevertheless fixed in nineteen twenty-one and twenty-two, well after contact: the narrative itself names whalers, and the region was under mission influence at the time of the recording. The value here is therefore contact-route-disputed and not contact-route-absent, following the correction made for the Māori entry, and the tradition may not be counted as a party to a convergence until the dispute stands as its own node. See prima-materia#101 and prima-materia#102.",
          "Read in the opening of the volume: the account of the working method, the first meeting near Repulse Bay, the old singer Ivaluardjuk, and the autobiography of Takornâq, printed pages ten to thirty-two. The chapters on the shamans, on the spirits and on the taboo system, which carry the terms this tradition is usually reported by, were not reached in this run. The limit is one of the access path and not of the work: the volume is digitised and public, and the portion delivered by the tool in use broke off at printed page thirty-two. See prima-materia#99 and prima-materia#100.",
          "The material read is testimony of an unusual kind for this graph. It is not a doctrinal corpus but a life story told in the first person by a woman who is herself said to have practised, recorded by an ethnographer who spoke a related language and worked without an interpreter. What that buys is a speaker attached to each claim; what it costs is that almost every capacity is ascribed by the narrator to somebody else, which is why the nodes below stand on third-party ascription and not on first-person report."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos; Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Caribou Eskimos",
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-32",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A collar of red list bound with white tape, worn in Loango by a person who has taken the bark and survived it, to show the people that he did so successfully. The figures worked on it are read out by the wearer: signs for the common descent of the parties, for the strife that divided them, for the witnesses called and saluted, for the accusers set aside, and for the place given to the wearer in the town where he took it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RedCollarOfTheSurvivedOrdeal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der rote Kragen derer, die die Rinde genommen haben",
        "en": "The red collar worn by those who took the bark"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A sign of success that is public, durable and worn, and the file holds it apart from the conditions of failure for the reason SPEC §15 gives: signs of success are announced in advance and are reported by the person to whom they were announced. Two cases are recorded by name and date, an elderly woman who visited the recorder wearing one, and Matueka on the fifth of January nineteen hundred, who explained each figure on his."
      },
      "source": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 73 and pp. 74-75 and p. 77",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Platonic dialogues and the ascent they describe from particular to intelligible beauty."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Platonic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Platonische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Platonic tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition the Symposium was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The Stephanus pagination is stable across the modern editions, so the citations locate the text; what is unknown is whether the Greek or a translation was read."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 210a-212a",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two marks set on the practice: one is not to hear the sound of one's own breathing, and a feather held before nose and mouth is not to stir; and one is always to give out less than one has taken in."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der für den Übenden selbst unhörbare Atem, kürzer aus als ein",
        "en": "The breath inaudible to its own bearer and shorter out than in"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Reopened and confirmed at the wording, with the test made exact. The chapter names the material and the place: a goose down is laid on the nose and the mouth, and that it does not stir while the qi is let out is the sign. Nothing is said about the intake. That is an outcome a second person can observe, which is rare enough in this graph to carry a protocol of its own.",
          "The intake is at the nose and the release at the mouth. The distribution is the reverse of the one a modern draft assigns, which treats mouth breathing as a fault on the intake and says nothing of the release. The passage also fixes the pace by an external test rather than by a count of seconds, which is what makes it examinable at all. See prima-materia#26."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome of that casting, given as a plain prediction: the figures of wood will come out well, they will speak and talk on earth. The prediction is about a future state of affairs and not about a duty, so it is the kind of answer that an outcome can meet or miss."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TziteAnswer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Antwort des Loses über die Holzmenschen",
        "en": "The answer the lot gave about the men of wood"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapter 2",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ExtasyPresupposesTheDrum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Zustand setzt die Trommel voraus",
        "en": "The state presupposes the drum"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source names the drum's function and the consequence of its absence in one clause. It is the rare case in which an instrument is given a mechanism rather than a meaning, and the mechanism is what a later examination would have to address."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The drum is needed for the shamans' self-excitation, without which they cannot bring themselves into the state of extasy."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusExtasy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusShamanDrum"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, p. 272",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The channels of the subtle body through which the winds are held to move; Tsongkhapa's presentation counts one hundred and twenty principal ones."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Rtsa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kanäle",
        "en": "Channels"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of placing one concept in relation to another."
      },
      "id": "pm:Relating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beziehen",
        "en": "Relating"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to touch friends and enemies at a distance, however remote, for good or for evil, marked in the chart by lines drawn from the hands; alongside it the ability to traverse all space, marked by lines from the feet."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Berühren in der Ferne, zum Guten wie zum Bösen",
        "en": "Touching at a distance for good or ill"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The early modern attempt to give magic a philosophy: sympathies, images, memory as an operative art, and angelic communication kept as a record."
      },
      "id": "pmt:RenaissanceMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gelehrte Magie der Renaissance",
        "en": "Renaissance learned magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Dee's diaries are a working log with dates, which makes them a different kind of witness from the treatises around them."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ficino, De vita libri tres; Agrippa, De occulta philosophia; Bruno, De umbris idearum; Dee, Monas Hieroglyphica and the spiritual diaries",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether exposure to cold supplies the practitioner with something, in the physical sense the word energy carries, or draws on him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ColdAsSourceProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Kälte Quelle oder Widerstand ist",
        "en": "Whether cold is a source or a resistance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second reading, that the claim reports an experience of being charged, is not established here and the correction matters. This node first cited an intervention study as showing that cold exposure raises circulating catecholamines. Read at the wording, that study attributes the rise to the breathing technique — cyclic hyperventilation with breath retention, producing intermittent respiratory alkalosis and hypoxia — and its participants practised breathing, not cold immersion, during the measurement. Cold was one component of a ten-day training and is not the variable the epinephrine measurements isolate. The claim's experiential reading therefore has no evidence in this file, and the study is retained only for what it does show.",
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The claim has two readings and they come out in opposite directions, so the protocol keeps them apart as prima-materia#341 requires. Read physically, it is turned around: cold exposure raises energy expenditure rather than supplying it. The heat produced in the brown adipose tissue that the cold recruits comes from oxidising the body's own substrate, and the deposit is depleted by the work, not filled by it. Cold costs; it does not deliver.",
          "The source transmission stands on the other side, and the issue that opened this said so before any evidence was consulted. The tibetan inner-heat literature sets cold as the resistance against which the heat is raised, never as what the heat is raised from. Where the draft and its transmission disagree, the measurements agree with the transmission. That is the reverse of the expected result and is the reason this node is worth having."
        ]
      },
      "pm:baseRate": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Energy expenditure and substrate use at thermoneutrality in the same participants."
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not carried. No search was made for work disputing the thermogenesis account or failing to replicate it. The reversal recorded in the notes is therefore one side of a question nobody has asked here."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "A. M. Cypess and others, Identification and importance of brown adipose tissue in adult humans, New England Journal of Medicine 360 (2009), no. 15, pp. 1509-1517, doi:10.1056/NEJMoa0810780",
        "M. Kox and others, Voluntary activation of the sympathetic nervous system and attenuation of the innate immune response in humans, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111 (2014), no. 20, pp. 7379-7384, doi:10.1073/pnas.1322174111",
        "V. Ouellet and others, Brown adipose tissue oxidative metabolism contributes to energy expenditure during acute cold exposure in humans, Journal of Clinical Investigation 122 (2012), no. 2, pp. 545-552"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the thermogenesis literature; the second reading off the intervention study cited below",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Practitioners exposed to cold are found to expend less energy, or to end the exposure with greater substrate reserves, than matched participants at thermoneutrality."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterYieldsHeilung",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Knüpfen vertreibt die Gichter",
        "en": "The knotting drives out the fits"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The symbol Celsus reports from the mysteries: a ladder of gates with a further gate at its top, standing for the two celestial revolutions — that of the fixed sphere and that of the planets — and for the passage of the soul through them. The first gate is of lead, the second of tin, the third of bronze, the fourth of iron, the fifth of an alloy, the sixth of silver, the seventh of gold."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Leiter mit sieben Toren und einem achten darüber",
        "en": "The ladder of seven gates with an eighth above it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is a representation and not an instruction. The witness says what the symbol shows; he does not say that anyone climbs it, what is said at a gate, or what happens to a soul that fails at one. Beside the ophite speeches, which give the words, this is the same architecture with the operative half missing.",
          "The count of seven rests on a conjecture. The manuscript reading is a ladder of lofty gates; a seven-gated ladder is Bouherel's emendation, printed in the notes of the translation used and repeated ever since without the qualification. Seven metals are then listed, so the emendation is well motivated — but the phrase in which the number is usually quoted is not the transmitted one, and every argument that counts this ladder as a seven-station ascent is counting the metals, not reading the word."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The axis on which a source's account of how a condition comes to someone is placed: through effort directed at it, or not."
      },
      "id": "pm:StateAcquisition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erwerb des Zustands",
        "en": "State Acquisition"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The single rule by which the drum is read. If the ring travels round towards the right hand, with the course of the sun, health, fortune and increase of men and beasts are promised. If it goes the contrary way, towards the left, sickness and all the evils of ill success are expected. The later recorder gives the same rule in weaker terms on the negative side: with the sun it augured a favourable event, against the sun a doubt then arose of a prosperous one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SunCourseRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Regel des Sonnenlaufs",
        "en": "The rule of the sun's course"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One further reading is given by the later recorder alone, from a named practitioner: that if the copper bird laid on the skin falls from the drum to the earth, it is a sign that the person enquired of will soon die. That is a third outcome beside the two directions, and it is entered here because a binary instrument with a third outcome is not binary.",
          "The difference between them is not nothing. The earlier makes the contrary turn a prediction of evil; the later makes it only a doubt. Whether that is a change in the practice, a difference between regions, or the difference between a compiler stating a rule and a missionary reporting what a man told him is not decidable from the two passages.",
          "Two recorders, ninety years apart, one compiling from priests' reports in Uppsala and one living among the people, give the same reading rule tied to the same physical direction. It is the best-attested single claim in this file."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 474 and 477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of how the ranks come to be: a power of the Father moves the true God and radiates within him, he gives voice, and when he had given voice there came forth this voice which is the emanation; the three lines drawn in the diagram are said to be the voices he gives out when he is commanded to sing praises to the Father, so that he brings forth emanations."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuVoxEmanans",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Stimme ist die Emanation, nicht ihre Anrufung",
        "en": "The voice is the emanation, not an invocation of it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the strongest thing the excerpt carries and it is easy to lose in the diagrams. The voice does not summon the emanation and does not name it: the voice is it. A tradition that holds this cannot treat a spoken name as a key to something else, and it puts the vowel chains of this corpus in a different class from the addresses of the ophite gates before any comparison of their sounds. See prima-materia#607."
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 5-6 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A centre of the subtle body, described in the source with its seed syllables and its position along the central channel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Cakra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Cakra",
        "en": "Cakra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The count varies across the older tantras, which give four, five or six. The tradition does not assign the centres the psychological qualities that modern presentations attach to them."
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The disclosure of the distinction of bitter from sweet, given in the text alongside the secrets of wisdom."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfBitterAndSweet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung von bitter und süß",
        "en": "Discernment of bitter and sweet"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What one who has danced the Sun dance to its completion may expect: a communication granted by the chief of the gods, in a vision, at any place and time before the dispersion of the next winter camp. The corpus also states what the completion is worth in degrees: tearing the sticks from the wounds unaided is the most effective manner, being freed after a swoon the least effective, and being released at the dawn of the next day counts as a rescue and is as meritorious as an escape."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CommunicationFromTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Mitteilung von der Sonne",
        "en": "A communication from the Sun"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The term is the reason this node is here. A promise with a deadline can fail; a promise without one cannot. The corpus fixes it as the dispersion of the next winter camp, which is a date that everyone in the camp shares and that arrives whether or not anything has come."
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and pp. 119-120",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The substance is held to be transmitted by unilinear descent from parent to child of the same sex: all the sons of a male witch are witches and none of his daughters, all the daughters of a female witch are witches and none of her sons."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguSexLinkedInheritance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Hexerei geht auf die Kinder desselben Geschlechts über",
        "en": "Witchcraft passes to the children of the same sex"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is checkable in a way few in this graph are, because the transmission itself draws consequences from it in practice and then declines the widest of them: in theory every clansman of a proven witch would be one, and in practice only close paternal kinsmen are so regarded."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 2-3",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HulaRetentionPresupposesKeptVows",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Behalten der Gesänge setzt das Halten der Gelübde voraus",
        "en": "Keeping the songs presupposes keeping the vows"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A memory sanction is a rare shape and it is nearly self-confirming, which is worth marking here rather than discovering later. A pupil who forgets is by that fact convicted of having been heedless, and a pupil who remembers proves the rule; no outcome is excluded. The claim is therefore weaker than it sounds even though the consequence is quotable."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The pupils who are heedless of their vows are told that the songs they have learned will fly away. The sanction is a loss of memory and not a punishment laid on by anyone; it is stated as what follows.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HulaKnowledgeRetained"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HalauConduct"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, p. 34",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZuniEfficacyPresupposesExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das öffentlich gesprochene Gebet setzt buchstabengetreue Wiedergabe voraus",
        "en": "The public prayer presupposes letter-perfect delivery"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered one step below the Māori and Navajo parallels and for a reason that is a property of the passage and not of the tradition: the requirement is stated, the consequence of failing it is not. Raising this to stated necessity would need a place in the corpus that says what a misrecited prayer does, and the body of the collection was not read. See the convergence node and prima-materia#112."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1917",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The arrangement of the Lakota gods into four classes of four, the superior, the associate, the subordinate and the god-like, gathered again into four groups of four called the Chief God, the Great Spirit, the Creator and the Executive, and those four taken as one. The compiler states that the shamans recognized a scheme and a system but had never formulated them into a single whole, that no one shaman was found who could give them in a comprehensive or sequential manner, that the scheme was formulated with their aid, and that it was afterwards approved by every informant to whom it was submitted."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WalkerSystematizationOfTheGods",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Vier-mal-vier-Schema der Götter als Ordnungsakt",
        "en": "The four-times-four scheme of the gods as an ordering act"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered because the corpus says so itself, in the introduction, and because a reader who takes the scheme for a transmitted doctrine will read every node that mentions a god wrongly. Approval after the fact is not authorship: the informants were asked whether an arrangement was right, which is a different act from producing it.",
          "The interview with Finger, printed in the same paper and taken a few days before the compiler left the reservation, does not reproduce the scheme. Asked how many there are he names eight, then says there is but one, then says there are four and that the other four are the same as those four. The compiler notes of that interview that there was no opportunity for verifying the information."
        ]
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "J. R. Walker, agency physician at Pine Ridge, with the Oglala shamans who approved the scheme after it was formulated",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 56 and pp. 79-81 and pp. 154-155",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhakaIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung",
        "en": "Retention alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. A suspension of the breath held as the accomplished form of the practice is the case this coordinate names by its own definition; the practitioner is awake and the ordinary sensory traffic has stopped being the point."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity claimed: that the operator, one out of so many tens of thousands, is transformed to immortality in this same hour; that his human soul-power subsists for a little while in purity and will afterwards be transmitted back to him; and, in the closing salutation, that being born again he passes away in being made great, and having been made great he dies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvImmortalitasHorae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unsterblichkeit für eben diese Stunde",
        "en": "Immortality for this same hour"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The last sentence is not a flourish. Being made great and dying are put in sequence and the second follows from the first, which places the death inside the attainment rather than after it.",
          "The promise carries its own term, and the term is short. A rite that says the state lasts an hour and then returns the operator to his necessity is making a smaller claim than the ascents this graph holds elsewhere, and a more checkable one: it says the practitioner will be back."
        ]
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, fifth and tenth utterances and invocatory logos (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteOnoel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Onoel, der Eselsgestaltige",
        "en": "Onoel the ass-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Onoel",
      "pm:nameRole": "seventh of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, asinine in appearance; Celsus gives the name as Thaphabaoth or Onoel, the diagram Origen read as Onoel or Thartharaoth",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Thaphabaoth"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Thartharaoth"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese office of yin-yang divination and calendrical prohibition, practised by a licensed bureau of the state."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Onmyodo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Onmyōdō",
        "en": "Onmyōdō"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hoki naiden; the onmyō provisions of the Engishiki",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that where no state is deliberately chosen for transmission, the one the sender is in transmits instead."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UnchosenSendingCarriesTheActualState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Senden ohne gewählte Signatur überträgt den eigenen Ist-Zustand",
        "en": "Sending without a chosen signature carries the sender's actual state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A claim about a failure mode rather than about a capacity, and that is what makes it the most tractable of the three. It says what happens when the practice is done wrong, and a claim about going wrong names an outcome that can be looked for."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#316",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaTerminusMortisDisputatus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Tod auf den folgenden Tag oder nur auf bald gesetzt ist",
        "en": "Whether the death is set for the morrow or only soon"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would need the medieval recensions, which were not opened; that the two readings stand side by side in one edition is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either.",
          "The Latin of the printing Turner worked from says of the sick man who answers badly that he is proculdubio in proximo moriturum, that is that he will die shortly, with no day named. Turner's English says: then doubtless conclude, He will dye on the morrow. Both stand in the edition read, on facing sides of the same passage.",
          "The difference falls on the only part of the claim that could be missed. A death that must come tomorrow can be watched for and can fail to arrive; a death that will come shortly has no last day, and no outcome ever refutes it. The English is therefore the examinable version and the Latin is not, and a protocol has to say which of the two it tests."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaInterrogatioAegroti"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30a, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
        "Ars notoria, the Latin of the Beringos printing appended to the Agrippa Opera, § 30a, printed beside the translation in the edition of Peterson"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity claimed for the art: to profit in all the sciences and to attain them in an extraordinary manner, with memory, understanding and eloquence increased and established in the speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zunehmen in allen Wissenschaften",
        "en": "Profiting in all the sciences"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to hear a figurative demand and produce the thing meant. The witness does not treat this as a skill beside descent but as the same fact: what the examination asks is whether the candidate can explain how the ruling men came and whether he is of the lineage of rulers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuyuaNaat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verstehen der Sprache von Zuyua",
        "en": "Understanding the language of Zuyua"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The text says nothing about where this language is learned or whether the answers are taught. That silence is the whole difficulty of the procedure as a test and is taken up at pmc:ZuyuaExamination."
      },
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-93",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made in or about a work, that its wording was received from a speaker other than the person who wrote or spoke it down."
      },
      "id": "pm:Receiving",
      "label": {
        "de": "Empfangen",
        "en": "Receiving"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:Originating, which names the author with whom a claim begins and the ascription it circulates under. A receiving makes no finding about who really composed the material; it records that the work claims a speaker, so that the claim is addressable, countable and disputable instead of riding invisibly in a citation.",
          "Whether an agreement between two such corpora can ever be more than reception is a separate question and a hard one. The route is documented across the whole block: the vocabulary runs on from Theosophy. A convergence there needs the demonstration that a receiver did not know the earlier literature, and that demonstration is expensive and rare."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus",
        "en": "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the corpus calls a supernatural communication and the English of the recorders calls a vision: a thing seen or heard, waking or asleep, in the form of anything that breathes or of something inanimate, which makes known by what it says or does that it is the thing sought. It may speak intelligibly, or in words the receiver does not understand, or in the language of birds and beasts."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Wowihanble",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wowihanble, eine empfangene Mitteilung",
        "en": "Wowihanble, a communication received"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The term is printed in the corpus as Wowihanble and the seeking as hanblapi. The digitised copies read from render the second word with an initial th, which is a known confusion of the scanning and not a variant of the print; the identifier follows the reading hanblapi. A check against a printed copy is held as an issue."
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and p. 156",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The making of a nail figure begins in the wood. A council is held first to decide whose life is to preside over the figure, and a boy of great spirit or a great and daring hunter is chosen; the party then goes into the bush and calls his name, the practitioner fells the tree, and blood is said to come from the cut. A fowl is killed and its blood mixed with what comes from the tree."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MuambaFelling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fällen des Muamba-Baums für eine Nagelfigur",
        "en": "The felling of the muamba tree for a nail figure"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bavili at Loango, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the trader R. E. Dennett during his residence on the Loango coast from 1879, the book published in 1906; given in the Vili dialect, whose orthography the recorder sets out himself and whose terms he prints, the accounts themselves in English only.",
      "source": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianAsmatYieldsRestraintOfDevils",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die an der Tür gesprochenen Namen halten die Teufel ab",
        "en": "The names said at the door hold the devils off"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The wording makes the protection follow from knowing the names rather than from saying them at any particular moment: it is to him that knoweth these that no devil draws nigh. The instruction to say them at the door is given first and the guarantee is stated after it without repeating the condition, so the two do not close on each other."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianKeepingOfTheDevilsInRestraint"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The speaker the work claims for the wording, given as the work gives it. A literal and not a link to a named being: what is recorded is the claim, and typing it as a bearer would grant the claim the thing it is asserting."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Receiving",
      "id": "pm:claimedSpeaker",
      "label": {
        "de": "behaupteter Sprecher",
        "en": "claimed speaker"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianWornAsmatYieldsDeliverance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die getragenen Namen erretten vom Feind",
        "en": "The worn names deliver from the enemy"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatWornUponTheBreast"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianDeliveranceByTheWillOfGod"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1888",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens",
        "en": "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58.",
          "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing.",
          "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Helena P. Blavatsky",
      "source": "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShamanizingYieldsKnowledgeOfTheHidden",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schamanisieren bringt das Wissen um das Verborgene hervor",
        "en": "Shamanizing brings the knowledge of what is hidden"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusKnowledgeOfTheHidden"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 121, pp. 331-332",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AhwalAreNotByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zustände werden nicht durch Anstrengung erlangt",
        "en": "The states are not attained by effort"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:notByEffort"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Ahwal"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The handbooks describe these as descending on the wayfarer as a gift and distinguish them from the stations precisely by their not being attainable through effort. That is the criterion this coordinate names, stated by the source itself."
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the living Jesus tells the apostles to do: to receive his word with certainty and to know it, to fulfil it, and thereby to receive the soul from the race of the understanding mind so that it ceases to be earthly and becomes understanding."
      },
      "id": "pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wort mit Gewissheit erkennen",
        "en": "Knowing the word with certainty"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No procedure is given here, and that is worth recording rather than filling: the corpus is famous for seals, ciphers and vowel chains, and the part of it that was read prescribes none of them. What it prescribes is knowing and fulfilling a word. The operative apparatus appears in the diagrams and in the second book, and neither was opened."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1950",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a people without instruments held, as transmitted knowledge, the movements and characteristics of a star that cannot be seen: its period around Sirius, its extreme density, and its priority in creation. The claim is put forward in an article of nineteen fifty which sets the question of how such knowledge could exist and expressly declines to answer or even to pose it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Dogon kannten den Begleiter des Sirius",
        "en": "The Dogon knew the companion of Sirius"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination and not as a dispute, because a dispute needs two sides that each say something about the matter. Here one side says nothing: no transmission carries the identification, and the tradition file holds only the seed-star. The identification begins in the work named below.",
          "The decisive sentence is the one the reception never quotes. On p. 274 the recorders write that the question of how men without instruments know the movements and certain characteristics of practically invisible bodies has not been decided, nor even raised. That sentence grants the premise and withholds the enquiry in one move, and everything the claim later became rests on the granted premise.",
          "The later form of the claim, in which the knowledge is explained by contact with visitors from the Sirius system, begins in a work of nineteen seventy-six that has not been read at the wording in this run and is therefore not entered. See prima-materia#251.",
          "The second decisive sentence is a footnote. Where the article gives the orbital period as some fifty years, the note observes that this period is very close to that of the companion of Sirius and cites a French astronomical periodical of nineteen thirty-one for it. The identification is therefore the recorders' and is made in the recorders' own apparatus; the body of the text never says Sirius B. What the informants are reported as saying and what the note makes of it are separable on the page, and separating them is what this node exists for."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "ancient Dogon astronomy: a pre-contact African tradition holding the existence, period and density of Sirius B, cited both as evidence of a lost indigenous science and as evidence of extraterrestrial contact",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen",
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274, 282 with its first footnote, and 287",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AraqielTeachesSignsOfTheEarth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Araqiel offenbart die Zeichen der Erde",
        "en": "Araqiel discloses the signs of the earth"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheEarth"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Araqiel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A class whose instances form a closed set of permitted values. A property whose range is a scale takes one of that scale's declared instances and nothing else."
      },
      "id": "pm:Scale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Skala",
        "en": "Scale"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Closure is the point. rdfs:range states an intention and constrains nothing; until pm:ScaleValueShape was written, a misspelt or invented value passed validation in silence and was indistinguishable from a declared one."
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of being free or common, that is, not under the restriction of tapu. The witness states that persons and things under tapu can be brought into this state by ceremonies, so that the two are not fixed conditions but ends of an operation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Noa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Noa",
        "en": "Noa"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus",
        "en": "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourthJhana"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Awareness turned to the senses and to the world they report, with the body in ordinary use."
      },
      "id": "pm:outwardWaking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nach außen gerichtetes Wachen",
        "en": "Outward waking"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Set this where the source has the act performed openly, in the world, among others: an invocation spoken aloud, a divination read off an object, a healing performed by touch. The Māṇḍūkya calls this jāgrat."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Māori transmission of mana as an efficacy that attaches to persons and things, of tapu as its restriction, and of karakia as the form of address."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Maori",
      "label": {
        "de": "Māori-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Māori transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Shortland 1882, chapters II to V and the appendix, in the 1882 London edition. Chapters VI and VII, on claiming and naming land, were not read; nothing else of the principal corpus was opened.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the reason is a rule of this project rather than a judgement about Polynesia. pm:contactRouteAbsent asks whether a route was available for the period in which the corpus was fixed. This corpus was fixed in writing from the eighteen-forties onward, a generation after the first mission stations, by Māori writers and by European recorders working with mission orthography. Shortland himself reports a rite of removing tapu that was carried out with a Christian morning service inside it and adds that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial; that is not a suspicion about the material but an observation printed in the witness. The transmission is older than the contact; the corpus this graph can cite is not. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry, and prima-materia#108 for the source criticism that decides it.",
          "The word from which every western theory of an impersonal magical force was built. The graph uses the notion throughout and has never held the tradition it came from."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ngā Mōteatea; Best, The Maori; Best, Tuhoe",
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that no man presume to say any of the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without great reverence and discretion, and that it be treated only at certain determinate and appointed times."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaEbrietasKautel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nicht nach Trunk oder Ausschweifung, und nicht nüchtern ohne Bedacht",
        "en": "Not after drink or luxury, and not fasting without discretion"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Fasting appears on both sides of the corpus: it is prescribed for the first day of a rehearsal and warned against here without reverence and discretion. The two passages are held apart rather than reconciled; whether the witness contradicts itself or distinguishes two situations is not decided from what was read."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 44, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the confession is made for: the faults of a man are held to anger his ancestors and his huacas, and the anger to be the reason he falls sick. The confession, the blowing of the powders, the rubbing with the pardon-stone and the washing of the head are done so that the anger is turned aside and the sickness with it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Abwenden des Zorns der Huacas und der Ahnen",
        "en": "Turning aside the anger of the huacas and the ancestors"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Beside the bark the corpus lists seven lesser ordeals with their Kikongo names, by hot knife, by boiling palm oil, by a bracelet in the oil, by knotted grass, by a bean on a twisted string, by a glass bead under the eyelid and by hot water. Of these the corpus states a condition on the practitioner: unless he is prepared to undergo each of them, the bark excepted, before the accused does, he is little respected and would soon lose his practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheOrdealGiverMustStandTheOtherOrdeals",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Betreiber muss die übrigen Proben selbst bestehen",
        "en": "The practitioner must stand the other ordeals himself"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A qualification requirement that the practitioner meets in public, and the only one in the file. The corpus states in the same place how it is met: the practitioner rubs baobab juice on the arm of any accused person who pays for it, and on one who will not pay he rubs another decoction which is not protective, and the recorder adds that the practitioners undoubtedly know of juices that help them bear the tests and are willing to sell the knowledge.",
          "The bark is excepted, and the exception is the whole of the difference: the ordeal that kills is the one the practitioner does not have to take."
        ]
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 264-265",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteErataoth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erataoth, der Hundegestaltige",
        "en": "Erataoth the dog-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Erataoth",
      "pm:nameRole": "sixth of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, with the face of a dog",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative transmission of the science of letters and of the divine names, with squares, talismans and hours of working."
      },
      "id": "pmt:IslamicLetterMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Islamische Buchstaben- und Namensmagie",
        "en": "Islamic letter and name magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The ascription of the best known handbook to al-Būnī is contested, and the corpus grew after him under his name. The dispute is the first node this tradition should carry."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Šams al-maʿārif corpus ascribed to al-Būnī; Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the soul leaves the body and returns to it, reported of Aristeas of Proconnesus and of Hermotimus of Clazomenae."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SoulJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Seelenreise ohne den Körper",
        "en": "Journey of the soul apart from the body"
      },
      "source": [
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tree is never cut for any other purpose, and only the ordeal-giver may cut it, using a set formula: that if the person for whom he is cutting is really a witch the blade should bend at the stroke, and if he is not, the blade should enter and the wind stop blowing. The corpus adds the reading of the sign in the world: the air is often quite still for two or three hours before a storm, and that stillness is credited to someone, somewhere, cutting the ordeal tree."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NkasaTreeCutting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schlagen des Ordalbaums, mit Formel und Zeichen",
        "en": "The cutting of the ordeal tree, with its formula and its sign"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two conditions of failure are stated here before the ordeal begins, and the corpus then records what is done with them. The recorder states that although the omens may be in favour of the accused the ordeal-giver proceeds all the same, that the results are not infrequently quite contrary to the omens, and that the belief is unaffected by the discrepancy. A sign that is read, contradicted and kept is a different thing from a sign that is never tested, and the file holds it as the first."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms while the accounts are printed in English only.",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 262",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rule of verification the transmission sets for itself: concordance in the teaching of the spirits is the best control. It is stated with its conditions and with its limits. The revelations must come spontaneously, through a large number of mediums unknown to one another, and from different places. A single medium questioning many spirits is expressly not a sufficient guarantee, because one obsessing spirit could present the same content under different names; and mediums of a single centre are not sufficient either, because they may stand under a common influence. The criterion is restricted to what attaches to the principles of the doctrine and does not extend to communications on secondary matters."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ControleUniversel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die allgemeine Kontrolle der Geisterlehre",
        "en": "The universal control of the teaching of the spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Both exclusions are the founder's own and both are exactly the ones an outside critic would raise. That is worth marking, because it makes the rule better than the practice that followed it: the same author describes elsewhere how the scattered material was gathered and weighed at a single centre, and that centre was himself."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The making of the lodge itself: slender saplings thrust into the ground in a circle whose diameter is a little longer than the height of a tall man, their tops bent and bound to form a dome, the whole covered with robes so as to confine the vapour, and an opening large enough for a man to crawl through made anywhere on the border except toward the north, and closable."
      },
      "id": "pmp:IniTiConstruction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Errichten der Ini ti",
        "en": "Building the ini ti"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 66-67",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZiqelTeachesSignsOfShootingStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ziqel offenbart die Zeichen der Sternschnuppen",
        "en": "Ziqel discloses the signs of the shooting stars"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with Ezeqeel and the knowledge of the clouds."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfShootingStars"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Ziqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity a yielding claim arrives at. That the tradition says so is the claim; that the practice produces it is not asserted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Yielding",
      "id": "pm:yieldsCapacity",
      "label": {
        "de": "bringt Vermögen hervor",
        "en": "yields capacity"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The purgative part of the exercises, given over to the consideration of sin."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FirstWeek",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die erste Woche",
        "en": "The first week"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of method. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other: a passage, an edition, and the counter-search."
      },
      "id": "pm:methodOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung der Methode",
        "en": "method orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No domain is declared, deliberately. The axis is stated of whatever a source states it of — a transmission as a whole, a single practice, a stage within one — and a declared domain would force the level before the material has settled it."
      },
      "range": "pm:MethodOrientation",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A cross-tradition structural analogue, weaker than owl:sameAs and distinct from skos:closeMatch in that it asserts role-in-process similarity rather than lexical overlap."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "id": "pm:analogueConcept",
      "label": {
        "en": "analogue concept"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tantric transmission of a sixfold yoga whose completion is marked by a sequence of signs appearing in a darkened retreat."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Kalacakra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kālacakra",
        "en": "Kālacakra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sign sequence is stated in fixed order and is therefore comparable with the Dzogchen visions and with the phosphene literature, which is a convergence question the graph cannot currently pose."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Vimalaprabhā; Sekoddeśa and its commentary",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The word as the recorder of sixteen twenty-nine glosses it: one veiled or disguised under the appearance of an animal. He connects the name with calendars he had found among such people, which distribute the names of animals across the days."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Nahualli",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nahualli",
        "en": "Nahualli"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The gloss is the recorder's and is given in Spanish; the Nahuatl word is his lemma. That he offers an etymology at all is worth noting, because it shows he had the word from the people and not from a predecessor's book."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A division of practitioners into three kinds, the bound, the heroic and the divine, which governs the rites a person is competent for. The source describes the divine as all but a god, pure of heart, alike towards opposites and unattached, and the bound as one who must gather his own offerings and who may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BhavaClassification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei Dispositionen: paśu, vīra, divya",
        "en": "The three dispositions: paśu, vīra, divya"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the tradition's own answer to the question why one person takes one way and another another: not by choosing, but by what he is held competent for. The text presses the point polemically — it argues that if the bound disposition cannot exist in this age, the divine cannot either, and that only the heroic rites bear visible fruit. A record that reports the two paths as a choice between camps reports the later Western reading and not this one. See prima-materia#54."
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.50-57 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.19-20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the correct rendering is held to secure. The transmission places the outcome not with the reciter but with the party the chant is made for, which is the unusual part of this witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Person oder Familie",
        "en": "The good fortune of the person or family the chant honours"
      },
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The corpus states the diagnosis as a pair of alternatives with a remedy for each. A charged thing that fails to perform its office must either have a sacrifice offered to it or have its power renewed by the practitioner who first made it, or by a member of the same cult: it is either sulky because it has been neglected, or weak from loss of power. The sacrifice removes the sulkiness and the practitioner restores the power."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Ding, das versagt, schmollt oder ist erschöpft",
        "en": "A charged thing that fails is either sulky or weak"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The same corpus records the second, wider form of the move: when a man is not helped, he as a rule does not blame the practitioner but concludes that the diagnosis was wrong and that his trouble is not under the control of that particular charged thing, and simply changes practitioners, his confidence in the order untouched. The transmission thus has two exits from a failure before any question about the practice arises.",
          "This is the node the file is built around. It is a complete account of non-occurrence that never reaches the claim: whatever happens, the outcome is assigned to the state of the instrument and not to the question whether the instrument works. It costs a fee in either branch."
        ]
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 235 and pp. 230-231 and p. 250",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that the mass of dreams occurring in full sleep carries no divination, and that only the state at the edge of sleep is treated as a sign of divine presence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivineDreamIsNotOrdinarySleep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der göttliche Traum ist nicht der gewöhnliche Schlaf",
        "en": "The divine dream is not ordinary sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Load-bearing against any claim that sleep as such is an access. The source that comes closest in the whole collection to naming the threshold state is also the one that refuses the wider claim."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seed resembling a lentil is drunk; the recorder says the draught takes away the judgement, being very vehement. Those who drink it consult it as an oracle for everything they wish to know. Some hold the drinking of the seed for such consultations as their office, and such a specialist bears his own name."
      },
      "id": "pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Trinken des Ololiuhqui, um es zu befragen",
        "en": "Drinking the ololiuhqui in order to question it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Three things stand in the passage and are kept apart here: that the substance is drunk, that the drinker is questioned as an oracle, and that there is an office for it. Only the third makes this a transmission rather than a usage."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos II and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source prescribes the practice across the states this axis distinguishes: not in a set-apart session but through work, speech and sleep alike."
      },
      "id": "pm:anchoringUnbound",
      "label": {
        "de": "An keine Sinnesbindung gebunden",
        "en": "Not bound to an anchoring"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately not added to the other three axes. There is no case in the holdings for an awareness that is unbound as to its locus or its agency, and a value written for a case nobody has met is vocabulary running past the matter rather than ahead of it.",
          "Distinct from pm:anchoringDeclined, and the difference is which claim the tradition makes. A tradition that declines the axis denies that the distinction applies to it at all; a tradition that prescribes unbroken prayer grants the distinction and says the practice spans it. The hesychast literature is the clear case: it knows perfectly well that a man sleeps and wakes, and requires the prayer through both. See prima-materia#440."
        ]
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition that makes an ordinary man a shaman: he is a master of spirits, at least of a group of them. The mastered spirits are not guardians and do not elect him; the master must feed them and handle them, and introduces them into himself when he wants to. The shaman uses his own body as a placing for spirits."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Meisterung der Geister",
        "en": "Mastering of spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The discrimination the transmission itself draws, and the one most reliably lost in later reports of it. The difference between a person possessed by spirits and a shaman is that the shaman introduces them at his own will. The recorder states that the Tungus are always very careful as to the diagnosis, whether the person is possessed by the spirits or the spirits are possessed by the person, and that nobody would recognise anyone as a shaman who could not possess spirits in that sense.",
          "The relation is named by a term the recorder gives in five dialect forms at once. The digitised copy used is an optical transcription and its rendering of these forms is not trustworthy at the level of single letters, so the forms are not reproduced here as if they had been read cleanly. What is reported without doubt is that one term covers the relation across the Manchu, Birarcen, Kumarcen, Nercinsk and Khingan dialects, and that the spirits are said to use it of their master through the shaman's mouth."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 A, p. 271",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning against turning the wheel too hard, printed inside the spoken charm and addressed to the operator rather than to the thief the surrounding words address."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRadKautel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Treib das Rad nicht gar zu stark um",
        "en": "Do not drive the wheel too hard"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The place of the sentence is the finding. A caution set inside the words to be spoken cannot be read aloud without being obeyed or noticed, and an operator working from the page meets it at the moment it applies. What it does not say is what turning too hard would do."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicRadNabe"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 84",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 53"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Haitian transmission of spirits who arrive in a named person and speak, with a liturgy that assigns each an appearance, a rhythm and a manner."
      },
      "id": "pmt:HaitianVodou",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haitianisches Vodou",
        "en": "Haitian Vodou"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deren's account is a first-person report of being mounted by a spirit written by the observer herself, which is a rare form: participant testimony with the observation intact."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Métraux, Le vaudou haïtien; Deren, Divine Horsemen",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "One of three things the chapter names as the essentials, beside the guarding of the essence and the taking of the one great medicine. It says of itself that there are several methods within it, that no method is given whole at the first teaching, and that the essential is told only to one who has not slackened."
      },
      "id": "pmp:XingqiBaopuzi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zirkulation des Qi, wie das achte Kapitel sie darstellt",
        "en": "The circulation of the qi as the eighth chapter sets it out"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Late Bronze Age Levantine ritual and incantation, including the address to the deified dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:UgariticRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ugaritisch-levantinisches Ritual",
        "en": "Ugaritic and Levantine ritual"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The KTU corpus, including the Rapiuma texts and the serpent incantations",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A candidate for the office is expected to be the pupil of a babalawo known for wide knowledge of the religion and for skill in divining, for at least three years, and the course is divided into three parts covering those years. In the first the candidate learns the names of all the signs and the marks that stand for them and shows his command of them in practice; in the second he learns the traditions attached to them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BabalawoPupilage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lehrzeit des Anwärters auf das Amt des Babalawo",
        "en": "The pupilage of a candidate for the office of babalawo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "What the third year requires was not recovered from the portion of the extract delivered in this run. The gap is a property of the retrieval and not of the work, and it is recorded so that the next reader opens the appendix at that place rather than repeating the whole."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A bowl of hollowed pine, fir or birch with a skin stretched over it, painted with figures of the gods, the angels, the evil spirits, the associates of the noaidi, the sun, the dwellings, the sacred mountains and the beasts of the country. A ring or a brass cover is laid on the skin, the skin is struck with a hammer of reindeer horn, and the ring travels across the painted figures. Where it comes to rest, or which way it runs, gives the answer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung der Trommel",
        "en": "Consulting the drum"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two recorders describe the same instrument ninety years apart and their descriptions of the mechanism agree in every part that matters: the hammer of horn, the ring or cover laid on the skin, the painted field, the reading from where the ring goes. The later adds what the earlier does not: that the drum is not thought fit for use unless got by inheritance, and that women may not touch it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48 and p. 57",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, their Language, Manners and Religion, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, Of the Magic Arts of the Laplanders, pp. 474-477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tibetan transmission that claims descent from Zhangzhung rather than from India, with its own dzogchen cycles and its own founder."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Bon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bön",
        "en": "Bön"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The independence claim is the point. If it holds, Bön is a witness beside Tibetan Buddhism rather than a variant of it; if it fails, the failure is one of the better documented cases of a tradition constructing its own antiquity. Either outcome is a finding."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhang Zhung Nyan Gyud; the Bön dzogchen cycles in the published Bön canon",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the striking of spirits, the killing of the embryo and the serpent's bite."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kasdeja",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kasdeja",
        "en": "Kasdeja"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TraversalPresupposesSequence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Durchquerung setzt voraus, dass ihre Stationen der Reihe nach genommen werden",
        "en": "The traversal presupposes that its stations are taken in order"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, on purpose and against the reading a modern draft makes. The sources say kramaśaḥ and kramāt kramam, in sequence and step by step, and they say it more than once. What no source consulted says is what happens to one who skips a station. A prohibition needs a consequence somebody can quote, and this one has none; without it the claim is an instruction that a later reader hardened into a rule. See prima-materia#36."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:WorldPathTraversal"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre"
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The enumerative transmission of the evolutes of nature and of a witnessing principle distinct from them, on which the yoga of the graph already rests."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Samkhya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sāṃkhya",
        "en": "Sāṃkhya"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhyakārikā; Yuktidīpikā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tungus and Manchu transmission of an office whose holder is defined as a master of spirits rather than as one they possess, entered through an illness that resolves only on acceptance, carried by a drum and a brass mirror, and conferred by a formal recognition that a candidate can fail."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SiberianShamanism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sibirische Schamanenüberlieferung",
        "en": "Siberian shamanic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run at the wording: the monograph of nineteen thirty-five, chapters XXII to XXXI, that is the whole of the part on shamanism, pages 268 to 402, in the London edition of that year. Left unread: parts one to three, pages 49 to 240, on positive knowledge, on the hypotheses and on the practical consequences of the hypotheses, which carry the system of spirits this part presupposes; the introduction, pages 1 to 48, which is missing from the digitised copy used; and the whole of the Sakha material named in the registration, which the author does not carry.",
          "That correction is the reason the entry was worth opening first. The registration noted that this is the region the word shaman was taken from and that the graph used the category while holding no witness from its source. What the source says is that the complex it names is comparatively recent and largely borrowed. Every convergence in this graph that leans on a Siberian parallel is affected by it, and the affected nodes are exactly the ones nobody would have checked.",
          "The author is one recorder and not a chorus. He worked among the Tungus of Manchuria and Transbaikalia and among the Manchus over some twenty years, in their languages, and he says at the outset which groups he saw and which he did not. Where he gives his own reconstruction rather than what he was told, the notes on the nodes below say so.",
          "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and this is the finding of the run rather than a formality. The route runs through the instruments and through the word. The brass mirror without which no performance is possible is bought from the Mongols, who receive them from China and Tibet, and it is the same object that Lamaism uses on its altars; the headdress of the Tungus of Manchuria carries five images of Buddha; the term burkan, applied to a great number of spirits, is the Mongol modification of Buddha; and the word saman itself is traced by the author to sramana through the Chinese san-men. A tradition whose indispensable instrument, whose spirit vocabulary and whose own name run to a corpus this graph already holds cannot be counted as an independent witness for anything they share."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus; Shirokogoroff, Social Organization of the Northern Tungus; the published Olonkho epics; Anisimov's field records",
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95, pp. 271-274",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sign held to be the principal one, called the leader, the commander and the king. Its appearance on the divining bowl is taken to mean that the message delivered is of very great weight."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EjiOgbeFirstOfTheSigns",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eji Ogbe als erstes der Zeichen",
        "en": "Eji Ogbe as the first of the signs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A stated order of rank among the signs, and therefore a place where a later reader can check whether a modern list has kept the tradition's own order or imposed another."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht",
        "en": "The imperfections of insight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Piti"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The mid twentieth century transmission of teachings ascribed to visitors from other worlds, reported first as bodily meetings and later as messages received without one."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ContacteeTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Contactee-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Contactee transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The shift from a claimed physical encounter to a claimed interior reception happens inside this literature and is datable. It is the hinge between the older revealed corpora and the later channelled ones."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Adamski, Inside the Space Ships; Menger, From Outer Space to You; van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that several traditions name something conducted or borne in the body and susceptible of deliberate direction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VitalEnergyCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Lebensenergie",
        "en": "The vital-energy correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The correspondence is weaker than it looks. Pneuma is a principle of cosmic cohesion and not a faculty; the breath of the Sefer Yetzirah is a medium of formation and not a conduction through the limbs. Only the Chinese and the Indian material carries the operative sense the modern draft assumes."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Kundalini"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Piti"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Pneuma"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Prana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Qi"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Ruach"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "The two witnesses that carry the claim of independence are fixed long before any route between them. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows; the Chāndogya passage belongs to the early Upaniṣads and is older still. The earliest documented traffic that could have carried Indian breath technique into China is the Buddhist transmission of the first centuries of the common era, and both fixations precede it. Neither is an ethnographic gathering, so no question of a recorder's feedback arises; what each carries is the wording of a received text.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:Daoist"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:Upanisadic"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa the translator and is therefore reception, not independent attestation. Buddhist and Daoist exchange along the trade routes is documented and forbids treating the Chinese and Buddhist testimony as wholly separate. The Greek and Hebrew items are neither route-linked nor operatively parallel.",
      "source": [
        "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben",
        "en": "The downward wind driven upward by force"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksusIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das göttliche Auge wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt",
        "en": "The divine eye is exercised in withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The sūtra reads bhuvanajñānaṃ sūrye saṃyamāt: knowledge of the worlds from saṃyama on the sun. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The five elements of the rite belonging to heroic practice, named in the source as wine, meat, fish, parched grain, and the union of man and woman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Panchatattva",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf Tattvas des heroischen Ritus",
        "en": "The five tattvas of the heroic rite"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The fourth element is parched grain and not a ritual gesture. Wilson renders the same list in 1832 with mudrā glossed as gesticulations of the fingers and maithuna as women; the divergence is his and not the text's, and it matters because grain makes that element a food where a gesture would make it an act. Recorded so that the current gloss can be checked against a verse instead of against another summary. See prima-materia#53.",
          "The same passage carries its own caution, and the caution is not a Western addition: the source says that the men of this age, being greedy and lustful, will neglect the discipline, drink for the pleasure of it, and lose the distinction of right and wrong. The warning against the rite stands inside the text that prescribes it."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.58-59 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.61 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The two recorders of this transmission report opposite things about the same ceremony. The first prints a rule for the days on which the Sun does not show his face, assigns such a day to an offence in the camp, and provides that the day be struck from the count of four, which presupposes that such days occurred. The second reports that the medicine men prayed for fair weather for a month beforehand, that their efforts were always successful, and that the oldest men could not remember the falling of rain during a Sun dance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SunDanceWeatherDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Wetter bei einem Sonnentanz je versagte",
        "en": "Whether the weather ever failed at a Sun dance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Neither report is a study and neither can be preferred on its face. What separates them is not doctrine but memory of events: the first states a contingency and the second denies that the contingency ever arose. The dispute is entered because the second statement is checkable in principle against the recorded weather of the years and places at which the ceremony was held, and because the first would be untestable without it.",
          "The two are not equally situated. The first was written down at Pine Ridge from shamans who conducted the ceremony, the second at Standing Rock from men who danced in it, and the last tribal Sun dance the second witness names was held in eighteen eighty-one. A reader who wants to resolve this has to decide whether never having rained is a claim about the weather or a claim about what the rule on the other side was for."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:TheSunHidesHisFaceOverAnOffence"
      },
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 99",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 100 and p. 105"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission of calming and contemplation, with a systematic account of the obstacles, illnesses and demonic states that arise in practice."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Tiantai",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tiantai",
        "en": "Tiantai"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhiyi, Mohe zhiguan; Zhiyi, Xiao zhiguan",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A value on one of the awareness axes. The class exists so that a single constraint can keep pm:axisValue to the declared coordinates; every value below carries it alongside the axis it belongs to."
      },
      "id": "pm:AwarenessValue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wert einer Bewusstseinsachse",
        "en": "Awareness Axis Value"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The qualifying material of the drum. It must be a pine, fir or birch grown in a particular place and turning directly according to the sun's course, which the recorder defines: the grain of the wood, running from the bottom of the tree to the top, winds itself from the right hand to the left. A tree so grown is held acceptable to the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Holz, das sich mit dem Sonnenlauf dreht",
        "en": "Wood that turns with the sun's course"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second condition on the same material stands in the same passage: the piece must be taken from the root, cleft asunder and hollowed on one side, and the skin is stretched over the hollow. It is entered here rather than as a node of its own because it names no criterion by which a piece could fail, only a manner of working.",
          "The direction is stated twice in this file in frames that do not obviously agree. Here the sun's course is the grain winding from the right hand to the left as it rises; at pmc:SunCourseRule it is the ring travelling round towards the right hand. Whether a spiral seen along a rising trunk and a ring seen on a flat skin describe one direction or two is not decidable from the passages, and is left standing rather than harmonised.",
          "The recorder gives the sun-acceptability as his own conjecture — 'from this perhaps they believe' — not as something told to him. The requirement is reported; its reason is his."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The one chain of vowels the translation prints in full, invoked as the deathless names innate with life which have not yet descended to mortal nature nor been made articulate by human tongue, with the instruction to utter them all with fire and spirit once to the end and then begin again, until the seven immortal gods of the cosmos are completed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvVocesSepties",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vokalkette, siebenmal durchgesprochen",
        "en": "The vowel chain, uttered through seven times"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A dose and a stopping rule, which is rare. The chain is not said once but run through until a count of seven is reached, and the count is given by the gods rather than by the operator; the announced consequence is thunder and crashing in the surround, felt in the body at every crash.",
          "The claim the words make about themselves is worth separating from their use. They are called names that have not yet come down to mortal nature and have never been made articulate by a human tongue — that is, the text asserts that what it prints cannot be what is meant. Any comparison of these syllables with the chains of the Jeu material or of the Pistis Sophia runs into that before it runs into the transliterations. See prima-materia#607."
        ]
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, third utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the posited claim in its two parts: whether the event is located at the transition into or out of sleep, and whether the mechanism is the switching off of motor control there."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThresholdShutdownProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Lähmung eine Abschaltung ist und ob sie am Übergang geschieht",
        "en": "Whether the paralysis is a shutdown and whether it happens at the threshold"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "How far the mechanism evidence reaches. The transmitter and receptor pathway was identified in rats, by pharmacological inactivation of trigeminal motoneurons; the atonia of the stage is a defining feature of it across the species studied, but the receptor-level account is not a human finding. The direction of the correction does not depend on that account, only on the atonia belonging to the stage rather than to the transition, which the human review also reports.",
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The location holds. Reported episodes occur on falling asleep or on waking and not in the middle of consolidated sleep, and the systematic review classes them accordingly.",
          "The mechanism does not hold as stated, and the correction is not a quibble. Motor control is not switched off at the transition: it is already off throughout the sleep stage in question, and the experience arises when that shutdown persists past the transition instead of ending with it. The claim has the direction backwards. What happens at the threshold is the failure to switch motor control back on, not the switching of it off.",
          "The word regular carries a second and separate deviation. The mechanism is regular, but the recurring form of the experience is classified as a parasomnia in the standard nosology, which is to say as a disorder. Both can be true at once and the claim does not distinguish them: a normal mechanism appearing at an abnormal moment. Whoever repairs the claim must say which of the two the word regular is doing.",
          "This is why the two posits in this file were kept apart. The universality claim survives its protocol; this one does not, on the same evidence, and a single node carrying both would have had to be entered as confirmed or as deviating and would have been wrong either way."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not carried. The receptor account and the nosological classification were taken as settled without any search for dissent, and the receptor work is an animal study whose translation is itself contested in the field."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "American Academy of Sleep Medicine, International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (Darien, Illinois, 2014), recurrent isolated sleep paralysis among the parasomnias of the relevant sleep stage",
        "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the same two bodies of evidence that carry the neighbouring protocol, plus the standard nosological classification",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The atonia is shown to begin at the transition rather than to persist across it; or reported episodes are found in consolidated sleep away from either threshold."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A four-part examination of an inner movement, set out in the image of the money-changer who tests a coin: for the metal, for the stamp, for the lawfulness of its provenance, and for its weight."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TestingOfThoughts",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Prüfung der Gedanken",
        "en": "The examination of thoughts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Ignatius adds the rule that the whole course of a train of thought is to be examined, since a bad end reveals the origin, and that a consolation arriving after the fact is to be examined well before it is believed."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "John Cassian, transmitting Abba Moses; supplemented by Ignatius of Loyola",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A movement fails the test if its metal is false, its stamp is not that of right teaching, its provenance is unlawful, or it weighs light of the fear of God and heavy of human vanity; and it fails if the end of the train of thought is bad, however good its beginning.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-28",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 334",
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Testing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The last entry: further feasts, the personation of the bear spirit, the sacred drum and the chanted prayers."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFourthDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den vierten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the fourth degree"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The seven days of the lunation on which the orations are to be put in operation: the fourth, the eighth, the twelfth, the sixteenth, the twenty-fourth, the twenty-eighth and the thirtieth day of the moon."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Mondtage, an denen die Orationen gesprochen werden",
        "en": "The days of the moon on which the orations are said"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 15, p. 12, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Balinese palm-leaf transmission of healing, protective diagrams and the composition of the person, at the far edge of the Indian sphere of influence."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BalineseTantra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Balinesische Lontar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Balinese lontar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Written, catalogued and barely used by comparative work. The contact question is genuinely open: how much is Indian import and how much is local is undecided in the literature."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Usada healing lontar; Kanda Pat; the Aji Saraswati texts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Of the bedchamber arts the chapter says that the great essential lies in one thing, and that this method is passed by the true men from mouth to mouth and is fundamentally not written down; that whoever takes the famed medicines without knowing this essential does not attain long life either; and that the older authorities set down only the coarse matters and never put the essential on paper."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MouthToMouthOnly",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wesentliche geht von Mund zu Mund und wird nicht geschrieben",
        "en": "The essential is passed from mouth to mouth and not written"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it is a statement the graph can use about its own limits rather than about a capacity. A transmission that says its decisive step was never written is telling every later reader, including this project, that the written corpus is not the transmission. The claim is unfalsifiable and its being so is the point; it is recorded as what the source says, not as an obstacle discovered here."
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The node being placed: a practice, a capacity claim, a concept or a prerequisite claim."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Situating",
      "id": "pm:situatedNode",
      "label": {
        "de": "verorteter Knoten",
        "en": "situated node"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The term set on the wearing of the seventh prayer: it must be written down and worn by the woman who has children to suckle and bring up until the years of childbearing are ended."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianWearingUntilChildbearingEnds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Getragen, bis die Jahre des Gebärens enden",
        "en": "Worn until the years of childbearing are ended"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A stated term, which is rarer in this graph than a stated effect. It gives the claim a span rather than a moment, and it says when the wearing may stop without the protection being forfeited."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission is registered and its principal corpus is named. No passage has been read, no edition has been settled, and no node stands under it. A tradition in this state witnesses nothing and may not be counted as a party to a convergence."
      },
      "id": "pm:corpusNamed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Korpus benannt",
        "en": "Corpus named"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhakaLocusNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten gibt keinen Ort an",
        "en": "Retention alone states no locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:locusNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The verses were read for this question and are silent on it. They describe the breath and what the practitioner can do with it, and say nothing about where the awareness is held to be; the seated posture the chapter requires places the body and not the awareness."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transfer of the seat of awareness into a body other than one's own."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eintritt in einen anderen Körper",
        "en": "Entering another body"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account, given by a named Māori authority in a letter, that what comes back from a third party for a valuable passed on is the hau of the valuable first received, and that keeping it back for oneself would be fatal to the keeper."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauOfTheGift",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Hau der Gabe",
        "en": "The hau of the gift"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held at mediated attestation because the letter is an archival manuscript and was read in the study that prints and collates it, not in the archive. The published version of 1909 could not be opened in this run either; the paths tried are recorded in prima-materia#106. This node is therefore an open post and is to be raised to textual attestation when either the manuscript or the 1909 printing is read."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "Manuka Hēnare, \"Ko te hau tēnā o tō taonga…\": The words of Ranapiri on the spirit of gift exchange and economy, Journal of the Polynesian Society 127/4 (2018), pp. 455-457",
      "source": "Tamati Ranapiri, letter to Elsdon Best, 23 November 1907, Polynesian Society Papers, Alexander Turnbull Library, MS-Papers-1187, folder 271",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The initiation of the soldier grade as Tertullian describes it to shame Christian soldiers: in a cave, a crown is offered to the candidate on the point of a sword and set on his head, whereupon he is required to lift it off with his own hand and move it to his shoulder, saying that Mithras is his crown. From then on he is never crowned; and if he is ever put on trial about his religion, this is the mark by which he is known — he throws the crown away and says that he has his crown in his god."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MithraicCoronaRecusata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der auf dem Schwert dargebotene Kranz, mit einem Satz zurückgewiesen",
        "en": "The crown offered on a sword, refused with a saying"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The nearest thing to a passing formula that this graph holds outside the gnostic material, and it is the shape the Passierformeln project is looking for: an assertion of what the speaker has, not a request for anything. Nothing is asked of Mithras and nothing is granted by him. The sentence does not open a door; it identifies the speaker, and it does so under interrogation, where a wrong answer costs something.",
          "The same author elsewhere adds, from memory and saying so, that Mithras marks his soldiers on the forehead, celebrates an offering of bread, brings in an image of resurrection, opens the way to the crown with a sword, restricts his high priest to a single marriage, and keeps virgins and continents. Held here rather than as separate nodes because the author hedges the whole list.",
          "The witness is hostile and says why he is telling it: he wants Christians shamed by a comparison with a soldier of Mithras. That cuts both ways for reliability. He has no motive to invent a rite that makes his opponents look steadfast, and every motive to sharpen the detail that does. What he reports as an enduring mark — never crowned again — is the kind of claim a contemporary reader could have checked."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Tertullian, De corona 15, with De praescriptione haereticorum 40 (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The initiatory cult of the Roman empire, organised in graded degrees and practised in artificial caves, whose own transmission is almost entirely monumental and epigraphic rather than literary."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Mithraic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mithras-Mysterien",
        "en": "Mysteries of Mithras"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The lopsidedness of the corpus is the first thing to say about it. This tradition left several hundred sanctuaries, reliefs and inscriptions and no book of its own; every connected account of what it taught comes from someone outside it, and mostly from someone attacking it. That is the opposite of every other tradition in this graph's ascent material, where a text survives and the material remains do not, and it is the reason the tradition can matter here as a witness independent of the heresiological channel — once the material rather than the reports is opened.",
          "The name Mithras appears in this graph twice over, and the two occurrences are not the same thing. Here it is the cult. In traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl it is a modern title given in 1903 to PGM IV.475-829, and whether that piece is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Nothing in this file rests on that identification.",
          "What was read: Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22, in the Ante-Nicene Fathers translation with the Greek of the disputed words in its notes; and Porphyry, De antro nympharum, complete, in Thomas Taylor's translation (London 1917). Neither is a Mithraic text. The first is a report about Mithraists inside a Christian refutation; the second is a Platonist reading Homer, who reaches for the cult four times because it suits his argument about the descent of souls."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The monuments and inscriptions of the cult in their standard corpus; the literary testimonia, among them Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (reporting Celsus), Porphyry, De antro nympharum, Tertullian, De corona 15, Jerome, Epistula 107.2, and Firmicus Maternus, De errore profanarum religionum 5",
      "source": [
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)",
        "Porphyry, De antro nympharum (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ObjectiveConsciousnessPresupposesSelfConsciousness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der objektive Zustand setzt den dritten voraus",
        "en": "The objective state presupposes the third"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at stated necessity and not at prescribed order, because the source names what befalls the one who arrives without the prior step rather than merely setting a sequence. The consequence is a loss of the result and not a punishment, which is the form a prerequisite claim takes when it is about capacity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An ordinary person brought into the objective state artificially and returned will remember nothing of it and will believe he lost consciousness for a time; only from within the third state can flashes of the fourth be retained."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage on the only right way to objective consciousness (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SituatingEthiopianAsmatAnchoring",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sprechen der Namen wird im nach außen gewandten Wachen verortet",
        "en": "The saying of the names is placed in outward waking"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:outwardWaking"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The roll sets the saying at the front and at the doors of a house that is about to be entered, and the sixth section of the supplementary roll sets it at the moment a man raises a spear. Both are acts done with open eyes in the ordinary channel; nothing in either passage asks for withdrawal, sleep, vision or absorption, and no state is named as a condition.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The set of seven names the diagram gives the rulers of the gates: Ialdabaoth, Iao, Sabaoth, Adonaeus, Astaphaeus, Aloaeus and Horaeus. Origen adds that three of them, Ialdabaoth, Astaphaeus and Horaeus, are taken from magic, and four, Iao, Sabaoth, Adonaeus and Eloaeus, from the Hebrew scriptures, where they are names of one and the same God."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteHebdomasNominata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sieben Namen der Hebdomas und ihre Tore",
        "en": "The seven names of the hebdomad and their gates"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Aloaeus and Eloaeus are taken here as one bearer under two spellings of the printed text, since VI.31 addresses Aloaeus and VI.32 lists Eloaeus among the seven while omitting Aloaeus. The identification is the compiler's and is stated so that it can be denied.",
          "The gates are counted from below. Horaeus holds the first, Aloaeus the second, Astaphaeus the third, Sabaoth the fifth realm, and Ialdabaoth is addressed as first and seventh. The fourth and the sixth are never numbered in the passage."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The keeping of the great mask carved for each Sigui in a rock shelter beside those of the preceding ceremonies, and of the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each Sigui in the hogon's house. Both accumulate as series, and the article treats the series as the firmer evidence that a ceremony took place at all."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SiguiMaterialRecord",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bewahrung des Sigui in Holz und Faser",
        "en": "Keeping the Sigui in wood and fibre"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the one part of the account that leaves an object a later worker could count and date independently of any informant. The article reports nine masts at Ibi in 1931 with traces of three more, and eight or fourteen ferment-holders at Sanga and Arou. Held as a practice here and as a protocol at pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination in examinations/dogon-sirius.ttl."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 277-279",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A property of a person's hands, named in the chapter that assigns offices according to fitness: some have bitter nails and poisonous hands and in doing things are apt to injure, and such a person is to be set to pressing on accumulations and holding down numbness. The chapter opposes it to the sweet hand. It stands in a list beside the assignment of the well-jointed and supple-sinewed to the leading and pulling of the qi, and of the sharp-tongued and slighting to the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shoudu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die giftige Hand",
        "en": "The poisonous hand"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is that the difference is a property of the person and not of what he does, since the chapter assigns offices by it and says that where the person is not the right one the work does not succeed and his teacher goes without a name."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A route of transmission to traditions already held is documented. Agreement with them counts as reception."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg belegt",
        "en": "Contact route documented"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KasdejaTeachesStrikingOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kasdeja offenbart das Schlagen der Geister",
        "en": "Kasdeja discloses the striking of spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:StrikingOfSpirits"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kasdeja"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite by which a person or place under tapu is made common again, in the case witnessed by the eating of food cooked in an oven opened for the purpose and distributed to everyone present."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Whakanoa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Aufheben des Tapu",
        "en": "Making noa"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The rite the recorder describes as an eyewitness was already carried out with a Christian morning service inside it, and he remarks that the teacher had done wisely in keeping so much of the old ceremonial. The passage is the clearest single reason the contact route of this tradition is entered as disputed rather than absent."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 27",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus",
        "en": "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that.",
      "pm:dependentStep": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbasota"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
        }
      ],
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The uttering of efficacious words over a person at birth, said to determine that the person will be able to come to life again after dying."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte",
        "en": "The words spoken at a birth"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernhören hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields hearing at a distance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "One node per pairing, following traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl, because a protocol would have to take the pairings one at a time and because the four capacities of this degree are not equally examinable."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The chant the transmission names as an ancient prayer for the dedication of a high chief: a birth chant that establishes rank by reciting a descent that runs from the beginning of the world to the living. What it is held to accomplish is therefore a standing, not a cure or a protection."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kumulipo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kumulipo",
        "en": "Kumulipo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The prose note prefixed to the chant makes two datable claims beside the genealogical ones: that the chant was recited to Alapai-wahine at Koko on Oahu while Keeaumoku lay dying, and that it is the chant Puou recited to Cook at the heiau of Hikiau at Kealakekua. Those are historical assertions and not claims of efficacy, and they are the only part of the text that an outside record could meet or miss.",
          "The text this edition rests on is disputed, and the dispute is not entered as a node because neither side was read at the wording in this run. It stands at prima-materia#472."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 1, the prose note",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorder's enumeration of what must all be present: the shaman is a master of spirits; he has a group of mastered spirits; there is a complex of methods and paraphernalia recognised and transmitted; there is a theoretical justification of the practice; the shamans assume a special social position."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusFormalCharacters",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf Merkmale des vollen Schamanentums",
        "en": "The five characters of shamanism in full function"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "He states in the same passage that all of these but the first two are met in complexes that are not shamanism at all — in the Manchu clan priesthood and in the Lamaist priest — and that he therefore restricts the term to complexes covering all five. The enumeration is his and is entered as his; what is not his is each of the five conditions, which the preceding sections carry as reports."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95, p. 274",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that self and non-self are mutually superimposed, that this superimposition is what learned men call nescience, and that it is the presupposition of every practical distinction — those of ordinary life and those laid down in scripture alike — between means of knowledge, objects of knowledge and knowing persons, in texts of injunction and prohibition and in texts concerned with final release."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SuperimpositionUnderliesEveryPath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Überlagerung trägt jeden Weg, auch die Texte über die Befreiung",
        "en": "Superimposition underlies every path, including the texts on release"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "For the axes in prima-materia#52 this is neither a value nor a third position but a denial of the question's standing. It does not say that the bounded self is dissolved or kept; it says that the level at which either could be said is the level of nescience. Recorded so that a scheme which files it as a unitive telos can be checked against the argument. See prima-materia#66.",
          "The clause that decides the matter is the last one. Not only the ordinary distinctions but the texts on release itself are said to rest on the superimposition. A path, a choice between paths and the scripture that recommends one are therefore all inside what the argument calls nescience — not false, but valid only where the superimposition holds.",
          "The same section removes the one who would choose. Without the superimposition the self does not become a knowing agent at all, and where there is no knowing agent the means of knowledge do not operate. The agent that a choice between paths presupposes is thus itself among the things superimposed."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction, at the definition of superimposition as nescience and the statement that all practical distinctions including the texts on final release presuppose it (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AdvaitaVedanta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Daoist transmission of visualised journeys, of deities resident in the body, and of nocturnal encounters recorded as they were received."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shangqing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shangqing",
        "en": "Shangqing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Zhen'gao is a dated record of revelations with the recipient named, which places it among the few pre-modern first-person corpora available anywhere."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Tao Hongjing, Zhen'gao; Huangting neijing jing; Dadong zhenjing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A caution whose object is a speech act and not a deed. What the descender must not do at the sixth palace is not touch, not turn back and not doubt, but ask; the transmission attaches the whole penalty to the question about the nature of the water."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WaterQuestionCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Frage nicht nach dem Wasser",
        "en": "Do not ask about the water"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A rule against asking is the form a tradition takes when the thing to be hidden is that there is nothing there. This is not offered as a reading of the passage's intent, which the graph cannot establish, but as the shape the rule has: the prohibition falls exactly on the act that would reveal the absence."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:YeridatMerkavah"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Small stones of native brown sandstone, usually spherical, regular in outline and untouched by any tool, found on the tops of high buttes and not buried. To dream of one was held to be a sign of great import, meaning that the dreamer who fulfilled the requirements of the dream would become possessed of a power exercised through the stones. The word is said to be an abbreviation of the word for grandfather and belongs to the sacred speech."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Tunkan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tunkan, die heiligen Steine",
        "en": "Tunkan, the sacred stones"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The stones are kept wrapped, one of them in eagle down, of which it was said that there is something between the down and the stone, because when surrounded by down it cannot get away. A stone sold to another remains subject to recall by the man who dreamed it."
      },
      "source": "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 204-206 and pp. 208-211",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The manual layer of Theravāda: the objects of concentration, the marks by which each absorption is recognised, and the higher knowledges said to follow from mastery."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TheravadaCommentarial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theravādische Kommentarhandbücher",
        "en": "Theravāda commentarial manuals"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered apart from the canon because the canon states that the attainments occur and the manuals state how to reach them and how to tell whether one has."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga; Upatissa, Vimuttimagga; Paṭisambhidāmagga",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission holds the rubbing-board inferior to the other oracles because the man who works it can cheat with it, and only he may work it. The charge is raised in practice when the oracle has spoken against the questioner or when he dislikes the operator. Of the poison oracle, by contrast, the source records that a manipulation is never even suggested."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheRubbingBoard",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Reibbrett lässt sich vom Betreiber führen",
        "en": "The rubbing-board can be worked by the operator"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The warning names the failure mode of an oracle whose outcome one party controls, and names it from inside the transmission rather than from the recorder's side. It is the counterpart of the passage on the second test that is not made."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 173 and p. 153",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Yogasūtra of Patañjali and its commentarial literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pātañjala-Yoga",
        "en": "Pātañjala Yoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Only Yogasūtra III.38 carries the edition it was read from; it was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For every other citation in this file, and for the Yogabhāṣya, the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable: the session that entered them did not record it, and it cannot be reconstructed from the wording of the nodes. It is stated here rather than guessed, because an edition entered by inference would be a claim about the compiler's work that is not true. Until the passages are reopened, the verse numbers in this file stand on the received four-pāda division and nothing further."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two transmissions state the same claim in the same form. A named procedure, entered by stages, is said to produce two named capacities: perception of what is beyond the range of the senses, and knowledge of what is in another mind. In the one the procedure is the sustained application of attention to a single object, and the capacities follow verse by verse. In the other the procedure is an initiation into a graded society, and the capacities are read off the marks of the degree."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GradedAttainmentYieldsDistantPerceptionAndOtherMinds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine gestufte Erlangung soll Fernhören und Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervorbringen",
        "en": "A graded attainment is said to yield hearing at a distance and knowledge of other minds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The contact question, carefully. No route is proposed by anyone between the Anishinaabe of the upper Great Lakes and the Sanskrit commentarial literature, and the dispute recorded at pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute does not touch this: whether the society formed before European contact or in reaction to it, a seventeenth-century Great Lakes formation has no access to the third pāda either way. What the dispute does affect is the age of the Midewiwin claim, not its independence.",
          "The one real objection to independence lies in the recording and not in the traditions, and it is stated here rather than left for a reader to find. The Midewiwin account was written down between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine, by which time the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for a decade through theosophical publishing. Two things weigh against the objection. The recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts — marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The objection is not thereby closed; it is entered so that it can be closed or sustained by someone who reads the charts.",
          "Two differences that a correspondence must not smooth. The Indian text ranks its capacities as obstacles with respect to the goal of the practice; the American report names no such rank and warns instead against the member who turns them to harm. And the Indian claim is a claim about attention, repeatable in principle by anyone who applies it, where the American one is a claim about an institution, obtainable only from priests and only for a fee that the report says impoverishes the candidate. The capacity is the same; what it costs and what it is for are not.",
          "What makes this worth holding is not the capacities, which are common, but the form of the claim. Both traditions bind the capacity to a procedure and to a place in a sequence, rather than ascribing it to a figure or a birth. That form is what a protocol can address; an ascription to a hero is not."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "This is the case in the collection where the gathering situation decides, and it is entered in full because the date alone would clear a claim the date cannot clear. The American material was gathered between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine and printed in eighteen ninety-one; the capacities of the third pāda had been in English-language circulation for roughly a decade by then through theosophical publishing, so the fixation does not precede the material it agrees with. What carries the independence instead is twofold: the recorder was a bureau ethnographer and not a member of that milieu, and the capacities are given as readings of lines incised on birchbark charts, marks that were on the bark before anyone asked what they meant. The second point is what a date cannot supply, because it puts the agreement in an object rather than in an answer. The ground is not thereby closed; it can be sustained or overturned by someone who reads the charts.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:Midewiwin"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
        "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168 and 170"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The two recorders describe the same instrument and disagree about what is invoked over it. The compilation of sixteen seventy-four has the drum made from a tree whose grain turns with the course of the sun, because the tree is held acceptable to the sun, which they worship under the image of Thor; the whole art is treated as heathen and the answers as the devil's. The account of seventeen sixty-seven reports a named practitioner who, going to use his drum, marked himself and it with the sign of the cross as an earnest of good success, said the Lord's Prayer, invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel to assist him, and in the middle of the work broke out into a prayer to the God who created the heaven and the earth, calling himself a man liable to error, old and unworthy, and saying it were better that he should die than that God should deny help to the one on whose behalf they entreated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhoseTheDrumIs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Trommel der Sonne gehört oder Gott",
        "en": "Whether the drum belongs to the sun or to God"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Ninety years lie between them and the second recorder had read the first, so this is not two independent descriptions of one moment. What it is, is a transmission caught in the middle of taking something over: the instrument, the hammer, the ring and the rule of the sun's course are unchanged, and what is said over them has been replaced. The reading rule survived the theology that was given for it, which says something about which of the two the practice actually rested on.",
          "That the later station added something to what it received is exactly the shape pm:Reworking is for, and no reworking node is written: the addition here is Christian and the transmission it came from is not the one that performed the act. The class as it stands wants one tradition taking over from another, and this is a tradition taking over from the mission that was suppressing it. The gap is recorded rather than forced.",
          "The second recorder notices one detail against himself and reports it: that in calling the Holy Ghost an angel the man used a word he had never heard the Lapps use for it anywhere he knew, where they say Passe Vuoign. He records the anomaly instead of correcting it, which is why the passage can be used at all."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 47-48",
        "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, pp. 476-477"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Awareness of objects generated inwardly, with the sensory channel detached. Whether the dreamer knows he dreams is a separate question and not this axis."
      },
      "id": "pm:dreamingWithin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Im Traum",
        "en": "Dreaming within"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Māṇḍūkya calls this svapna. Lucidity is deliberately not a value here: it varies within the state rather than replacing it, and a source that trains recognition inside the dream is placed here and says so in its own words."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The forms of makutu employed to turn back the working of another tohunga, that is, a counter-operation of the same kind rather than a protection of a different kind."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Whakahokitu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Whakahokitu",
        "en": "Whakahokitu"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 35",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest organised Daoist transmission, in which illness is treated as the consequence of fault and healing proceeds by confession and petition."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CelestialMasters",
      "label": {
        "de": "Himmelsmeister",
        "en": "Celestial Masters"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Xiang'er commentary on the Laozi; Zhengyi fawen; the Taiping jing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A living gnostic transmission with its own scripture and priesthood, teaching an ascent of the soul past stations of examination."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Mandaean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mandäische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Mandaean transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ginzā Rabbā; Drāšā d-Yahia; Qolastā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The senior specialist gives notice when the time comes, the chicha is brewed, and he goes through the households collecting the offerings. On the appointed day he goes with his assistants to the principal huaca and makes the offering, then to the lesser huacas and the ancestor bodies. On his return nobody sleeps that night: they sing, dance and tell tales, and children are beaten if they fall asleep. This night is called the pacaricuc, and from it the fast begins — no salt, no chilli, no sleeping with wives — lasting five days in some places and longer in others according to different traditions. Three such feasts are held in the year, tied to the Pleiades, to the first rains and to the maize harvest."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HuacaFeast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fest der Huaca",
        "en": "The feast of the huaca"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The African American transmission of workings for protection, luck, love and revenge, carried by practitioners without a priesthood or a central corpus."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hoodoo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hoodoo",
        "en": "Hoodoo"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Hyatt collection is thousands of practitioner statements taken down verbatim with the speakers identified only by place. As a body of first-person practice testimony it has no equal in the project's reach."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hyatt, Hoodoo, Conjuration, Witchcraft, Rootwork, five volumes",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HarbaDeMosheUsePresupposesPreparation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Gebrauch des Schwertes setzt die dreitägige Vorbereitung voraus",
        "en": "Using the Sword presupposes the three days' preparation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prior step named here is the secrecy and purity condition rather than one of the counts, because the handbook binds them together in a single sentence and attaches the penalty to the whole. Which of the counts, taken alone, the penalty would follow is not stated and is not inferred."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "If he will not act accordingly the angels of wrath, ire, fury and rage come near him to minister to him, and they lord over him, and strangle him, and plague him all over. The consequence is stated for the whole of the preparation and not for a single part of it.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HarbaDeMoshe"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HarbaDeMosheSecrecy"
      },
      "source": "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A condition of a different kind from the counts. No one is to know that the user intends to use the Sword, and it is not to be communicated except to the chaste and the pure, because in it are the mysteries of the universe and they are practised only in secret."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HarbaDeMosheSecrecy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verschwiegenheit, die das Schwert verlangt",
        "en": "The secrecy the Sword requires"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The secrecy clause is the gate this project exists to remove, stated by the transmission in its own words, and it is worth holding as a node rather than as background: it names the criterion of admission, and the criterion is moral rather than lineal."
      },
      "source": "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account by which the society traces itself: the culture hero instructed the otter in its mysteries, gave him the sacred rattle and the drum, shot the shell into his body, and conferred the secrets on him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MidewiwinOrigin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Ursprung der Gesellschaft und die Kette ihrer Übertragung",
        "en": "The origin of the society and the chain of its transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The chain runs through an animal and not through a lineage of men, which is why the society's own account of its age cannot be read as a genealogy and does not bear on the dispute recorded above."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 166-167",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that Christ descended through the seven heavens, having assumed the likeness of their sons, and emptied them of their power by degrees, so that the whole besprinkling of light rushed to him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteDescensusPerSeptem",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg durch die sieben Himmel in der Gestalt ihrer Söhne",
        "en": "The descent through the seven heavens in the likeness of their sons"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The counter-movement to the ascent of the speeches, and the only passage in either witness that says what passing through a sphere does to it. It works by likeness: the descending one takes the form of what holds each heaven, and that is the same logic the speeches use when the speaker names what he already bears. Whether the two are one doctrine seen from both ends is not asserted here."
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.12 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The commoner form outside the oracle proper: short sticks a few inches long, pieces of knotted cord, or a handful of cowries or nuts are thrown on the ground and the answer is drawn from the position in which they fall. A less common mode is named beside it, in which a fowl or an animal is impaled and the answer drawn from how long it takes to die."
      },
      "id": "pmp:DivinationByThrownThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagen durch geworfene Dinge",
        "en": "Divination by things thrown on the ground"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The second mode is the reason for the node. An answer read off the time a creature takes to die is fixed to an outcome the operator does not steer, like the poison ordeal and the casting, but it is measured in duration rather than in a binary sign, and this graph holds no other case of that."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Ewe informants of the western and eastern tribes, unnamed; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, from his own residence and from earlier printed reports he names as he uses them, published 1890; given in Ewe, of which the work prints the terms with etymologies, the accounts in English.",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KhecariYieldsRetention",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die über den Gaumen gelegte Zunge bringt das Zurückhalten des Nektars hervor",
        "en": "The tongue set above the palate yields the retention of the nectar"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The contact at the palate is attested, and what it is said to do is stop a descent. It is not bound to a phase of breathing anywhere in the chapter, and it is not said to carry anything upward. The same holds for the seal at the throat, which is described as closing off a downward flow. A scheme that pairs the palate with inhalation and with upward carriage takes a stopping and reports it as a conveyance. See prima-materia#28."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Khecari"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.42 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor",
        "en": "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Piti"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that exposure to cold is a source from which the practitioner draws, rather than a condition he works against."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold",
      "label": {
        "de": "Energie lässt sich aus Kälte aufnehmen",
        "en": "Energy can be taken in from cold"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The narrowest of the draft's uptake claims and the only one that names a measurable boundary condition, which is why it is entered first."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#133",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A scribed corpus in three parts, teaching that the perceived world is a projection of guilt, and carrying a workbook of three hundred and sixty-five daily exercises in fixed order."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CourseInMiracles",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Kurs in Wundern",
        "en": "A Course in Miracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The workbook is a stated curriculum with an order and a duration, which is rare in this block and puts the corpus closer to the manual traditions than to the revealed cosmologies."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "A Course in Miracles: Text, Workbook for Students, Manual for Teachers",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol the transmission states for itself, complete in every part. The doorkeepers of the sixth palace place upon the descender a thousand thousands of waves of water, and there is not in that place even a single drop. The test is what he does next: the fault is not a wrong answer but the question. If he says what is the nature of this water, they run after him and stone him, saying that he is of the seed of them that kissed the calf and is unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice goes out from the seventh heaven confirming the sentence; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SixthPalaceWaterTest",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wasser, das keines ist, am Tor des sechsten Palastes",
        "en": "The water that is not there, at the gate of the sixth palace"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Silence is the pass and the question is the failure, which inverts the shape every other examination in this graph has. It also makes the test unusually cheap to administer and impossible to prepare for by learning an answer, since there is none.",
          "What the protocol cannot survive is the same thing that undid the Maya examination in traditions/maya.ttl: the text that transmits the test also transmits the correct behaviour. From the moment this chapter is written down, the trial separates those who have read it from those who have not."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case in which a descender held to be fit asked about the water, or in which one held unfit kept silent and passed. The transmission claims the gate decides fitness, so a single clean case on either side falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:HekhalotFitness"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the moon, given in place of its course in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen des Mondes",
        "en": "Signs of the moon"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "20th century",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of the eight limbs as a ladder that must be climbed in order, each limb a precondition of the next."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SerialReadingOfTheEightLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die serielle Lesart der acht Glieder",
        "en": "The serial reading of the eight limbs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The inner and outer categories come from the commentarial layer, and the hardening of the ranking into a rule of practice belongs to twentieth-century yoga pedagogy. Recorded here so that the reading is available as what it is. The base text at II.29 enumerates and does not order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "The commentarial tradition, sharpened in twentieth-century yoga pedagogy",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The eighth chapter sets itself against the books this file also rests on. Of the five thousand words it says that they are general and cursory throughout and nowhere set out a matter from head to tail so that it could be taken up and followed, and that to recite the classic by heart without the essential way is labour thrown away. Of Wenzi, Zhuangzi and Yin Xi it says that their writing derives from the Yellow Lord and Laozi and follows their broad intent but never states the decisive word, and that where they level life and death, call them no different, treat being alive as a corvée and dying as a rest, they are already a hundred thousand million li away from the transcendents. Their parables it allows for small uses only."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BaopuziAgainstTheZhuangzi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Laozi und Zhuangzi den Weg zu den Unsterblichen tragen",
        "en": "Whether the Laozi and the Zhuangzi carry the way to the transcendents"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is a dispute inside one tradition and inside one file, and it is worth a node because the graph would otherwise carry the Zhuangzi passage and the Baopuzi passage side by side as though they belonged together. The most systematic practitioner in the corpus says they do not. Whoever builds a convergence on Daoist material has to say which of the two sides he is counting."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
        "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second sign, stated in the chapter that explains the nine needles: where emptiness is to be filled, it is warm under the needle, for the qi becomes full and then it is warm; where fullness is to be drained, it is cold under the needle, for the qi becomes empty and then it is cold. The same chapter makes the warmth the criterion for withdrawal, saying that in needling an emptiness one waits for the fullness, and that when the yang qi has arrived in force and it is warm under the needle, then the needle is taken out."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZhenxiaReHan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wärme und Kälte unter der Nadel",
        "en": "Heat and cold under the needle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held apart from the vessel criterion above, and the difference is the point. This one is felt by the operator at the needle and is not stated to be checkable by anybody else; the other is a rule over a finding that at least two people can take. A tradition that carries both is a tradition that has not settled which of them decides, and the file does not settle it either."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 54 (Zhen jie pian), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaIsInnerToThePrecedingLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei inneren Glieder sind innerlicher als die vorhergehenden fünf",
        "en": "The three inner limbs are inner to the preceding five"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The base text ranks the last three limbs as inner with respect to the preceding five, and then ranks even those three as outer with respect to the seedless absorption. It sets a relation of inner and outer, not a rule that one cannot be attempted before the other, and it says nothing about what follows from disregarding the ranking."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.7",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.8"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourthJhana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vierte Vertiefung",
        "en": "The fourth absorption"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The cleansing by which a member defiled through contact with a corpse is restored to the company: the teacher offers prayer, sprinkles the offender with sea water and turmeric, and orders a bath in the sea, after which the person is clean. A wilful transgression is not covered by it and is restored only after penitence and confession."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Huikala",
      "label": {
        "de": "Huikala",
        "en": "Huikala"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The distinction between accidental defilement and wilful breach is drawn by the transmission itself and drawn at the point where it matters: the rite removes the first and does not touch the second. A restoration procedure that admits it cannot cover every case is rarer in this collection than one that claims to cover all."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, p. 15",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The needling chapter of the Lingshu states the same prohibitions as a two-sided rule and adds a state the other list does not have. Newly after intercourse do not needle, and newly needled do not lie together; already drunk do not needle, and already needled do not get drunk; newly angered, newly exhausted, already filled, already hungry, already thirsty, each in the same double form. On great fright and great fear the rule is not a prohibition at all but a condition: settle the qi first, then needle. Two further clauses set waiting times by the manner of arrival, the time of a meal for one who came by cart and the time of walking ten li for one who came on foot. The chapter then names what breaking the twelve does: the vessels are disordered and the qi scattered, a yang illness enters the yin and a yin illness comes out as yang, the evil qi is generated afresh, and the text calls it hewing at the body, wasting the brain marrow and losing the qi."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ShierjinZhongshi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zwölf Verbote und was ihr Bruch bewirken soll",
        "en": "The twelve prohibitions, and what is said to follow from breaking them"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The relation to the Suwen list is not similarity but disagreement, and the Song collation note printed with the Suwen chapter says so in as many words: it states that the seven clauses there and the Lingshu clauses go in and out of one another, and it prints the Lingshu wording beside them so that a reader can see it. The two differ on fright, which one forbids and the other admits after a delay, and on intercourse, which only the Lingshu has.",
          "This is also the passage in this corpus that comes closest to a stated failure condition for the operator rather than the patient. It says what the transgression produces, in the body, and names the direction of the change."
        ]
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Cizhen"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sequence of announced sights, each given before the stage at which it is to occur: the disposition of the gods, some ascending and some descending; the pipe depending from the disk from which the wind comes; the gods bearing down and then desisting; a thunder-clap that startles; the disk expanding; a host of five-pointed stars filling the air; an infinite encircling with doors of fire fast closed; the doors thrown open; a god with locks of flame in a white tunic and scarlet mantle; seven virgins with serpent faces and golden sceptres; seven gods with the faces of black bulls; and a descending god holding the golden shoulder of the calf."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvSignaSuccessus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die geordnete Liste dessen, was zu sehen sein wird",
        "en": "The ordered list of what will be seen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as one node and kept apart from anything that could be tested, for the reason SPEC §15 gives: these are signs of success, they are told to the operator in advance, and there is no base rate for a sight nobody measures without the telling. A corpus this rich in announced sights and this empty of stated failures is itself the finding.",
          "The second half of that finding is withdrawn. This node said until 2026-09-09 that the rite carries no condition of failure at all, and that was a statement about the translation that had been read, not about the text: Mead breaks off with the liturgy, and the forfeiture clause stands in the magical appendix that follows it in the papyrus. What holds is the imbalance, and it is large — a dozen announced sights against one clause. What does not hold is the zero. See pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno and prima-materia#643."
        ]
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, first through eighth instructions and utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Those who have reached the third degree are said to see distant things even against their will. A first-person report is given: a man who surrendered his drum to the recorder on complaint confessed with tears that though he parted with it and made no other, he would have the same visions as before; he instanced the recorder himself, giving a true and particular account of what had happened to him on his journey to Lapland; and he complained that he did not know how to make use of his eyes, since things altogether distant were presented to them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheSightItself",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen kommt ungerufen und lässt sich nicht ablegen",
        "en": "The sight comes unbidden and cannot be laid down"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The same passage carries a claim of a verified hit — the man is said to have told the recorder correctly what had happened on his own journey — and the recorder reports it without endorsing it. It is a claim standing in the work and no more; nothing about the account can be checked, and the man is not named.",
          "The warning is not against misuse and not against the anger of a spirit. It is that the capacity, once complete, is not under the will and cannot be given up with the instrument. That is a caution of a kind this graph holds very few of, and it is the transmission warning against the thing it exists to produce."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the groups themselves state about the beginning of their shamanism. The Manchus refer it to a definite person and a definite year, 1033, and name him: a soldiers' commander who left his spirits to spread over three provinces, and who is himself now one of those spirits. The Reindeer Tungus of Manchuria state that they received shamanism, before their move to Manchuria in the first half of the nineteenth century, from subjects of the Emperor of China. The Birarcen name a Dahur or a Goldi as their first shaman, or say they had no shamans at all until the seven mastered spirits came from the Mongols. On the decisive point the recorder reports the groups as unanimous: the art of mastering spirits did not originally exist among them but was practised by their neighbours, and especially by lamas."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusOriginTestimony",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Überlieferung sagt, die Kunst sei nicht ihre eigene gewesen",
        "en": "The transmission says the art was not originally its own"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "He also states why such testimony is usually missing from the record, and the remark is a finding about the discipline rather than about the Tungus: answers of this kind look to an investigator accustomed to the primeval character of shamanism like an error of the informer, and would most probably not even be recorded. That is his and is entered as his.",
          "This is testimony about itself and not a scholar's reconstruction, and it is the reason the node stands here rather than at an examination. The recorder separates it explicitly from folklore, which he holds unreliable for the question, and states that the groups themselves consider these statements historical facts.",
          "What he holds the older complex to have contained is reported with the same care: only the spirit of heaven, buga, which he says shows no direct sign of alien influence and is recognised by all Tungus as their own, and the managing of the souls of the dead. The Birarcen are quoted saying that before, the Tungus had only buga and buni. He then argues against his own inference, that other complexes may have been lost and forgotten, and calls the scepticism perhaps too great."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 98, pp. 276-277, and sect. 100, pp. 282-283",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A man who has passed the ordeal is led back to the town with songs of praise and dressed in fine cloth, and the corpus states that he may then mulct his accuser in a very heavy fine; in the milder form used for theft and adultery, the accused who retains the dose may demand compensation from his accusers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AccuserPaysOnAnAcquittal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Freigesprochene darf den Ankläger büßen",
        "en": "The acquitted may fine his accuser"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A cost on the accuser is the one feature of the procedure that works against its abuse, and it is worth recording that the transmission has it. The second witness records the cost running the other way at the same point, since in some districts the ordeal-giver loses his fee when the accused vomits, so the two incentives are opposed within one procedure."
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 263 and p. 264",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NkisiYieldsItsOffice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das geladene Ding verrichtet sein Amt",
        "en": "The charged thing performs the office it was made for"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the practice named is the composing and charging of an object by a named practitioner, and the capacity is an effect in the world outside it, on a thief, a gun, a disease or a birth. The transmission also states what is to be concluded when the effect fails to appear, and that stands on the neighbouring node."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Kimenga"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only.",
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Nkisi"
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 235",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Four grades, each with a stated reach and each ascribed to a conduct rather than to a birth. The true persons of high antiquity lifted and held heaven and earth, grasped the yin and the yang, breathed the essence-qi, stood alone and guarded the spirit, and their flesh was as one, so that their years wore out heaven and earth and had no ending time. Next the perfected persons, whose virtue was whole, who were in accord with yin and yang and in tune with the four seasons, who left the age and departed from custom, who accumulated the essence and made the spirit whole, who roamed between heaven and earth and whose seeing and hearing went beyond the eight reaches. Next the sages, who dwelt in the harmony of heaven and earth and followed the pattern of the eight winds, whose bodily form was not worn out and whose essence and spirit were not scattered, and who likewise could be counted in hundreds. Last the worthies, who took heaven and earth for their rule and modelled themselves on sun and moon, and who could lengthen their years but had a limit."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZhenrenZhirenShengrenXianren",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vier gestuften Personen des ersten Kapitels",
        "en": "The four graded persons of the first chapter"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Nothing in the chapter says that the two upper grades were observed, and nothing says how one would tell a perfected person from a worthy. The grades are entered as what the corpus claims and not as a scale the graph can place anyone on.",
          "The two upper grades carry the claims that make this a capacity list rather than a moral one: a lifespan coextensive with heaven and earth and without end, and a seeing and hearing that reaches beyond the eight quarters. The two lower grades carry no such claim and are bounded in the text itself, the third at a count in hundreds and the fourth explicitly at a limit."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrnehmung des Unerreichbaren aus einem willentlich betretenen Zustand",
        "en": "Perception of what is out of range, from a state entered at will"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No pm:Testing points at this node, and that is its function rather than a defect. SPEC §16 makes the untested generalizations the queue for the examining layer; this one heads it, because it rests on more attested claims and more widely separated transmissions than anything else the holding can currently form.",
          "Seven attested claims from five transmissions: the Sámi journey made while the body lies still, two applications of saṃyama in the Yogasūtra, two of the graded degrees of the Midewiwin, the Iglulik spirit calling, and the Lakota sending of the sacred stones. Each names its own work and passage; this node names none, because none of them says what it says.",
          "The derivation is contestable at two points and both are left visible. First, whether the four transmissions describe one kind of practice at all: the Sámi journey, saṃyama, the Midewiwin degrees and the Iglulik calling agree in that the state is entered deliberately and differ in almost everything else. Second, whether reporting on a distant place and reading another mind are one effect or two — the sources treat them as neighbouring and this node treats them as one, which is a decision and not a finding.",
          "The seventh claim was added after the sixth and its addition is the only one that changed the shape of the derivation, so it is recorded rather than absorbed. The Lakota case is the first in this set that comes with narrated cases attached: a stone sent to find a rifle dropped into a river at a named place, and a stone sent to bring a buffalo in front of a fur trader who had staked ten articles against it. Cases do not make evidence — nobody devised the procedure and nobody recorded an outcome that could have been checked by a third party — but they are what a protocol would have to address, and no other claim under this node has any. Against that stands the same objection as for the Midewiwin material, since the recording is of nineteen eleven to nineteen fourteen; and beside it the fact that the same transmission names the cause to which a failure of the instrument is attributed, so that its own successes cannot be counted as a run.",
          "Why the wording is as heavy as it is. The traditions say that the capacity is reached; a statement that repeated that could not fail at anything. What can fail is a comparison, so the statement is written as one, with the comparison group named — people working from the same prior information without the state. Whoever thinks the sentence overreaches should attack that clause, because it is the clause that carries the whole claim."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:generalizedFrom": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:TunkanYieldsDistantKnowledge"
        }
      ],
      "pm:generalizedStatement": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Where a practitioner enters a stated inner state at will and then reports on a person, place or object outside the range of ordinary perception, the reports agree with independently established facts more often than reports produced, from the same prior information, by people who have not entered such a state."
      },
      "type": "pm:Generalizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaPresupposesLunationes",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirkung setzt den bestimmten Tag voraus",
        "en": "The effect presupposes the appointed day"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The text says the orations are to be pronounced at certain determinate times of the moon and are not to be undertaken without consideration of the end; it does not say what follows if the day is missed. The strength is set to what the passage carries and no higher."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that there is no red, green or yellow magic: there is mechanics, which is what happens, and there is doing, which is magic, and doing can be of one kind only. What looks like a second kind is an imitation of the outward appearance of doing and yields no objective result."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NoTwoKindsOfDoing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Es gibt nicht zwei Arten des Tuns",
        "en": "There are not two kinds of doing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It stands beside pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal, where the same source varies only the means and holds the goal fixed. Both point the same way: this transmission classifies by completeness and by genuineness of operation, and it declines to classify by direction of the will. See prima-materia#65.",
          "Recorded because it is this tradition's answer to the question the right-hand and left-hand path terms are built to ask, and the answer is that the question has no second term. The refusal is directed at a division of kinds of operation, not at a norm about means and not at an end state, so it is held as a claim in its own right rather than as a value on pm:MethodOrientation or pm:TelicOrientation."
        ]
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eleven, at the answer on black magic and the two kinds of doing (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that to the mindless, the wicked and depraved, the envious and covetous, Mind is far off and yields its place to the avenging daimon, who sharpens the fire and torments him, rushes upon him through his senses, renders him readier for transgressions so that he meets with greater torment, and leaves him never ceasing to desire inordinate appetites, insatiably striving in the dark."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticDaemonUltor",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der rächende Daimon tritt an die Stelle des Mind",
        "en": "The avenging daimon takes the place of Mind"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also inverts the usual reading of such warnings. The daimon does not punish the transgression; he makes the transgression more likely so that the punishment increases. The mechanism is a spiral and not a sentence, and a summary that calls it hell loses that.",
          "The counterpart of the ascent and the nearest this treatise comes to a stated outcome of failure. It is worth keeping apart from the seven zones: the tormented one is not turned back at a gate, he is never described as arriving at one."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:HermeticVitaPia"
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.23 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The breath practice of the second chapter, directed at the clearing of the central channel."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Atemregulierung im Haṭhayoga",
        "en": "Breath regulation in Haṭhayoga"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Böhme's transmission of a divine self-disclosure through opposition, reported as having been seen rather than reasoned."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChristianTheosophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Christliche Theosophie",
        "en": "Christian theosophy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Böhme, Aurora; Böhme, De signatura rerum",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The doctrine holds that a man may carry the substance and never use it: it may remain inoperative, cool, throughout his life, and a man whose witchcraft never functions is hardly counted a witch. In practice the question asked of the oracles is never whether a man is a witch but whether at this moment he is bewitching the questioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguMayRemainInoperative",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Substanz kann kühl bleiben",
        "en": "The substance may remain cool"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The clause is what keeps the post-mortem from telling against the inheritance rule, and it belongs in the record beside that rule for exactly that reason. A finding of the substance where no injury was ever ascribed costs the doctrine nothing, and the absence of the substance is the only outcome that decides anything."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about where the higher perceptual capacities are exercised. The handbooks of absorption place them in a waking, fully concentrated state and say so with a stated necessity; the ascetic literature marks the light half-sleep as the place of counterfeit consolation; the theurgic account, by contrast, treats the edge of sleep as the mark of a genuine divine communication."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThresholdIsNotTheExecutionState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Schwellenzustand die Vermögen trägt",
        "en": "Whether the threshold state carries the faculties"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Two of the three sides name the edition or translation they were read in. For Diadochus the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; the passage is to be reopened, and until then the chapter number stands on whichever numbering the earlier session used.",
          "Recorded as a dispute between sources and not as a verdict on the modern draft. A draft is not a side: it carries no attestation, so a claim it makes has no standing here. What the sources actually disagree about is narrower and more interesting than the draft's question, namely whether the threshold is evidential at all, and both sides of that are attested."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission descending from Rāmānuja, which gives the devotional relation a scholastic form and disputes within itself over whether the devotee acts or is acted upon."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Srivaisnava",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śrīvaiṣṇavische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Śrīvaiṣṇava transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The internal dispute of the two schools, over whether surrender requires an act of the devotee or none, is a candidate for a pm:Disputing node with two attested sides inside one transmission."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rāmānuja, Śrībhāṣya and Gadyatraya; Vedānta Deśika, Rahasyatrayasāra; Piḷḷai Lokācārya, Śrīvacanabhūṣaṇa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The question has not been carried out. Registered explicitly rather than left absent, because an unstated contact route and a route shown to be absent are the difference between a witness and a candidate."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg unentschieden",
        "en": "Contact route undecided"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act, within a tradition, of warning against a capacity, a practice, or the pursuit of either."
      },
      "id": "pm:Cautioning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Warnen",
        "en": "Cautioning"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node rather than as commentary, because what a tradition warns against is part of what it teaches. A record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports the tradition it claims to hold."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Mangu, described as an oval blackish swelling attached to the edge of the liver and lying beneath the xiphoid cartilage, in which small objects are sometimes said to be found. A witch performs no rite, utters no spell and possesses no medicines: the act is held to proceed from the substance itself. The location is known because the substance was formerly extracted by autopsy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ManguSubstance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hexerei als Substanz im Leib",
        "en": "Witchcraft as a substance in the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder adds that he believes the organ in question to be the small intestine in certain digestive periods. That is his identification and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here as his."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 1-2",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A parallel counting on a different modulus. Thirty-six are looked to for the stalks; heaven divides by three and ends at six completions, hence eighteen, and since heaven does not give and earth does not complete, the number is doubled and earth leaves three empty against heaven's eighteen. One is separated and hung on the little finger of the left hand, the rest is divided in the middle and searched through by threes, and the remainder is set beside the notch. After one notch the remainder is counted: seven counts as one, eight as two, nine as three, and at six reckonings the way of the stalks is exhausted. The four positions so obtained give one of eighty-one heads, and each head carries nine appraisals ranked as thought, fortune and misfortune, each in a lower, middle and upper degree."
      },
      "id": "pmp:XuanShu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Abzählen des Höchsten Geheimnisses",
        "en": "The counting of the Supreme Mystery"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The interest is the contrast with the Changes, and it is exact: the same act of reading a figure off an ungoverned remainder, on a modulus of three instead of four, over four positions instead of six, into eighty-one figures instead of sixty-four. Two traditions in one language, three centuries apart, differing in the arithmetic and agreeing in the form. That is the strongest internal evidence this file holds that the form is not the content."
      },
      "source": "Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh, with the commentary of Fan Wang, juan 8, Sibu congkan facsimile",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The seeker is to wait until he receives the communication or until he is so exhausted that he cannot wait longer without danger of losing his life. One who receives it returns to his tipi singing a song of victory; one to whom it is not granted is to come away from the quest meekly and as privately as possible."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ProvisionForTheQuestThatYieldsNothing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was geschieht, wenn keine Mitteilung gewährt wird",
        "en": "What is done when no communication is granted"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also fixes the abort condition on the practitioner and not on the clock: the quest ends at exhaustion, not after a set number of days. The corpus read here gives no number of days for this quest, which is the point at which the popular reports of it differ most sharply from it.",
          "The passage is the reason this transmission was opened before others in the registry. A procedure that names an exit for its own non-occurrence, and prescribes a different bearing for it, is stating that the non-occurrence happens and is expected. Nothing else read in this run says as plainly that the promised thing may fail to come."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative Jewish transmission of adjurations, amulets and bowls, distinct from the speculative literature and older than it in its written witnesses."
      },
      "id": "pmt:JewishPracticalMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jüdische Praxismagie",
        "en": "Jewish practical magic"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sefer ha-Razim; Ḥarba de-Moshe; the Cairo Genizah magical fragments; the Aramaic incantation bowls",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a named person always knows in advance when anything remarkable is going to happen. The claim is examinable because it is universal and because the tradition itself counts the announcement, not the interpretation, as the thing that happens."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitSecondSightExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Vorherwissens-Behauptung ihre eigene Allgemeinform übersteht",
        "en": "Whether the foreknowledge claim survives its own universal form"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One confirming case is related in the same passage and no failing case is. The case does not count against the condition and does not count for it either: it was told after the event it is said to have foretold, by the wife of the man it is told about, and a dream of a friend eaten by his kin is a description that the encounter afterwards fitted rather than a prior announcement of that encounter.",
          "The remainder of the volume was not read in the run that opened this protocol, and it is precisely there that a failing case would be found if one is recorded at all. The state is therefore procedure-without-cases and not cases-without-deviation: the search has begun and has not been carried."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/inuit-angakkuq.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One recorded remarkable event in the bearer's presence that he did not announce beforehand. The claim says always, so a single unannounced case refutes it as stated. A weaker form, that such announcements occur more often than chance would give, would require announcements collected before the events they name, with the failures kept alongside the hits.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-31",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the goddess as sound and diagram, with an initiatory sequence tied to the levels of a single mantra."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Srividya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śrīvidyā",
        "en": "Śrīvidyā"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Yoginīhṛdaya; Nityāṣoḍaśikārṇava; Bhāskararāya, Setubandha",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A warning that comes from the practitioners and not from the priesthood. The witness reports that the craftsmen always excused themselves from the work, because they believed that either they or someone of their household would die, or would suffer heart attacks or strokes."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MayaIdolCarvingCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schnitzer entzogen sich dem Auftrag",
        "en": "The carvers excused themselves from the commission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the rarer kind of caution: not a rule laid on the practice from above but a refusal by the people who would have to carry it out, with the harm they expected named. The named harms are sudden, bodily and unrelated to the work, which is what makes the belief hard to disappoint."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:MayaIdolCarving"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, pp. 76-77",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Declares by which route a claim entered the record. Mandatory on every attribution, so that non-evidence cannot be forgotten."
      },
      "id": "pm:attestedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "bezeugt durch",
        "en": "attested by"
      },
      "range": "pm:Attesting",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account that the eighth supreme chief, having discovered the star, escaped the sacrifice his seven predecessors had undergone by feigning death, returned claiming to have been inside the star and to know its secrets, and then taught men its characteristics and reordered the reckoning of time."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EighthHogonTeaching",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der achte Häuptling lehrte die Menschen die Merkmale des Sterns",
        "en": "The eighth chief taught men the characteristics of the star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "An ascription inside a myth of origin, and the article gives it as such: it is offered expressly as an indication of the changes and combinations of reckoning that appear in the course of what the recorders put in quotation marks as the history of the Blacks."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "the eighth supreme chief, in the Dogon account of the origin of the sixty-year reckoning",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-284",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition that the oration be spoken secretly, that the speaker be alone, and that he pronounce it with so low a voice that he scarcely hears himself; with fasting on the first day of the rehearsal and a chaste and devout life."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaSolitudo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Allein, und kaum sich selbst hörbar",
        "en": "Alone, and scarcely audible to himself"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also not the same requirement as the one it most resembles in this graph. The European folk-magic condition recorded at pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen forbids the act to be called at and spoken of, that is it regulates what other people say about it; this one regulates how loudly the operator says the words himself. One shields the act from other voices, the other shields the voice from other ears, and a correspondence between them would be built out of the single word silence. Held apart here rather than joined, which settles the second question of prima-materia#530 in the negative; whether either has a counterpart outside the European sphere is unsearched.",
          "The condition is on the manner of speaking and not on the words. It is the counterpart of the exactness demands the graph holds elsewhere: there the wording carries the effect, here it is the inaudibility that is prescribed, and the two are not the same requirement."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 14, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The setting up and consecration of the hula altar. The plants that may decorate it are a fixed short list held acceptable to the goddess, among them maile, ieie, lehua, ohia, ferns, hau, breadfruit, the native banana, ti and ilima; the recorder marks non-native flowers as repugnant to the tradition. During the consecration the voice of prayer must continue to be heard until the work is entirely accomplished, and the teacher may repeat a prayer or begin another to keep it so."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Kuahu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Weihe des Kuahu",
        "en": "The consecration of the kuahu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The condition here is on continuity and not on wording: what must not happen is a silence, and the teacher is expressly free to change the prayer in order to avoid one. That is the opposite of the exactness rule this same tradition sets for the chanted genealogy, and the two stand side by side in one transmission. The graph should not read either as the tradition's general doctrine about speech."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, pp. 19-21",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PerformancePresupposesTheMirror",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorstellung setzt den Spiegel voraus",
        "en": "The performance presupposes the mirror"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source states the outcome of the want in plain terms and in the same sentence as the requirement. The substitute is admitted and is said not to be considered as good as the original; that qualification stands here rather than in the strength, because it weakens the object and not the necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Where there is no mirror or its substitute, no performance is possible."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusToli"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 99, p. 278",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A class named apart from all the other specialists and said to be the only ones who properly deserve the name of witch. They are said to gather at night under a master, who goes to a chosen house scattering a powder made of the bones of the dead, by which everyone in the house is put so deeply asleep that neither person nor animal stirs; he comes to the one he means to kill, draws a little blood with his nail from any part of the body and sucks what he can, for which they are also called suckers; what is so taken is carried to the assembly, where it is said to be multiplied into flesh, and is cooked and eaten, and the person dies within two or three days. The common phrase for the assembly is that tonight we are to eat the soul of such a one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheEatersOfMen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Menschenesser",
        "en": "The eaters of men"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The recorder states how the class came to be known and the account is unusually traceable for this file: it was not established until one man volunteered it after his examination was over, naming others, who were then arrested and examined singly and against one another. Sixty-three were found culpable in four villages of the coast, none at all in the highland villages at that time, and more later in two other provinces. He also reports the confessions in the words of the accused, including one who said he had been made to eat by force and threatened with death if he did not come again.",
          "The whole of it was obtained under an examination conducted by a tribunal with the power to punish, and the recorder says the punishment given during the visitation was deliberately light in order to keep them quiet until a heavier one could be arranged. What such an examination produces is a well-known thing and the count of sixty-three is not evidence of sixty-three witches. It is entered because it is what the record says the transmission says about itself, and because the shape of the claim — the night assembly, the taking of a little blood, the eating of the soul, the death within two or three days — is the material a comparison is made from, not because the events are credited."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the cauchus or runapmicuc",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The third of the three parts, the knowledge of God."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Theologike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theologikē",
        "en": "Knowledge of God"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "It is proclaimed at the dawn of each holy day that one who knows himself unworthy to appear before the face of the Sun must not enter the ceremonial camp circle, because if such a one appears the Sun will hide his face with a veil of clouds until the offender withdraws or until the Winged God sweeps or washes away the offence. If the face is hidden at sunrise the marshals seek the cause of offence and expel it, and the people wait until the Sun shows his face. If on any day he does not show it, that day is not counted as a holy day, and enough days are counted to make four."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheSunHidesHisFaceOverAnOffence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Sonne verbirgt ihr Gesicht, solange ein Unwürdiger im Lager ist",
        "en": "The Sun hides his face while an unworthy person is in the camp"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A sign read off the weather, with a stated cause, a stated remedy and a stated procedure for the case where the sign appears. It is the closest thing in this file to a falsifying condition applied in public, and it is also the clearest case in this file of a condition that cannot fail: an overcast morning is assigned to an offender, and if no offender is found the day is struck from the count instead. Both halves are in the corpus and both are entered."
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 100 and p. 105",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state produced in the performance is bounded on both sides: it must not turn into an uncontrolled fit and it must not be suppressed. Within those limits it varies in degree. Producing it requires the ability to double, a power of controlling the thinking mechanism, and knowledge of the methods for entering the state, maintaining it and ending it as the performance requires."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusExtasy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Zustand hat zwei Grenzen, und beide sind verfehlbar",
        "en": "The state has two limits, and both can be missed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the passage that decides the reading of the whole file. A person in whom the state turns into a fit, who cannot control himself, is held to be possessed by the spirits and for that reason cannot become a shaman: he must be treated. A person who cannot produce the state at all cannot become a shaman either, but may become a clan priest. The transmission thus excludes on both sides of a band, and the popular identification of the office with uncontrolled trance names precisely one of the two exclusions."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-363",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that four terminologies name the same four bodies: carnal, natural, spiritual and divine; carriage, horse, driver and master, glossed as body, feelings and desires, mind, and the I with consciousness and will; physical, astral, mental and causal; with the assertion that comparisons of this kind are found in most systems that grant a person more than a physical body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourBodiesAcrossTerminologies",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Vierteilung unter vier Namensreihen",
        "en": "One fourfold division under four sets of names"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held here because of the shape of the claim, not its content. An identity is asserted between a Pauline triad extended to four, an unnamed eastern set of images, and a nineteenth-century theosophical scheme. No route of contact is given for any of the three, and none is asserted by this node. The correspondence is recorded as claimed and not as established: this is exactly the case pm:Converging exists to keep separate from, and it does not meet that bar, because a shared table of names is not a shared claim.",
          "The theosophical column is the one that dates the table. Physical, astral, mental and causal in that sequence is a set fixed in the late nineteenth century; a table containing it cannot be older than that, whatever the antiquity of the images beside it. Anyone reading the four columns as four independent witnesses to one ancient doctrine has taken a nineteenth-century arrangement for a convergence."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A correspondence found in most teachings, presented as recovered rather than as drawn up.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), at the table of the four bodies and its four terminologies",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "He returns with a knife, a ring or some other token of his knowledge of what is done in those parts, and on rising shows what he has brought from thence, so that the account may seem certain. The recorder notes that the other report he uses mentions no such thing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TokenBroughtBack",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Zeichen wird von dem Ort mitgebracht",
        "en": "A token is brought back from the place"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That the two reports the compiler used disagree about whether the token exists is recorded with the claim and not smoothed away. He names the report that lacks it. Which of the two is right is not decidable here, and the disagreement is itself the reason no examination node is written.",
          "This is the most examinable claim in the file. It is not a report of a perception but the production of a physical object said to have come from a named distant place, in front of witnesses, at a stated moment. What would refute it is obvious and requires nothing but the object and the place, and it is entered chiefly for that reason."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "In the same clan history, an election carried out when nobody knew whom to select: a man from every generation put on a shaman's headdress, they went out and stayed in the frost while the act was carried on for a long time. Many of them could not stand it and ran away. The spirit descended into one of those who remained and declared that new shamans should not be initiated in winter but in spring."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusTrialInTheFrost",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Probe im Frost",
        "en": "The trial in the frost"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A selection by endurance with an outcome the participants do not control, followed by a rule derived from the cost of the procedure. The transmission here corrects its own practice on the strength of what happened, which is the shape of an operative reworking rather than of a copied instruction.",
          "It stands inside a clan narrative told to the recorder and not in an account of a procedure he watched. The narrative also carries a live burial, a decapitation and a series of deaths, and no part of it is separable from the rest by anything in the text."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, Case 2, p. 347",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "One prohibition is attached to the town charm and the corpus says it must be scrupulously observed: nothing tied in a bundle may enter the town, or the charm becomes ineffective. Women returning with firewood untie their bundles before they reach the post, men carrying thatching grass unfasten them, carriers loosen their cords or make a wide detour, and people take off their girdles."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NothingTiedMayEnterTheTown",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nichts Gebündeltes darf in den Ort",
        "en": "Nothing tied in a bundle may enter the town"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because it is the clearest case in the file of a condition that is public, physical, continuously in force, and observable by anyone. It is also the condition most easily satisfied after the fact: any misfortune in the town can be traced to a bundle somebody carried in."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:NkindaAEvata"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only.",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 220-221",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission received by Jane Roberts from 1963, teaching that reality is constructed by belief and that the constructing can be observed and altered by the one who does it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SethMaterial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Seth-Material",
        "en": "The Seth material"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "States its central claim in a form that asks for a procedure: beliefs are to be examined and changed, and the change is said to show. The sessions were numbered and dated from the start."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Roberts, Seth Speaks; Roberts, The Nature of Personal Reality; The Early Sessions",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the practice of discerning the origin of inner movements, running from the desert monastic literature through the Byzantine ascetic corpus into the Latin spiritual exercises."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment",
      "label": {
        "de": "Christliche Unterscheidung der Geister",
        "en": "Christian ascetic discernment"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The gap is load-bearing here: Evagrius is transmitted in Greek and in Syriac with divergent chapter counts, the Collationes are numbered differently in the older editions, and the marginal numbering of the Spiritual Exercises belongs to a modern editorial layer and not to the autograph."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "John Cassian, Collationes I-II",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A leaf of the ngindu-ngindu tree is held to counteract the influence of all charms and charged things, so that a practitioner who finds one in his house must reinvigorate everything he owns, the presence of the leaf having rendered them useless."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NginduLeafNullifiesEveryCharm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Blatt hebt jedes geladene Ding im Haus auf",
        "en": "One leaf annuls every charged thing in the house"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because it is a physical and checkable condition of failure rather than a moral one, and because it is general: it does not annul one charged thing but the whole stock of them. What it shares with the other failure conditions is that it is discovered after the fact."
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 241",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is neither dissolved nor kept apart but carried into a union of two that preserves both parties to it, so that what is transformed persists as one side of a relation rather than as an absolute or as nothing."
      },
      "id": "pm:conjugalTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bräutliches Ziel",
        "en": "Conjugal telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:separativeTelos for the same reason from the other side: what persists here is not its own absolute but a party to a bond, and it is transformed on the way rather than fortified.",
          "Distinct from pm:unitiveTelos, and the distinction is not verbal. A dissolution has one term at the end; a marriage has two. A record that files a bridal end state as unitive reports the disappearance of something the source keeps."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A capacity ascribed to a named ruler, in an account whose author remarks that those who were present still tell it although there is now no reward for the lie."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VespasianHealing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Heilung durch Berührung, Vespasian zugeschrieben",
        "en": "Healing by touch ascribed to Vespasian"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The remark about eyewitnesses is part of the ascription, not an endorsement of it. It records how the historian wanted the claim received."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:HealingByTouch"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Vespasian"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": [
        "Suetonius, Divus Vespasianus 7",
        "Tacitus, Historiae IV.81"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A written letter sewn into a piece of cloth and hung around the neck, where cloth, tape and thread must never have been in water, the thread must carry no knot, and cloth and tape must have been spun by a child of not more than seven years; the hanging on is unbeschrauen and falls in a named Friday of the moon, and the Lord's prayer and the articles of faith are said immediately after."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der eingenähte Brief gegen das Reissen",
        "en": "The sewn letter against rheumatism"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is addressed falls into two kinds. The fixed, which cannot be taken away because they do not move: the sun, the moon, the Pleiades, the lightning, the sea, the earth, springs, rivers, snow peaks, high hills and certain very large stones, and the pacarina, the place each lineage says it came out of. And the movable, which are the ordinary huacas of a place: mostly stone, mostly without a figure, each with its own name, each belonging to a lineage, each with its own priests. Beside them stand the malquis, the bones or whole bodies of the ancestors, held to be children of the huacas and given the same offerings; and the conopas, held in each house and inherited by the eldest son, which are the same worship made private."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HuacaAsAddressedPower",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Huaca als angesprochene Macht",
        "en": "The huaca as a power that is addressed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The division is the recorder's and it is his working distinction as an extirpator: the movable ones could be seized and burned and the fixed ones had to be taken out of the heart instead. It is entered because it is also a real division in the practice — a lineage huaca is public and a conopa is secret — and not because his purpose was neutral."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named as the one who disclosed the signs of the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shamsiel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schamsiel",
        "en": "Shamsiel"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Charles brackets the clause naming Araqiel and Shamsiel as absent from part of the Ethiopic tradition; the bracketing belongs to the text as printed and is recorded here rather than resolved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Shamsiêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stopping of a fluid held to fall from above and to be consumed below, whose loss the manual makes responsible for ageing and death."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RetentionOfTheDescendingNectar",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zurückhalten des herabfließenden Nektars",
        "en": "The retention of the descending nectar"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.51-52 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.77 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AzazelTeachesAdornment",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel offenbart Schmuck und Kosmetik",
        "en": "Azazel discloses adornment and cosmetics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:AdornmentAndCosmetics"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Azazel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Anapanasati",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ānāpānasati",
        "en": "Ānāpānasati"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Majjhima Nikāya 118"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prayer of stereotyped phrasing, held by an appointed keeper for each kiva and taught over four nights after each planting of prayer sticks, the instructor intoning while the pupils join in by degrees and afterwards reciting it back to him."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ZuniPrayer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuni-Festgebet",
        "en": "Zuni fixed prayer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The teaching method is itself a check: the pupil returns the prayer to the keeper, so that a divergence is caught before it is carried into the ceremony. Because the phrasing is stereotyped the witness names the sequence, and not the wording, as the hard part of learning a long prayer."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 616",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Places a tradition, a practice or a claim on the axis of the end state. Asserting it is a claim about a source and carries the same burden as any other."
      },
      "id": "pm:telicOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung des Zieles",
        "en": "telic orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Where a source claims one end state for a stage and another for the whole, both are stated on their own nodes rather than averaged. An averaged orientation reports a path no source teaches, in the same way an averaged reading reports a text no witness gives."
      },
      "range": "pm:TelicOrientation",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumdivination setzt die Reinheit des Fahrzeugs voraus",
        "en": "Dream divination presupposes purity of the vehicle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The condition is on the state of the seer, not on a technique of sleeping. Recorded as order rather than necessity because the treatise makes the clarity of the dream depend on the vehicle without stating at that place what befalls one who proceeds with an unpurified one. Chapter divisions vary between editions; the work and its subject are given so that the passage can be found in whichever edition is at hand."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:DreamDivination"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PneumaticVehicle"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the corpus of this tradition has been carried into the graph."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:coverageState",
      "label": {
        "en": "coverage state"
      },
      "range": "pm:CoverageState",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TheopemptosCedesAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum tritt die Trägerschaft ab",
        "en": "The god-sent dream cedes agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The dream is held to come from a god rather than from the sleeper, and the sleeper is described as receiving it. The axis records the account the source gives of who acts, not a verdict on that account."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TungusStateHoldsOwnAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Zustand der Darbietung behält die eigene Trägerschaft",
        "en": "The state of the performance holds its own agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:ownAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusExtasy"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Producing the state is said to require a power of controlling the thinking mechanism and knowledge of the methods for entering it, maintaining it and ending it. A person in whom it turns into an uncontrolled fit is held to be possessed and for that reason cannot hold the office. The transmission thus places the office on the acting end of this axis and excludes the other end by name."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-363",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A breath practice inscribed in forty-five characters on a twelve-sided jade block, setting out the deliberate movement of qi through the body."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Xingqi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zirkulation des Qi",
        "en": "Circulating the qi"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two axes of disagreement belong to the claim and are recorded with it. Dating: Harper places the inscription in the late fourth or early third century BCE, Needham in the middle of the sixth. Reading: Harper takes the opening two characters as the title and begins the text at the second line, where Roth counts and construes differently. A citation that names only the inscription conceals both."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "H.D. Roth, Original Tao (1999)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in J. Needham, Science and Civilisation in China II (1956), p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The deliberate use of dreams as a source of knowledge, treated in this literature as available to anyone and requiring no instrument beyond the sleeper."
      },
      "id": "pmp:DreamDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumdivination",
        "en": "Dream divination"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The absence of an instrument and of an office is the point at which this tradition differs from the incubation cult: no temple, no priest, no fee."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the school promises the pupil who keeps his vows: that memory will be strong and that the training, the knowledge and the songs will stay."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HulaKnowledgeRetained",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Gesänge und die Schulung bleiben beim Schüler",
        "en": "The songs and the training stay with the pupil"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter V, p. 34",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The casting rule carries its own condition of nullity: if more than two nuts remain in the hand, the throw does not count and yields no mark."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FaVoidThrowRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Wurf, bei dem mehr als zwei Nüsse bleiben, ist ungültig",
        "en": "A throw that leaves more than two nuts is void"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also has an opposite side that belongs beside it. One or two nuts are dropped on purpose during the throw, so the number remaining is not left wholly to the cast; how much that leaves to the caster is not stated in the source and is not asserted here.",
          "The only stated void condition found in this run in the travellers' literature on the oracle, and the reason this transmission was worth opening before others in the registry. A procedure that can produce a non-answer is not the same as one whose every outcome is an answer, and the difference is exactly what makes a count possible: the admissible answers can be fixed before the consultation."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), p. 475",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Five requirements, given together: to maintain the greatest propriety of demeanour, to suppress all rudeness of speech and manner, to abstain from all carnal indulgence, to deny themselves specified articles of food, and above all to avoid contact with a corpse."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HalauConduct",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die von der Hula-Schar geforderte Führung",
        "en": "The conduct required of the hula company"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The list mixes conditions of different kinds without marking the difference, and the mixture is itself the finding. Propriety and rudeness are matters of judgement and could be argued about; contact with a corpse is an event that either happened or did not. A rule set in which only some members are decidable is the ordinary case in this collection, and it is what makes a transmission look stricter than it is testable."
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter II, p. 15",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Turkic and Mongol transmission of a sky power that confers mandate and fortune, and of practitioners who address it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:InnerAsianTengrism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Innerasiatische Tengri-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Inner Asian sky transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Secret History of the Mongols; the Orkhon inscriptions; the published Manas cycle",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A fowl or a goat is killed and its blood poured over the figure or the bundle, or the toe of a fowl or of a frog is cut and the blood allowed to fall on it. The corpus gives the rule as a schedule and a scale: to be kept at full power the sacrifice should be made regularly, at every new moon or on certain market days, and the larger the favour expected the more costly the sacrifice must be, so that a charged thing receiving only the drippings from a frog's toe is not to be expected to confer large favours. The flesh may not be sold but is eaten by the man who offers it and his clan, the offering is made at sunset with the face turned to the sun, and a pig is never offered."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Kimenga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kimenga, das erneuernde Opfer",
        "en": "Kimenga, the sacrifice that renews the charged thing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Recorded with its scale because the scale is what makes the practice answerable: a transmission that ties the size of the favour to the size of the offering has said something that its own practitioners can find false, and the corpus elsewhere records exactly that complaint in the form of a charged thing that failed.",
          "The recorder adds that his informants could not say why the pig is excluded, and he supplies a guess of his own. The guess is his and is not entered."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, mostly unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms in the text while the accounts themselves are printed in English only.",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 235-236",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is to be strengthened and kept as its own absolute, and treats its dissolution as what is to be avoided."
      },
      "id": "pm:separativeTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Absonderndes Ziel",
        "en": "Separative telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Distinct from self-assertion, ambition or the pursuit of power, none of which say anything about an end state. What places a path here is a claim about where it ends, not about how its practitioners behave."
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SunDanceYieldsCommunicationFromTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zuendetanzen bringt eine Mitteilung von der Sonne hervor",
        "en": "Dancing to completion yields a communication from the Sun"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The dancers of the buffalo dance are told the same thing in the praise the herald proclaims over them: that they now belong to the people of the Sun, that they will not have to pay the price when they take a wife, that they will have many children who will honour them, and that they may now receive a communication from the Sun. The four are given together, which is worth noting because only the last of them has a term.",
          "The strongest form in which this transmission makes a claim that could fail, and the reason the tradition was taken from the registry. The practice is public and its completion is witnessed by the camp; the promised effect is a communication; the term within which it is to arrive is named. What the corpus does not say is what follows if the term passes empty, and that silence is a property of the corpus and is recorded rather than filled."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:WiwanyangWacipi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:CommunicationFromTheSun"
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and p. 115 and p. 120",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Nobody has stated a condition under which the claim would count as refuted. This is a statement about the means of examination, not about the claim."
      },
      "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kein Verfahren ersonnen",
        "en": "No procedure devised"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the mark the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi sets on the closing off of the breath: a goose down is laid on the nose and the mouth, and that it does not stir while the qi is let out is the sign. The passage gives this as the criterion by which the practitioner knows he is doing the thing, alongside the rule that he is not to hear his own breathing and that he is always to take in more than he gives out."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FeatherAtTheNostrilExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Flaum an Nase und Mund stillsteht",
        "en": "Whether the down at nose and mouth stays still"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The scale beside the sign belongs in any run. The chapter starts the beginner at a silent count of one hundred and twenty and says the count may in the long run reach a thousand, and it attaches to the thousand a claim that does not belong to the down at all: that the old grow younger and a day is given back for a day. A protocol that measured the count and reported the second claim as tested would be measuring one thing and reporting another.",
          "The state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised, and here the procedure was devised by the source rather than by this project. Nothing stands in the way of running it: the down is cheap, the observation needs no instrument, and the criterion is binary. What has not been done is the running.",
          "What such a run would and would not settle. It would settle whether a person can exhale below the threshold at which a goose down moves, which is a question about airflow and not about the qi; the chapter treats the two as one, and this protocol does not. It would not touch the second and larger claim of the same passage, the embryonic breathing that uses neither nose nor mouth, for which the down criterion is satisfied by construction and therefore says nothing."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/daoist.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A practitioner who claims the attainment and whose down stirs on the exhalation, observed by someone other than himself. The source states the sign without qualification and attaches no condition of hour, place or state to it, so a stirring falls against the claim as the chapter makes it. The converse does not establish the doctrine: a down that lies still shows a slow exhalation and nothing about the qi.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-09",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol against the claim that the casting of the nuts makes known the outcome of the business asked about."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FaForeknowledgeProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokoll: der angesagte Ausgang gegen das Ereignis",
        "en": "Protocol: the announced outcome against the event"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One case, and it is entered as one case and not as a rate. The king's complaint of 1871 names a prediction of an easy victory and a defeat, which is a deviation; what it does not supply is how many consultations he had, how many were borne out, or what the base rate of victory was for the campaigns of that reign. A single failure reported by an aggrieved client is evidence that the claim can fail and no evidence at all about how often.",
          "The counter-search was carried inside the works opened and is recorded below. It found no case of a consultation whose outcome was reported as borne out; what it found instead is the remedy at pmp:VoSisa, which absorbs a bad prediction, and the observation that the oracle is the most lucrative branch of the priesthood. Both belong to the protocol rather than against it: the first says why cases are hard to count, the second says who has an interest in there being no count."
        ]
      },
      "pm:caseCount": 1,
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundNothing"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Searched in the three works opened in this run for a reported consultation whose prediction was borne out, and for any reply to the king's complaint of 1871: Skertchly 1874 records the complaint and no vindicating case; Burton 1864, vol. II, records the remedy for an evil prediction and the saying that the oracle pays best, but no case; Ellis 1890 does not describe the procedure at all and refers the reader to his Yoruba volume. Nothing was found that reports a Fa prediction as confirmed by the event."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia compiler, from the wording of Skertchly 1874, pp. 379-380 and 474-476, with the search stated in the counter-search note",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A consultation whose stated outcome is contradicted by the event, reported by the person who consulted."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-08",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:FaYieldsForeknowledge"
      },
      "source": "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 379-380",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The session in which a shaman calls up his helping spirits and puts a question to them, held in the household on the occasion that requires it rather than at a fixed time."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitSpiritCalling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geisterrufen",
        "en": "Spirit calling"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The portion read gives the occasions and the outcomes and not the procedure. Nothing was found here about the posture, the words, the darkness or the binding that the later chapters of this volume are known to describe, and none of that is asserted by this node."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tradition holds that the distinction between an awareness in the body and an awareness apart from it does not apply, because it does not grant the localisation the axis presupposes."
      },
      "id": "pm:locusDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ortsunterscheidung abgelehnt",
        "en": "Locus distinction declined"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessLocus",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the two stages of the highest tantra, treated before the completion stage in the training."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GenerationStage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erzeugungsstufe",
        "en": "Generation stage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The maidens who wish to attend the dancers are called by name and each declares that she has never had carnal intercourse with a man. Anyone may challenge the declaration. If the challenged maiden is silent she is held not to be a maiden; if she repeats the declaration and bites a snakeskin or the effigy of a snake and the challenger then keeps silence, her declaration is held true. If the challenge is repeated the challenger must bite the snake too; if he does not, his challenge is held a slander. If he does, the decision is held in abeyance until a snake decides it by biting the one who gave false testimony, as the corpus says a snake will surely do."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MaidenDeclarationDecidedByASnake",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine bestrittene Erklärung entscheidet eine Schlange",
        "en": "A contested declaration is decided by a snake"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also the only ordeal in this file in which both parties are put at risk, and the only one in which the transmission provides that the accuser may lose by refusing the same test.",
          "The most nearly falsifiable procedure in this file. It has a named outcome, a named agent, and a defined pair of contestants, and it does not resolve until the outcome occurs. What it does not have is a term, and that is the whole difference between it and the Sun dance promise on the node above: the verdict of the snake can be waited for indefinitely, so no length of silence tells against it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 99",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "All diseases are things that have got into the body and do violence to it, and the thing to do is to get them out. The medicines drive the disease out in the sweat, in the breath, in the defecation, in the urine or in the vomit, and the corpus ranks these: in the sweat is the best and easiest, in the breath the next best, then the defecation, then the urine, and the vomit is very hard although some diseases will come out no other way. Where the cause is an influence a shaman cures best; where it is something else, a medicine man does; and if the sickness is of long duration someone should seek a communication and learn what to do."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheOrderOfExitsForADisease",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rangordnung der Wege, auf denen eine Krankheit ausgetrieben wird",
        "en": "The ranked ways a disease is driven out"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A ranked therapeutics with a stated criterion, given by a named informant whose father was a medicine man. It is entered because a ranking of routes is a claim that can be wrong in a way that a list cannot.",
          "The second witness has the same rule from a different man on a different reservation, put as a regime rather than a ranking: the first thing a sick person should do is take a sweat bath so that the body will respond to remedies, then a lighter diet and rest, and no unnecessary exertion. He adds a limit that the first witness does not — that if the disease is not too far advanced the person usually recovers — and a caution that the sick person did not jump into cold water afterwards, as is sometimes stated, but was covered with furs and allowed to cool gradually."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 251-252",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 163"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The making of new wooden images, begun by the priest, the chacs and the carver together with a fast. During the work they were not to consort with their wives, the witness adds even in thought, and nobody could enter the place where they worked. Blood was drawn from the ears and incense burned."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MayaIdolCarving",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schnitzen der Götterbilder im Monat Mol",
        "en": "The carving of idols in the month Mol"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The condition that reaches furthest is the one on thought. A rule about an act can be kept and seen to be kept; a rule about what may pass through the mind of the worker has no observer, and a failure of the work can always be referred back to it. That is the shape of a claim that cannot fail, and it is worth marking where a transmission takes it on."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, pp. 76-77",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A list given in one breath: circulating the qi may cure the hundred illnesses, may take one into a place of pestilence, may hold off snakes and tigers, may stop the blood of a wound, may let one stay in water, may let one go on water, may keep off hunger and thirst, and may lengthen the years."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiCapacities",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was die Zirkulation des Qi vermögen soll",
        "en": "What the circulation of the qi is said to accomplish"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Eight items, of which the middle ones are decidable and the outer ones are not. Staying in water, going on water and holding off a tiger are outcomes a witness could report; curing the hundred illnesses and lengthening the years are not, in this form. The list is recorded whole rather than filtered, because a transmission that puts both kinds in one sentence is telling something about itself."
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The power ascribed to the ordeal of showing whether an accused person is a ndoki, a witch, the corpus stating that the ordeal is firmly held to be the surest means of detecting one, and that the accused is thereafter either honoured or killed according to the effect the poison has had on his stomach."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DetectionOfTheNdoki",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Aufdecken des ndoki",
        "en": "The detection of the witch"
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), pp. 51-52",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination held at the close of one katun and the opening of the next, in which the first head-chief demands of each town chief a series of objects named figuratively, and the candidate must produce the thing actually meant. The demands are answered by everyday things: the sun is a large fried egg, the brains of the sky are copal gum, the house six spans long is a very large hat, the things that stop the bottom of the cenote are white and yellow jicamas."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ZuyuaThan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verhör in der Sprache von Zuyua",
        "en": "The interrogation in the language of Zuyua"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The source names the moment exactly and not vaguely: the katun that ends is 3 Ahau and the one that begins is 1 Ahau, so the procedure carries a date rather than an occasion."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98; the same chapter is pp. 42-50 in the first edition, Carnegie Institution of Washington Publication 438, 1933",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HanblapiYieldsWowihanble",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Suchen bringt die Mitteilung hervor",
        "en": "The quest yields the communication"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the practice is a stated bodily regime in a stated place, and the capacity is a communication with a content that the seeker is afterwards to be governed by. The transmission also states that the two do not always go together, which is on the neighbouring node."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Hanblapi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Wowihanble"
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The young woman wraps her first menstrual discharge and places the bundle in a plum tree, high enough that coyotes cannot reach it. The reasons given are three: the plum is the emblem of fruitfulness preferred by the Buffalo God; anyone or anything obtaining part of a first discharge thereby gains an influence over the woman that may be used to make her do foolish or shameful things; and the bundle has a potency of its own, so that if it is disturbed it may cause eruptive diseases of the skin and the falling of the hair."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheFirstMenstrualBundle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Bündel des ersten Flusses und was seine Störung bewirken soll",
        "en": "The bundle of the first flow and what disturbing it is said to cause"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corpus adds elsewhere that the menstrual flow is very wakan and will cause diseases of the skin and the genitals, that some medicine men make a love medicine of it if they invoke the right spirits, and that its influence gives paralysis. The three passages are consistent with each other and are cited together.",
          "The last clause is the reason this node is in the file. The corpus does not leave the claim as a doctrine: it names the evidence it rests on, in the form see young men with pimply faces and many coyotes without hair. That is an appeal to a distribution in the world, made by the transmission itself, and it is the only one of its kind found in this run. It is also plainly checkable and plainly weak, and both belong in the record."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 143 and p. 162",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stated reading of the divination: if the fork falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live. In the case related the fork settled upright and the sick child lived."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das an der Gabel gelesene Zeichen",
        "en": "The sign read from the fork"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the sign is stated in advance and in both directions, which is rare in the material this graph holds. A divination that names its own failure condition before the outcome is known is examinable; one that is interpreted afterwards is not. The single case related is of course selected by the narrator's survival."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What in the source carries the placement, in the compiler's words: the condition the passage describes, and why it is read as this coordinate."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Situating",
      "id": "pm:situationGround",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verortungsgrund",
        "en": "situation ground"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required. Without it a placement is a bare assignment, and a later reader can neither check it nor contradict it — the same reason pm:counterSearchNote is required as soon as a counter-search state is set."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A heat said to arise when the raised wind reaches the region of fire, lengthening its flame, until the sleeping power is warmed through and straightens."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KindledInnerFire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das entfachte innere Feuer",
        "en": "The kindled inner fire"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The property the transmission holds to be decidable and to be decided at a particular place. The hashmal is said to divide, among those who descend, between those who were fit to descend and those who were not."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HekhalotFitness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Eignung zum Abstieg zur Merkava",
        "en": "Fitness to descend to the Merkavah"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "hashmal"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A ceremony celebrated once in sixty years whose stated purpose is the renewal of the world. The star that governs its reckoning is named after it: sigi tolo, star of the Sigui."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sigui",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sigui",
        "en": "Sigui"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 274 and 280",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two things, according to the witnesses. Where a mother is cast, the day denoted by its symbol is the propitious day for the business asked about, and where a child is cast no day is more propitious than another; and the oracle is said to predict evil, in which case a prescribed remedy follows. Simple answers of yes and no are said to be obtainable by anybody, while more stands with the practitioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ForeknowledgeFromFa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was Fa kundtun soll",
        "en": "What Fa is said to make known"
      },
      "source": [
        "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 475-476",
        "Richard F. Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome (2nd ed., London: Tinsley Brothers, 1864), vol. II, p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaIsByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama wird durch Anstrengung erlangt",
        "en": "Saṃyama is attained by effort"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:byEffort"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The practice is the deliberate application of three named means to one object, and the text elsewhere makes the arising of knowledge conditional on the wearing away of impurity through the practice of the limbs. Nothing here arrives unworked for."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The person through whom the wording was received, named as the work names them."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Receiving",
      "id": "pm:receivedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "empfangen durch",
        "en": "received by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late Victorian transmission that welded kabbalistic, grimoire and Egyptian material into a graded initiatory curriculum with examinations."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GoldenDawn",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn",
        "en": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The syncretism is documented in its own papers, which makes it the best available record of how a modern system is assembled from older parts and then presented as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The cipher manuscripts and knowledge lectures; Regardie, The Golden Dawn",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the narrow half of the claim: whether contact through the left hand differs in its reported or measured effect from contact through the right."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HandSideTransferProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Seite der Hand einen Unterschied macht",
        "en": "Whether the side of the hand makes a difference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A caution about what a positive result would mean. Handedness, the side a practitioner was taught on, and the expectation carried into the contact would all have to be controlled before a difference between the sides could be read as a property of the sides. A protocol that found one without controlling them would have measured the teaching.",
          "Only the lateral half is examined here. Whether anything passes at all is the section's broad claim and is not addressed by this protocol; a result on the sides would say nothing about it either way, and the two must not be allowed to borrow from one another.",
          "The state is procedure without cases, which is rare in this holding and is the point. The falsifying condition below needs no instrument the field lacks and no access to a tradition: blinded practitioners, both hands, and a comparison. Nobody has run it. That is a gap in the record and not a verdict, and the distinction is the one SPEC §13 exists to keep."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The falsifying condition is the ordinary two-sided comparison, stated because the claim names a side and thereby invites it",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Practitioners blinded to which hand is in contact report or show no difference between left-hand and right-hand contact beyond chance."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromAPersonThroughTheLeftHand"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The power is imagined rising in the form of lightning, at each centre in turn and step by step, upward as far as three fists, and at the end of that ascent stands the great awakening."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AscentThroughEachCentre",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Aufstieg, der in einer Bewegung jedes Zentrum durchläuft",
        "en": "The ascent that passes through every centre in one movement"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two phrases that carry this are praticakram, at each centre, and kramāt kramam, step by step. Recorded because it settles two questions at once. It gives a body measure in the base text, three fists for the upward reach, where a modern draft gives an arm's length; and it makes the passage through several centres a single exercise, where the same draft allows one centre per session and calls a change of centre a break. See prima-materia#38 and prima-materia#40."
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 29 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the metals of the earth and of working them into blades, shields and body armour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Metalworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Metallverarbeitung und Waffenherstellung",
        "en": "Metalworking and weapon-making"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest European account, from travels of eighteen sixty-eight to eighteen seventy-one, already describes the whole apparatus: an oily preparation from a red wood, given to a hen, whose death or survival decides the question; the rubbing-board consulted before any undertaking of consequence; and the use of both to establish the guilt or innocence of the accused, with suspected witches tried by the same ordeal. It records a case in which a hostile chief called off an attack because his fowl had died."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeAtFirstContact",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Giftprobe beim ersten dauerhaften Kontakt",
        "en": "The poison ordeal at the first sustained contact"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corroborative second test is not in this account. That is an argument about the account and not about the transmission: the writer was a botanist passing through with an ivory company, not an observer of consultations, and he reports the outcome of single fowls because that is what he saw. What the silence does establish is that the double test is not attested before the monograph in anything read here, and the question of when it can first be shown is left open as an issue.",
          "The passage is the reason the contact route on this tradition is not simply documented. The oracles stand in the record at the first sustained contact with the Islamic north and cannot have come in with it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Wearing the written names upon the breast instead of uttering them firmly with the voice. The roll gives this as the second case of one instruction and not as a separate procedure: it is what is done by the man who will not speak them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatWornUponTheBreast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die auf der Brust getragenen Namen",
        "en": "The names worn upon the breast"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese esoteric transmission claiming buddhahood in the present body, achieved through the coordination of gesture, speech and visualisation."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shingon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shingon",
        "en": "Shingon"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kūkai, Sokushin jōbutsu gi; Kūkai, Hizō hōyaku",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What meets the soul at the end of the third night after death, in the shape of a woman. The oldest witness read here fixes three things a later reader can hold the transmission to: the moment, the end of the third night, after a wind that comes sweet-scented from the south; the appearance, of the size of a maid in her fifteenth year, fair, bright, white-armed, strong, tall-formed, high-standing, thick-breasted, beautiful of body, noble, of a glorious seed; and the answer she gives when asked who she is, that she is the soul's own conscience."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Daena",
      "label": {
        "de": "Daēnā",
        "en": "Daena"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The account of the journey under mang describes the same encounter and gives the figure no age at all. The fifteenth year stands in the older Avestan fragment and not in the Pahlavi book, and the two are kept apart here rather than merged, because a reader who takes the age from one and the setting from the other is reading a text no witness gives."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "daena"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "den"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Hadhoxt Nask, fragment 2.7-11, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KokabelTeachesConstellations",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel offenbart die Sternbilder",
        "en": "Kokabel discloses the constellations"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Constellations"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kokabel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of seated practice as enactment rather than means, together with a literature on the illnesses that practice itself produces."
      },
      "id": "pmt:JapaneseZen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Japanischer Zen",
        "en": "Japanese Zen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Hakuin's account of meditation sickness is a self-warning of the strongest kind the project can hold: a text prescribing a practice and describing its casualties, with a remedy."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Dōgen, Shōbōgenzō; Hakuin, Yasenkanna; Hakuin, Orategama",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The conflict between the accounts of initiation this graph holds: seven named grades in a Latin letter of about 400, four animal ranks including women in a Greek report drawn from a lost history of the cult, and eighty ascending and descending disciplines in a Greek commentary of the sixth or seventh century."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicDisputatioGraduum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drei Zeugen, drei unvereinbare Auskünfte über die Grade",
        "en": "Three witnesses, three incompatible accounts of the grades"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "None of the three knows of the others, and none is arguing. The conflict is the compiler's observation, and it is recorded because the seven-grade list is quoted everywhere as if it were the account rather than one of three. The others are not obviously worse witnesses: the four-name list comes from someone who had read a multi-volume history of the cult that is now lost, and the seven-name list from a bishop making a rhetorical point about a relative's baptism.",
          "The wall brings its own disagreement with it. Its planetary guardianships do not match those of the mosaic floor at Ostia, so the material channel is not the single voice the literary one lacks. See pmc:MithraicTutelaePlanetarum.",
          "What would decide it is not another text. The grades are attested outside the literary channel altogether, on a mosaic floor and in painted inscriptions, and one of those is now read: the painted procession at Santa Prisca has seven, labelled one by one, which settles the number for that room in 220 and against the eighty. It does not settle the four animal ranks, which name women and may describe another thing entirely."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:MithraicGradusSeptem"
      },
      "source": [
        "Jerome, Epistula 107.2 (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)",
        "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)",
        "Porphyry, De abstinentia IV.16 (trans. A. S. Geden, London 1925)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "At a named ceremony in 1871, before the assembled army and the priesthood, the king of Dahomey complained that his Muslims with their Arabic inscriptions and charms had deceived him, that his cult people made all sorts of mistakes, and that the diviners who cast lots to learn the will of the gods told him many lies. He set the complaint against what he had paid: he gave them everything in his power and everything they asked for, cowries, cattle, goats, slaves and wives, and still they did not tell him the truth. The instance he named is a war: the diviners told him he would go out and meet an easy victory, and he was beaten back and disgraced."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheKingSaysTheDivinersLied",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der König sagt öffentlich, die Wahrsager hätten ihn belogen",
        "en": "The king says publicly that the diviners lied to him"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The exit the king takes is the one this graph has now seen in three transmissions: the fault is put on the practitioners and not on the procedure. He says they lied, not that the casting does not answer. The Kongo corpus states the same division as a rule, that doubt of the practitioners costs nothing and doubt of the ordeal costs a life.",
          "The most valuable single passage found in this run. It is a failure of the oracle reported by the client, in public, with the prediction, the outcome and the fee all named, and it was taken down by a European who was present and had no stake in the complaint."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 379-380",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ApatheiaPresupposesAscesis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Apatheia setzt asketische Arbeit voraus",
        "en": "Apatheia presupposes ascetic labour"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "Apatheia is called the flower of ascetic labour and ascetic labour is defined as the keeping of the commandments, so that where the labour is absent the freedom from the passions does not appear at all rather than appearing in a lesser degree.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Apatheia"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Heat held to be generated within the body by deliberate practice rather than received from outside it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InnerHeat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Innere Hitze",
        "en": "Inner heat"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteRaphael",
      "label": {
        "de": "Raphael, der Schlangengestaltige",
        "en": "Raphael the serpent-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Raphael",
      "pm:nameRole": "third of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, serpent-like; in Celsus amphibious and frightfully hissing",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MaqamatAreByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Stationen werden durch Anstrengung erlangt",
        "en": "The stations are attained by effort"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:byEffort"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Maqamat"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The handbooks describe these as attained by effort and enumerated in a set order. The order is why prerequisite claims are found here and not at the other end of the axis."
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A set of sixteen named stations of the body, called supports, which the seal at the throat is said to bind; the manual names the number in a compound and treats the binding of all sixteen as one operation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SixteenSupports",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Stützen",
        "en": "The sixteen supports"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because the number is the whole of the finding. A modern draft gives thirteen segments as the path along which the force is led; sixteen is what the base text names here, and other works of the wider corpus give eighteen. The count therefore varies across the transmission, which means there is no single path to be had, and a scheme that presents one number as the path has chosen a branch without saying so. The other witnesses to the sixteen and the eighteen have not been read at their wording and are not entered here. See prima-materia#35."
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.73 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The binding of persons or events by spoken formula."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SpellCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Beschwörung",
        "en": "Spell-casting"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The central Mexican transmission of address to the powers in things, of the day count, of an animal companion given at birth, and of a seed drunk in order to be questioned."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NahuaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nahua-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Nahua ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run: the first treatise of the manual of sixteen twenty-nine, chapters one to seven, in the running text. The remaining eight treatises, which carry the incantations themselves and the calling back of the tonal, were not reached: the reproduction used delivers its running text only to that point. That is a property of the access path and not of the work, and the next run resumes there rather than at the beginning. See prima-materia#228.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the change is a correction rather than a finding. The registry recorded absent, which asserts that no route exists for the period in which the corpus was fixed. But this corpus was fixed a century after the conquest, by a Spanish priest holding an ecclesiastical office, among people who had been under mission for three generations, and the recorded material carries Christian elements in places. Whether what he wrote down transmits the pre-conquest practice, and in what proportion, is a question nobody in this graph has carried out. Undecided says that; absent said something stronger that was never earned.",
          "The edition is not fully stated, and that is a defect this file carries openly. The reproduction used gives the Spanish in an old orthography and does not name the edition it reproduces. Every source line below says so. Settling it is the first thing to do before more nodes are written on this tradition, because a later change of edition would touch all of them.",
          "The incantations were taken down in Nahuatl in order to be suppressed, so the words survive in the language they were spoken in inside a hostile frame. Both facts belong in the record."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sahagún, Florentine Codex, especially books VI and XI; Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas (1629); Codex Borgia",
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The non-dual commentarial tradition on the Vedānta corpus, represented here by the commentary on the Brahmasūtra."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AdvaitaVedanta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Advaita Vedānta",
        "en": "Advaita Vedānta"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Kept separate from the Upaniṣadic file although it comments on that corpus. A commentary is a later act of reading and states things its base text does not: what is held here is the commentator's argument, and a record that filed it under the older heading would date a ninth-century position to the age of the verses it expounds.",
          "The three decisions preceding this file. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Greek or Chinese material; a correspondence with either would count as a finding, and none is asserted yet. Edition: the Thibaut translation of 1890 in the Sacred Books of the East, named on the node. Number of citable passages: one, read in the introductory section on superimposition."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Śaṅkara, Brahmasūtrabhāṣya, introduction (trans. G. Thibaut, Sacred Books of the East 34, Oxford 1890)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Samadhi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sammlung",
        "en": "Concentration"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Awareness is located where the body is, whatever it perceives at a distance."
      },
      "id": "pm:embodiedLocus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Im Körper",
        "en": "Embodied"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Distant sight reported from a motionless practitioner is embodied. The axis asks where the seer is, not how far he sees."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessLocus",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Awake and not asleep, with the senses withdrawn from their objects and attention held on a single one. The condition the handbooks of concentration describe and require."
      },
      "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgezogene Vertiefung",
        "en": "Withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Set this where the source binds the act to a named degree of absorption, to a suspension of the breath, or to a prayer of the heart held past the point at which the outward senses report. This value has no counterpart in the fourfold list, which is the main reason that list could not be used."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteIaldabaoth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ialdabaoth, erster und siebter",
        "en": "Ialdabaoth, first and seventh"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one place in the passage where a ruler is tied to a star: immediately after this speech Origen reports that the star Phaenon is said to be in sympathy with the lion-like ruler. Phaenon is Saturn, and the lion-like one belongs to the other heptad, so the sentence is at once the sole planetary datum in the text and the sole bridge between its two sets of seven."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Ialdabaoth",
      "pm:nameRole": "ruling spirit of the seventh gate, addressed as first and seventh, rational ruler of a pure mind and a perfect work to son and father, who closed a gate against his own kingdom",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "de",
          "@value": "Ialdabaoth"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Jaldabaoth"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SpiritPresupposesBreath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verfeinerung des Geistes setzt die des Atems voraus",
        "en": "Refining spirit presupposes refining breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded from the Yuan-period witness, not from the Han or Song works to which the sequence is commonly credited."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NeidanThreeStages"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Qi"
      },
      "source": "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome claimed for the spoken form: the spear of the man who wishes to thrust it shall be turned back."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianSpearTurnedBack",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zurückgewendete Speer",
        "en": "The spear turned back"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stated without condition and without reservation. Nothing is said about the speaker's worthiness, his belief, the state of the roll, or the will of God, and the outcome named is a single public event at a single moment."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A single large revealed cosmology published in 1955, presenting itself as a series of papers by named celestial authors and describing a graded administration of the universe."
      },
      "id": "pmt:UrantiaBook",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Urantia-Buch",
        "en": "The Urantia Book"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The human circumstances of its production were withheld on purpose by the group that published it, which leaves its provenance the weakest in a block whose provenance is otherwise its strength."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Urantia Book",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the whole transmission is consulted for: to know what is happening at a place too far off to be seen or reached, and to report it on returning."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DistantSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen des Entfernten",
        "en": "Seeing what is at a distance"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 46-47 and pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account in which one twin tore away a piece of the placenta that became the earth, was turned into the pale fox for it, and communicated his own impurity to the earth so that it became dry and barren; his fate is to pursue his twin, who is also his female soul, until the end of time. On the mythical plane the seed-star is this fox, held in space and turning tirelessly around Sirius, that is around the pursued twin, without ever reaching her."
      },
      "id": "pmc:YuruguPursuit",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yourougou in Verfolgung Yasiguis",
        "en": "Yourougou in pursuit of Yasigui"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pursuit that never arrives is the same figure as the orbit that never closes on its centre. Whether the star is the image of the myth or the myth the reading of the star is exactly the question the article declines to raise."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 292",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in identifying a person is absorbed into what occupies them and becomes a thing, and that those who observe this state in themselves regularly take it for a merit, calling it enthusiasm, zeal, passion, spontaneity or inspiration, and believing that good work is possible only in it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der aufzulösende Zustand wird für den erstrebten gehalten",
        "en": "The state to be undone is mistaken for the state to be sought"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It stands beside the self-caution recorded at pmc:Panchatattva, where a text prescribes a rite and warns in the same breath that men of the age will take it up for pleasure. Two traditions with no route between them both place the danger inside the practitioner's own approval. Not asserted as a convergence here; noted as a second instance of the same shape.",
          "Recorded as a caution that names its own misrecognition, which is the form worth collecting. The warning is not that the state is unpleasant — it is that the state is valued, under names that make it sound like the goal. A caution of this shape cannot be tested by asking practitioners how they feel, since the claim predicts they will report it favourably."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "An observation of the school, reported as long known.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the passage on identifying and the names given to it",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that what brings the state about is not the technique but the practitioner's settled expectation that his body will stay where it is."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExpectationOfStillnessIsTheOperator",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Erwartung, dass sich physisch nichts bewegt, ist der eigentliche Operator",
        "en": "The expectation that nothing moves physically is the operator"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Unusual among the draft's claims because it is about a mechanism rather than a capacity, and unusual in its direction: it places the working part in the practitioner's expectation, which is where a sceptical account would also place it. A draft that says this about its own method is saying something against interest, and that is worth recording whatever else follows."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#304",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rule by which an oracular verdict stands or falls: across the two tests one fowl must die and the other must live. If both die or both live the verdict is contradictory and therefore void, and the matter must be put to the oracle again on another occasion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Huhn muss sterben und das andere leben",
        "en": "One fowl must die and the other live"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is a falsifying condition stated by the transmission itself, applied by its own practitioners, and printed with four worked cases. It is the reason this tradition was opened before others in the registry. What the transmission does with the condition when it is met is a separate matter and stands on its own node below."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139, the four cases lettered A to D",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The same bark is used in serious accusations of theft and in bad cases of denied adultery, given in small doses in the town by a practitioner of divination, and there the rule runs the other way: if the accused retains the ordeal he is guiltless of the charge and may demand compensation from his accusers, and if he vomits he pays the fines for his crime and the matter ends."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ReversedRuleForTheftAndAdultery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bei Diebstahl und Ehebruch gilt die umgekehrte Regel",
        "en": "For theft and adultery the rule is reversed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The exact inversion of the witchcraft rule with the same substance, in the same transmission, recorded on the following page. It is the strongest single argument in the file against reading the ordeal as a belief about a poison, and the strongest for reading it as a procedure whose reading rule is chosen with the charge. A second witness has the milder dose for theft too and gives the reason in physiological terms, that the retained poison then only causes illness."
      },
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 264",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, p. 279"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where the claim was put forward, given as an identifier rather than as a bibliographic reference: an issue number, a draft designation. Deliberately not dcterms:source, because what is named here is not a work a reader can obtain and must never be counted as one."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Positing",
      "id": "pm:positedIn",
      "label": {
        "de": "gesetzt in",
        "en": "posited in"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol taken from the tradition's own report. The lot predicted that the men of wood would come out well and would speak on earth; the chapters that follow have them without memory of their maker, walking on all fours, and destroyed. The transmission preserves the failure of its own divination instead of removing it, which is what makes the passage worth holding."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TziteExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Los, das falsch lag",
        "en": "The lot that was wrong"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The state is not raised above procedure without cases, and deliberately. One narrated instance inside a creation account is not a case in the sense this scale uses: nobody counted consultations, and the outcome is fixed by the story rather than observed. What the passage supplies is a procedure with a stated, missable outcome, which most divination material does not."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A series of castings recorded before their outcomes, in which the rate of answers met is no higher than the rate expected from the distribution of the seeds alone.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TziteAnswer"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part I, chapters 2 and 3",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Lakota transmission of a vitalizing lodge that precedes every other rite, of a solitary fasting quest undertaken for a communication, of a graded sun-gazing ceremony whose completion is said to be answered within a stated term, and of small stones held to be sent out and to bring back word of what lies beyond sight."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LakotaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lakota-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Lakota ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second witness was opened for the counter-search and is cited wherever it carries something: the bulletin of nineteen eighteen, recorded on the Standing Rock reservation from named men who had themselves danced. It is a different recorder, a different reservation and a partly different division of the tribe, and on one point it contradicts the first witness outright. Read in it at the wording: the Sun dance chapter, printed pages eighty-four to one hundred and fifty-one, the chapter on the sacred stones, printed pages two hundred and four to two hundred and eleven, and the account of Goose on printed pages two hundred and fifty-one and two hundred and fifty-two. The remainder, which is mostly musical analysis, was not read.",
          "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. This transmission states a term within which the promised communication is to arrive, provides in as many words for the case in which the quest yields nothing, hangs the validity of its greatest ceremony on a sign in the weather, decides a contested declaration by an ordeal with a named outcome, and names the cause to which a failure of its instrument is attributed. Little else in this graph carries a deadline and a stated non-occurrence side by side.",
          "Read in this run at the wording: the paper of nineteen seventeen in its expository part in full, printed pages fifty-one to one hundred and sixty-three, that is the Sun dance from the choosing of the mentor to the completion of the ceremony, the Hunka ceremony, the Buffalo ceremony, and the translated texts of Sword, Tyon, No-flesh and One-star together with the interview with Finger; and printed pages two hundred and seventeen to two hundred and nineteen, the two legends of the origin of the Buffalo ceremony. Left unread at the wording: the narratives on printed pages one hundred and sixty-four to two hundred and sixteen, which were passed over with a search for the four owed kinds and yielded none that the expository part does not already carry. That is a decision and not an exhaustion, and it is recorded so that a later reader can overturn it.",
          "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the correction is the finding of this run. It is documented three times over and each time from inside the corpus. The compiler of the paper of nineteen seventeen states that Wakan Tanka, two words in the old speech designating a class of gods, has become one word in modern Lakota designating the God of the Christians, so the central term of the doctrine was already contaminated when it was written down. His chief informant wrote an autobiography saying that in war with the white people he found their Wakan Tanka superior, that he took the name Sword, joined the church and is a deacon in it. And the editor of the volume identifies the Hunka ceremony with the Pawnee Hako and notes that the Lakota year name which dates its present form corresponds to eighteen hundred and five and turns on the tail of a horse, so that the ceremony took that form after the horse. The route is single-valued and the value entered is the one that governs the doctrinal nodes; that the informants of the second witness on the sacred stones were medicine men on whom the missionaries had made no impression is recorded on those nodes and does not change it.",
          "The recorder problem is stated here rather than left in the sources. The compiler writes that no one shaman was found who could give the mythology in a comprehensive or sequential manner, that the scheme was formulated with their aid, and that it was afterwards submitted to the informants for approval. A scheme formulated by the recorder and approved afterwards is an ordering act and not a transmitted doctrine, and it is entered below as one. The lodge, the quest, the ceremony, the ordeal and the stones carry their own passages and are untouched by it."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota, Anthropological Papers of the American Museum of Natural History, vol. XVI, part II; Densmore, Teton Sioux Music, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61; Brown, The Sacred Pipe; DeMallie, The Sixth Grandfather, as the record of the mediation",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 55-59",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment of the Hebrew prophets to the seven: Moses, Joshua, Amos and Habakkuk to Ialdabaoth; Samuel, Nathan, Jonah and Micah to Iao; Elijah, Joel and Zechariah to Sabaoth; Isaiah, Ezekiel, Jeremiah and Daniel to Adonai; Tobias and Haggai to Eloi; Michaiah and Nahum to Oreus; Esdras and Zephaniah to Astanphaeus. Each is said to glorify his own father as god."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteProphetaeDistributi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Verteilung der Propheten auf die Sieben",
        "en": "The prophets distributed among the seven"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The only place in either witness where the seven are individuated by content rather than by rank. It is also the only distribution the sources actually make across the seven, and it is a distribution of prophets and not of planets — which is worth saying beside pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae, where a distribution is exactly what is missing."
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.11 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Going before sunrise to a juniper bush, bending it towards the rising sun with the left hand while telling it that it will be bent and pressed until the thief has carried the stolen goods back, then laying a stone on the bush and the skull of a malefactor beneath stone and bush."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gebogene Wachholderbusch",
        "en": "The bent juniper bush"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 65, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The western Christian transmission of graded interior states, with criteria for telling one state from another and warnings against taking consolation for progress."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LatinMysticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lateinische Mystik",
        "en": "Latin mystical transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Teresa's dwellings and John of the Cross's nights are the western material closest to a stage map with failure modes, and the failure modes are stated more plainly than the attainments."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Richard of St Victor, Benjamin Major; Meister Eckhart, German sermons; The Cloud of Unknowing; Teresa of Ávila, Las Moradas; John of the Cross, Subida del Monte Carmelo and Noche oscura",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Putting a small piece of blue vitriol into the hollow tooth when the pain begins, not too much, and spitting out the water that collects rather than swallowing it."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicBlauerVitriol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Blauer Vitriol im hohlen Zahn",
        "en": "Blue vitriol in the hollow tooth"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim this protocol attempts to fail."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:tests",
      "label": {
        "de": "prüft",
        "en": "tests"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where two or three persons are implicated in one charge, the witch-finder takes as many small boys, one to represent each suspected adult, gives a small quantity of the ordeal to each and watches the result. If the symptoms shown by one or two of the boys warrant it, the adults they represent are accused and must take the ordeal properly; if none of the boys shows the signs, others are suspected and fresh representatives are appointed. Only members of the same family are held able to bewitch one another, and only boys of the family of the deceased are used, and they are paid for it."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ProxyDosingOfTheAccused",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Dosieren von Stellvertretern zur Auswahl des Beschuldigten",
        "en": "The dosing of substitutes to choose the accused"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A screening step before the ordeal proper, and the only case in the file where the transmission runs the procedure on a person who is not the party to the question. It is also where the physiology of the substance is made to do the work of the answer, since what is watched is the effect of a small dose on a child."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks over some thirty years of residence, the book closed in 1913; given in Kikongo, of which the work prints the terms while the accounts are printed in English only.",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 261",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that love in the mode of friendship passes through five increasingly higher stages: friendship itself, attachment, supreme love, tenderness, and passion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SakhyaRatiIntensification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf aufsteigenden Stufen der Freundesliebe",
        "en": "The five rising stages of love as friendship"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Given in the text as increasingly higher, so the ordering is asserted and the direction is stated. The series is named for one relation only; the edition read notes that a longer series of the expansions of love stands in the Ujjvalanīlamaṇi, which was not opened here."
      },
      "source": "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 3.3.106 (trans. Haberman 2003)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An exercise in dividing attention: attention is directed at the observed thing and at the one observing at the same time, so that the sense of I is held alongside the perception rather than lost in it. It is performed in the middle of ordinary activity, walking a street or entering a shop, and not in withdrawal."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering",
      "label": {
        "de": "Selbsterinnern",
        "en": "Self-remembering"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A sign of success is named and is kept apart from the exercise on purpose: a state of inner peace and confidence said to follow great efforts of this kind. It is recorded as a sign and not as evidence. The practitioner is told beforehand what to expect, so his report of it is not independent of the instruction, and there is no base rate for the same state without the instruction.",
          "The novelty of the exercise is disputed inside the source itself. A contemporary told the recorder that it is apperception as Wundt defines it and nothing further; the recorder answers that Wundt came close and went elsewhere. Held as a note and not as a pm:Disputing node, because Wundt has not been read here and a dispute node would need both sides at their own wording.",
          "The recorder distinguishes it in the same passage from two neighbouring things, and both distinctions are the tradition's own. It is not the stopping of thought described in the yoga literature he had already tried, because there the whole of attention goes to the effort of admitting no thoughts, whereas here attention is split, one part to that effort and one to the feeling of self. And it is not self-analysis or self-feeling."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:firstPersonReport"
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter seven, at the account of the first attempts at self-remembering and the division of attention (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Going onto another man's land, saying that one goes before another court and knots one's seventy-seven kinds of fits, cutting three shoots there and making a knot in each, on a Friday before sunrise in the waning moon and unbeschrauen, then making the crosses over the body at the place where the fits are felt."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Knüpfen dreier Schüsse gegen die Gichter",
        "en": "The knotting of three twigs against the fits"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Restraint, observance, posture, breath regulation, withdrawal of the senses, concentration, meditation and absorption, listed together in a single sūtra."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die acht Glieder",
        "en": "The eight limbs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sūtra names them and says nothing about traversing them in order."
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The exchange reported around what ecclesiastical writers call the seal: he who impresses it is called father and he who is sealed young man and son, and the sealed one answers that he has been anointed with white ointment from the tree of life. With it a number is given: seven angels attach themselves to either side of the soul of the dying body, the one party called angels of light, the other archontics, whose ruler is termed the accursed god."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteSigillum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Siegel und die zwei Parteien am Sterbebett",
        "en": "The seal, and the two parties at the deathbed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Origen says of the dialogue that he never heard of such a thing even among the heretics, and he attacks the name given to the ruler at length. Both the rite and his disbelief in it are recorded; the passage is the closest the witness comes to something done in life rather than after death."
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.27 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A graded emptying set out in three steps. The pupil is told not to wait for the hearing of his ears but for the hearing of his mind; then not to wait even for the hearing of his mind, but for the hearing of the third term. The hearing of the ears is to rest with the ears and the mind to rest in its own verification; the third term is free of all pre-occupation and so waits for things to appear. Where the way is, there is that freedom, and that freedom is the fasting of the mind."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fasten des Herzens",
        "en": "Fasting of the heart"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The name is a second case of the same thing. The tradition's term is xinzhai, the fasting of the xin, and the label here says heart where the edition read says mind. Both are the same character, and the graph carries the German and English labels of an earlier run beside a source that translates it otherwise. The label is left standing and the divergence is recorded, because renaming it would hide that the term does not resolve into either word.",
          "The third term is where the translations part, and the passage was reopened in this run because of it. The edition read renders it 'the spirit' in the body of the text and states in its own footnote that the character standing there is qi, 'the breath'. Whoever reads only the translation gets a graded ascent from ears to mind to spirit; whoever reads the footnote gets an ascent that ends in the breath, which is what the whole of this file is about. The earlier wording of this definition ended with the way gathering in emptiness alone, which is a third rendering again and follows a translation this file could not name. All three are recorded rather than harmonised."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The work in which the passage was actually read, given as a bibliographic reference, where that work is not the one carrying the claim."
      },
      "id": "pm:readVia",
      "label": {
        "de": "gelesen über",
        "en": "read via"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately separate from dcterms:source, for the same reason pm:evidenceFrom is. dcterms:source names what a reader must obtain to check the claim; this names what the writer of the node had in front of him. A record that fused them would report second-hand reading as first-hand and would lose the one distinction that makes an unreachable source admissible at all.",
          "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:mediatedAttestation. Carries a bibliographic reference and never a URL, enforced by pm:ReadViaIsLiteratureShape."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Iddhividha",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft",
        "en": "The kinds of supernormal power"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolZaehlungStrittig",
      "label": {
        "de": "An wem die sechzig Anwendungen geschahen",
        "en": "On whom the sixty applications were made"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The eighteen twenty printing says the author drove away his own severest toothaches more than sixty times. The eighteen fifty translation says he has cured the severest toothache more than sixty times and names no patient, which reads as a series over other people. The number is the same and the population it runs over is not, and a count whose population is unsettled cannot be compared with anything."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The pounded bark of the nkasa tree, given to a person accused of witchcraft. The corpus gives the setting and the dose: the accused is taken to the bare top of a hill, a hut of palm fronds is built with a lath across it, and a stone is pushed towards him bearing twenty-seven small heaps of the ordeal, each ground to powder and fed to him in turn, during which he must hold his hands spread out and touch nothing. A curse is then laid on him, that if he is a witch he will die by the ordeal. A second witness has the accused drink cup after cup of the bark paste mixed with water, in the market place and on market day, standing on a stone or a marked spot he may not leave."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BauKiaNkasa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bau kia nkasa, das Rindenordal",
        "en": "Bau kia nkasa, the ordeal by the bark"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The Vili witness at Loango has the same substance in the same office, administered at the ruler's court by a practitioner of the ordeal, and adds what happens to the condemned there: the body is burnt and the ashes scattered, and no burial is granted.",
          "The corpus states that it is not the medicinal property of the bark that acts but the spirit in the tree, which reveals the witchcraft or does not. That is the claim the procedure rests on, and it is what makes the case a candidate for the class of answers fixed to an outcome the operator does not control: the operator is expressly said not to be the one who decides.",
          "The same second witness reports that the mixture is supposed to be the bark only, and that a practitioner who considers it desirable to kill mixes one or two other poisons with it, which the recorder says he was told by a man who had himself been of the trade. The two statements stand in the file together on purpose: the transmission asserts an outcome nobody steers, and a witness from inside states how it was steered."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, in this passage largely unnamed, and in the second work by a man who had himself administered the ordeal; recorded by the Baptist missionaries John H. Weeks and W. Holman Bentley during their residence at San Salvador, Wathen and in the Ngombe Lutete district from 1879 onwards; given in Kikongo, of which Bentley compiled the dictionary of 1887, and printed in English with the Kikongo terms.",
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 262-264",
        "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 25 and p. 83",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 276-277"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a person is a machine governed by outward influences, that everything happens and nobody does anything, and that in order to do it is first necessary to be; together with the account of a person as lacking unity and consisting of a multiplicity of I's rather than one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DoingPresupposesBeing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Es wird nichts getan, solange niemand da ist, der tut",
        "en": "Nothing is done until there is someone to do it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It bears directly on the disagreement recorded in convergences/left-hand-path-divergence.ttl. Both sides there dispute what becomes of the self at the end; this source holds that at the beginning there is no single self to be kept or surrendered, and that one has to be made first. That is not a third answer to their question but a claim that the question has a precondition neither states.",
          "The interest for this graph is the order of precedence. The claim is not that people do the wrong thing but that the doer is not yet assembled, which puts every question of method downstream of a question about who is applying it. A path chosen by a multiplicity is chosen by whichever part happens to be present."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A finding of an older school, transmitted rather than argued.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the first talks on the human machine and in the chapter on the absence of unity",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second companion said to run a wider course in the same direction and the same time as the seed-star, four times lighter and larger than it, called the sun of the women, and named as the seat of the female souls of all beings living or to come. It is said to have a satellite of its own, the star of the Women."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EmmeYaTolo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Emme ya tolo, der Weibliche-Hirse-Stern",
        "en": "Emme ya tolo, the Female Sorghum star"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 287-288",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the Changes are said to do, stated twice in the same treatise. To exhaust the numbers and thereby to know what is coming is called prognostication, and what yin and yang do not sound out is called spirit. And: the Changes are without thought and without doing, still and unmoving; touched, they thereupon penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. The same section says that whoever is not the most spiritlike thing under heaven could not do this, and that the Changes receive a charge as an echo receives a sound."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JishuZhilai",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zahlen erschöpfen, um das Kommende zu wissen",
        "en": "Exhausting the numbers to know what is coming"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The formulation is the strongest in this file and the least examinable, and both for the same reason. Without thought and without doing means that nothing about the operator's state is offered as a condition, so no failure can be assigned to him; and knowing what is coming is not tied to any statement that could turn out false. What the corpus does supply against this is the one condition on repetition, below, and the counter-argument from a work of the first century, held as a dispute."
      },
      "source": "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiYieldsAnswer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Trinken bringt die Antwort hervor",
        "en": "The drinking yields the answer"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:OloliuhquiConsultation"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tradition holds that the distinction between what is worked for and what is given does not apply to what it teaches."
      },
      "id": "pm:acquisitionDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erwerbsunterscheidung abgelehnt",
        "en": "Acquisition distinction declined"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateAcquisition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of a detailed geometry of subtle anatomy together with a claimed ancient lineage of guardianship, delivered through a living transmitter and taught in graded courses with their own technical vocabulary."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KeylonticScience",
      "label": {
        "de": "Keylontic Science und die MCEO-Lehren",
        "en": "Keylontic Science and the MCEO teachings"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two claims to keep apart. That it transmits is a fact: texts, a transmitter, courses, students, successors, and it is registered on that ground alone. That it descends from an ancient priesthood is an ascription with a modern author and a modern date, attested nowhere outside the corpus, and it enters as a pm:Originating node when it enters. The material is under active copyright, so nothing is reproduced; the definitions here are the compiler's own paraphrase, as everywhere in this graph."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ashayana Deane, Voyagers I and Voyagers II; the published MCEO Freedom Teachings course materials",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Suhrawardī's transmission of knowledge by presence and of an intermediate world of images held to be neither material nor mental but a place with its own standing."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Illuminationism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Illuminationismus",
        "en": "Illuminationism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim that the imaginal world is a place and not a faculty is an ontological commitment the graph will have to sort against every tradition that treats vision as internal."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Suhrawardī, Ḥikmat al-Ishrāq; Mullā Ṣadrā, al-Asfār al-Arbaʿa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that if a man wishes to thrust a spear into the speaker and the speaker repeats the names, the spear shall be turned back. It is examinable because the claim names a single public event at a single moment and attaches no condition to it beyond the speaking."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianSpokenNamesSpearExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die gesprochenen Namen einen Speer zurückwenden",
        "en": "Whether the spoken names turn a spear back"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case has been collected in either direction, and the obstacle is a property of the record: the men who carried these rolls are not a recorded set, and neither the deaths nor the survivals of the eighteenth-century owners of Ethiopian parchment rolls were written down anywhere the claim could be checked against. Refutable in principle, unattended in fact, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised.",
          "One escape is nevertheless built into the object rather than into the sentence, and a protocol has to meet it: every prayer of every roll read names the person it was written for. A bearer who was struck can always be said to have carried a roll made out to somebody else. That is recorded at pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer and it is a condition of the object, not of the claim.",
          "The escape the wording does not take is the point of entering this at all. Nothing here says the spear returns only if the speaker is worthy, only if he believes, or only if God wills it — and the second half of the same sentence says exactly the last of those about the other form. The bare half was not written by an author who had no such clause available."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ethiopian-orthodox-magic.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One man who repeated the names as the thrust came and was struck by the spear. The wording admits no degree, names no further condition, and reserves nothing, so a single such case refutes the claim as it stands. A weaker form, that speakers are struck less often than others, would require the speakers to be counted before the outcomes are known, and no such count exists for any transmission in this holding.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-07",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianSpokenAsmatYieldsTurnedSpear"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that an experience of luminosity or consolation may be counterfeit, and that the counterfeit is recognised by its circumstances rather than by its brightness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AngelOfLightDeception",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Täuschung, die als Licht erscheint",
        "en": "The deception that appears as light"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Diadochus distinguishes the true consolation, which comes with the body awake and in warm love, from the false, which comes in a light half-sleep."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters 40",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 39"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the asymmetry the claim asserts between the journey out and the journey back."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ImmediateReturnProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Rückkehr sich von der Hinreise unterscheidet",
        "en": "Whether the return differs from the outward journey"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Against the claim stands the same passage as at the neighbouring node, in a weaker way. If the time lying still follows the distance and ends at a day, the account being given is of an absence with a duration, not of an absence with a slow half and an instant half. The transmission says nothing about the return specifically; it is the shape of the whole report that sits awkwardly with a claim of asymmetry.",
          "Procedure without cases rather than no procedure, and this is the one of the three where that holds. A comparison of reported durations for the outward and the return passage, gathered from practitioners not told which half the question is about, needs nothing that does not exist. It has not been done.",
          "The asymmetry is where this claim is reachable, and it is reachable in principle without settling anything about the journey itself. Whatever the outward passage is, the claim says the return is not like it, and a report that describes both would show whether the practitioner experiences them as different."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The falsifying condition follows from the claim asserting a difference between two things the practitioner can be asked about separately",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Practitioners asked about both passages, without being told that the asymmetry is at issue, report durations for the return that match those for the outward journey."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ReturnIsImmediate"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "During the fast everyone confesses to the holder of that office, both sitting on the ground, at places appointed in the open country. What is confessed is theft, ill-treatment of others, having more than one woman, adultery, and failure to attend to the huacas; inward faults are not confessed, and simple fornication is not held a fault at all. Powders of the offerings are laid on a flat stone and the penitent is made to blow them; a small stone called pasca, which means pardon, is rubbed on his head with ground white maize, and the head is washed in a stream or where two rivers meet, which is called tincuna."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Beichte während des Fastens",
        "en": "The confession made during the fast"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "It is also done outside the feasts, when a man is sick, because they understand that their ancestors and huacas are angry at their faults and that this is why they fall ill. Some purify themselves without telling anyone, by rubbing the head with the pasca and washing it in a river, saying that the water carries the faults away; and one man said the specialist took him and his wife to a large ditch, washed them, made them put on new clothes and leave the old ones on the road, saying that their faults stayed there and that whoever took the clothes would take them."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The list Jerome gives in passing while urging a mother not to despair of her father's conversion: that her kinsman Gracchus, as prefect of the city, overthrew and broke up a cave of Mithras with all the monstrous images by which the initiates are named — raven, a second name, soldier, lion, Persian, sun-runner and father — and sent them ahead of him as hostages to obtain baptism."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicGradusSeptem",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sieben Grade, benannt nach den Bildern, die ein Präfekt zerschlug",
        "en": "Seven grades, named from the images a prefect destroyed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The most quoted list in the study of this cult, and the second name in it is not transmitted. The manuscripts read nymphius, bridegroom, or gryphus, griffin; the standard edition prints cryphius, hidden one, on the strength of inscriptions and against the manuscripts. That is the same shape of thing as the seven-gated ladder at Contra Celsum VI.22: two of the load-bearing data points of this tradition rest on emendations made because the emended text agrees better with what is known from elsewhere.",
          "The translations wobble too. One English rendering of the passage produces eight items by splitting one grade in two, so a reader working from that version counts eight grades. The seven are secure only because the epigraphic and iconographic material has seven; the text alone would not settle the number.",
          "What the passage does not do is assign planets. Jerome names the grades and nothing else — no tutelary star, no order of ascent, no doctrine. Every table pairing a grade with a planet is drawing on the mosaics and inscriptions, not on this witness. That matters for prima-materia#595, because it means the two ancient literary sources for anything sevenfold in this cult, VI.22 and this letter, do not overlap: one has planets and no grades, the other grades and no planets."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Jerome, Epistula 107.2, to Laeta (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The standing of a protocol: how far the attempt to fail a claim has actually been carried."
      },
      "id": "pm:ExaminationState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfstand",
        "en": "Examination State"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The first two states and the third must never collapse into one another. A claim nobody has devised a way to examine is not a claim that has been examined and found wanting. A record that reports both as unsupported would not be reporting on the claims but on the reach of its own instruments."
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:recordScale"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:GadreelTeachesWeaponsOfDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gadreel offenbart die Waffen des Todes",
        "en": "Gadreel discloses the weapons of death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:WeaponsOfDeath"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Gadreel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The long lists of divine and angelic names that stand at the head of each prayer of the roll and are called the glorious names, the names of their God. They are the operative part: what the prayer asks for is asked by their might, and the roll gives them without explanation, as things to be said or carried rather than understood."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianAsmat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die abwehrenden Namen",
        "en": "The names that hold off"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The identifier keeps the Geez term of the genre rather than an English rendering, because the volume read gives no single term for the lists and calls the whole class magical prayers, which is the editor's name for it and not the transmission's."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "asmat"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I-IX, pp. 216-220",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of a healing energy conveyed by the hands, transmitted through an attunement conferred by a teacher in a stated lineage."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Reiki",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reiki",
        "en": "Reiki"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Attunement is a transmission claim in the strict sense: the capacity is said to pass from person to person by a discrete act. That is unusually well suited to examination, because the tradition itself distinguishes those who have received it from those who have not."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Usui Reiki Ryōhō Hikkei; Hayashi, Reiki Ryōhō Shishin",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The diviner by maize makes small heaps of grains without counting them, then takes one away from one side and one from another, and according as they are left even or odd the lot is good or bad. The earlier report gives the same mechanism under another name and with another material: they cast lots with maize and llama dung, and if they came out even or odd they gave their answers accordingly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParityDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagung durch gerade und ungerade",
        "en": "Divination by odd and even"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "In one village a practitioner of this office produced instead a bag of small stones which he said were called by a particular name and that he had inherited them from his grandfather for this purpose. The variant is recorded because it shows the office surviving a change of instrument.",
          "Two recorders, forty-six years and several hundred leagues apart, name different practitioners and the same mechanism. The count is deliberately not made before the drawing in the later account, which means the outcome is not determined by anything the diviner knows; that is the same feature the poison-oracle material in this graph rests on, and it is stated here of a maize heap."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the achicoc",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the diviner by maize"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Christian Egyptian operative texts in which biblical names, angelic invocation and older Egyptian procedure stand side by side without apology."
      },
      "id": "pmt:CopticMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Koptische Zaubertradition",
        "en": "Coptic magical transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Coptic magical papyri and ostraca in the standard collections; the Testament of Solomon as its adjacent Greek witness",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Ohr",
        "en": "Divine ear"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DreamYogaPresupposesClearLight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumyoga setzt das Halten des klaren Lichts voraus",
        "en": "Dream yoga presupposes holding the clear light of sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The treatise takes the recognition of the clear light before the training in the dream. It does not state at that place what befalls one who begins elsewhere, which is why the strength is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:DreamYoga"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that if the same oration is said while touching the pulse of a woman with child, it shall be revealed whether she shall bring forth a male or a female."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaSexusInfantis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes am Puls",
        "en": "The sex of the unborn child at the pulse"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The most examinable claim the run found in this corpus, and it is examinable for a reason that has nothing to do with how strongly it is asserted: the outcome is binary, its base rate is known and close to even, it becomes public within months, and the prediction can be recorded before it does. Nothing in the passage makes the result depend on the operator's worthiness."
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of being sacred and thereby prohibited, held to be communicated from an atua or a sacred person to whatever they touch, and from that object onward to whatever it touches in turn."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Tapu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tapu",
        "en": "Tapu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The re-transmission by contact is the part later theory generalised. A record that carries only the prohibition and not the transmissibility loses exactly what the nineteenth-century comparativists picked up and turned into an impersonal magical fluid. See prima-materia#103 and prima-materia#104 for the originations that are not yet entered."
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 25-26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The instruction that follows from it. Questions asked from a motive of curiosity or of test are not to be put; the spirits addressed do not answer them and go away, and the transmission adds that such questions please the imperfect spirits, who are amused by them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EpreuveCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegen die Prüfung der Geister",
        "en": "Against putting the spirits to the test"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The rule is self-sealing in a way the graph should record plainly rather than argue about. A superior spirit that withdraws when tested and an absent spirit produce the same observation; and a spirit that does answer a test question is by that answer classed as imperfect. Both outcomes are accounted for in advance, so the test yields no information either way."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Evocation"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Who devised or carried the protocol. A protocol without a named author is unaccountable, and an unaccountable protocol is not evidence of anything."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:examinedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "geprüft von",
        "en": "examined by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The commentator's claim that the calm state is not one aesthetic state beside the others but their source: the various feelings arise out of calm through their particular causes, and melt back into calm when those causes fall away."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SantaAsSourceOfAllRasa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Ruhe als Quelle der übrigen ästhetischen Zustände",
        "en": "Calm as the source of the other aesthetic states"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Attestation is mediated and the reason is the language and not the reachability. The critical edition of the commentary is digitised and openly available, and so is the edition of the rival position named in prima-materia#363; the passage below was read in the English rendering of a study that prints and translates it. The mediated mode is therefore an open posting to be raised as soon as someone reads the Sanskrit, not a statement that the work cannot be had.",
          "The base treatise arranges its states by derivation, deity and colour and ranks none of them. This claim is the commentator's and belongs to him, which is why it stands as its own node and not as a property of the treatise.",
          "The reason it matters for this graph. It is a claim about the top of an order and not about an order running through: it makes one state the ground of the rest on metaphysical grounds, and it does not place unlike states on a common measure. Anyone citing it as evidence that the transmission ranks aesthetic states on a scale is reading it for something it does not say."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "J. L. Masson and M. V. Patwardhan, Śāntarasa and Abhinavagupta's Philosophy of Aesthetics (Bhandarkar Oriental Series 9, Poona: Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, 1969), where the passage is printed and translated",
      "source": "Abhinavagupta, Abhinavabhāratī, on Nāṭyaśāstra VI",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SelfRememberingLocus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Bewusstsein bleibt beim Selbsterinnern im Körper",
        "en": "Awareness stays in the body during self-remembering"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:embodiedLocus"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The one arrowhead of attention stays on the observed thing and the second turns on the observer, who is walking and speaking where he stands; nothing in the account has the practitioner perceive from a place other than his body."
      },
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source states that no Zande would report the declaration of an oracle otherwise than it was given, and that a man who wishes to lie fabricates a declaration without consulting the oracle at all. Against the fabrication the transmission sets three things: the elder who makes an official test takes two or three witnesses with him, the counterparty consults his own oracles to check the first man's, and a man who believes he was not fairly tested may appeal to the king."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FabricationRatherThanMisreport",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lüge ist eine erfundene Befragung, nie eine falsch berichtete",
        "en": "The lie is a fabricated consultation, never a misreported one"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The cross-check by the counterparty is the part worth noticing. It is a control against the questioner rather than against the instrument, and it is the only one in this file that does not depend on the instrument being honest.",
          "The sanction is stated and is not merely moral: the king sends for the elder, tells him he is a cheat and a liar, and bars him from ever again conducting official consultations."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 37 and p. 154",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A claim that this ordering act groups. The claims keep their own attestation; only the grouping belongs to the compiler."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Systematizing",
      "id": "pm:ordersClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "ordnet Behauptung",
        "en": "orders claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The entry rite of the society: instruction under a paid teacher, often carried over years, gifts to the officiating priests, a feast, and the shooting of the shell into the candidate by the officiating priests."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationFirstDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den ersten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the first degree"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The report says candidates sometimes spend a number of years in preparation and impoverish themselves in the payment of fees and the preparation of the feast. The cost is stated as a fact of the institution and not as a warning."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The training a late Greek commentator reports: that nobody may share in the rites without first passing through the disciplines in succession, of which eighty are counted, ascending and descending, easier at first and then harder — fasting alone in a desert place, immersion in deep water for many days, throwing oneself into fire, prolonged exertion, and the like. One version gives a schedule: fasting about fifty days, then two days of extreme heat, then twenty days in snow, the severity increased by stages."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MithraicProbationes",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gestufte Proben durch Feuer, Kälte, Hunger und Durst",
        "en": "Graded ordeals of fire, cold, hunger and thirst"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Eighty grades, not seven. The number is irreconcilable with every other account and the source is late, derivative and hostile, so the graph holds it as what it is: a claim about this cult made centuries after it ended, by someone commenting on an invective. Two lexica repeat it in abbreviated form, which multiplies the copies and not the witnesses."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Nonnus the Mythographer, commentary on Gregory Nazianzen, Invectives against Julian (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The conduct the treatise describes for those with whom Mind is present: living piously, pure and merciful, winning the Father's love by pure lives, giving thanks and chanting hymns intent on him with ardent love, and turning from the body's sensations before giving it up to its proper death."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HermeticVitaPia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das fromme Leben unter der Gegenwart des Mind",
        "en": "The pious life under the presence of Mind"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Mead's Mind is here an agent and not a faculty: it is said to be present with the good, to become an aid, to close up the entrances as a door-keeper and to cut off the mental actions that base energies induce. What the practitioner does and what is done to him are not separated in the passage."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.22 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A lost renunciant transmission teaching a fixed destiny through which every being passes, known only through the reports of its opponents."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ajivika",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ājīvika-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Ājīvika transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as the limiting case: a tradition with no surviving corpus of its own. Whatever it yields will be an ascription by hostile witnesses and must be recorded as one."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted corpus; the doctrine is reported in the Buddhist and Jain canons, chiefly Sāmaññaphala Sutta and the Bhagavatī Sūtra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1927",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment of a fixed set of seven centres to spectral colours and to psychological qualities such as will, expression and security. An ordering imposed in the twentieth century on material that did not carry it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zuordnung von sieben Zentren zu Eigenschaften",
        "en": "The seven-centre quality mapping"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Colour scheme in Leadbeater 1927, rainbow spectrum in Hills 1977, on the textual basis made available by Avalon's 1919 translation of the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa. Recorded here so that the mapping is available in the graph as what it is, and cannot be mistaken for the tantric material it was built on."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "C. W. Leadbeater, extended by Christopher Hills and Ken Dychtwald",
      "pm:ordersClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:Cakra"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. Avalon, The Serpent Power (1919)",
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras (1927)",
        "Christopher Hills, Nuclear Evolution (1977)",
        "Ken Dychtwald, Bodymind (1977)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:XingqiYieldsCapacities",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zirkulation des Qi bringt jene Vermögen hervor",
        "en": "Circulating the qi yields those capacities"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:XingqiBaopuzi"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:XingqiCapacities"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Oration der Heilkunst bringt die Kenntnis von Tod oder Leben hervor",
        "en": "The oration of the physical art yields the knowledge of death or life"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the shortest way in the witness from an act to an outcome a third party can check. The outcome is binary, it arrives in days, it is recorded anyway, and the judgement can be written down before it arrives. The claim the tradition is known for, the sciences within a month, needs a measuring instrument that has first to be chosen; this one needs none."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioPhysicae"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaCognitioAegritudinis"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26-30a, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect announced for the breathing: the operator will see himself raised aloft and soaring towards the height, so that he seems to be in the midst of air; he will hear nothing, neither man nor beast, and will see nothing of the sights upon the earth in that same hour, but everything he sees will be immortal."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvSublatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Emporgehoben werden, nichts Sterbliches hören und sehen",
        "en": "Being raised aloft, hearing and seeing nothing mortal"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stated as what will happen and not as what may. The withdrawal of hearing and of earthly sight is given first and the vision second, which makes the sensory cut the condition rather than a side effect."
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UbbegaPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erhebungsverzückung",
        "en": "Uplifting rapture"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The statement that the light in the twelve aeons was many times greater than the light that had been on him in the world, and that the world would not be able to bear the light as it is in its truth, since otherwise the world and everything on it would be destroyed together."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaLuxIntolerabilis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Welt könnte das Licht nicht ertragen, wie es ist",
        "en": "The world could not bear the light as it is"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a caution and not as cosmology because of what it is used for: it explains why the manifestation was reduced, and it makes the reduction a mercy rather than a limit. A transmission that says its own highest state is unbearable below has stated a condition on its own transmissibility."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum"
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 14 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The disclosure of harmful operations: the striking of spirits and demons, the killing of the embryo in the womb, the serpent's bite and the stroke of the noonday heat."
      },
      "id": "pmc:StrikingOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schlagen der Geister und der Leibesfrucht",
        "en": "Striking of spirits and of the womb"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:12 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSightIsAbiding",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das zweite Gesicht bleibt",
        "en": "Second sight abides"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:abidingState"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A standing property of a person is not an episode. This is the placement that broke the older single axis: not attained and not passing either, which the two ends of that axis could not hold at once. See prima-materia#439."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The thing each speech names as already belonging to the speaker: the symbol of life borne in the character of a type at the seventh; his own beard as symbol at the second Iao; a faultless symbol preserved by the stamp of an image, and a body liberated by a pentad, at the fifth; purification with the spirit of a virgin at the third gate; the symbol of the mother at the second; and at the first, a symbol sculptured on the figure of the tree of life and formed in the likeness of innocence."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was der Sprechende ans Tor mitbringt",
        "en": "What the speaker brings to the gate"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That reading must not be pressed further than the wording bears, and the wording pulls both ways. Every one of the speeches ends with a request: let grace be with me, O father, let it be with me. A description of the speeches as assertion of identity rather than supplication holds for their body and not for their close, and a file that recorded only the first half would be selecting.",
          "The speeches are not petitions to be granted something. Each of them states a condition the speaker is already in and asks that it be seen: look upon me, seeing thou beholdest, bearing now mine own beard as symbol. Whether the ruler could withhold what is asked is a question the passage never raises."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The statement that carries a claim of independent attestation: when the testimony relied on was fixed or gathered, and against which publication or route that date stands."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:independenceGround",
      "label": {
        "de": "Grund der Unabhängigkeit",
        "en": "ground of independence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Required wherever pm:independentAttestation is set, enforced by pm:IndependenceGroundShape. The burden is reversed on purpose. Feedback from a published account back into the field it describes can almost never be demonstrated, so a predicate that marked only the demonstrated cases would let the ordinary case pass in silence — the failure pm:CoverageState and pm:ExaminationState exist to prevent. A claim of independence must instead carry the fact that makes it checkable.",
          "The date alone does not settle it, and the sentence must say more where more is at stake. Feedback within a single enquiry — few informants, several years, the same team returning, material gathered by an informant between visits — precedes any publication and no date catches it. Where the corpus was fixed that way, the ground states the gathering situation as well. Whether it does so honestly is settled at the merge and not by a shape; naming that limit is better than a guard that pretends to check it."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "comment": {
        "en": "Consciousness-first meta-ontology. Processes, not substances."
      },
      "id": "https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology",
      "license": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",
      "title": {
        "en": "prima-materia core ontology"
      },
      "type": "owl:Ontology"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The eastern European transmission of devekut as a continuous attachment attainable within ordinary life, and of contemplation as its means."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hasidism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chassidismus",
        "en": "Hasidism"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Toledot Yaakov Yosef; Keter Shem Tov; Shneur Zalman, Tanya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tummo bringt innere Hitze hervor",
        "en": "Tummo yields inner heat"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The one pairing in this file that names a capacity with an outward, measurable side. Any later protocol has a clearer target here than anywhere else in the collection."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Tummo"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InnerHeat"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When two noaidi wished to know which of them excelled the other in the art, they painted on a drum two reindeer striking each other with their horns, and he whose reindeer had the victory was the better."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wettstreit zweier Praktiker",
        "en": "The contest of two practitioners"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A procedure with a stated criterion, applied by practitioners to practitioners, with an outcome that could fall either way and that neither controls. Nothing else in this file, and very little in this graph, has that shape.",
          "What the source does not say is how the victory of a painted reindeer is determined — whether by the ring, by the movement of the drum, or otherwise. Without that the procedure cannot be reconstructed, only recorded, and no protocol node is written on it."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 477",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1902",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that every feeling has a colour, that the colour can be read off a body of subtle matter surrounding the person, and that the brightness of the colour measures the strength of the feeling."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lesung des Gefühls aus Farbe und Schwingung",
        "en": "The reading of feeling from colour and vibration"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Opened for prima-materia#361, on the suspicion that the modern one-dimensional ranking of emotions begins here. Read at the wording. The suspicion is only half borne out, and the half that fails is the more interesting one.",
          "What is here: a fixed key from feeling to colour, given as a list of the commonest hues; brightness and depth of colour stated outright as a measure of the strength and activity of the feeling; the vocabulary of vibration throughout; and an axis of self-reference applied to every colour alike, on which a thought with something of self in it turns downwards and one without turns upwards. The axis is the part that later scales inherit.",
          "What is not here, and the point of the node: a ranking of the feelings against one another. The plate is a grid of twenty-five fields, not a series, and the chapter introduces its colours as a list of the commonest ones. Where the text does rank, it ranks within a single colour by purity and luminosity, so that the same feeling has a lower and a higher form. That is a second dimension and not a scale. A later list that puts unlike feelings in one numbered order therefore does not have it from here, and prima-materia#12 cannot be answered by pointing at this node."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "An ancient science of the aura, reported as eastern in origin and as the common property of the older traditions.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "C. W. Leadbeater; Annie Besant",
      "source": "C. W. Leadbeater, Man Visible and Invisible (London: Theosophical Publishing Society, 1902), the frontispiece plate and the chapter Colours and their Meaning, pp. 81-82",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The assignment is made at birth and not chosen later: the child is dedicated or subjected to the animal at the time it is born, on the strength of an agreement the recorder attributes to the parents."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NahualliGivenAtBirth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Tier wird bei der Geburt zugeteilt",
        "en": "The animal is assigned when the child is born"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder frames the agreement as a pact with the devil, which is his own theology and not the tradition's account of itself. What is left when that frame is subtracted is the timing — at birth — and the fact that someone else contracts it for the child. Both are entered; the frame is not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prohibition the teacher justifies in a sentence that turns on the word itself: if one indulged in sugar cane his work as a practitioner would amount to nothing, aohe e ko ana kana mau hana, his work will not be accomplished. The recorder adds a practical ground beside it, that chewing the stalk roughens the voice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HawaiianKoCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuckerrohr ist der Hula-Schar verboten",
        "en": "Sugar cane is forbidden to the hula company"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two grounds for one rule, and they are of different kinds: one is a pun and the other is a physical effect on a singer's instrument. The transmission gives both without ranking them. Where a rule carries a homophonic justification, the graph should not read the justification as the reason the rule exists."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Ailolo"
      },
      "source": "Nathaniel B. Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii: The Sacred Songs of the Hula, Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 38 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1909), chapter IV, p. 30",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it: it prescribes its means without placing them for or against the norm of the order it stands in."
      },
      "id": "pm:methodNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung der Methode nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Method orientation not stated"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The counterpart of pm:normDistinctionDeclined from the other side, and required on every subject axis by SPEC §17. Without it a source that was asked and stayed silent is indistinguishable from a node nobody has reached, and any query of the form 'only conforming methods' reports the state of our reading instead of the state of the traditions. Silence is common here and is worth recording: a great many operative texts simply do not raise the question."
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The offices are named separately because they are separate. The one who speaks with the huaca, keeps it, carries the offerings, sets the fasts and answers the people in its name; the ones who do the same for the ancestor bodies, for the lightning and for the sun; the lesser minister who assists each of them; the one who cures; the one who hears confession; the one who makes the chicha; and a series of diviners named by their instruments — by heaps of maize counted odd or even, by the twitching of the upper arms, by the legs of a large spider broken one by one, by dreams, and by opening a guinea pig with the thumbnail and reading where the blood runs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GradedSpecialists",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die gestuften Spezialisten",
        "en": "The graded specialists"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Every office in it is held by women as well as men, the confessing included, on the recorder's express statement; he adds that the principal offices were more often men's and the lesser ones, divining and making the chicha, more often women's.",
          "The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen names four classes with Quechua terms and the extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one names two of the same as its general terms. Two recorders of opposite standing therefore agree on the vocabulary while disagreeing about almost everything else, which is the strongest thing that can be said for this list."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 278 [280], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition, attached to a procedure and not to a formula, that the act be carried out without being called at and without the doing being spoken of. It is set on cutting a sod, on knotting twigs, on hanging a written letter around the neck, on passing water into a vessel and on going to a wagon, that is on acts of every kind, and never on the words themselves."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unbeschrauen",
        "en": "Unbeschrauen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The eighteen fifty translation renders the term as unbeshrewedly, a coinage with no currency in English, which is why the identifier keeps the German word of the printing that carries it."
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 14, 27, 28, 29, 53, 83",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 17, 18, 32"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Among the Reindeer Tungus of Transbaikalia the decisive moment of the trial: the candidate, in the state, names particular marks of the animals which the spirits will send for the making of his costume — a white spot on a leg, white hoofs, a white head, a mutilated ear. A clansman then hunts for animals so marked. The search may take several months. If the animals named cannot be found, the spirit may be asked to send another animal instead."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusMarkedAnimals",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die an ihren Zeichen benannten Tiere",
        "en": "The animals named by their marks"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A prediction with a stated content, made before witnesses, checkable by anyone who brings back the animal. It is the most examinable claim in this file, and it comes with its own escape clause in the same sentence.",
          "The recorder closes the passage with four words that are the whole finding: the needed animals are always found. He gives no count and reports no failure."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 130 I, p. 351",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacities of the third chapter are named obstacles with respect to samādhi and attainments only with respect to the outward-turned state. The tradition that catalogues them is the same one that warns against them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kräfte sind Hindernisse für samādhi",
        "en": "The powers are obstacles to samādhi"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A sign set up before a journey from which the traveller may not return. Each of the two plants a cane in the middle of the house and states both readings in advance: if it dries, that is the sign of their death; if it sprouts again, they are living. The grandmother who stays behind is told what to say in each case."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MayaAjSign",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das in der Mitte des Hauses gepflanzte Rohr",
        "en": "The cane planted in the middle of the house"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Both branches are named before the outcome, the reader is a third party who was not present at the event, and the sign is not the thing it reports. That combination is rare in this collection: most signs a tradition names are read by the practitioner himself, during the practice, after being told what to expect."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Aj"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 7",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The songs belonging to a rite, held by the priest of the rite and his assistants, containing expressions that are obsolete or without current meaning and that have therefore to be carried by memory rather than by sense."
      },
      "id": "pmp:NavajoSacredSong",
      "label": {
        "de": "Navajo-Kultgesang",
        "en": "Navajo sacred song"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of imposing an order on material that did not carry it: a list, a sequence, a correspondence table. Named, dated, and attributed to the compiler who performed it, never to the source that is being ordered."
      },
      "id": "pm:Systematizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Systematisieren",
        "en": "Systematizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the three parts, the contemplation of created things."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Physike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Physikē",
        "en": "Contemplation of nature"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How the cited passage was taken down: who spoke, or that the speaker is expressly withheld; who recorded; when and where; in which language; and whether the wording in the language of the telling is carried in the work or only a translation."
      },
      "id": "pm:fieldRecord",
      "label": {
        "de": "Aufnahmelage",
        "en": "field record"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It names the gathering of this passage, not the standing of the corpus. The situation of a whole ethnographic tradition — span of the fieldwork against the year of publication, how many informants, whether the same people were revisited, whether material was delivered between visits, whether the recorder stood in a milieu already expecting what he found — belongs as a skos:note on the pm:Tradition instance. See prima-materia#260. The two are needed together: one says how far this sentence stands from its speaker, the other how far the corpus stands from the transmission.",
          "Required wherever pm:attestedBy is pm:fieldAttestation, enforced by pm:FieldAttestationShape. Prose and not a scale, for the reason pm:independenceGround is prose: there is no threshold at which a gathering becomes sound, and a scale would counterfeit a check. What the sentence is worth is decided by whoever reads it."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether a sender's own affective state passes to a recipient in the absence of a deliberately chosen one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UnchosenSendingProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der ungewählte Zustand übergeht",
        "en": "Whether the unchosen state transmits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Affect transfer between co-present people remains widely reported, but the mechanism the contagion literature proposes for it is not secure. The canonical test of the facial feedback step returned a rating difference of 0.03 units, confidence interval -0.11 to 0.16, across seventeen laboratories and 1,894 participants, where the original had reported 0.82. A claim that the state passes without either party choosing it therefore stands without its stated mechanism.",
          "The consequence for the draft, and it is not a refutation. What the claim describes occurs; it occurs through a channel that requires nothing the section posits. A practitioner who finds his own state arriving in the other person has not thereby found evidence for the transfer the section is about — he has found the effect that would be there anyway. The claim is confirmed and its confirmation supports nothing beyond itself.",
          "The earlier sentence here, that the states a person is not presenting are the ones that leak most reliably, is retracted. It reversed the meta-analytic finding.",
          "The leakage evidence is withdrawn. Ekman and Friesen 1969 is a theoretical paper with three illustrative analyses of films of forty female psychiatric inpatients, of whom deception could be certified for three interviews; the authors state that the studies do not directly test their formulation, and that the leakage they describe should be relatively easy to eliminate once a person is aware of it. Its channel prediction is not borne out either: over independent estimates numbering twenty-nine, twenty-eight, eighteen and thirty-two, hand movements stand at d = 0.00, foot and leg movements at d = -0.09, self-fidgeting at d = -0.01 and eye contact at d = 0.01. The body areas named as the reliable leakage sites sit at zero; what discriminates is verbal and vocal, and it is faint.",
          "The narrower claim that would carry the section is not examined here and is not touched by this node: that the unchosen state passes where the ordinary channels are closed, out of sight and out of earshot. That is a different protocol, it has no cases, and it must not borrow the confirmation recorded above.",
          "Two claims were run together here and are now separated. Detectability is the claim that an observer can read a state the sender is not presenting. Transmission is the claim that the recipient comes to be in that state. The evidence entered for the first was cited for the second, which is the same error as at the Kox case in prima-materia#383: the work was named correctly and stood for something it does not say."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Carried 2026-09-03. Searched: the meta-analytic record on behavioural cues to deception, the accuracy of lie-truth judgments, and the replication status of the mimicry-and-feedback mechanism, in each case for work opposing the claim rather than supporting it. Found: the cue meta-analysis reports that many behaviours have no discernible or only weak links to deceit, with the hand, foot, fidgeting and gaze channels at or near zero; and the multi-laboratory replication of the facial feedback step recovered no effect. Both were read at the passage. Not yet opened: Hatfield, Cacioppo and Rapson 1994, which is carried in prima-materia as an unread citation, and the judgment-accuracy meta-analysis of Bond and DePaulo 2006, seen only in abstract."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "B. M. DePaulo, J. J. Lindsay, B. E. Malone, L. Muhlenbruck, K. Charlton and H. Cooper, Cues to deception, Psychological Bulletin 129 (2003), no. 1, pp. 74-118",
        "E.-J. Wagenmakers et al., Registered Replication Report: Strack, Martin, & Stepper (1988), Perspectives on Psychological Science 11 (2016), no. 6, pp. 917-928"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off the contagion literature and the work on involuntary nonverbal disclosure",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Recipients in the presence of a sender in an unchosen state show no shift toward that state against a neutral condition."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-03",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:UnchosenSendingCarriesTheActualState"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The songs attached to a particular imparted potency, learned with it and sung when a request is made of it. The corpus states that a shaman preparing a fetish devises a formula which must be repeated to invoke its powers, that a medicine man knows the songs of his medicines and that those songs are his potency, and that a stone transferred from one man to another was transferred together with the song that was to be sung whenever a request was made of it."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SingingTheSongsOfAPotency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Singen der Lieder, die zu einer Potenz gehören",
        "en": "Singing the songs that belong to a potency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 211",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 88 and pp. 158-159"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of terrestrial import from the position of the heavenly bodies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Astrology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Astrologie",
        "en": "Astrology"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The report Porphyry takes from Eubulus: that Zoroaster first consecrated to Mithras a naturally formed cave in the mountains of Persia, flowering and with springs, as a likeness of the world Mithras had made; and that the things placed in the cave were arranged at commensurate intervals, so as to be symbols of the elements and regions of that world. Wherever the god was known, Porphyry adds, he was approached in a cave, and the initiate of the Persians is admitted in a place they call a cavern, which signifies the descent of the soul into the sublunary and its return."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicSpelaeumImagoMundi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Höhle ist ein Abbild der Welt, und ihre Ausstattung sind deren Gegenden",
        "en": "The cave is an image of the world, and its furnishings are its regions"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chain of hands is three long and the middle link is lost. Porphyry reports Eubulus, whose work on Mithras does not survive; Eubulus reports the Persians. The graph holds the outermost link only, and every statement here about what Zoroaster did is at that distance.",
          "This is the only statement in the graph that a ritual space is laid out to scale. It does not merely say that the cave means the world; it says the interval between two things in it corresponds to the interval between two things in the world. A claim of that shape is checkable against a surviving sanctuary in a way that an allegory is not, and the check has not been made here — the seven semicircles of the mithraeum at Ostia are the obvious place to make it, and they are unread. See prima-materia#642."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the cave consecrated by Zoroaster (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 2 of that edition's division)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that the raising of the body hair is codified as a sign of an inner state in two Indian genres that have no common subject matter: a meditation handbook and a treatise on dramatic performance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HorripilationCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Haaraufrichtung",
        "en": "The horripilation correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The strongest item in this file, and still not what it first appears. The independence is generic and not cultural: both texts belong to the Indian sphere, so a common background cannot be excluded. What can be excluded is that one borrowed the observation from the other, since neither is about the other's subject. A universal physiology codified twice is a finding about the physiology."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:KhuddikaPiti"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Romanca"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "Here the ground is not a date and the node says so rather than borrowing an argument it does not have. The commentary is of the fifth century of the common era and the treatise on performance is older, so priority runs the wrong way for an argument from age. What carries the independence is that neither text treats the other's subject: a handbook of meditation had no occasion to take an observation about gooseflesh from a manual for actors, or the reverse. Both are received texts, so no gathering situation is at issue. A common Indian background remains available and is not excluded by this ground.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:Theravada"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Both codifications arise within the Indian cultural sphere; independence holds at the level of genre and subject matter, not at the level of culture.",
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Nāṭyaśāstra VII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the balance, of elixirs and of the generation of life by art, carried under the name of Jābir by a corpus larger than one man."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ArabicAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Arabische Alchemie",
        "en": "Arabic alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Jābirian corpus; al-Rāzī, Sirr al-Asrār",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has the condition pass of itself, so that it must be entered again rather than merely kept."
      },
      "id": "pm:transientState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vergehend",
        "en": "Transient"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A capacity said to come and go is a different object of examination from one said to last: it is tested against frequency and occasion, not against a course of training."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateDuration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A procedure used to decide whether a sick person will live: the meat fork is set up in the cooking pot, and the way it comes to rest is read as the answer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitMeatForkDivination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrsagung mit der Fleischgabel",
        "en": "Divination with the meat fork"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sacred shell of the society, said in the origin account to have been shot into the body of the first recipient so that he might have immortality and be able to confer the secrets on his kinsmen."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Migis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mī'gis",
        "en": "Mī'gis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The shell is not a token of membership but the vehicle of the transfer: the account makes the entry of the shell into the body the act by which the capacity passes, and the same act is repeated at every degree."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 167",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of a figure is reported to follow an established rule, and the rule is reported to be known only to those initiated into it. The fee paid to a priest for being taught the art is reported as very heavy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaRuleOfInterpretationClosed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Deutungsregel ist fest und verschlossen",
        "en": "The rule of interpretation is fixed and closed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two gates named in one sentence, and they are of different kinds. That the rule is fixed is a claim about the system and is checkable against any later account of it; that it is known only to the initiated is a claim about access, and the fee gives its price. The account is an outsider's and reports what he was not told, which is a weaker witness for the content of the rule than for the fact of its closure."
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission holds both at once: that the objects the witch-doctors draw from the bodies of their patients are produced by sleight of hand, with the manner of the deception described in detail by laymen, and that some of them nevertheless work real cures. Scepticism is directed at particular practitioners and turned into an explanation of their failures, so that it supports the standing of the rest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FraudKnownAndBeliefHeld",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Betrug ist bekannt und der Glaube bleibt",
        "en": "The fraud is known and the belief is held"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A layman's account taken down by the recorder explains not only how the trick is done but how a cured man comes to believe he was cured by it. The doubt is stated in the terms of the transmission itself: a witch-doctor is a cheat because his medicines are poor and a liar because he has no witchcraft in him.",
          "This is the passage this graph most needs from the tradition, and it cuts against the usual report of it. What is described is not credulity but a transmission that has a settled place for its own failures, states its scepticism openly, and is not thereby weakened. Whether that makes it closed against refutation is the whole later argument, and none of that argument was read here."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 105-107",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The procedure prescribed against the ills foretold for a year whose bearer is Ix. An image of Kinch-ahau Itzamna is set in the temple, incense and many offerings and prayers are made to it, blood is drawn to anoint the stone called Sac-acantun, there is much dancing in which old women take part, and a new oratory is built for the god or the old one renewed."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Abwehr der für ein Ix-Jahr geweissagten Übel",
        "en": "The conjuring of the evils foretold for an Ix year"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The ills the procedure answers are named in the same section and are of two kinds. Some are conditions of the world: shortage of water, hot spells withering the maize, hunger and the theft and enslavement that follow, locusts, a bad year for bread and a good one for cotton. Others are conditions of the polity: discords within the town or with other towns, changes of rule through war, and the failure of men seeking a chieftaincy. A remedy addressed to both at once is not falsified by either."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Spirits belonging to a particular shaman, called up by him and reported to act in the world on his behalf: to guard a child, to raise a barrier against the dead, to find what has been lost."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitHelpingSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hilfsgeister",
        "en": "Helping spirits"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prohibition inherited in a family, with a penalty stated in advance and matched in kind to the thing forbidden: hippopotamus flesh and elephantiasis, a fish with opal eyes and diseased eyes, the great bull frog and bulging eyes, spotted animals and a spotted skin disease. A parent who keeps the prohibition is said to bless his children with immunity from that disease and one who breaks it to curse them with liability to it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Mpangu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mpangu, das ererbte Verbot",
        "en": "Mpangu, the inherited prohibition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corpus records its own counter-witness at the same place, and the node exists as much for that as for the rule. Some people told the recorder there are no such prohibitions in their families; and a well-informed man told him that anyone could eat what his inherited prohibition forbade without evil consequence, provided he ate it fearlessly and did not boast of it afterwards. That is a stated condition of failure for the prohibition itself, and it is stated by a member of the transmission.",
          "The same corpus notes that the animals under prohibition are not treated as omens and are not respected: they are killed like any others and only not eaten."
        ]
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 245-246",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where human ingenuity fails to establish the truth of a charge, the decision is transferred to the gods. The commonest ordeal among these tribes is stated to be the drinking of a decoction of odum wood."
      },
      "id": "pmp:OrdealByOdumDecoction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ordal mit dem Sud aus Odum-Holz",
        "en": "The ordeal by a decoction of odum wood"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered although the source gives no reading rule, because the absence is itself informative: the graph now holds a poison ordeal from the Lower Congo with twenty-seven doses and a stated verdict rule, and here the same institution is named in one sentence by a witness who describes much else in detail. What is missing is the rule, not the practice."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Ewe informants of the western and eastern tribes, unnamed; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, from his own residence and from earlier printed reports he names as he uses them, published 1890; given in Ewe, of which the work prints the terms with etymologies, the accounts in English.",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the three parts of the way, consisting in the keeping of the commandments and the labour against the passions."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Praktike",
      "label": {
        "de": "Praktikē",
        "en": "Ascetic practice"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol against the claim carried by the movement of about eighteen hundred and seventy-two, that the destruction of every charged thing would end sickness and death."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KiyokaProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokoll: das Versprechen des allgemeinen Verbrennens",
        "en": "Protocol: the promise of the general burning"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The case is the movement itself and is entered as one case and not as a count. What the source records is that the deaths went on as before and that the people themselves drew that conclusion; it gives no number, no period and no comparison, so the deviation is qualitative and rests on the summary of one resident observer.",
          "The response to the failure is the part worth keeping. The claim was not withdrawn: the failure was assigned to hidden charms, an unobservable condition that can absorb any outcome, and the theory was left standing. That is the same move the transmission makes at the level of a single charged thing in pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower, carried out at the scale of a country."
        ]
      },
      "pm:caseCount": 1,
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundNothing"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Searched for a second and independent record of the outcome of the kiyoka movement in the other works opened in this run: the term does not occur in Weeks 1914 or in Dennett 1906; it occurs once in Weeks, Folk-Lore vol. XX, pp. 57-58, but there as a quotation from the same compiler's dictionary appendix of 1895, pp. 848-849, and therefore not as a second witness. Nothing was found that reports the movement as having succeeded, and nothing that gives a count of deaths on either side."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia compiler, from the wording of Bentley 1900, vol. I, pp. 290-292, with the search stated in the counter-search note",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A district in which the general destruction of charged things was carried out and sickness and death did not cease."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-08",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Kiyoka"
      },
      "source": "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 290-292",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of heka as an operative power held by gods, kings and the transfigured dead, carried in mortuary and temple literature."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EgyptianRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pharaonisch-ägyptisches Ritual",
        "en": "Pharaonic Egyptian ritual"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transformation spells are a capacity list in the strict sense: each states what the speaker becomes and what that state permits."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Pyramid Texts; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead; Papyrus Westcar; the demotic magical papyrus of London and Leiden",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Jung's transmission of an objective psyche and of active imagination as a procedure for engaging figures encountered in it, with a record kept of the engagement."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AnalyticalPsychology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Analytische Psychologie",
        "en": "Analytical psychology"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered as a tradition and not as science on the rule stated at the head of this file: its central claims rest on testimony, including the author's own record of his experiment, and not on procedures that could have come out otherwise. Where it does produce evidence, that evidence enters at an examination node like any other."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Jung, Liber Novus; Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos; Jung, Mysterium Coniunctionis",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether contact through the palms differs in its reported or measured effect from contact through another part."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SendingOrganProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der berührende Körperteil einen Unterschied macht",
        "en": "Whether the part in contact makes a difference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Procedure without cases, and for the same reason as at pmc:HandSideTransferProtocol: the comparison needs no instrument the field lacks, and nobody has made it. What is absent is the case, not the means.",
          "The controls that would have to hold before a difference could be read as a property of the part: which part the practitioner was taught to use, which part the recipient expects, and the difference in ordinary touch between a palm and a forearm. A protocol without them would have measured the training and the expectation."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The falsifying condition is the ordinary comparison the claim invites by naming one part and excluding the rest",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Recipients blinded to the part in contact report or show no difference between contact through the palm and contact through another part beyond chance."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:PalmsAreTheSendingOrgan"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act, within a tradition, of naming an addressable being: the name as a received work gives it, together with what that work takes the bearer to be."
      },
      "id": "pm:Naming",
      "label": {
        "de": "Benennen",
        "en": "Naming"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Not a substance class in disguise. What is held is the act of naming and what a transmission says under that name, not a being asserted to exist. The graph reports that a work names Asael and calls him a watcher; it asserts no watcher.",
          "Three things must not collapse into one, and this class holds only the first two. The bearer and the name form belong here. The operative claim — that invoking under this name does something — stays a pm:Yielding whose practice is the invocation, because that claim needs its own falsifying condition and its own protocol.",
          "Variants between recensions are not smoothed into a lead form. Where two editions give different names for what a reader takes to be one bearer, that is a pm:Disputing node, by the same rule SPEC §10 sets for diverging text: an averaged node reports a witness nobody gives.",
          "pm:ascribedTo deliberately keeps no rdfs:range. Where the bearer is a named being it points at an instance of this class; where it is humanity before a stated event, or a class of beings the source does not name, it points elsewhere, and a range would have forced those cases out of the record or into a name nobody gave them."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has the awareness, or a body made of mind, leave the place of the physical body and act elsewhere."
      },
      "id": "pm:displacedLocus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verlagert",
        "en": "Displaced"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The holdings already gather the material for this value across traditions; see convergences/stream.ttl and examinations/displacement.ttl."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessLocus",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A child whose dreams differ from ordinary ones, who is subject to strong emotions and changes of mood and whose behaviour is unlike that of other children is taken for a candidate, especially where there were shamans in the direct ancestral line. Such a child is told of the possibility and gradually prepared. Between sixteen and twenty the first entering of the spirit occurs. Before it the youth refuses food, wanders about without purpose, sleeps too long or cannot sleep; after two weeks or more he jumps up on the bed during the night and begins to sing as the shamans do. If a real state follows, he is considered a future shaman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusCandidateCondition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Zustand des Anwärters",
        "en": "The condition of the candidate"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The stated boundaries of the age are two and they differ in kind. That nobody becomes a shaman before fifteen is reported as an absence in the record the author gathered; that candidates are exceptionally rare after full maturity is reported with a reason attached, namely that whoever must look after a family has no leisure for it.",
          "The transmission announces the signs to the candidate in advance and then reads them off him. The recorder says of one case that the woman acted according to the candidates' model — she trembled, ran away, climbed a tree, refused food, forgot everything — and of another candidate that the entering of the spirit was seemingly a ritualistic performance. These are signs of success in the strict sense of SPEC §15, told beforehand and reported afterwards, and they are held apart from the conditions of failure in this file for that reason."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, pp. 346-350",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what the sixteen figures of the oracle are. One account has them as the base figures of the system itself, called mothers, from whose columns every further figure is compounded, and gives the practice no derivation from outside Yorubaland. The other, written by a Sierra Leonean author and reprinted in the same appendix as the bishop's extract, counts the sixteen as twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, one judge and a further judge got by combining the judge with the figure of the first house, and calls the operations of the diviners geomantic throughout."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaGeomanticIdentificationDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die sechzehn Figuren der Apparat der Geomantie sind",
        "en": "Whether the sixteen figures are the apparatus of geomancy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Neither side of this was read in a modern study at the wording in this run. What the twentieth-century literature makes of the question — whether the figures were taken over and grafted on an older practice, whether the sand science itself has North African roots, and how either is dated — is untouched here and is the first thing the next run on this tradition should open.",
          "The second account is the reason pm:contactRoute on this tradition stands at disputed. Twelve houses, two witnesses and a judge are the working parts of the Arabic sand science and of the European geomancy descended from it, and a system that has them is not without a route to the Mediterranean and Islamic material this graph already holds. The first account describes the same casting and the same sixteen without any of those parts.",
          "The second account was read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints it, and carries no imprint there. It is entered as a side of a dispute and not as a finding about the history of the system."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:IfaSixteenMothers"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
        "J. A. Abayomi Cole, Astrological Geomancy in Africa, the account of the sixteen palm nuts, read in R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The kind of question a scale answers. Three families are distinguished because a statement about the state of the record, a statement about the firmness of a source and a statement about the subject matter must never be read alike."
      },
      "id": "pm:ScaleFamily",
      "label": {
        "de": "Skalenfamilie",
        "en": "Scale Family"
      },
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sila",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sittlichkeit",
        "en": "Virtue"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The graded effect the text attaches to the number of repetitions: said once by a man troubled or involved in a great business, eloquence shall be added to him as much as is needed; said twice, great eloquence shall be given to him; said three times by a man who desires to be made eloquent, he shall be made eloquent."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaGradatioFacundiae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nach der Zahl der Wiederholungen zugemessene Beredsamkeit",
        "en": "Eloquence measured out by the count of repetitions"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A dose and its effect, which the corpus this graph holds gives almost nowhere else. The interest is not the promise but the monotony: more of the same act is said to produce more of the same effect, and a monotony can be examined without the underlying claim being settled first.",
          "The order in the passage does not rise. Three times stands for the man who wishes to become eloquent, once and twice for the man under pressure, so the text may be setting out three separate applications rather than one scale. Which of the two it is decides whether there is a dose-response claim here at all, and the passage read does not decide it; the Latin beside it is no clearer."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A structural correspondence observed across traditions, held as a node so that it can carry its routes of transmission and its counterexamples instead of asserting universality."
      },
      "id": "pm:Converging",
      "label": {
        "de": "Konvergieren",
        "en": "Converging"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Every instance is an ordering made by a compiler and carries pm:compilerInference, never textual attestation. No source says that the concepts correspond; the correspondence is the reading, and it is held as a node so that it can be argued with. Its sources are the sources of the concepts it brings together, and they attest those concepts and not their correspondence.",
          "Independence is also a weaker instrument than it looks, and this is the reason breadth is held beside it rather than under it. It guards against none of the three commonest sources of agreement: a shared physiology that produces the same experience wherever the same thing is done to a body, a structural necessity of the subject matter that makes any competent version converge, and the form of graded institutions, which resemble one another because institutions with grades do. What decides in the end is what can be reproduced, and independence is a way of sorting candidates for that, not a verdict.",
          "Most apparent universality in the Western esoteric record is diffusion from few sources. A graph that counts a citation chain as independent confirmation measures reception history and reports it as structure.",
          "That is a warning against one way of counting and not against reception itself. Reception history is a finding of its own: how many transmissions carry a claim at all, and how deeply the paths between them branch, are two separate numbers and neither cancels the other. Many witnesses on one cable is a different map from many witnesses on three, and both are more informative than the earlier arrangement, in which the first case was simply not shown. The one thing the graph must be able to do is say which of the two maps a reader is looking at: dense transmission and strong effect must never look alike, or the gate of authority returns in statistical dress."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā of Svātmārāma and the manuals around it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hathayoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Haṭhayoga",
        "en": "Haṭhayoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Every verse cited in this file was read in the Sanskrit of the widely circulated vulgate, in the recension that the 1915 edition and translation of Sinh carries, and each citation now says so. The critical edition drawn from the manuscript tradition numbers differently in places; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute holds what is at issue."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that this self has four fourths, enumerated in order: the waking state, outwardly cognitive; the dreaming state, inwardly cognitive; the deep-sleep state, in which the sleeper desires no desire and sees no dream; and the fourth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourfoldSelf",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Selbst in vier Vierteln",
        "en": "The self in four quarters"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The three named states are given by what the sleeper or waker cognizes, which is the same criterion the sensory-anchoring axis uses and the reason three of its values can be recognised in them. The fourth is given differently, by negation, and that difference is the whole point of the passage."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 2-6, in Robert Ernest Hume (trans.), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads (London: Humphrey Milford, Oxford University Press, 1921), pp. 391-392",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Upanisadic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that several traditions mark the passage between sleeping and waking as the place where a communication is received, while none of them makes it the place where a faculty is exercised."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThresholdReceptionCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schwelle als Ort des Empfangs",
        "en": "The threshold as a place of reception"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two axes have to be held apart, and the modern draft that prompted this examination runs them together. On the reception axis the threshold is well attested: a voice, a light and an immobility of the eyes in the Neoplatonic account, the nightly ascent of the soul in the Kabbalistic one, the true dream in the Islamic one. On the execution axis, where sight, hearing and the mind-made body are actively exercised, the sources bind the capacities to waking absorption instead. No source found so far puts an actively exercised faculty in the threshold state."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "The correspondence must not be counted as independent attestation. The Tibetan material reaches Tibet from the Indian mahāsiddha Nāropa through Marpa and is reception, as the file that holds it records. The Mediterranean witnesses are worse still for this purpose: the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory rests on a Neoplatonic substrate, so an agreement between the Greek, the Jewish and the Islamic account is a shared inheritance and not three independent observations. What would count as a finding is a tradition outside both blocks, and none has been located.",
      "source": [
        "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShemihazahTeachesSpellCasting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa offenbart die Beschwörung",
        "en": "Shemihazah discloses spell-casting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SpellCasting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Shemihazah"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission holding that awakening is sudden and that the mind is not to be polished but recognised."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Chan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chan",
        "en": "Chan"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Platform Sūtra; Linji lu; Biyan lu",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of household charms, blessings and remedies in printed handbooks passed from hand to hand, in which the words are given together with the conditions of time, silence and material under which they are to be used."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Europäische Volksmagie",
        "en": "European folk magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read for the first time in two printings of one handbook and in the booklet that stands behind it: Hohman's Der lange verborgene Freund in the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, its Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty, and the Romanus-Büchlein first dated to Glatz seventeen eighty-eight. The reading was directed by term through the full text of both Hohman printings and not carried page by page; the pharmacological recipes, which state no condition and claim no capacity, were passed over. The remaining works named in the principal corpus were not opened.",
          "The two Hohman printings are held apart deliberately. They differ at two places where the difference falls on the examinable part of a claim, and both differences stand in this file as their own nodes rather than being smoothed into one reading."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Merseburg charms; Lacnunga; Bald's Leechbook; Romanusbüchlein; Sechstes und Siebtes Buch Mosis; Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund",
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa.: gedruckt für den Verfasser, 1820)",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), the literal English translation of the preceding"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A transmission resting on one man's sustained reports of conversation with the dead and with angels, kept as a dated journal over decades."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Swedenborgian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Swedenborgische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Swedenborgian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Quantitatively the largest body of first-person reports available to the project, and dated throughout, which makes internal consistency checkable in a way most such material is not."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Swedenborg, De caelo et inferno; Swedenborg, Diarium spirituale",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The node carrying what this station added and the exemplar does not have. Required: an addition that cannot be pointed at is an assertion about a chain and not a link in it."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:addedElement",
      "label": {
        "de": "hinzugefügtes Element",
        "en": "added element"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DrumMustBeInherited",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Trommel muss ererbt sein",
        "en": "The drum must be inherited"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drums were not thought sufficiently fit for their uses unless got by inheritance, and does not say what follows from using one that was not. The rule sits oddly beside the same recorder's report of a practitioner explaining how his own drum was made, and the oddity is left standing rather than resolved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SunCourseRule"
      },
      "source": "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 474",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The initiatory act by which the descent of power is conferred."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SaktipataTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übertragung der Herabkunft der Kraft",
        "en": "Conferring the descent of power"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrasāra XI",
        "Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka XIII"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1949",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that all known ways to immortality fall into three — that of the fakir working on the body, that of the monk on the emotions, that of the yogi on the mind — each long, hard and one-sided; and that a fourth works on all three at once, requires no withdrawal from life, and reaches the same goal sooner for that reason."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourWaysAtConstantGoal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vier Wege zu einem Ziel, unterschieden danach, wie viel am Menschen sie bearbeiten",
        "en": "Four ways to one goal, distinguished by how much of a person they work on"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Recorded chiefly as a negative finding. The fourth way is regularly listed among the third options beside a right-hand and a left-hand path, and the source will not carry that. All four ways are said to lead in one direction, and the direction is named: immortality. What separates them is how much of a person is worked on and whether life must be left. That is an axis of completeness, not of the direction of the will, and it is compatible with either end of the other one. See prima-materia#65.",
          "The case is more useful than a mere correction, because it is a whole system in which the goal is held fixed and the entire classification falls on the means. Whoever doubts that method and goal can be varied independently has here a taxonomy that varies only one of them. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67.",
          "The goal as named — an immortality that the person acquires and is not born with — sits on the separative side of the telic axis rather than the unitive one. That placement is not asserted on this node: it would need the passages on the four bodies read as well, and those have not been read here."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "Fragments of an unknown and older teaching, as the book's own subtitle has it, rather than a doctrine set out in talks of the early twentieth century and published in 1949.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky",
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the chapter on the ways to immortality and the fourth way",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The later step: the practice, state or capacity said not to be reached without the prior one."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:dependentStep",
      "label": {
        "de": "abhängiger Schritt",
        "en": "dependent step"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who led Eve astray and disclosed the weapons of death."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Gadreel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gadreel",
        "en": "Gadreel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:6 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account repeated at every stage of the ascent: the gates open of themselves, the rulers are shaken by the great light, they look at the light-garment and see on it the mystery that holds their names, they are struck with fear, all their bonds loose, each leaves his own order, and they fall down and worship, saying that the Lord of the All has passed through them without their knowing it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaTransitusVisu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Herrscher sehen ihren eigenen Namen, und ihre Bande lösen sich",
        "en": "The rulers see their own name and their bonds fall away"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The sequence is stated four times with the same wording, at the firmament, the first sphere, fate and the twelve aeons, with the light increasing forty-ninefold at each step. A repetition that exact is a form, and the form says that passage here is recognition and not negotiation.",
          "This is the same event as an ophite gate and its opposite in method. There the passer addresses the ruler by name and office and asks to pass; here nothing is said at all. The ascending one is not even seen — the sources say the disciples and the rulers saw only the light — and what releases the bonds is the ruler reading his own name on someone else's clothing."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 11-14 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about whether the eight limbs form a sequence that must be climbed. The sūtra that names them enumerates and says nothing about order; the commentarial layer introduces the categories of outer and inner limb, and later teaching hardens these into a rule of practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LimbOrderDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Glieder der Reihe nach zu durchlaufen sind",
        "en": "Whether the limbs must be traversed in order"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The disagreement is not between two traditions but between a base text and its readers, which is the commonest shape a dispute takes in this collection and the easiest to lose, because the readers usually win by default."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:AstangaLimbs"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.29",
        "Vyāsa, Yogabhāṣya on Yogasūtra II.29"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A mixture of air and fire that pervades bodies and, by its tension, holds them in cohesion; a principle of the world rather than a capacity of a practitioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Pneuma",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pneuma",
        "en": "Pneuma"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The associated doctrines are that of tension and that of total mixture, by which two bodies occupy the same place throughout."
      },
      "source": "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:StoicPhysics"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Every death is held to be caused by witchcraft and to be avenged by lethal magic, and the death of the witch so struck is in turn avenged by his own kin. The transmission keeps the names of those killed by avenging magic secret between the kin and the prince, and the source states that were the names known the whole procedure would stand exposed as futile. Some Azande are reported as doubting the honesty of the princes who hold the oracles, and a few as having seen that the system is fallacious."
      },
      "id": "pmc:VengeanceSystemVeiledBySilence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Racheverfahren trägt nur, solange die Opfer ungenannt bleiben",
        "en": "The vengeance system holds only while the victims are unnamed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The defence offered when the recorder put the objection is recorded with it: a prince whose oracle has declared that one man died of another's vengeance-magic will refuse to put the second death to his oracle at all, so that the chain is cut by the authority that holds the instrument.",
          "The strongest self-warning in this file, and one reported as spoken rather than inferred. It names the exact condition under which the transmission's own central procedure collapses, and names the arrangement by which that condition is kept from arising."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 6",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from terrestrial phenomena."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheEarth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Erde",
        "en": "Signs of the earth"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Diné transmission of chantways: long ordered ceremonials for restoring a condition named as order, with the sequence fixed and its errors held to be consequential."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Navajo-Zeremonialüberlieferung",
        "en": "Navajo ceremonial transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Read for the first time in the introduction to the legends, sections 41 and 42 on the sacred songs, printed pages 24 and 25. The legends themselves and the ceremonial texts were not read, and the fuller ceremonial account of the night chant published in nineteen hundred and two was not opened in this run."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Wyman, Blessingway, with the recorded Navajo texts; Matthews, The Night Chant",
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), pp. 24-25",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZoroastrianReturnPresupposesWatch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rückkehr setzt die ununterbrochene Wache über dem Körper voraus",
        "en": "The return presupposes the unbroken watch over the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the difference is the finding rather than a formality. The transmission prescribes the watch in full detail and never says what would follow if it lapsed. There is no sentence anywhere in the chapters read about what happens if the traveller does not return, none about a condition under which the journey fails, and none about a body left unwatched. The risk is conceded in practice, by the testament made before drinking and by the undertaking given to the sisters, and it is not conceded in doctrine. A procedure this exact about what to do, and this silent about what goes wrong, is the shape a claim takes when nobody has been in a position to report a failure."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:WirazReturn"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:WirazWatch"
      },
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 2.18-33 and 3.2",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Piti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pīti",
        "en": "Pīti"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The driving out of the corpse-demon by having a dog look at the body. The dog is specified: a yellow dog with four eyes, or a white dog with yellow ears. The way is to be traversed three times; if the dog will not go, six times; and if it still will not, nine times. The transmission states what follows: the demon flies away to the regions of the north in the shape of a raging fly, with knees and tail sticking out."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Sagdid",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sagdīd, der Blick des Hundes",
        "en": "Sagdid, the gaze of the dog"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A rare case of a procedure whose success is defined by an event nobody can observe and whose failure is defined by one that anybody can: the demon's flight is unobservable, but the dog's refusal to walk the way is not, and the refusal is what multiplies the requirement from three to six to nine. The transmission has built its escalation on the one part of the operation that is public."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "sag-did"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "sagdid"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Vendidad, fargard 8.16-18, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cases have been recorded and show a deviation from the stated base rate. What deviates is what the protocol measured, which is rarely the whole of what the claim asserts."
      },
      "id": "pm:casesWithDeviation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fälle mit Abweichung",
        "en": "Cases with deviation"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A practice repeated under a stated procedure that records its failures alongside its successes."
      },
      "id": "pm:protocolledPractice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Protokollierte Praxis",
        "en": "Protocolled practice"
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The list given for the upward journey through the Harmony: to the first zone the energy of growth and waning; to the second, the device of evils, now de-energized; to the third, the guile of the desires, de-energized; to the fourth, domineering arrogance, de-energized; to the fifth, unholy daring and the rashness of audacity, de-energized; to the sixth, the striving for wealth by evil means, deprived of its aggrandizement; and to the seventh, ensnaring falsehood, de-energized."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was an jeder der sieben Zonen zurückgegeben wird",
        "en": "What is surrendered at each of the seven zones"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read against the ophite speeches, this is the complement and not the parallel. There the passer names what he brings and the gate opens; here he names nothing and leaves one thing behind at each stage. One is a liturgy of assertion, the other an inventory of divestment, and the sequence of seven is what they have in common.",
          "The repeated de-energized is the load-bearing word and is easy to read past. What is handed over is not the vice but the vice already stripped of its working: the surrender is described as the last step of something that has happened, not as the act that undoes it. Whether the undoing is the dying itself or the life that preceded it, the passage does not say.",
          "The zones are not named, numbered to planets, or assigned rulers here, although the same treatise says at I.9 that the seven rulers govern what men call Fate. The pairing of zone with planet is therefore as absent in this witness as the pairing of archon with planet is in the ophite one, and for the same kind of reader it is supplied just as readily."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a second Mind, itself brought forth by the Mind that is God, and being of fire and spirit, formed seven rulers who enclose the world the senses perceive, and that what men call Fate is the ruling of these seven."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticSeptemGubernatores",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sieben Herrscher, deren Herrschaft die Menschen Schicksal nennen",
        "en": "The seven rulers whose rule men call Fate"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The identification is made by the source and not by a reader: Fate is not likened to the seven, it is said to be their rule. That is the reason this node stands here rather than as a compiler's reading of a cosmology."
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.9 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvSpiritusYieldsSublatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der dreifach eingezogene Atem hebt den Handelnden empor",
        "en": "The threefold indrawn breath raises the operator"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pairing is as tight as this graph holds anywhere: a named bodily act, a stated count, and an outcome that arrives in the same hour. What it still lacks is the other half — the text nowhere says what the operator sees or fails to see if it does not work."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:PgmIvSublatio"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the motor paralysis at the threshold of sleep is a regular feature of every sleeper rather than a disorder of some, and that it becomes noticeable only where waking awareness outlasts the body's entry into it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lähmung liegt bei jedem vor, selten ist nur ihr Bemerken",
        "en": "The paralysis is present in everyone and only its noticing is rare"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Split deliberately from the weaker claim that sleep paralysis is the regular shutdown of motor control at the sleep transition, which is prima-materia#137 and stands on its own. This one adds the two things that make it testable: a universality and an explanation of why a universal thing is rarely reported."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#138",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The position Porphyry assigns him: a place beside the equinoctial circle as his proper seat, holding the sword of Aries, a martial sign, and borne on the Bull, which is the sign of Venus, because Mithras like the Bull is demiurge and lord of generation; he stands with the northern parts on his right hand and the southern on his left."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicSedesAequinoctialis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mithras sitzt am Äquinoktium, zwischen den beiden Toren",
        "en": "Mithras is seated at the equinox, between the two gates"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held apart from the ladder of Contra Celsum VI.22 on purpose. That account has seven gates in a line and a soul that traverses them; this one has two gates at the solstices and a god who does not traverse anything. They are two cosmologies for one cult, reported by two outsiders a generation apart, and the graph does not reconcile them.",
          "The two gates in this treatise are Cancer for the descent of souls and Capricorn for their ascent, and they are the doctrine of the theologians Porphyry follows — Numenius and Cronius — not a Mithraic teaching. What is Mithraic in the passage is only the seat: the god is placed at the crossing between the two, that is, at neither gate but at the point where descent and ascent are equal."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the seat of Mithras at the equinoctial circle (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 11 of that edition's division)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ValidConfessionPresupposesCompleteness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine gültige Beichte setzt voraus, dass nichts verschwiegen wurde",
        "en": "A confession that holds presupposes that nothing was withheld"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source names the outcome of the want and not merely the rule: the confession was not good and is to be made again. That the finding of the want is itself made by a randomising device is the point of the neighbouring node and does not weaken the necessity, which is stated independently of how it is detected."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Where the bead breaks in two rather than three, or the straws come out odd, the confession was not good and the penitent is told to confess his faults again."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:ConfessionValidityTest"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Reciting the list of names at the front and at the doors before entering a house which is old, or in ruins, or unclean."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatRecitedAtTheDoors",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Namen, gesprochen vorn und an den Türen",
        "en": "The names said at the front and at the doors"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim stands in a received work at a passage that can be named, but the passage was read in a second work that quotes or reports it, because the first could not be obtained."
      },
      "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vermitteltes Zeugnis",
        "en": "Mediated attestation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Admissible only where the work itself is out of reach — not digitised, out of print, held internally, in a language not commanded. Effort is not a reason: the mode exists so that unreachable material can enter at its true strength, not so that reachable material can enter cheaply. See SPEC §10 and SPEC §15.",
          "Every claim carrying this mode must name the intermediary with pm:readVia, enforced by pm:MediatedAttestationShape. dcterms:source keeps naming the work that carries the claim, because that is what a later reader must obtain; pm:readVia names what the writer of the node actually had in front of him. Collapsing the two would hide exactly the fact this mode exists to record.",
          "Weaker than pm:textualAttestation and stronger than nothing, and the record has to be able to say which of the two it has. A claim read through an intermediary carries that intermediary's selection, its translation and its silence; a reader who cannot see that the passage came second-hand cannot weigh any of it."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins",
        "en": "Knowledge encompassing another mind"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorder states that the practitioner is paid very well for the consultation and is additionally courted with food and drink after their own fashion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheSpecialistIsPaid",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Fachmann wird bezahlt, und gut bezahlt",
        "en": "The specialist is paid, and paid well"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because payment is one of the gates this project counts, and because it stands beside the other one: the same chapter says the practice is hidden from outsiders. A transmission can be closed by price and by secrecy at once, and the two are not the same gate."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConsultationPresupposesTaboos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung setzt die Tabus voraus",
        "en": "The consultation presupposes the taboos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the source does not merely prescribe the abstinence but says what its want does to the instrument. An unclean man may still address the oracle from a distance, which is a graded rather than an absolute exclusion and is recorded here rather than smoothed away."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Contact of an unclean person with the oracle is certain to destroy its potency, and even the close proximity of an unclean person may have that result."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeTaboos"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 132",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The correspondence between the ophite gate speeches and the hermetic surrender of energies: in both, the ascent after death is divided into seven stations that are passed singly and in order, and at each one a particular thing is transacted rather than the passage being granted as a whole."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConvergingGradedAscent",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Seele durchläuft sieben Stationen einzeln, und an jeder wird etwas verhandelt",
        "en": "The soul passes seven stations one at a time, and something is transacted at each"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second difference decides whether either can be examined. The hermetic passage names the seven things; the ophite speeches name the gates and never the passions. Every table that pairs an ophite archon with a vice is therefore doing what this node refuses to do: filling one witness from the other.",
          "The correspondence is asserted by the compiler and by neither source. What is shared is the form — seven, singly, in order, with a transaction at each — and it is worth naming because it is not the trivial part. A doctrine of ascent does not have to be graded: it could be one passage, or a judgement, or a threshold. These two divide it the same way.",
          "What is not shared is what is transacted, and the difference runs in opposite directions. The ophite passer brings something and names it: a symbol of life, his own beard, the spirit of a virgin, the symbol of the mother. The hermetic one leaves something and it is named for him: growth and waning, the device of evils, the guile of the desires, arrogance, daring, the striving for wealth, ensnaring falsehood. One ascends by asserting what he has, the other by being emptied of what he was given. Reading the two as one doctrine of shedding garments takes the hermetic half and supplies it to the other."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber"
        }
      ],
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Both corpora are Graeco-Egyptian of the second and third centuries and circulate in the same milieu; the hermetic writings and the diagram Origen obtained are separated by neither language, region nor period, and the ophite material carries names Origen himself derives partly from magic and partly from the Hebrew scriptures, which is the same pool the hermetic and papyrological material draws on. No transmission from one text to the other is claimed; what is documented is that no route needs to be found.",
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has the condition remain with the one who has it, whether it was worked for or not."
      },
      "id": "pm:abidingState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bleibend",
        "en": "Abiding"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateDuration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The regulation of the breath as a distinct limb of practice, following posture and preceding the withdrawal of the senses."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Pranayama",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prāṇāyāma",
        "en": "Prāṇāyāma"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.49-53",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Amt setzt das Fasten voraus",
        "en": "The office presupposes the fast"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage. The source sets the fast, its lengths and its abstinences, and says nothing about what befalls a man who takes the office without it. A stated consequence would raise this; none stands in what was read. The variation between one month, six and a year is itself against reading a necessity into it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The term the corpus gives to the case in which the body is worn out and the blood exhausted and the treatment establishes nothing. Asked why that happens, the answer is that the spirit does not lend itself. Needle and stone, the passage continues, are a way; where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured; and where the essence is ruined and the spirit gone, the constructive and defensive qi can no longer be gathered back, because desire is without end and grief without cease."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shenbushi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Geist gibt sich nicht her",
        "en": "The spirit does not lend itself"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the failure condition of this corpus that is hardest to test and most worth recording, because it is located in the patient and not in the operation. Any outcome of a treatment can be assigned to it after the fact, and the passage supplies no mark by which its presence could be established beforehand. Recorded as the tradition's own account of why a correct procedure fails, and marked here as the shape of claim that absorbs every outcome."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "It is held that without paraphernalia shamanizing is impossible, so that a person who has none cannot function as a shaman. The composition varies greatly: from a brass mirror and a drum up to several complete costumes with headdress, coat, trousers, apron, shoes and many placings for spirits. Equipment made for a single occasion is admitted, so the lack of permanent paraphernalia does not by itself mean the complex is absent."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusParaphernalia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gerät ist unerlässlich und wandelbar",
        "en": "The paraphernalia are indispensable and variable"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The variability is stated together with a floor: reduction of the paraphernalia to zero is held responsible for the disintegration of shamanism, and the recorder names cases where local authorities forbade shamanizing and destroyed the equipment. Where the equipment is limited the power is said to be reduced in a specific way — among the Nercinsk and Barguzin Tungus a shaman cannot go into the lower world without a complete harness."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, pp. 272-273",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of kami, of purification as the central operation, and of formal address preserved in the imperial liturgies."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shinto",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shintō",
        "en": "Shintō"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kojiki; Nihon shoki; the norito of the Engishiki",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NavajoEfficacyPresupposesExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie setzt die Genauigkeit der Gesänge voraus",
        "en": "The efficacy of the ceremony presupposes exactness in the songs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The consequence is graded rather than absolute: an error may be fatal, and only in some cases does a single syllable do irreparable harm. That grading is worth keeping, because the parallel claims elsewhere are usually reported without it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The witness says an error made in singing a song may be fatal to the efficacy of a ceremony, and that in some cases the error of a single syllable works an irreparable injury.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SixtyYearReckoningPresupposesTheDiscovery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Sechzigjahres-Comput setzt die Entdeckung des Same-Sterns voraus",
        "en": "The sixty-year reckoning presupposes the discovery of the seed-star"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable, and not because the sequence is historical. What the passage establishes is that the tradition itself makes the calendar depend on the star, which is the claim the outside reception took over and the claim the examination addresses."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The account states what held before the discovery: the year was not yet fixed as a unit, the only reckoning known was by seven harvests, and at the end of the seventh the supreme chief was sacrificed. The consequence of the prior step being absent is therefore named twice over, as a defect of the calendar and as a death.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-283",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A questioner addresses the oracle while a second man, the operator, administers a measured number of doses of the poison benge to a fowl. The address alternates: sentences framing the matter so that an affirmative answer is asked for and ending with the command that the oracle kill the fowl, then sentences framing it so that a negative answer is asked for and ending with the command that it spare the fowl. Whether the death of the fowl means yes or no is therefore decided by the form of the question and not by the death."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung des Giftorakels",
        "en": "Consulting the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The operators are generally boys between twelve and sixteen, old enough to keep the food taboos and young enough to keep the sexual one. The consultation is held in public, and the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 136-138 and p. 153",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteOrdoDisputatus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wer von den Sieben zuletzt steht",
        "en": "Which of the seven stands last"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Irenaeus makes Ialdabaoth the first and Astanphaeus the seventh and last, in a chain where each is begotten by the one before. Origen has the soul address Ialdabaoth as first and seventh, and puts Astaphaeus at the third gate, with Horaeus at the first. The two orders cannot both be read off one diagram: one counts downwards from the mother, the other counts gates upwards from the earth, and Ialdabaoth stands at the far end in each.",
          "Whether that is a real disagreement between two branches or the same scheme read from two directions is not decidable from the translations used. It is entered as a dispute rather than harmonised, because a resolution here would settle by preference what the witnesses settle differently."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:OphiteHebdomasNominata"
      },
      "source": [
        "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.5 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ValidVerdictPresupposesCorroboration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein gültiger Spruch setzt den zweiten Test voraus",
        "en": "A valid verdict presupposes the second test"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source names what fails to happen without the second test and not merely the order of the two. The qualification that a single test is often accepted stands on the practice node and does not weaken this: where the transmission requires the corroboration, it also says what the want of it costs."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Without the second test no judgement is accepted as valid; where both tests fall alike the verdict is contradictory and void and the matter must be put again on another occasion."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-139",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act, within one tradition, of taking over a claim from another and adding to it something the source it came from does not contain: a term, a consequence, a caution, a sign, a condition of failure."
      },
      "id": "pm:Reworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bearbeiten",
        "en": "Reworking"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Deliberately expensive: pm:addedElement points at a node, so the addition has to be entered before the reworking can be claimed. A station that added something and cannot say what added nothing that this graph can use. As with pm:Testing, absence is informative and is not to be stubbed: no instance means nobody has shown an addition, not that none was made.",
          "Distinct from pm:Systematizing, which is an ordering act performed by an editor in the present and named as his. A reworking is an act inside a transmission, attested in a work like any other claim, and it belongs to the tradition that performed it.",
          "The class exists because reception is two things and the record could not tell them apart. Textual transmission is somebody copying a sentence, and it shows that the sentence was handed on. Operative uptake is somebody taking the instruction, carrying it out, and afterwards writing what was not in the exemplar. Only the second turns a chain of copies into a series of attempts, and only the second is proof against feedback: what makes the Dogon Sirius case weak is not that it is received but that nothing was added at the second station which was not already in the question.",
          "This is therefore the quantity worth counting, and it is not the number of independent witnesses. A tradition that takes over a claim about an effect and attaches a condition of failure to it has delivered more than three that pass it on word for word — and the addition is the material a pm:falsifiedBy is made from, so the examining layer comes out of the reception history rather than against it."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns the moon; the Ethiopic gives its course, the Aramaic-Greek witness gives its signs under the name Sahriel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sariel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel",
        "en": "Sariel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Sariêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sahriel"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DancingPresupposesOfferingAndFlowingBlood",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Tanz setzt eine Gabe und eine blutende Wunde voraus",
        "en": "The dance presupposes an offering and a wound that bleeds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity: the corpus does not merely order the two before the dance but says what their absence costs. The wound may be as small as the skin of a louse in the first form and as large as the dancer wills."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Before beginning to dance the candidate must make an acceptable offering to the Sun and have a wound that will cause his blood to flow while he dances; when the blood flows as a token it is the surest guarantee of sincerity, and without such a guarantee the people or the Sun may doubt the professed purposes of the dancer."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:WiwanyangWacipi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:OfferingAndWoundBeforeTheDance"
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and p. 93",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorder states that the people of this land, like those of Peru, hide the matter with the greatest diligence; that they deny it stubbornly even when accused and convicted, until they die impenitent; and that neither the fear of God nor an oath nor anything else avails to make them confess."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConcealmentFromTheRecorder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Praxis wird dem Fragenden verborgen",
        "en": "The practice is concealed from the questioner"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The comparison with Peru is the recorder's and is not evidence of any connection between the two. It is entered because it shows he was generalising across two colonial situations, which is one of the ways a report acquires content its informants never supplied.",
          "This is the most valuable sentence in what was read, and it cuts two ways. It records a gate: the transmission was held closed against an outsider who had the power to punish, which is a fact about the tradition. And it records the limit of the whole manual as a witness, in the words of the man who wrote it: what he has is what could not be hidden from him. Every other node in this file is to be read against it."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos I and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the poison oracle is chiefly consulted for: to learn whether witchcraft threatens an undertaking or a person's health, and if so which neighbour is at this moment exercising it, so that he may be approached and asked to withdraw it. The question is never whether a man is a witch but whether he is now bewitching the questioner."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Benennung dessen, der gerade behext",
        "en": "Naming the one who is bewitching at this moment"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 4 and pp. 35-36",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KhuddikaPiti",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geringe Verzückung",
        "en": "Minor rapture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reported attitude that these are great and difficult questions best left alone, that men know so little beyond food and sleep and rest that it might easily seem presumptuous to form any opinion about hidden things, and that people who are content should not trouble themselves with thinking."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstOpinionAboutHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Warnung davor, sich über die verborgenen Dinge eine Meinung zu bilden",
        "en": "The caution against forming opinions about hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A tradition warning against the very enquiry the rest of it makes possible. It is entered because a record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports what it holds, and because this caution is directed at the enquirer and not at the practitioner: it does not say the hidden things are unreal, it says that opinion about them is presumption.",
          "The passage also gives a second and worldlier ground for the same reticence, which the ethnographer reports of himself and not of the tradition: that his hosts feared their séances and their taboo observances would be laughed at. Both grounds are in the same pages, and separating them is the reader's work."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-20",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:YangshengYieldsDubaisui",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Regimen bringt die volle Spanne hervor",
        "en": "The regimen yields the full span"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state the faster is held to keep by carrying a fast through, and to lose by breaking one he has begun. The witness reports the belief without endorsing it and without naming what the harm would be."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MayaHouseholdUnharmed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unversehrtheit an Person und Haus",
        "en": "Remaining unharmed in person and household"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XL, p. 70",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The number of cases recorded under this protocol so far."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:caseCount",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fallzahl",
        "en": "case count"
      },
      "range": "xsd:integer",
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AwakeningPresupposesAnotherWhoDoesNotSleep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Erwachen setzt einen voraus, der nicht schläft",
        "en": "Awakening presupposes another who does not sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prerequisite is not a rule of etiquette towards teachers but follows from the caution below: the sleeper cannot detect his own lapse, so the detection has to come from outside him. A second and narrower form of the same claim stands in the talks on groups, where the chief fault of a person is said to be findable only by a teacher and never by the person himself, and the source calls that practically a law."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A person cannot awaken alone; and an agreement among twenty to wake one another is still insufficient, because all twenty may fall asleep at the same time and dream that they are waking. Without someone who does not fall asleep as easily and who is charged with waking them, awakening is held to be impossible."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness"
      },
      "source": [
        "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage on the impossibility of awakening alone (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
        "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eleven, at the talk on the chief feature and the teacher (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two pieces of wood, the upper rubbed across the moistened surface of the lower. Sticking gives one answer and smooth running the other, and a lid that runs from side to side or alternately sticks and runs is taken as a refusal to answer. The instrument is the cheapest and most frequently used of the oracles and the one least trusted."
      },
      "id": "pmp:RubbingBoardConsultation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung des Reibbrettorakels",
        "en": "Consulting the rubbing-board oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earliest witness describes the same instrument two generations before the monograph, under the name borru, made from the wood of a tree he names, and reports that it was consulted before any undertaking of importance and hidden from the Muslim traders. The two descriptions of the mechanism agree; the names do not, and the divergence stands on the dispute node at the end of this file."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 170-171",
        "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InipiPresupposesTheCustomaryBuild",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Hütte muss nach dem Brauch gebaut sein, sonst wirkt die Zeremonie nicht",
        "en": "The lodge must be built after the custom or the ceremony is of no avail"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the source does not prescribe the build and stop there: it names what follows if the build is wrong, and what follows is total. The sentence is from the text written in Lakota by Sword and translated, not from the compiler's exposition."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The ceremony must be performed in an ini ti; the ini ti must be made according to Lakota custom, otherwise the ceremony would be of no avail."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Inipi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:IniTiConstruction"
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 156",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where more than one oracle is consulted the lesser is always consulted before the greater, in the order rubbing-board, termites, poison. A verdict of the termites is not put to the rubbing-board for confirmation, and a verdict of the poison oracle is not put to the termites. On all important matters the decision of the termites must be corroborated by the poison oracle."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OracleRankOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rangordnung der Orakel",
        "en": "The graded order of the oracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order is a rule about the direction in which confirmation may run and not merely a ranking by esteem. It is what makes the cheaper oracles usable at all: a man narrows the field with the termites and spends poison only on the final decision."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher whose teaching concerns celestial signs; the two recensions differ on which."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Baraqel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel",
        "en": "Baraqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Barâqîjâl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Baraqijal"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The harvest has been carried out for the works actually opened, and what was left unread is recorded. The state claims completeness for what was opened, never for the corpus."
      },
      "id": "pm:corpusHarvested",
      "label": {
        "de": "Korpus geerntet",
        "en": "Corpus harvested"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Stages of the path attained by effort and held by the one who reaches them, enumerated in the handbooks in a set order."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Maqamat",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stationen",
        "en": "Stations"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The same extract that states the standard states that it is not reached: scarcely anyone has been found who has learnt the whole of the narratives and can recite them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaMemorisationStandardUnmet",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der als unerreicht berichtete Gedächtnismaßstab",
        "en": "The memorisation standard reported as unattained"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The node exists because the shortfall stands in the same breath as the demand. A record that carried the demand alone would report a feat of memory that the tradition does not claim is performed, and this is one of the few places where a transmission states the distance between its own rule and its practitioners.",
          "This is the point at which the tradition touches the exactness material already in the graph, and it touches it from the other side: there the rule is enforced, here the rule is conceded to be unfulfilled. No convergence node is written for it. The contact route of this tradition stands at disputed, so an agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The operative means take what the surrounding order prohibits and use the force of the prohibition itself as the vehicle. The transgression is prescribed, not incidental, and it is bounded by the rite that prescribes it."
      },
      "id": "pm:transgressiveMethod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übertretende Methode",
        "en": "Transgressive method"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Not the same as licence. A path is transgressive on this axis only where a source sets the breach as a means; a practice that merely fails to observe a norm is not oriented by it at all."
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Book of Formation and its commentaries, in which the world is formed through letters, number and breath."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SeferYetzirah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sefer Jetzira",
        "en": "Sefer Yetzirah"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Sefer Yetzirah 1:9-12 (Short Version; numbering of Kaplan 1997)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1997",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the left-hand path is constituted by the goal of self-deification rather than by any particular practice, and that it falls into two branches: an immanent one, materialistic in outlook and pronounced in its transgression, and a transcendental one, centred on intellect and eternal forms."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LeftHandPathTwoBranches",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der linke Pfad, über sein Ziel bestimmt, mit zwei nach der Methode unterschiedenen Zweigen",
        "en": "The left-hand path defined by its goal, with two branches differing in method"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That is the case this graph was given a separate method axis for. In the same work the goal is held constant across both branches while the degree of transgression varies between them — an author varying one axis and holding the other fixed, without drawing the axes. See prima-materia#52 and prima-materia#67.",
          "The passage that matters most for this record is the one that denies a methodological definition outright. The source states that there are no categorical methodological proscriptions on the path in any of its traditions, eastern or western, and that methods are chosen pragmatically; the antinomian element is described as often strong, not as constitutive. The author who is usually cited as fixing the path by transgression therefore says the opposite of what he is cited for.",
          "The transcendental branch is the standing candidate for the quadrant that a one-axis scheme cannot name: a goal of self-deification without transgression as its means. Whether it actually sits there is not settled by this node and is the question of prima-materia#72."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "The old distinction of the Indian tantric traditions, reported as though the two-criteria definition were carried by them rather than formulated in 1997.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Stephen E. Flowers",
      "source": "S. E. Flowers, Lords of the Left-Hand Path: A History of Spiritual Dissent (Smithville 1997), in the chapter setting out the criteria and the two branches",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Graeco-Egyptian transmission of an ascent through the spheres in which the ascending one sheds a vice at each, and of a rebirth reported as having happened to the speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hermetic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hermetische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Hermetic transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Mead's diction is Victorian and interpretive in places where the Greek is disputed; his zone list in particular has been rendered differently by later translators. What is entered here is what one named translation says, which is the standard this project sets, and it is why the edition stands on every node rather than in this header alone.",
          "The Discourse on the Eighth and the Ninth is an initiation with a sequence, a moment of change and a demand that the initiate confirm what he now sees. It has no equivalent in what the graph currently holds.",
          "The three decisions that precede a tradition file. Route of contact: documented in every direction that matters here — the corpus is Graeco-Egyptian of the first three centuries, and the traditions it would be compared with in this graph, the ophite diagram and the magical papyri, stand in the same milieu. Nothing entered from this file may be counted as an independent witness against them. Edition: read in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, public domain, named on every node; Nock and Festugière and the translation of Copenhaver were not consulted, and where Mead's wording carries a decision it is flagged. Number of citable passages: the first treatise only, and within it the ascent."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Corpus Hermeticum; Asclepius; the Stobaean excerpts including Korē Kosmou; Nag Hammadi VI,6",
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I (Poimandres), in the translation of G. R. S. Mead",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Pāli canon and its commentarial literature, of which the Visuddhimagga of Buddhaghosa is the systematising handbook."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Theravada",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theravāda",
        "en": "Theravāda"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Visuddhimagga citations name the translation they were read from, and Dīgha Nikāya 2 was reopened for this purpose. For Majjhima Nikāya 118, Dīgha Nikāya 11 and Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8 the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that the bearer of the booklet cannot drown, cannot burn, cannot die without the whole corpus Christi and cannot have an unjust sentence spoken over him. It is examinable because the claim is stated as an impossibility over named outcomes and not as an increase in safety."
      },
      "id": "pmp:CarriedBookletProtectionExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Schutz des getragenen Büchleins etwas ausschließt",
        "en": "Whether the protection of the carried booklet excludes anything"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case has been collected in either direction, and the obstacle is stated rather than left to be inferred: the bearers of a cheap handbook are not a recorded set, and the claim is therefore refutable in principle and unattended in fact. That is a property of the record and not of the claim, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised.",
          "The claim carries over unchanged from the booklet of seventeen eighty-eight into the Reading printing of eighteen twenty, and in both it refers to the booklet in the reader's hand. A protocol therefore has to name which booklet a bearer carried, and both answers are covered by the same wording.",
          "The escape the wording does not take is worth naming, because the same corpus takes it elsewhere. Nothing here says the protection fails if the bearer sins, doubts or acquires the booklet wrongly. Where such a clause is present, as it is in the passages that assign a failure to a broken prohibition, every outcome is absorbed and no protocol is possible. Here the sentence stands bare."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/european-folk-magic.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One bearer, carrying the booklet at the time, who drowned or burned to death, or who died without the sacrament, or over whom a sentence later held to be unjust was pronounced. The wording admits no degree, so a single such case refutes the claim as it stands. A weaker form, that bearers meet these ends less often than others, would require the bearers to be counted before the outcomes are known.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-05",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBuechleinSchutz"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Valentinian school of the second century, known in this file through the account of an opponent and not yet through its own writings."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ValentinianGnosis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Valentinianische Gnosis",
        "en": "Valentinian gnosis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A caution governs everything read out of this file at present. What is held here comes from a refutation: the heresiologist reports the doctrine in order to defeat it, selects what serves that end, and is under no obligation to state it as its holders would. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription and not textual attestation, and none of them may be read as a Valentinian self-statement until the same claim is found in a text of the school itself.",
          "The three decisions that precede a tradition file, stated rather than left implicit. Route of contact: none is documented towards the Indian or Chinese material, so a correspondence with either would count as a finding rather than as reception — but no such correspondence is asserted here yet. Edition: the account is read in the Keble translation of 1872, named on each node; the Nag Hammadi witnesses are not yet read and their translation is not yet chosen. Number of citable passages: one. That is thin, and deliberately so, on the model of traditions/daoist.ttl — a file grows in passages, and a heading without passages under it would be a claim about the corpus that the corpus does not carry."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I (trans. J. Keble, Oxford 1872)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The apāna is raised from below while the prāṇa is pressed down from the throat, and the two are brought together in the region of the navel."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die beiden Winde werden zugleich in entgegengesetzte Richtungen getrieben",
        "en": "The two winds are driven in opposite directions at once"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The manual assigns direction to the two winds, not to the two phases of breathing. Both directions run in the same operation and against each other, so a scheme that reads inhalation as upward and exhalation as downward, or the reverse, has no purchase here. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.50 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A prohibition laid on one person by the practitioner who treats him, sometimes for life and sometimes for a stated term. The corpus states that the thing forbidden bears no relation to the complaint and is chosen at the practitioner's discretion, and it gives instances: a woman with fits forbidden to look in a mirror or at her reflection in a stream, men forbidden cassava in any form for the rest of their lives. Eating the forbidden thing is said to break the protective spell and to bring the complaint back. Where the prohibition is for a term, say six months, the practitioner removes it at the end and takes his fee."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Nlongo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nlongo, das auferlegte persönliche Verbot",
        "en": "Nlongo, the imposed personal prohibition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held apart from pmc:Mpangu because the two differ in exactly the way that matters here: the inherited prohibition names its penalty in advance and in kind, and the imposed one names no relation at all between the forbidden thing and the disease. The second cannot fail, since any return of the complaint is evidence that something was eaten."
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 246-247",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission begun by three anonymous manifestos that announced an order and a knowledge, and that produced both by being read."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Rosicrucian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Rosenkreuzerische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Rosicrucian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A tradition whose founding documents describe a body that did not exist before them. Of interest to the project precisely as a documented case of a transmission created by its own announcement."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Fama Fraternitatis; Confessio Fraternitatis; Chymische Hochzeit Christiani Rosencreutz",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ThreefoldAscentOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Naturschau setzt asketische Praxis voraus",
        "en": "Contemplation of nature presupposes ascetic practice"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Physike"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 1",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The circumstances of the receiving: the state the receiver is said to have been in, who put the questions, who took the wording down, and whether a record of the proceeding itself exists beyond the edited text."
      },
      "id": "pm:receptionCircumstance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Umstand des Empfangs",
        "en": "reception circumstance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required on every receiving, enforced by pm:ReceivingShape. Without it the node says only that a speaker is claimed, which a reader could have guessed from the title; with it the claim can be weighed against the ones this literature makes about itself."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PatanjalaCapacityDurationNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Für die Vermögen wird keine Dauer angegeben",
        "en": "The capacities are given no duration"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:durationNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The sūtras that carry the capacities of this chapter were read for the question and are silent on it: each names an object of saṃyama and the knowledge or entry that follows, and none says whether what follows passes or remains. The sūtra that weighs the capacities weighs them for what they are worth and not for how long they last."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.37",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Zande transmission of witchcraft as an inherited substance in the body, of a graded set of oracles by which it is located, and of a corroborative second question by which an oracular verdict is held valid or void."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AzandeOracle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azande-Orakelüberlieferung",
        "en": "Azande oracular transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chosen from the registry on the testability entrance rather than on demand. The transmission states a condition under which its own principal oracle delivers no verdict at all, names eight causes to which a failure is attributed, and prints two consultations at length so that a reader may count the outcomes for himself. Very little else in this graph names a failure condition its own practitioners apply.",
          "Read in this run: the abridged edition of nineteen seventy-six in full for chapters I, III, V, VI, VIII, IX and X and for the appendix of terms and the appendix on dreams. Left unread in that edition: chapters IV, VII, XI, XII and XIII, on whether witches are conscious agents, on the place of witch-doctors in the society, on magic and medicines, on the association for communal magic, and on the situation of death; and the third appendix. The unabridged edition of nineteen thirty-seven was not opened: it is in copyright and stands in the large archives only as a lending copy or as a search-only index, which is a property of the work and not of the search. Every place below is therefore given from the abridged edition, and the abridgement is named in each reference because an abridgement is a selection and a later reader must be able to see that he is looking at one.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent, and the correction is not a finding but the removal of one: the registry had recorded no route because none had been looked for. What a search finds is on both sides. Against a route: the poison oracle stands already in the earliest account, taken down at the first sustained European contact, so it cannot have entered with that contact. For a route: the same monograph places the belief in a witchcraft substance in the body among many peoples of Central and West Africa and calls Zandeland the north-eastern limit of its distribution, which is an areal connection to the West African sphere in which this graph already holds a transmission; and the corridor from the Islamic north was open from the eighteen-sixties, since the earliest witness travelled in with an ivory company out of Khartoum. Neither has been carried out for the oracular material in particular. Until it is, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation.",
          "Two further witnesses were opened for the counter-search and are cited where they carry something: the travel account of eighteen seventy-three, which is the earliest European description of these oracles and predates the anthropological literature by two generations, and the Belgian ethnography of nineteen twenty-one, which describes the same transmission from the other side of the colonial border and sixteen years earlier than the monograph. Both were read at the wording in full-text reproductions of the printed editions named in the references."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande; de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande; Larken, An account of the Zande, in Sudan Notes and Records; Lagae, Les Azande ou Niam-Niam",
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, the list of Zande terms, pp. 226-228",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Saplings set in a circle a little wider than a tall man is high, bent and bound into a dome and covered so as to hold the vapour, with an opening anywhere on the border except toward the north. The one who vitalizes strips and crawls in naked with a pipe and tobacco; assistants heat stones outside, pass them and the water in and close the opening; those inside place the stones at the centre and pour water on them at intervals, and what rises is held to be the released spirit-like of the water, which cannot escape upward and must therefore enter the bodies exposed to it. What comes out again on the skin carries the harmful things with it and is wiped or washed away. In the simplest form it lasts as long as two pipefuls take to smoke, and it may be prolonged for a day and a night or longer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Inipi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Inipi, die Belebungshütte",
        "en": "Inipi, the vitalizing lodge"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The name is given in the corpus both as Inipi and as Ini kaga, and the lodge as ini ti. The second witness calls it the vapor lodge and reports one built by each band beside its camp at the Sun dance for the use of those who took part."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 98",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 66-67 and p. 156"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome the ordeals decide. The transmission treats it as the thing at stake in each house and reports both outcomes for named persons rather than only the successful one."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XibalbaSurvival",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das lebende Verlassen der Häuser von Xibalbá",
        "en": "Coming out of the houses of Xibalba alive"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The earliest alchemical transmission, in which the operation on matter is reported together with dreams that interpret it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GraecoEgyptianAlchemy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechisch-ägyptische Alchemie",
        "en": "Graeco-Egyptian alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zosimos of Panopolis, the Visions and the authentic memoirs",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim the whole doctrine rests on: a person receives his body from his parents, and when the root bones obtain the qi the body left behind is shaded. The classic is quoted for it — the qi is touched and responds, and the fortune of the dead reaches the living — and the resonance is illustrated by the bronze mountain that collapsed in the west while the numinous bell answered in the east."
      },
      "id": "pmc:YitiShouyin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Gebeine empfangen das Qi und die Nachkommen werden beschirmt",
        "en": "The bones receive the qi and the descendants are shaded"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is transitive across a generation and across death, which is what makes it examinable in principle: it predicts something about descendants from something about a grave. What it does not carry is a way of telling the shading from its absence in any single case, and the commentary printed with the text takes the transitivity further rather than bounding it, extending the shading to adopted children and to the sons of a second mother."
      },
      "source": "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MangIWistaspanLocus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise verortet das Bewusstsein außerhalb des Körpers",
        "en": "The journey places the awareness away from the body"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:displacedLocus"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:MangIWistaspan"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The body stays on the carpet and is watched for seven days by named persons, while what travels sees, is addressed and is guided elsewhere, and returns to that body on the seventh day. The transmission does not treat the two as one place at any point of the account.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Indian medical transmission, including the rasāyana section that claims restoration of vigour and extension of life by regimen."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ayurvedic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Āyurvedische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Āyurvedic transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Caraka Saṃhitā; Suśruta Saṃhitā; Vāgbhaṭa, Aṣṭāṅgahṛdaya",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reason the text gives for taking the third: so that when the men who are in the world call upon the rulers in their mysteries — those which the angels who transgressed brought down, that is their sorceries — they should no longer be able to complete them; and, as Mary restates it, so that from that hour the casters of nativities and the questioners and those who announce to men what is to happen should no longer be able to announce anything."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaMagiaFrustrata",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der genannte Zweck: dass die Anrufungen nicht mehr wirken",
        "en": "The stated purpose: that the invocations should stop working"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "This is the closest thing to a falsifying condition in the whole of this graph's ascent material, and it is unusual in a second way: it is a claim about somebody else's practice failing. A transmission says here that from a stated hour, invocation and astrology stopped delivering, and it says which practices it means.",
          "What keeps it from being examinable is not vagueness but reach. No sign is given by which a practitioner would notice the difference, and the change is dated to an event no observer could time. It is entered as a claim and not as a protocol; what a protocol would need is a rate before and after, and the text offers no before."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 15 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition of the patient on which the corpus makes the whole operation depend: the essence and the spirit, and with them the intent, held to be present or absent and to be won or lost. The Lingshu instructs the needler to observe the bearing of the sick person in order to know whether essence, spirit, hun and po are present or gone; where these five are damaged, it says, the needle cannot treat him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Jingshen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Essenzgeist und Absicht des Kranken",
        "en": "The essence-spirit and the intent of the sick person"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the posited claim in two parts: whether the motor paralysis of the sleep threshold is a feature of every sleeper, and whether the rate at which it is noticed is low enough that a universal mechanism could go unreported."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParalysisUniversalityProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Lähmung allgemein ist und ihr Bemerken selten",
        "en": "Whether the paralysis is universal and its noticing rare"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A word on why the claim is posited and not sourced. It comes from an unpublished method draft, which SPEC §10 excludes as a witness, and the exclusion is not a formality: a draft that entered as a source could be confirmed by the very research it was written from. Holding it as a posit keeps the direction right, and the direction is that the draft is examined and never grounded.",
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The claim has two halves and they are carried by two different bodies of evidence, which is the reason this protocol is worth entering at all. The universality is a matter of mechanism: the atonia of the relevant sleep stage is produced by an identified transmitter and receptor pathway and is a regular feature of that stage rather than a fault in it. The rarity of noticing is a matter of prevalence: a systematic review over thirty-five studies and more than thirty-six thousand participants finds that some seven and a half per cent of the general population report at least one episode in a lifetime, with markedly higher figures among students and psychiatric samples.",
          "The two halves fit, and that is the finding. A mechanism present in every sleeper and an experience reported by roughly one in thirteen are not in contradiction: they are exactly what the claim asserts, namely that what is rare is not the paralysis but the waking awareness that coincides with it. The state is therefore cases without deviation.",
          "What this does not settle, and the limits are sharper than the fit. The prevalence figure counts self-reported episodes and not measured ones, so it is a floor for noticing and not a ceiling for occurrence. The mechanism evidence establishes the atonia of the sleep stage and not that every reported episode coincides with it; the two are joined here by the ordinary explanation and not by a study that measured both in the same subjects. And a claim confirmed in this shape says nothing about what any transmission builds on the state, which is the part this graph actually cares about."
        ]
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "7.6 per cent of the general population report at least one lifetime episode, aggregated over 35 studies and 36,533 participants; 28.3 per cent among students and 31.9 per cent among psychiatric patients",
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not carried. Neither the prevalence review nor the mechanism study was checked against later work, and a systematic review of 2011 is old enough that the question is not idle."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "B. A. Sharpless and J. P. Barber, Lifetime prevalence rates of sleep paralysis: a systematic review, Sleep Medicine Reviews 15 (2011), no. 5, pp. 311-315, doi:10.1016/j.smrv.2011.01.007",
        "P. L. Brooks and J. H. Peever, Identification of the transmitter and receptor mechanisms responsible for REM sleep paralysis, Journal of Neuroscience 32 (2012), pp. 9785-9795, doi:10.1523/JNEUROSCI.0482-12.2012"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The protocol assembles two existing bodies of evidence, one on the mechanism and one on the prevalence, and states the joint they leave open",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A population of ordinary sleepers is found in whom the atonia of the sleep stage is absent; or reported episodes are measured and found not to coincide with it."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Upaniṣads as the speculative layer of the Vedic corpus."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Upanisadic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Upaniṣadische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Upaniṣadic tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Chāndogya 7.15.1 carries the edition it was read from and was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For 1.11.5 the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Chāndogya Upaniṣad",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert",
        "en": "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule.",
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it.",
          "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read.",
          "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls.",
          "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."
        ]
      },
      "pm:caseCount": 3,
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination for why the undocumented case is treated as uncarried."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Attic mystery cult of Demeter and Kore, with a lesser rite at Agrai in the spring and the greater rite at Eleusis in the autumn."
      },
      "id": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eleusinische Mysterien",
        "en": "Eleusinian mysteries"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The citation names the corpus in which the inscription is edited, which for an epigraphic source is the edition; what is not recorded is whether the text was read in that corpus or in a secondary report of it, and that is to be established before the reading of face C is relied on."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CompletionPresupposesGeneration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vollendungsstufe setzt die Erzeugungsstufe voraus",
        "en": "The completion stage presupposes the generation stage"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order is set by the arrangement of the treatise, which takes the generation-stage meditations before the completion-stage yogas. That the six yogas belong to the completion stage places tummo after the generation stage as well."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:CompletionStage"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:GenerationStage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A conclusion drawn by an editor or compiler that the source does not state."
      },
      "id": "pm:compilerInference",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schluss des Bearbeiters",
        "en": "Compiler inference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Admissible as attestation of an ordering act, never of the claim being ordered. Enforced by pm:AttributionShape."
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state ascribed to the cooling retention: fever, bile, hunger, thirst and the effect of poisons are said to be put down."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CoolingAndQuenching",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kühlung und das Stillen von Hunger und Durst",
        "en": "Cooling and the quenching of hunger and thirst"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Prayers held to carry power in themselves, so that having them is a source of power and losing or impairing them is a great danger."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerPossession",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Gebet als Besitz",
        "en": "The prayer as a possession"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The nearest thing the passages read give to a stated consequence of getting a prayer wrong, and deliberately not entered as one: the sentence speaks of loss and impairment of the prayer as a possession, not of the effect of a misrecited performance."
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whether a route of transmission is known between a tradition and those the graph already holds. It decides whether an agreement may be read as independent attestation or only as reception, and it is therefore a property of the tradition and not of the individual correspondence."
      },
      "id": "pm:ContactRoute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg",
        "en": "Contact route"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:recordScale"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The axis on which a source's account of how long a condition holds is placed: passing of itself, or abiding once it is there."
      },
      "id": "pm:StateDuration",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dauer des Zustands",
        "en": "State Duration"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1995",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that emotional and mental states occupy fixed positions on a single numbered scale, ascending from shame through fear, anger and pride to reason, love, joy, peace and enlightenment, and that the position can be established by testing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Ordnung der Affekte nach gemessener Stufe",
        "en": "The ranking of emotions by measured level"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "An earlier form of this note held that no transmission ranks affects on a single ordered scale. That was withdrawn: the ground for it had been a search across the holdings of this graph, which says what has been entered here and not what the transmissions say. The search that had not been carried out finds one at once. Rūpa Gosvāmin ranks the five primary forms of devotional love in a single ascending order in the sixteenth century, in the verse itself, and traditions/gaudiya-vaisnava.ttl now holds it. The finding of this node is therefore not that ranking affects is modern. It is narrower and survives: the particular scale below, with its count, its order and its claim of measurability, begins here.",
          "The diagnostic markers, given so that any later list presenting itself as old can be measured against a dated first appearance. The count: seventeen steps. The order of the lower band: shame, guilt, apathy, grief, fear, desire, anger, pride. The turn at the middle: courage, then neutrality, then willingness in eleventh place. The upper band: acceptance, reason, love, joy, peace, enlightenment. A list that shares the count, the relative order of shame, guilt, fear and anger, and the eleventh position of willingness has not arrived at them independently.",
          "The nearest older relative is pmc:TheosophicalColourKeyToFeeling, and the comparison sharpens rather than dissolves this node. That transmission supplies the vibration vocabulary and an axis of self-reference on which a feeling rises or falls, and it was read at the wording for this purpose. It supplies no ranking of unlike feelings against one another. The single numbered order is therefore not inherited, and the question of where it does come from is open in prima-materia#12."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A perennial map of consciousness, reported as the distilled agreement of the mystical traditions.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "David R. Hawkins",
      "source": "D. R. Hawkins, Power vs. Force (1995)",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The putting of questions to a spirit through a medium. The founder makes the form of the question part of the operation: it is to be clear, precise and put in a logical order, and the answer given depends on how it was asked."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Evocation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Evokation",
        "en": "Evocation"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "évocation"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286-288",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect claimed for the worn prayer: preservation and protection from the disease caused by the evil eye of Barya, from coughs, bronchitis, pleurisy, inflammation of the lungs and bowels, diarrhoea, colic, dysentery, the disease caused by worms, fistula, the cracking of the skin, and the evil eye of the sar, of the sorcerer and enchanter, and of a list of named others."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bewahrung des Kindes an der Brust",
        "en": "The preserving of the child at the breast"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The name as it stands in the edition named by dcterms:source, in that edition's transliteration."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Naming",
      "id": "pm:nameForm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Namensform",
        "en": "name form"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Further forms of the same bearer go in skos:altLabel, so that a reader who met the name elsewhere finds the node. The form that decides identity is the one here.",
          "The edition is not decoration here. A name is carried by its letters, and transliteration conventions differ enough that Šemiḥazah and Semjaza are the same bearer under two scholarly hands. Recording the form without the edition it comes from would make a later comparison unfalsifiable."
        ]
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is done before a candidate begins to dance: an offering acceptable to the Sun, of as much value as the candidate can afford, and a wound cut in his person that will cause his blood to flow while he dances. In the first form the wound must not be smaller than that made by cutting away a bit of skin as large as a louse and may be as large as the candidate wills; in the other three the wounds are those through which the sticks are thrust."
      },
      "id": "pmp:OfferingAndWoundBeforeTheDance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Gabe an die Sonne und die blutende Wunde",
        "en": "The offering to the Sun and the wound that bleeds"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and pp. 116-117",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A knowledge tradition understood as an ongoing act of transmission rather than a static corpus."
      },
      "id": "pm:Tradition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tradition",
        "en": "Tradition"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of one hundred and six sessions received between 1981 and 1984 by a group of three working in fixed roles, teaching a cosmology of densities, a choice between service to others and service to self, and a harvest at the close of a cycle."
      },
      "id": "pmt:RaMaterial",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ra-Material",
        "en": "The Ra material"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The best documented reception in the block. Questioner, instrument and scribe are named, the sessions are numbered and dated, the working conditions are described including the instrument's physical decline, and the transmission continued under a different contact after this one ended. The cosmological claims are largely undecidable; the claims about the conditions of contact and about what the work cost the instrument are not, and that is where an examination would begin."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Elkins, Rueckert and McCarty, The Law of One, Books I to V; the L/L Research session transcripts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The conduct the opening chapter ascribes to the people of high antiquity who knew the way: they took the yin and the yang as their model and were in accord with the arts and the numbers, they were measured in food and drink and regular in rising and resting, and they did not work recklessly. The chapter states the counter-case in the same breath, of the people of the present who take wine for water, take the reckless for the regular, enter the bedchamber drunk, exhaust their essence in desire and are without measure in rising and resting."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Regimen derer, die den Weg kannten",
        "en": "The regimen of those who knew the way"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source holds that the question is malformed for its path: there is no bounded self that could either be dissolved or preserved, so neither end state is what it aims at."
      },
      "id": "pm:selfBoundaryDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgelehnte Selbstgrenze",
        "en": "Self boundary declined"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "As with pm:normDistinctionDeclined, this is a positive claim about a source and not a place to put an undetermined case.",
          "The value was first written as the single place for everything that is neither dissolution nor separation, and the material has since shown that too coarse: a marriage is not a non-position, and a non-position is not a claim about which level the question belongs to. The two clearest of those have their own values below. What remains here is the plain form — the source denies that there is a bounded self to be dealt with either way — and the case where a source instead declares the whole level of the question superseded, which is a further distinction still to be made when a second instance of it appears."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A power held to rest coiled at the base of the subtle body and to rise through it when roused."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kundalini",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kuṇḍalinī",
        "en": "Kuṇḍalinī"
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the attempt to bring back a dead man failed because a prohibition had been broken, and not because the shamans were unable."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ReturnPresupposesTheSewingProhibition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederaufleben setzt die Einhaltung des Nähverbots voraus",
        "en": "The return after death presupposes that the prohibition was kept"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The structure of this passage matters more than its content, and it is the reason the node is here. A failure of the practice is reported, and the account assigns the failure to a third party's breach of a prohibition rather than to the practitioners or to the claim. That move makes the underlying claim unfailable from inside the tradition: any outcome confirms it, since success shows the power and failure shows the breach. Recording the immunisation is not an objection to the passage; it is the part a later protocol has to work against."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The narrator relates that the moon would not let the man come to life again, because his mother had sewn garments of new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq, which is not allowed there; the two shamans who tried to catch the returning corpse did not succeed.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSewingProhibitionOnOqshoriaq"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 28-29",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The whole work of fire and water at a birth is committed to the specialist who holds it as his office, and who among these people ordinarily bears the name and the office of physician."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FireAndWaterRite",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Ritus mit Feuer und Wasser bei einer Geburt",
        "en": "The rite of fire and water at a birth"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sentence is a statement about an institution: the office exists, it has a name, and the rite is not performed by the family. That is the part a later reader can check, and it is why the passage is entered as a practice rather than as an item of belief."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo I, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The lowland South American transmission of songs used to direct the effect of a plant preparation, and of a body that can be exchanged for another."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Amazonian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Amazonische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Amazonian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No corpus in its own language and no writing before contact. What exists is transcribed song and ethnographic report, which this project cannot yet admit as a source. Registered so that the exclusion is visible rather than silent."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted written corpus; the published icaro transcriptions and the ethnographic record stand in its place",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShamanizingYieldsRelief",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schamanisieren bringt Befreiung der Befallenen hervor",
        "en": "Shamanizing brings relief to those afflicted"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim the whole institution rests on, and the one on which the source is least specific: it names no duration, no rate and no case in which the relief failed to come. That silence is the reason no protocol is written on it here."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusReliefOfSpiritAffliction"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 381",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of the chain as the edition prints it: the seven vowels recur in groups through the invocation of the seven immortal gods of the cosmos, and the groups are not built by any pattern the editor could establish. He states that he put them to a mathematician and that no law of permutation was found in their arrangement."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvVocesSineLege",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vokalgruppen der Sieben folgen keiner auffindbaren Regel",
        "en": "The vowel groups of the seven follow no discoverable rule"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "That bears directly on any comparison with the chains of the Books of Jeu and the Pistis Sophia. A comparison of two sequences is worth something if the sequences are generated; if one of them is unstructured, matching it against another matches accidents. See prima-materia#607.",
          "The contrast is what makes this worth a node. Where a comparable arrangement is inscribed for the seven archangels of the planets in a theatre in Asia Minor, the rule is trivial and visible at a glance: the leading vowel of each row moves to the end of the next. The chain in this papyrus does not do that, and it does not do anything else that has been found. Either it is corrupt, or its rule is not positional, or there is none and the sequence is the point.",
          "The recitation carries a manner as well as a count: the whole is to be said with fire and with breath, run through to the end and begun again until the seven are completed. The prescribed non-verbal sounds sit in the same layer — a long hiss and a clucking of the lips — and are reported by the edition as an old stratum of address, attested for lightning in fifth-century Athens and for calling snakes, not as an invention of the magicians."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-723, the third utterance and the editor's apparatus to it (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that the counting of the stalks makes known what is coming. The condition is not this project's: a treatise of the first century puts it as the decisive case. Let a diviner bore the shell emptily and count the stalks in vain, making sport of heaven and earth, and he still gets an omen and a number; let a man curse heaven while he bores and strike the earth while he counts, and he still gets an omen and a number. The treatise draws the conclusion that the omen and the number are therefore not an answer, and adds the counterfactual that if they were, the fire should go out, the hand be scorched and the fingers shake so that the count is spoiled."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EmptyConsultationExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Zeichen erscheint, wenn die Befragung nicht ernst gemeint ist",
        "en": "Whether the sign appears when the consultation is not meant"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The state is procedure-without-cases and the reason is exact. The treatise says that the empty and the abusive consultation still yield a figure; it does not report having made one. The wording is a supposition put to the reader, and a supposition is not a case however confident it sounds. Whoever raises this protocol beyond its present state has to make the runs, not to quote the passage more loudly.",
          "The two obvious refinements are both blocked from inside the transmission, and that is the finding this protocol carries. Asking the same question twice and comparing is forbidden by the judgement on the fourth hexagram, which calls repetition defilement and withholds the answer. Consulting where the answer is already known is excluded by the Supreme Mystery, which says that who does not doubt does not consult. A control arm therefore has no sanctioned form in either work, and the treatise's empty consultation is the only one that was ever proposed.",
          "What the protocol would not settle even if it ran. That a figure appears under mockery tells against the figure being an answer given by something; it does not tell against the weaker claim that the figures of earnest consultations correspond to what comes. That weaker claim needs outcomes and a count, which is the other protocol beside this one."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Wang Chong, in the treatise on divining of the Lunheng, first century; recorded here from the passage in traditions/chinese-divination.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A run of the counting performed emptily, in mockery or under abuse, which yields no figure, or yields figures distinguishable as a class from those of consultations meant in earnest. The claim as the classic states it — without thought, without doing, touched and thereupon penetrating — offers no place for the manner of the consultation to enter, so a difference between the two classes would fall against the treatise and no difference falls against the classic.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-09",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:JishuZhilai"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "No attempt has been made to find work that contradicts, fails to replicate, or supersedes the evidence cited."
      },
      "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegensuche nicht geführt",
        "en": "Counter-search not carried"
      },
      "type": "pm:CounterSearchState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning to watch for the return of the stolen goods and, when they are back, to carry the stone to the place it lay and to loose the bush again."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderKautel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Den Busch wieder losmachen, sobald das Gut zurück ist",
        "en": "Loose the bush again once the goods are back"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A caution about what the operator owes after the effect has occurred, not about the risk of performing it. Both printings and the older booklet carry it in the same order and with the same clause; no consequence of leaving the bush bent is named in any of them."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 65, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The performance: the shaman drums, continually increasing and decreasing tempo and intensity in a definite rhythm, brings himself into the bounded state, introduces the spirits into himself, and acts and speaks as the spirits. Assistants must know the art of drumming and must understand the shaman's movements. The performance may begin out of a sleep during which the spirit comes into him."
      },
      "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schamanisieren",
        "en": "Shamanizing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder holds that the audience is a component of the operation and not its public: bringing the audience into the state is not the aim but the means by which the suggestion is made. That claim is his reading and is entered as his; what is reported is the practice."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 119-121, pp. 325-332, and chap. XXIX, sect. 134, pp. 362-364",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made by the narrator about her second husband, that when a knife was lost he would hold a spirit calling and in that way recover it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Geisterrufen soll Verlorenes wiederbringen",
        "en": "Spirit calling is said to recover what has been lost"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The most examinable claim in the portion read, and the reason it is entered separately from the capacity it names. It states a repeatable occasion, a named class of object, and an outcome that either occurred or did not; it is not a claim about a state of the practitioner. What it does not state is any case in which the calling was held and the knife stayed lost."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:InuitSpiritCalling"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What was searched for and what came back, stated so that the search itself can be judged."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:counterSearchNote",
      "label": {
        "de": "Notiz zur Gegensuche",
        "en": "counter-search note"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required wherever pm:counterSearch is set, enforced by pm:CounterSearchNoteRequiredShape. Without it, pm:counterSearchFoundNothing is indistinguishable from a search nobody ran, and it is the state a hasty protocol would reach for."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The formula found written on the light-garment in five words of those of the height, which the text then resolves at length: an address to the mystery that is outside in the world, for whose sake the All came into being, that is the whole going-out and the whole coming-up, which emanated all emanations and everything in them; it is told that the speakers are its fellow-members, that they and it are one and the same, and that it is the first mystery which existed in the ineffable before it came forth, and whose name they all are."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaFormulaQuinqueVerborum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Mysterium der fünf Worte, mit der Auflösung, die der Text selbst gibt",
        "en": "The mystery of five words, with the interpretation the text gives it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The division of the five words is not certain. Schmidt prints them as five and notes in the apparatus that another division is possible, so any count of syllables built on this passage rests on an editor's spacing. See prima-materia#607.",
          "The one formula in this graph's ascent material that arrives with its own interpretation attached. It is not an address to a gatekeeper: the words are found written on the garment, and what they say is that the addressee and the addressers are the same thing under different names. Whatever the syllables are, the text does not treat them as an unintelligible password."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 10 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that after the oration of the physical art is said three times, the sick man's answer to the question how he feels tells without doubt whether he will recover or die."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaPrognosisExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Antwort des Kranken entscheidet, was die Kunst sagt",
        "en": "Whether the answer of the sick man decides what the art says it decides"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "One escape the corpus takes elsewhere is absent here. The art bars the man impeded by crime from saying the orations at all, and that condition is recorded as a caution; nothing in this passage says that a wrong prognosis shows the speaker was unworthy. Whether a fuller recension closes that gap is unread and would change the state of this protocol rather than its subject.",
          "The obstacle to cases is not the difficulty of the procedure but who would keep the record. The judgement has to be written before the outcome and by someone who is not the person nursing the patient; the corpus offers no such series, and none has been looked for outside this graph.",
          "The third branch is excluded from the falsifying condition on purpose. A patient who says he does not know is covered by dying and by getting worse together, and no fate he can meet falls outside that pair. Testing the rule as a whole would let that branch absorb every case the other two got wrong, which is exactly how a claim that says nothing comes to look confirmed.",
          "Which reading is being tested has to be said, and this protocol tests the English. The Latin of the same printing says of the badly answering patient only that he will die shortly; Turner says he will die on the morrow. Shortly has no last day and therefore no outcome that could arrive too late, so the Latin branch is not examinable at all. The divergence stands as pmc:ArsNotoriaTerminusMortisDisputatus and it is the reason this protocol names its witness rather than the tradition."
        ]
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "Unknown and not a single number: whether a person who lies languishing recovers depends on the illness, and the rule is stated for illness in general. Any protocol has to fix the population before it starts, because a rule applied to a ward of the nearly recovered and a rule applied to a ward of the dying score differently without either being right.",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passages recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "One sick person who answers that he is at good ease or begins to mend and then dies of that illness, or who answers that he is grievously ill or worse and worse and then recovers, with the answer and the judgement written down before the outcome. The first two branches each name one outcome and exclude the other, and the text says without doubt and doubtless, so a single such case refutes the rule as stated.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-07",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsCognitioAegritudinis"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The term saman is mentioned by the Chinese at the beginning of the twelfth century as a Nuichen word for sorcerer which they did not recognise as their own san-men, both being traced to the same source, sramana. The recorder proposes that wandering sramana, changed into samana, were transformed into the Nuichen and Manchu saman."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SamanFromSramana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wort selbst kommt von außen",
        "en": "The word itself comes from outside"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Beside it stands a second borrowing that needs no reconstruction: burkan, the term applied to a great number of spirits across the Tungus groups, is a Mongol word and a modification of Buddha, and where Buddha himself is mastered the ordinary word for a shaman's spirit is used of him.",
          "The etymology and the second sentence are of different weights and are kept apart here. That the Chinese record the word in that century, and that both forms are referred to sramana, is what the passage reports; that wandering monks were themselves the first bearers is the recorder's proposal and is his.",
          "This is the node for which the file exists. A category the whole comparative literature uses is named after a word its own bearers took from a Buddhist term for a wandering ascetic, in a transmission whose bearers say the art came from their neighbours. Whoever cites the Siberian case as the archaic form is citing the station of a transmission, not its source."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXIII, sect. 98, pp. 276-277, sect. 99, pp. 277-278, and sect. 100, p. 284",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The mid twentieth century transmission of an initiatory craft with a coven structure, a seasonal calendar and an inherited book copied by hand."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ModernWicca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Moderne Wicca",
        "en": "Modern Wicca"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Presented at its founding as the survival of an old religion. The construction is documented in detail, so the tradition and the refutation of its pedigree can be held together without either being lost."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Gardner, Witchcraft Today; the Book of Shadows in the published recensions",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Watcher named in the teaching list as the one who disclosed the undoing of spells."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Armaros",
      "label": {
        "de": "Armaros",
        "en": "Armaros"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Armârôs",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Hermani"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShemihazahTeachesRootCutting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa offenbart das Wurzelschneiden",
        "en": "Shemihazah discloses root-cutting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:RootCutting"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Shemihazah"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the gentler form of the practice, without the forceful breathing, produces the same effect as the form the tradition prescribes."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HeatWithoutForcefulBreath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Hitze entsteht ohne den kraftvollen Atem",
        "en": "The heat arises without the forceful breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it is the point where the method draft parts company with the transmission it draws on. The transmission prescribes the forceful breath; the draft says it can be left out. That is a claim about the draft's own variant and belongs to nobody else, which is why it is posited and not sourced."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#318",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the one part of the account that leaves objects a later worker can count and date without asking anybody: the great masks carved for each sixtieth-year ceremony and kept in rock shelters beside their predecessors, and the baobab-fibre ferment-holders woven for each and hung in the hogon's house. The account infers from series of nine, of eight and of fourteen that ceremonies were held back to the fifteenth and, on one reading, the twelfth century."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiguiSeriesExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Masken- und Fermentträgerserien als datierbarer Nachweis der Zeremonie",
        "en": "The mask and ferment-holder series as a datable record of the ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered because it is the falsifiable half of the account and the half nobody examined. The astronomical claims cannot be tested against anything but memory; wood and fibre can be dated. The protocol asks whether the intervals between the objects of a single shelter are compatible with sixty years, and whether the oldest are as old as the inference requires.",
          "No case series has been collected here and none is known to this graph. The state is therefore procedure without cases and not cases without deviation: nobody has carried the examination, which is a different report from having carried it and found nothing. See prima-materia#253 for the radiocarbon and dendrochronological work on Bandiagara material that would have to be searched before this protocol can be advanced."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A dated series from one shelter whose intervals depart from sixty years beyond what the dating method's error admits, or whose oldest object is materially younger than the count of objects requires. A single shelter dated cleanly against the count would equally carry the claim.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Iranian transmission of a dualist cosmology, of a soul journey undertaken under a named preparation and reported on return, of an accounting at a bridge whose width answers to the traveller, and of purifications stated in numbers of holes, washings and nights."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Zoroastrian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zoroastrische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Zoroastrian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Arda Wiraz Namag, chapters 1 to 5, in the Haug and West translation; Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2 and 3, in Darmesteter's translation; Vendidad, fargards 8 and 9, in Darmesteter's translation. Not opened: Yasht 19 on the glory, Menog i Xrad, Dadestan i Denig, the Denkard, and the Kirdir inscriptions. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#489.",
          "The contact route stays at disputed and is not moved, but the reason is now sharper than at registration. The earliest dated Iranian witness of an otherworld journey is not literary but epigraphic, in the inscriptions of the mage Kirdir under Wahram the Second, that is 274 to 293; the Book of the Watchers is third or second century before the era. Every Pahlavi book read here reached its surviving shape in the ninth century or later. Whoever wants to read the Iranian material as the source of the Jewish and Christian ascent literature must argue from the Avestan passages and not from these books. See prima-materia#490.",
          "Two editions stand behind the wording used here and they are of different weight. Haug and West of 1872 rests on a single, partly Pazand-contaminated witness; Gignoux 1984 and Vahman 1986 are the critical editions and were not reachable in full. Where a number in this file matters, it was read in the Haug and West text and the note on the node says so."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Avesta (Gathas, Yasna, Vendidad, Yashts); Hadhoxt Nask; Arda Wiraz Namag; Bundahisn; Denkard; Menog i Xrad; Dadestan i Denig",
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), chapters 1-5",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TunkanYieldsDistantKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Aussenden der Steine bringt Wissen um Unerreichbares hervor",
        "en": "Sending the stones yields knowledge of what is out of range"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at first-person report and not at field attestation, because the two cases that carry it were told by the man who says he did them, in his own words, to the recorder. What the recorder supplies is the setting; the claim is his informant's.",
          "This is the outside-sphere witness this graph has been short of. The generalization at pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState rested on four transmissions, of which one was North American; this adds a second, from a different language family and a different region, with a stated instrument and two reported cases. The independence is not thereby established: the recording is of nineteen eleven to nineteen fourteen and the objection raised about the Midewiwin material applies here too. What it is not subject to is the mission route that governs the doctrinal nodes of this file, since the recorder notes of one of these informants that the missionaries had made no impression on him."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:firstPersonReport"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:TunkanSending"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:TunkanDistantKnowledge"
      },
      "source": "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 210",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A luminosity held to arise at the passage into sleep, which the practitioner is trained to recognise and hold rather than to pass through unconscious."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ClearLightOfSleep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das klare Licht des Schlafs",
        "en": "The clear light of sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan 'od gsal, Sanskrit prabhāsvara. The state is not that of a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; it is a luminosity placed at the moment of falling asleep, and its purpose in the sources is the recognition of emptiness and the preparation for dying, not the acquisition of a faculty."
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Geistgemachter Körper",
        "en": "Mind-made body"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that when the tyrants of all the aeons made war on the light they were all made powerless and fell down like the dead, and that a third of the power of each was taken, and that fate and the sphere over which they rule were turned, so that they spend six months turned to the left and complete their influences and six months looking to the right and complete theirs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaTertiaPars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Drittel der Kraft von den Herrschern genommen",
        "en": "A third of the power taken from the rulers"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chapter reference given for this in the received reports is chapter 20. In the edition read it stands at chapters 15 and 16 and is restated by Mary at chapter 18. Anyone citing chapter 20 for it from a summary is citing a place the passage is not.",
          "The turning of fate is stated twice with a correction attached: the same passage says that Jeu, the overseer of the light, had originally set the spheres to look left at all times, so what is described is the reversal of an arrangement and not the invention of one."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 15-16 and 18 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that something passes from one person to another during contact, and that it enters through one named hand rather than the other."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromAPersonThroughTheLeftHand",
      "label": {
        "de": "Energie lässt sich aus einer Person über die linke Hand aufnehmen",
        "en": "Energy can be taken from a person through the left hand"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two claims in one sentence, and they are not equally reachable. That something passes is the general transfer claim of the section; that it enters through the left hand and not the right is a statement about sides, and a statement about sides is the kind that can be checked."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#328",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Japanese transmission of mountain austerity, in which power is held to follow from ordeals performed at named places in a fixed order."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Shugendo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Shugendō",
        "en": "Shugendō"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The self-mummification practice gives the project something almost nothing else does: a stated procedure with a documented outcome and documented failures, both counted."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Shozan engi; the Shugendō shūyō himitsu shū",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A ceremony in four graded forms, undertaken to fulfil a vow, to secure supernatural aid for another, to secure it for oneself, or to gain supernatural powers for oneself; the fourth purpose requires the fourth form. The candidate takes a mentor, is vitalized, seeks a communication, is consecrated with his tipi and implements, keeps eight rules until the dance begins, fasts and takes no drink from the beginning of his last day in the sacred lodge, and dances gazing at the sun and sounding a whistle while bound by thongs passed through wounds cut in his flesh, until he tears himself free. The forms differ only in the wounds and in what the thongs are made fast to: nothing, buffalo skulls, four stakes, or the sacred pole, with or without the dancer's feet leaving the ground."
      },
      "id": "pmp:WiwanyangWacipi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wiwanyang wacipi, der Sonnenschautanz",
        "en": "Wiwanyang wacipi, the sun-gazing dance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corpus distinguishes the ceremony from the dance and calls the dance the culminating rite of it. The whole is timed by four signs read off the world and not by a count of days: when the buffalo are fat, when new sprouts of sage are a span long, when the chokecherries are ripening, and when the moon is rising as the sun is going down. The second witness gives the time as the full moon of midsummer, when the trees are in full leaf, the June berries are ripe and the wild sage is fully grown.",
          "The second witness names the men who danced and how often, with the counts they gave: one who acted as intercessor four times and danced four times, once suspended; one who received a hundred cuts on his arms at twenty-four; one who danced four times at twenty-one, twenty-three, twenty-four and twenty-five, with about twenty of the last hundred cuts made on the chest for want of room on the arms. Counts of this kind are rare in this graph and are recorded because they are what a later reader can weigh."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 88-90 and pp. 98-99",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 60-62 and pp. 92-93 and pp. 116-120"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HathaChapterOrder",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Handbuch ordnet Haltung, Atem, Siegel und Versenkung",
        "en": "The manual orders posture, breath, seal and absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The four chapters run posture, breath, seal, absorption, and that arrangement is often cited as though it were itself a rule. The rule is in II.1; the chapter order is only an arrangement."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NadiPurification"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:presentationOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Asana"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I-IV (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late twentieth century transmission treating belief as a tool to be adopted and discarded, and holding that the symbolic system employed is arbitrary if the technique is sound."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChaosMagic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chaosmagie",
        "en": "Chaos magic"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim that content is interchangeable and only technique carries effect is directly examinable, and if it held it would reorganise every convergence question the project asks."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Spare, The Book of Pleasure; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A named case from Loango. A man was divined to be the owner of the leopard that had killed another man's wife; he denied it, was told by the ruler that nobody would believe him and that the decision lay with God, went to another town, demanded the ordeal, took it and vomited, and was thereby innocent. His accusers held instead that he had used his knowledge of the black art to escape the proper effect of the bark, and left their own town rather than live beside him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AcquittalDeniedByAscribingEvasion",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Freispruch, den man durch behauptete Umgehung entwertet",
        "en": "An acquittal denied by ascribing evasion"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The counterpart within the ordeal to the failure attributions on the charged things: an outcome that clears a man is not accepted as clearing him, and the exit is a capacity ascribed to him which the procedure cannot test. Recorded with the case because the case is named and dated by the recorder and can be argued with."
      },
      "source": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), pp. 147-148",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A needle driven into one of the depots is said to kill after a stated interval, and each interval is paired with a bodily sign by which the strike is to be recognised. The heart: death in one day, the movement belching. The liver: five days, speech. The kidney: six days, sneezing. The lung: three days, coughing. The spleen: ten days, swallowing. The gall bladder: a day and a half, vomiting. The same chapter continues with strikes that maim rather than kill and names the outcome for each: into the brain through the occipital opening, death on the spot; the vessel in the face, blindness; the marrow between the vertebrae, a bent back; the knee cap, lameness; the arm's great yin vessel, immediate death from loss of blood."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CizhongWuzang",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Treffer ins Depot: die Frist und das Zeichen",
        "en": "Striking a depot: the interval and the sign"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered because of the form and not the content. This is a claim that names an outcome it excludes: a person struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by any weaker reading of the sentence, and the sign attached to each interval says what the strike is supposed to look like while the interval runs. Very little in this graph is stated that way.",
          "The intervals are not stable across the witnesses, and the transmitted text says so about itself. That is held in the dispute node below rather than in this definition, which gives the numbers as the recension read prints them."
        ]
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Cizhen"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TrainingPresupposesPreliminaries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Training setzt die Vorbereitungen voraus",
        "en": "The training presupposes the preliminaries"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:GenerationStage"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Preliminaries"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "By succession, the son inheriting from the father, and where the heir is too young the nearest kinsman standing in until he is fit. By election, where succession fails, the other ministers choosing whom they judge fittest with the agreement of the lords. Or by a man taking the office himself, which is said to be ordinary among the old for the lesser offices of diviner and healer, and to be done for a living."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OfficeEnteredThreeWays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drei Wege in das Amt",
        "en": "Three ways into the office"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The third way is entered with the others because the recorder gives it the same weight and because it is the one a later reader would drop. A transmission that admits self-appointment for its lesser offices is saying where its own boundary is soft."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome claimed for the worn form: the names shall deliver the wearer from his enemy, and he shall escape from him by the will of God."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianDeliveranceByTheWillOfGod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Errettung vom Feind nach dem Willen Gottes",
        "en": "Deliverance from the enemy by the will of God"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The clause at the end is the whole difference between this node and the one beside it. A man who wore the names and was struck has not shown that they failed; he has shown that it was not willed. The same volume states the same protection three lines earlier without that clause."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SoulJourneyIsDisplaced",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Seelenreise ist ein verlagerter Ort",
        "en": "The journey of the soul is a displaced locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:displacedLocus"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:SoulJourney"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The reports have the soul leave the body and return to it. That is the criterion for this value, and it is what the accounts of Aristeas and Hermotimus turn on; whether anything of the sort occurred is a separate question and not this one."
      },
      "source": [
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect claimed for the names at the door: that no devil shall draw nigh, that the man shall be smitten neither by the arrows of his adversary nor by the stone hurled at him nor by the spear, and that the angels shall protect him and keep him in safety under their wings."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianKeepingOfTheDevilsInRestraint",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die abgehaltenen Teufel und die fehlgehende Waffe",
        "en": "The devils held off and the weapon missing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two kinds of harm stand in one sentence, and the second is the one that can be looked at. Whether a devil drew nigh is not an event anybody records; whether a man was struck by a stone or a spear is."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer I, p. 216",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The saying with which the teaching opens and the gloss given to it: blessed is he who has crucified the world and whom the world has not crucified; and he who has crucified it is he who has found the word of the speaker and has fulfilled it according to the will of him who sent him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuMundumCrucifigere",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wer die Welt gekreuzigt hat",
        "en": "He who has crucified the world"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the gloss identifies the act with a finding and a fulfilling and with nothing else — no rite, no seal, no name. In a corpus whose reputation rests entirely on its apparatus, the opening states the condition without any of it."
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 1 and 3 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The family a scale belongs to."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Scale",
      "id": "pm:scaleFamily",
      "label": {
        "en": "scale family"
      },
      "range": "pm:ScaleFamily",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the control decides. The transmission holds that the teaching had to be collective and not individual, that the spirits divided the work by scattering the subjects of study across many places, and that a centre formed of itself to compare what came in and to distinguish an individual opinion from the general teaching."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PrincipeDeLaDoctrine",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Zugehörigkeit eines Grundsatzes zur Lehre",
        "en": "A principle's standing as part of the doctrine"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, La Genèse, les miracles et les prédictions selon le spiritisme, chapitre I, articles 51-54",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The end named for those who have gained gnosis: they go in a band to the Father, of their own selves make surrender of themselves to the powers, and becoming powers are in God."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticFinisBonus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eins werden mit Gott",
        "en": "To be made one with God"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Placed on the telic axis as pm:unitiveTelos would be a reading and is not made here; the wording carries both a becoming-powers and a being-in-God, and which of the two is the end is exactly what a placement would have to decide. Left unsituated deliberately, so that the absence means nobody has decided rather than that the source is silent."
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a rank is set up corresponding to the treasuries and placed as watchers at the gate, three of them standing there, and that these watchers do not belong to the ranks of the treasuries of the light."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuCustodesAdPortas",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei Wächter stehen am Tor und gehören zu keinem Rang",
        "en": "The three watchers stand at the gate and belong to no rank"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The exclusion is stated and is the interesting half. A gatekeeper who is not a member of what he guards cannot be passed by belonging to it, and that is precisely the move the ophite speeches make when the passer names what he already bears. The two corpora are in the same milieu and give the gate two different logics."
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 6 and 8 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the stars, given in place of the constellations in the Aramaic-Greek witness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sterne",
        "en": "Signs of the stars"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteSuriel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Suriel, der Stiergestaltige",
        "en": "Suriel the bull-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Suriel",
      "pm:nameRole": "second of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, bull-like",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit",
        "en": "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:presentationOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Anapanasati"
      },
      "source": "Majjhima Nikāya 118",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Four transmissions make the same claim about spoken ritual language: the effect is not obtained unless the words are rendered as they stand. Three of them also say what a lapse does. One holds that an error, and equally a hesitation, is fatal to the efficacy of the address. Another holds that an error in a song may be fatal to the ceremony and that in some cases a single syllable does irreparable harm. A third requires the publicly chanted parts to be letter perfect and, in the passages read, states no consequence. The fourth names a breath taken before the close of a phrase alongside mistake and hesitation, and says the ill-luck falls on the party the chant honours rather than on the reciter."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Genauigkeit der Rezitation ist als Bedingung ihrer Wirksamkeit gesetzt",
        "en": "The exactness of the recitation is set as the condition of its efficacy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A defect in the vocabulary shows at this node and is recorded rather than worked around. pm:contactRoute is a property of a single tradition, but contact is a relation between two. The value pm:contactRouteAbsent as the registry uses it means that no route runs to what the graph already held at the time of registration; it cannot answer whether a route runs between two registered traditions that are later brought together in one correspondence, which is exactly the question a convergence asks. See prima-materia#101 for the vocabulary defect and prima-materia#102 for the registry entries it affects.",
          "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-04 and it is expressly not a fourth independent one. The Hawaiian transmission holds that a breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word is a sign of ill-luck. It stands beside the Maori witness and not beyond it: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so for the independence question the two Polynesian legs count as one. The count of transmissions carrying the rule goes from three to four; the count of independent ones does not move. Holding both numbers is what SPEC section 14 asks for, and this is the case that shows why one number alone would mislead.",
          "The counter-search did not produce a tradition that denies the claim. What it produced is of a different kind and belongs here: the recorded versions of the same Māori address differ from one another across the eighteen-forties to the early nineteen-hundreds, and which rendering was valid was disputed among Māori at the time. A doctrine of one correct wording and a transmission that visibly carries several are not a contradiction, but they are the point at which the claim becomes examinable instead of edifying. The place that says so has not been read at the wording and stands at prima-materia#110.",
          "Three witnesses agreeing on the form of a rule is a weaker finding than it looks unless the alternative is stated. The alternative exists and is common: traditions in which the sense of a prayer carries it and the wording may be varied, and traditions in which improvisation is the mark of the competent practitioner. That the three named here all fall on the fixed side is what the correspondence asserts. Where the rule holds and where it is absent has not been surveyed; the spheres left unsearched stand at prima-materia#116, the Indic parallel at prima-materia#109.",
          "What the fourth witness does add is a different thing, and it sharpens the correspondence rather than padding it. First, it names who suffers: the ill-luck falls on the person or family the chant honours and not on the reciter who made the fault, so the party that could report the lapse and the party that bears it are different people. Second, it names a countermeasure the transmission itself takes: a master of song gathers two or more of his fellows to edit and memorise the lines, and genealogies must be memorised by more than one reciter. A transmission that builds redundancy against its own stated failure condition has treated the condition as a real risk, which is evidence about the practice that a bare threat is not. Third, the same tradition sets the opposite rule for a different kind of utterance in the same file: at the consecration of the hula altar what must not lapse is the continuity of the voice, and the teacher is expressly free to change the prayer to avoid a silence. The exactness rule is therefore not a general doctrine about ritual speech even where it holds, and the correspondence should not be read as one.",
          "What the node claims and what it does not. Independent attestation is asserted for the two North American transmissions with respect to the Polynesian one and with respect to everything else this collection holds; no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the periods in which any of these corpora were fixed. It is not asserted between those two: they are neighbours in one region, and a route between Pueblo and Athabaskan ceremonialism is neither absent nor established here. The Māori witness is therefore the third leg and not a third independent one, because its own contact route is entered as disputed."
        ]
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:HawaiianChantExactness"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "Not a claim of priority in time. The two American corpora were fixed in eighteen ninety-seven and, from fieldwork of the nineteen-twenties, in nineteen thirty-two, both after the Polynesian witness was printed in eighteen eighty-two. What carries the independence is that no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the period in which any of the three corpora were fixed, so agreement between them cannot be one statement counted twice. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide: both American corpora were gathered by ethnographers working in a discipline to which the rule of exact recitation was already familiar, and neither passage says whether the rule was volunteered in the field or asked for. The second is the gathering question and it stands open.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": [
        {
          "id": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
        "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36",
        "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 615-616",
        "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), sections 41-42, pp. 24-25"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."
      },
      "id": "pm:locusNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ort nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Locus not stated"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessLocus",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Yoruba divinatory transmission: an enumerated set of signs obtained by casting, each sign carrying narratives that a diviner is expected to hold by memory and to recite on the sign falling."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Ifa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ifá",
        "en": "Ifá"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run: the section on Ifa in chapter II of the account of eighteen ninety-four, and its chapter IV for what that author says about the origin of the god; and the appendix of the volume of nineteen hundred and six, which reprints an extract on Ifa by a Yoruba bishop and a second extract by a Sierra Leonean author on geomancy. Left unread: the remaining chapters of both volumes, and the twentieth-century monographs named in the principal corpus, which are in copyright and available only as lending copies. The text of eighteen ninety-four was read in an electronic reproduction that carries no printed pagination, so the places are given by chapter and section and not by page.",
          "The closest thing to a written corpus that was never written. It is fixed, enumerated, internally ordered and transmitted through a pupilage of stated length, which is what made it the entry point chosen from the registry.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and the correction is the main finding of the run that opened this file. The registry had recorded no route because none was looked for. One of the two extracts read here identifies the sixteen figures of the oracle with the apparatus of geomancy — twelve houses of the heavens, two witnesses, a judge and a further judge — which is the apparatus of the Arabic sand science and, through it, of material this graph already holds on its Mediterranean side. Until that identification is settled, an agreement between this tradition and anything already in the graph counts as neither reception nor independent attestation. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all.",
          "Two of the works cited in this file were read at second hand, in the appendix that reprints them. Neither extract carries an imprint there, so neither citation gives place or year. A separately published copy of the bishop's pamphlet was searched for and not found: under both the author's name and the confusable name of the Yoruba historian, in the large text archives and in a library catalogue aggregator, and in a bibliography of early Yorùbá books. The nodes resting on those extracts therefore carry pm:mediatedAttestation and name the appendix with pm:readVia, and they remain open posts."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Odù corpus as published in Bascom, Ifa Divination, and Abimbola, Ifá: An Exposition of Ifá Literary Corpus",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The body of narratives through which a sign delivers its answer, given in parables. One thousand six hundred and eighty are counted to each Odu, and everyone aspiring to the office of babalawo is expected to commit them, together with those of the other signs, to memory."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaVerseCorpus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die den Zeichen anhängenden Erzählungen",
        "en": "The narratives attached to the signs"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the stories attached to the Odu",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Eight bodily signs held to arise from the inner constitution rather than from will: paralysis, perspiration, horripilation, change of voice, trembling, change of colour, tears, and fainting."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SattvikaBhava",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unwillkürliche Körperzustände",
        "en": "Involuntary bodily states"
      },
      "source": "Nāṭyaśāstra VII",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SanskritDramaturgy"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZangYieldsYin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bestattung auf dem lebendigen Qi bringt die Beschirmung der Nachkommen hervor",
        "en": "Burial on the living qi yields the shading of the descendants"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Zang"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:YitiShouyin"
      },
      "source": "Zangshu, inner section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded by Kardec on answers obtained through mediums and arranged as a doctrine, with a stated rule for admitting a principle into that doctrine and a stated rule for judging which spirit is speaking."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Spiritism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Spiritismus",
        "en": "Spiritism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Le Livre des Médiums, chapter XXIV, sections 263 to 268, and chapter XXVI, sections 286 to 288; L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, the introduction, part II, under the heading of the universal control of the teaching of the spirits; La Genèse, chapter I, articles 51 to 55. Not opened: Le Livre des Esprits, the chapter on obsession in Le Livre des Médiums, and the Revue Spirite. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#497.",
          "The contact route stays at documented, as the registration had it. Nothing in this transmission is a candidate for independent attestation: it is a nineteenth-century European construction with named authors, a dated founding text and a documented reception, and its value to this graph lies elsewhere - in being the one body of material in the collection that states its own rule of verification in writing.",
          "The editions are a difficulty this file does not hide. Kardec renumbered and enlarged Le Livre des Esprits between 1857 and 1860, from about five hundred questions to about a thousand, and everything since is cited by the later numbering. The digital witnesses read here carry the standard numbering and do not name the printing they were set from. The sections cited are therefore findable but the edition is not stated, which SPEC section 10 requires. See prima-materia#498."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kardec, Le Livre des Esprits; Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums; Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme; Kardec, La Genèse; the Revue Spirite, 1858-1869",
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-268 (French text in the standard numbering; the digital witness read does not name its printing)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InnerHeatFromBreathAndDirectedAttention",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wärme aus gehaltenem Atem und gerichteter Aufmerksamkeit",
        "en": "Heat from a held breath and attention directed into the trunk"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is nonetheless the most examinable generalization the holding can currently form, and for a reason that has nothing to do with the number of witnesses: it names a quantity an instrument reads. The two clauses of the statement are separately refutable — a rise that a matched untrained person shows from the breathing alone refutes the second without touching the first.",
          "The weakness is a route and it is stated rather than smoothed over. The Tibetan material received the Indic; these are not two independent witnesses but one transmission at two stations, and the count here is therefore one and not two. The class does not require independence, but a reader ordering the queue by the spread of the traditions under a generalization must see that this one has none.",
          "Two attested claims: the Tibetan tummo instruction and the root seal of the Haṭhayogapradīpikā. Two is the floor the class sets and not a recommendation, and this node stands on the floor.",
          "Two protocols in examinations/tummo-heat.ttl already address the tradition-level claim and the question whether the gentler form works without the forceful breath. Neither points here, and neither is extended by this node: a protocol on a generalization has to be devised against this wording, and the difference between the two levels is exactly what the wording adds."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:generalizedFrom": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:TummoYieldsInnerHeat"
        }
      ],
      "pm:generalizedStatement": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Practices that combine a retained or forcefully driven breath with attention directed to a named region of the trunk raise the practitioner's measured peripheral temperature above what the same person shows at rest under the same ambient conditions, and by more than the breathing alone produces in a person not trained in the practice."
      },
      "type": "pm:Generalizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The axis on which a source's account of who acts is placed: the practitioner, or another to whom the practitioner has yielded."
      },
      "id": "pm:AgencyHolding",
      "label": {
        "de": "Trägerschaft der Handlung",
        "en": "Agency Holding"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When a shaman dies among the Northern Tungus his soul must be taken to the lower world by another shaman who is stronger than the deceased. The spirits left behind often want to keep the dead man in their company, so the one who performs the operation must first fight them. If he is not certain of being able to master the spirits of the deceased, he must not undertake it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstCarryingAnotherShamansSoul",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die letzte Überführung nicht im Zweifel übernehmen",
        "en": "Do not undertake the last transport in doubt"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A prohibition addressed to the practitioner and conditioned on his own estimate of his strength, which is unusual: most cautions in this graph are addressed to the layman or to the beginner. If no sufficiently strong shaman exists, the soul is said not to reach the world of the dead and to disturb the clansmen without rest."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A question put in order to try the perspicacity of the spirits. The transmission classes it with the useless questions and with those put out of curiosity, and states the outcome: to those the spirits do not reply, and they withdraw. The founder warns against them expressly when he sets out how a question is to be framed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:QuestionDEpreuve",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Prüfungsfrage",
        "en": "The test question"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The French word épreuve carries both senses in this literature and the transmission uses both: the trial a soul undergoes in an incarnation, which it affirms, and the test question, which it forbids. A translation that renders them alike loses the distinction at the point where it matters most."
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "question d'épreuve"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, sections 286 and 288, item 3",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The social act by which a person becomes a clan shaman. The candidate must show a will and a proved ability to master spirits in at least one successful performance; the clansmen must want him. There follows a period of trial in which he must help sick clansmen, practise divination and interpret dreams. Only afterwards is he assisted in making the paraphernalia and in offering the regular sacrifice, and only then do people seek his assistance."
      },
      "id": "pmp:TungusFormalRecognition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die förmliche Anerkennung",
        "en": "The formal recognition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is an act with an outcome that can go the other way. Among all Tungus groups, he states, there are many persons who pretend to be shamans and are not recognised, so that people do not seek their assistance; a candidate may be rejected before the final trial on grounds of character, and among two candidates one may be elected and the other rejected and cured.",
          "The recorder rejects the words consecration and initiation for this act and gives his reason: nothing in it is sacred, and the candidate is already initiated before he attends. The term formal recognition is his and is entered as his; the procedure it names is reported."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 F, pp. 273-274, and chap. XXVIII, sect. 129-130, pp. 350-352",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Once a man or woman has become a shaman, he or she must go on to death or to psychic invalidity. Three reasons are given: by giving up control the shaman would leave the dis-mastered spirits to harm the clansmen, since in the presence of the old master the spirits will not easily submit to a new one; the person may again be affected by the condition that compelled him to become a shaman in the first place; and the clan holds that a shaman must not give up, so that breaking with it puts him in still greater trouble."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstLayingDownTheOffice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Amt lässt sich nicht ablegen",
        "en": "The office cannot be laid down"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "He closes the section with a judgement of his own — that a shaman's life is a continuous self-sacrifice. That is his and is recorded as his.",
          "The warning is against the release and not against the practice, which inverts the usual shape. The recorder states in the same section that if the shaman performs too often he may become the prey of his own spirits, so the office is hedged on both sides: too much practice and its abandonment carry the same named outcome."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:TungusShamanizing"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 140, pp. 380-381",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What one who is good at using the qi is said to do. He blows at water and the water runs backwards several paces; he blows at fire and the fire goes out; he blows at a tiger or a wolf and it crouches and cannot rise; he blows at a snake or a viper and it coils and cannot go. If another has been wounded by a blade, he blows at it and the blood stops at once. Where he hears that someone has been struck by a venomous creature, he blows and incants at a distance without seeing the person, over his own hand, the left for a man and the right for a woman, and though the other be a hundred li away he is healed on the spot. And for a sudden seizure it is enough to swallow the qi of the three nines, and it passes at once."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XuZhu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Anblasen von Wasser, Feuer, Tieren und Wunden",
        "en": "Blowing on water, fire, beasts and wounds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The distant healing is stated with a distance and an immediacy, a hundred li and on the spot, and with no condition attached to the patient. That makes it the most examinable claim in the chapter and the one furthest from anything the chapter offers to check it with.",
          "The laterality is the part this graph should not lose: the practitioner blows over his own left hand for a male patient and his own right for a female. A rule of that shape is not a description of an experience but an instruction that can be got wrong, and it is the kind of detail a later station adds rather than invents."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Associates a concept with one or more symbolic representations (sigils, diagrams, glyphs)."
      },
      "id": "pm:hasSymbolicForm",
      "label": {
        "en": "has symbolic form"
      },
      "range": "pm:Symbolizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The observation that a body or seat of awareness distinct from the physical one is claimed in the Pāli handbooks, in the Yogasūtra, and in Greek reports of men whose souls left them."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ExteriorisationCorrespondence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Entsprechung der Exteriorisation",
        "en": "The exteriorisation correspondence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Indian pair share a sphere and a technical vocabulary and are not independent of each other. The Greek reports are of a different kind entirely: they are second-hand ascriptions to figures long dead, made by historians rather than by practitioners, and a route of contact with the Indian material has been proposed but not shown."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:convergingConcept": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:ParakayaPravesa"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:SoulJourney"
        }
      ],
      "pm:independenceGround": "The Greek witness is fixed in the fifth century before the common era, before any documented route by which Indian technique could have reached the Greek world; a Scythian route has been proposed in the scholarship and not shown. The ground carries the independence and not the strength: the Greek report is a third-party ascription to figures long dead, made by a historian and not by a practitioner, which is a weakness of the testimony and not of its separateness from the Indian material.",
      "pm:independentAttestation": {
        "id": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
      },
      "pm:transmissionPath": "Theravāda and Pātañjala Yoga share the Indian sphere and a common technical vocabulary. A Scythian or shamanic route to the Greek reports has been proposed in the scholarship but is not documented.",
      "source": [
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.38 (Sanskrit sūtra text; received four-pāda numbering)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Converging"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim that moving air supplies the practitioner with something over and above respiration."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WindAsSourceProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Wind eine Quelle ist",
        "en": "Whether wind is a source"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered on no procedure devised, and the reason is worth stating rather than passing over. The neighbouring claim about cold names a boundary condition that instruments already measure, so it could be turned around within an afternoon. This one names no quantity that distinguishes it from breathing in still air, and until it does, there is nothing to fail.",
          "What would make it examinable is a difference the claim itself must state: the same breathing, in moving and in still air, with the two conditions expected to come apart in some named respect. That is a small addition to the claim and it is the whole difference between this node and its neighbour."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Nobody. The state records that no falsifying condition has been stated, which is a fact about the means of examination and not about the claim",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:EnergyTakenFromWind"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DieshiYieldsZhilai",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Abzählen bringt das Wissen des Kommenden hervor",
        "en": "The counting yields the knowledge of what is coming"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Dieshi"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:JishuZhilai"
      },
      "source": "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion and the section on prognostication, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the attempt to find work opposing the evidence has been carried."
      },
      "id": "pm:CounterSearchState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stand der Gegensuche",
        "en": "Counter-Search State"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A study cited under pm:evidenceFrom is a party and not an instance. It carries its own claim, its own method and its own critics, and a protocol that admits it without looking for opposition has not examined the claim against the world but against one report of the world. That is the same error as testing a transmission against this holding instead of against its source, which SPEC §13 forbids, and it is easier to commit because a study looks like a verdict.",
          "The state exists because the prose rule did not hold. It was written into AGENTS.md on 2026-09-02 after evidence was entered unread, and within the same session evidence was entered again without any search for opposition. A rule that has to be remembered at the moment of writing is a rule that fails at the moment of haste."
        ]
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:recordScale"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DiviningPresupposesPupilage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Befragen setzt die Lehrzeit voraus",
        "en": "Divining presupposes the pupilage"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage rather than of the tradition. The source sets the pupilage, its minimum length and the division of its course, and says nothing about what befalls a man who divines without it. A stated consequence would raise this to a necessity; none stands in what was read."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:mediatedAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:IfaCasting"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BabalawoPupilage"
      },
      "pm:readVia": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism",
      "source": "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The witness reports that a person would not dare to spit in the presence of anyone he feared might be disposed to injure him, if that person had a reputation for skill in this art."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MakutuCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nicht spucken in Gegenwart eines Kundigen",
        "en": "Not to spit in the presence of one skilled in the art"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A caution that names its own detection problem: the material the art needs is produced involuntarily, so the guard is against being present rather than against acting."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Makutu"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement between the recorders about what happens at the moment the whole transmission turns on. The indigenous chronicler of sixteen fifteen says outright that these priests deceived men, saying that the huacas eat and drink and speak, which they did not do. The extirpation account of sixteen twenty-one says that the one who speaks with the huaca answers the people what he pretends it tells him, and then adds that sometimes the devil does speak to them through the stone. The earlier report of about fifteen seventy-five says without qualification that they had many huacas and temples where the devil gave his answers."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhetherTheHuacaSpeaks",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Huaca spricht oder der Priester für sie",
        "en": "Whether the huaca speaks or the priest speaks for it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chronicler's denial has a provenance he states himself: he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. Whether that makes his denial better evidence, as an insider's, or worse, as a man writing for the side that was burning them, is exactly what a later reader has to weigh, and it is why the sentence is in this note.",
          "The disagreement is not the ordinary one between a believer and a sceptic. All three recorders are Christians who hold the huacas to be nothing or to be demons, and the one who denies the speaking outright is the one who is himself Andean. What separates them is whether the office rests on an event or on a performance, and that is a question about the transmission and not about their theology.",
          "What the three do agree on is that few laymen ever saw the principal huaca and that only the specialist went forward and spoke. That much is common ground between the denial and the two affirmations, and it is entered separately as its own node."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmp:MocharTheHuaca"
      },
      "source": [
        "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v",
        "Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno (1615), Copenhagen, Det Kongelige Bibliotek, GKS 2232 4°, p. 280 [282], the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom",
        "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III, on the huacapvillac"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission that relocates the alchemical work into the body, with a staged refinement of essence, breath and spirit and an embryo said to form and depart."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Neidan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neidan, innere Alchemie",
        "en": "Neidan internal alchemy"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian; Zhong-Lü chuandao ji; Xingming guizhi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The art of fixing speech in ink on paper, treated in the text as a transgression rather than a gift."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WritingWithInk",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schreiben mit Tinte",
        "en": "Writing with ink"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The author ascribes to his own great-uncle, the Immortal Duke, that whenever he was very drunk or the summer heat was great he would go into the bottom of a deep pool and come out after about a day, and that this was possible precisely because he could close off the breath and practise the embryonic breathing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GeXuanInTheDeep",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Großonkel, der einen Tag auf dem Grund eines Teiches lag",
        "en": "The great-uncle who lay a day at the bottom of a pool"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as an ascription and not as a report because the author says it of another and not of himself, and because he names the reason as well as the feat. The attestation is third-party for the same cause. It is also the only place in the chapter where a named person is given for a claim, which is what makes the length of the stay quotable at all."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Taixi"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that while the light is purified and carried into the treasury, the servitors of the rulers gather all the matter from them, and the servitors of fate and of the sphere below the aeons take it and fashion it into souls of men and of cattle, reptiles, wild beasts and birds, and send them down into the world of mankind; the material refuse is fashioned according to the circle of the rulers of that sphere and the configurations of its revolution."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaAnimaeExFaece",
      "label": {
        "de": "Seelen, aus dem Übriggebliebenen gemacht",
        "en": "Souls made from what is left over"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held because of what it does to the rest of this graph's ascent material. If a human soul is what remains after the light has been taken out, then the ascent is not a return of something that fell but a recovery by something that was made from the residue. The hermetic descent, where the seven give shares out of love, and this account of the same interval cannot both be right, and both are held.",
          "The fashioning is said to follow the configurations of the revolving sphere. That makes astrology a statement about the composition of the residue rather than about influence, and it stands beside the claim two chapters earlier that the casters of nativities can no longer announce anything — the same work removes the practice and keeps the mechanism."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A light held to be uncreated, and held to shine through the whole person, body as well as soul; the defence is explicitly that the body participates and is not merely the site of an event in the soul."
      },
      "id": "pmc:UncreatedLight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ungeschaffenes Licht",
        "en": "Uncreated light"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs."
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A treatise of the first century sets itself against the claim that the Changes penetrate the grounds of everything under heaven. Its arguments, in the order it gives them: the names of the stalk and the shell mean the aged and the old, so that consulting them means consulting the aged, and neither is spiritlike; heaven, if it has no ears, is not a body but a vapour, and a vapour cannot tell anybody anything; the dead stalk and the dead shell are asked about the living; and, reading the very passage of the great expansion, the division into two images heaven and earth, the counting by fours images the seasons and the remainder images the intercalation, which is imaging by likeness in order to set up the number of the figures, and says nothing about heaven and earth answering a man. Its own account of the hits is coincidence: what the shell is said to meet and the stalks to encounter is what was met with, not what was brought about, and following the answer does not necessarily bring fortune nor going against it misfortune."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZhanbuDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Stengel und Schale überhaupt antworten",
        "en": "Whether the stalks and the shell answer at all"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a dispute and not as a refutation because that is what it is: two works, one asserting and one denying, both read at the wording in this run, neither carrying a case. The value of having it as a node is that the denying side reads the same passage the asserting side rests on, and reads it as a construction by analogy rather than as a channel.",
          "The strongest move in the denying work is not an argument but a condition, and it is held separately as a protocol in examinations/. It is not in this node because a dispute records two positions and a condition is a third kind of thing."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:JishuZhilai"
      },
      "source": [
        "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on divining, juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zhouyi, Xici A, the sections on prognostication and on what the Changes are without, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The one interval the book states. Where the configuration is favourable and the shape unfavourable, one may still hope for a single day of fortune; where the configuration is unfavourable and the shape favourable, the misfortune does not turn a day. The commentary says that this is put so strongly in order to say how fast the answer comes, and elsewhere the book has a burial made at the hollow in the morning and wept for in the evening."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HuoBuXuanri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Unheil dreht keinen Tag",
        "en": "The misfortune does not turn a day"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the most examinable sentence found in the transmission and the reason the corpus was opened from the testability side. A doctrine that placed its effects a generation away could never be caught; this one places one class of them within a day of the burial, and the burial is a public and dated event."
      },
      "source": "Zangshu, outer section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The bearer to whom the capacity is ascribed: a named figure, a class of beings, humanity before a stated event."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Attributing",
      "id": "pm:ascribedTo",
      "label": {
        "de": "zugeschrieben an",
        "en": "ascribed to"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeartIsUnbound",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herzensgebet ist an keinen Zustand gebunden",
        "en": "The prayer of the heart is bound to no state"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:anchoringUnbound"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The prayer is prescribed as continuous, which is the point of it: not a set-apart exercise but something carried through whatever the practitioner is doing. The tradition is not declining the distinction between waking and sleeping; it requires the prayer across it."
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The society names a figure of its own kind who employs the powers for evil purposes, who can assume the form of any animal and in that shape destroy the life of his victim. The warning is not against an outsider but against a member who has passed the same degrees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BadMideCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der böse Midē, der die Vermögen zum Schaden wendet",
        "en": "The bad Midē who turns the powers to harm"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A tradition that catalogues capacities and warns against their use is the shape this collection meets in Pātañjala Yoga as well, and the two warnings are not the same warning. There the capacities are called obstacles to the goal of the practice; here they are called dangerous in the hands of a man who wants harm. One is a caution about the practitioner's direction, the other about his intention."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168-169",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MaoriEfficacyPresupposesExactRecitation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit einer Karakia setzt die fehlerfreie Wiederholung voraus",
        "en": "The efficacy of a karakia presupposes its repetition without error"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable. The same sentence is the tradition's own falsifying condition and is what makes the claim examinable at all; the protocol stands at pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination in convergences/recitation-exactness.ttl."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The witness says that to make an error, or even to hesitate, in repeating a karakia was held fatal to its efficacy. The consequence is stated for the whole address and not for a part of it, and hesitation is named alongside error.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:MaoriKarakiaEfficacy"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol the tradition states for itself. During the hair-cutting of a child under tapu nobody attending may eat; the witness says that a breach is discovered at once, because the skin of the child's head is cut while the hair is being cut, and calls this a sure sign."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TapuBreachSign",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die verletzte Kopfhaut als Anzeichen des gebrochenen Fastens",
        "en": "The cut scalp as the sign that the fast was broken"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because the tradition names an outward, observable event as the detector of a hidden breach and calls it certain. That is a falsifying condition offered by the material itself, which is rare enough in this collection to be worth holding as a protocol rather than as a remark."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case in which the fast was kept and the scalp was cut all the same, or in which the fast was broken and the scalp was not cut. The tradition claims the sign is sure, so a single clean counter-case on either side falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-08-30",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Tapu"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 42",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Called the offspring of apatheia and the keeper of the door to the contemplation of created things."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Agape",
      "label": {
        "de": "Liebe",
        "en": "Love"
      },
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 84"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "At least one passage has been read in a named edition and entered as a node. This is the state in which a tradition begins to witness."
      },
      "id": "pm:placesEntered",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stellen eingetragen",
        "en": "Places entered"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The form of the painted captions: a Persian word of greeting, then the grade in the dative plural, then the guardianship. The one at the head of the procession extends the greeting in space — hail to the fathers from the east to the west, under the guardianship of Saturn."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicNamaAcclamatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Heil den Vätern, vom Aufgang bis zum Untergang",
        "en": "Hail to the fathers, from the east to the west"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It is also the cult using a Persian word in a Latin sentence, in Rome, on a wall painted by Syrians. The word is not translated and not explained, which is how a term of address behaves when everyone in the room knows it.",
          "The grammar is the finding. The acclamation is addressed to the grade and not spoken by its holder, and it is addressed to all of them at once, in the plural: not to this father in this room but to the fathers, as far as the sun goes. What the wall records is therefore not a formula the initiate says but the standing of the rank he is entering, said over him."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, upper-layer dipinto above the seated figure, south wall (CIMRM 480, after Ferrua and Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Enoch literature: the Book of the Watchers, the Astronomical Book, the Book of Dreams, the Epistle and, in the Ethiopic canon alone, the Book of Parables."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Enochic",
      "label": {
        "de": "Henochische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Enochic tradition"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "G.W.E. Nickelsburg, 1 Enoch 1 (Hermeneia, 2001), introduction",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The purification Porphyry reports from within the cult: those initiated into the Leontic rites have honey poured over their hands in place of water, and are charged by the initiator to keep their hands clean of everything harmful, injurious and abominable; and their tongue is likewise purified of evil with honey. Other initiators into the same mysteries use fire instead, as a purifying agent."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicLeonticaMelle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Honig über die Hände derer, die in die Löwenweihe aufgenommen werden",
        "en": "Honey poured on the hands of those initiated in the rites of the Lion"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A named grade, a named substance, a stated substitution and a stated charge, all in one sentence — and the charge is a condition laid on the initiate rather than a promise made to him. That is rarer in this graph than any vision. What the passage does not say is what follows if the charge is broken.",
          "The substitution is the operative part. Water is what hands are washed with; honey is what preserves against decay, which is the property Porphyry has spent the surrounding pages establishing. The gesture therefore says that these hands are to be kept from corruption, not merely cleaned, and the alternative purification by fire says the same thing with the other means.",
          "Two further Mithraic details stand in the same passage and are held here rather than as separate nodes: the Persians offer honey to the guardian of fruits as a symbol of a preserving and warding power, and a bowl stands beside Mithras in place of a spring."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on honey in the Leontic rites (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 7 of that edition's division)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Those who burned llama fat and coca in the fire and saw in the shapes and the marks made at the time of the burning what was to come, and told it to whoever consulted them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FireReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Lesen am Verbrennen von Fett und Coca",
        "en": "Reading the burning of fat and coca"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The esoteric Theravāda transmission of Southeast Asia, working with syllables placed in the body and a nimitta led along an interior path."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BoranKammatthana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Borān kammaṭṭhāna",
        "en": "Borān kammaṭṭhāna"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Largely displaced by the modern reform movements and therefore poorly represented in every general account of Buddhist meditation."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Yogāvacara manuals of Sri Lanka and Siam in the published editions",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The judgement on the fourth hexagram states the only condition the canon attaches to consulting: at the first consultation it tells; asked twice and three times, that is defilement, and defiled it does not tell. The commentary on the judgement repeats the words and glosses the defilement as defiling the ignorance itself."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZaisanDu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zwei- und dreimal fragen entweiht, und entweiht antwortet es nicht",
        "en": "Asking twice and three times defiles, and defiled it does not tell"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a caution and not as a prerequisite because nothing is said to be reached or missed. The consequence is stated, unusually for this corpus, and the file records it in the definition for want of a predicate; see prima-materia#621.",
          "This is the node that decides how far this transmission can be examined at all, and it is worth having precisely because it is not a procedural rule. Nothing here says that a second casting is void, or wrong, or that the operator has erred. It says that repeating the question is an offence and that the answer is then withheld. The obvious test of any oracle is to put the same question twice and compare; the canon forbids exactly that and attaches no cost to the doctrine when the second answer differs, because by its own rule there should not be a second answer."
        ]
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Dieshi"
      },
      "source": "Zhouyi, hexagram 4, the judgement and the commentary on the judgement, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsFacundia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Orationen bringen die Wissenschaften binnen eines Monats",
        "en": "The orations confer the sciences within one month"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The term is the part that matters. Whoever pronounces the words at the determinate appointed time, setting all other occasions aside, shall profit in all the sciences in one month. A promise with a stated interval can be missed; the same promise without one cannot, and the corpus this graph holds gives intervals very rarely."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that binding an intended state to a strong affect shifts what the person attends to, how ready they are to act, how they carry themselves, and where their threshold for risk lies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kopplung eines Zielzustands an einen starken Affekt verschiebt den Koppelnden",
        "en": "Coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the one who couples"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately the weak reading. The neighbouring claim of the same section holds that the coupled state acts outside its carrier; that one is not examined here and nothing below bears on it. Separating them is the point: the weak reading is testable and the strong one is not, and running them together would let evidence for the first be read as evidence for the second."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#141",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol against the claim that the seal replaces study. The claim is unusually well placed for examination and the transmission itself supplies the reason: what it promises is not an inner state but a public performance before witnesses who can judge it. A man who sits in an academy and expounds what is forbidden and permitted either holds up under that or does not, and the people around him are competent to say which."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SarTorahExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das ohne Studium erlangte Gesetz",
        "en": "The Law obtained without study"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at procedure without cases, and the reason is that the passages carrying the numbers were not opened. The regime is said elsewhere in this literature to run forty days with immersions counted by the day, and to be annulled from the first day by a single lapse of purity; none of that was read at the wording here and none of it stands in this file. See prima-materia#496.",
          "This is the rarest thing in the collection: a claim about an acquired capacity whose success condition the tradition itself locates outside the practitioner, in a room full of people qualified to contradict him. Most capacities in this graph are reported by the one who has them."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded case of a man who completed the rite as the transmission prescribes it and could not afterwards expound what is forbidden and permitted better than before, judged by the academy the transmission itself makes the judge. The claim is that toil is replaced, so an outcome indistinguishable from the untrained falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:TorahWithoutToil"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SarielTeachesCourseOfTheMoon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sariel offenbart den Lauf des Mondes",
        "en": "Sariel discloses the course of the moon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:CourseOfTheMoon"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Sariel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Byzantine tradition of the prayer of the heart and of the vision of the uncreated light, defended in the fourteenth century against the objection that such a light must be created."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hesychasm",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hesychasmus",
        "en": "Hesychasm"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The Philokalia exists in Greek and in several translations whose contents and numbering differ, so a citation naming only the volume does not yet locate a text."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PenemueTeachesWriting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue offenbart das Schreiben mit Tinte",
        "en": "Penemue discloses writing with ink"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Book of Parables; no Aramaic fragment of chapters 37-71 is attested at Qumran."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:WritingWithInk"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Penemue"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "1 Enoch 69:9 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
        "J.T. Milik, The Books of Enoch: Aramaic Fragments of Qumrân Cave 4 (Oxford, 1976)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Lingshu states what it means for the qi to have arrived and to have had effect, and states it as a rule over the vessel and not over the patient. Where the operation drained, the vessel is to be as large as before but no longer hard; if it is hard as before, then although the patient says it is as it was, the illness has not gone. Where the operation supplemented, the vessel is to be as large as before and harder; if it is as before and not hard, then although the patient says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The chapter closes the rule from the other side: even where the pain does not follow the needle, the illness must be in retreat."
      },
      "id": "pmc:QizhiYouxiao",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ankommen des Qi und das zurückgewiesene Zeichen",
        "en": "The arrival of the qi, and the sign that is refused"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The reason for entering this rather than the many other success signs in the corpus. A success sign told to the practised beforehand is worth little, because he will report it; this passage names such a report and refuses it twice over, in the same sentence in which it gives the criterion that replaces it. The corpus therefore holds a stated distinction between what a patient says and what counts as the operation having taken, and the distinction runs against the interest of the operator.",
          "What the passage does not supply is a way of reading the vessel that two examiners would read alike. The criterion is hardness under the finger, which is why this is entered as a sign the tradition names and not as a procedure the graph can use."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The corpus is asked what it means by spirit and answers with a description of a perception that precedes the senses. The ear does not hear; the eye is bright and the heart opens and the intent goes first; he understands alone and clearly and the mouth cannot say it; all look and he alone sees; it is as though dark, and he alone is bright, as when wind blows the clouds away. The chapter sets this against the corresponding account of form, where what is sought is found by asking after the illness and searching it in the vessels."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dujian",
      "label": {
        "de": "Allein sehen, wo alle hinsehen",
        "en": "Seeing alone where all are looking"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it is the clearest statement in this corpus that a capacity of the practitioner is meant and not a technique. It is also the least examinable: the passage says in the same breath that the mouth cannot say it, so nothing in the description could be set against an outcome."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tradition holds that the distinction between acting and being acted through does not apply to what it teaches."
      },
      "id": "pm:agencyDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Trägerschaftsunterscheidung abgelehnt",
        "en": "Agency distinction declined"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AgencyHolding",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Before a seance the performers eat some of their medicines. The medicine goes into the stomach and is shaken through the body by the dancing, where it is held to become an active agent. The whole apprenticeship consists in the slow transfer of the knowledge of which plants the medicines are made from, paid for in a long string of fees, and a plant is known by stalk and leaf and not by root, which is why laymen may sit at the communal meal without learning anything."
      },
      "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Essen der Medizinen der Hexendoktoren",
        "en": "Eating the medicines of the witch-doctors"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89 and pp. 96-97",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Hawaiian transmission of a chanted genealogy of the world, of the mastery of chanting as an office with a stated condition of failure, and of the house of the hula with its conduct, its cleansing and its graduation."
      },
      "id": "pmt:HawaiianRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hawaiische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Hawaiian transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Coined as pmt:HawaiianCosmogony and renamed to pmt:HawaiianRitual on 2026-09-04 under prima-materia#471. The old identifier was written for the Kumulipo alone, and the reading widened the tradition, not the transmission: the file carries the mastery of chanting and the house of the hula, and a query that trusted the name would have taken a transmission of rite and office for a cosmogony. Renaming a coined identifier needs a compelling reason, and the reason here is that the name asserted something about the subject matter that the holdings contradict. The rename was carried in one pass, which is what SPEC §11 requires; nothing outside this repository is known to have referred to the old name.",
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Beckwith's Kumulipo, chapters one, three and seven; Emerson's Unwritten Literature of Hawaii, chapters two, four and five. Not opened: Malo's chapters on necromancy and on the kuni rite, the Fornander volume on the order of sorcery, Kamakau's newspaper series, and Pukui's article of 1949 which Beckwith cites for the chanting rule. The reasons and the paths tried stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#470.",
          "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and it is documented twice over. First against the Maori transmission this graph already holds: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so a correspondence between them is common inheritance and not two witnesses. Second against the Christian material: the mission arrives in 1820, the seminary at Lahainaluna is founded in 1831, and every work read here was written or printed after that by men trained in that school or by their successors. Absent was true of the islands before contact and false of the corpus this graph can cite."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Kumulipo in the Hawaiian text with Beckwith's edition; Malo, Moolelo Hawaii, in the Emerson translation and in the Langlas and Lyon edition; Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii; the Fornander Collection of Hawaiian Antiquities and Folk-Lore; Kamakau's series in Ke Au Okoa",
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on a dated prediction the account makes about itself. A footnote states that the next ceremony would probably fall in nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three if the reckoning holds, and adds that it was publicly known at the end of nineteen fifty that it would not be celebrated for another ten years or so."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SiguiDatePrediction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorhersage der nächsten Zeremonie im Artikel selbst",
        "en": "The article's own prediction of the next ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A rare thing in this collection: a claim that fixes its own due date, published before the date fell. Whether the ceremony fell in those years is a matter of record, and the record was not read at the wording in this run. The date is therefore not entered and the state stands at procedure without cases. See prima-materia#254."
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the account's own footnote; the date of the ceremony has not been established from a work",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A dated report of the ceremony beginning in a year other than nineteen sixty-one or nineteen sixty-three refutes the reckoning as the account states it, and with it the claim that the interval is governed by a fifty-year period doubled.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Sigui"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamadhiIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Sammlung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung",
        "en": "Concentration is placed at withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The handbook names concentration as the condition of the direct knowledges and develops it as the four absorptions, which it describes as awake and turned from the senses to a single object. The coordinate is read off that development and not off the word itself; concentration in a weaker sense would not carry it."
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "When the feast is over, guinea pigs are sacrificed in some places and it is seen by which part the blood runs in the entrails whether they have fasted well and kept all the ceremonies of the feast. The last act is named, and the name is given in the source."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FastKeptWellIsAlsoChecked",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Fasten gehalten wurde, wird nachträglich geprüft",
        "en": "Whether the fast was kept is checked afterwards"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A second procedure of the same shape as the confession test and applied to a different object: not to a statement but to a period of conduct. Together with the confession test it makes the checking of the participant, rather than of the answer, a recurring feature of this transmission and not a single case."
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The vital breath or energy held to circulate in the body and to be susceptible of deliberate direction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Qi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Qi",
        "en": "Qi"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming (jade inscription on circulating the qi)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A famous idol of one province was consulted through its priests by an Inca passing with an army, about whether he would return victorious from a battle or die in it; the answer was that he would die, and so it fell out. From this the huaca came to have so great a name that people came from very remote provinces to consult it and to make offerings, and it grew rich and had a sumptuous temple. His son, passing later and seeing that greatness, and learning that it was the huaca which had announced his father's death, ordered the temple and all in it to be burned; the priests stole the idol out of the fire and carried it elsewhere and built it another temple."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CatequillaPrediction",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Huaca, die einen Tod voraussagte und dafür verbrannt wurde",
        "en": "The huaca that foretold a death and was burned for it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A stated hit, with its consequence stated too: the reputation of the huaca is derived from a single prediction that came true, by the recorder and presumably by the tradition he heard it from. It is the only place in this file where a claim about accuracy is made about a particular occasion rather than about a method.",
          "Entered as a claim standing in the work and not as a report of an event. Its chain is long — an extirpator writing in sixteen twenty-one about what another visitor was told about a consultation before the conquest — and no part of it was checked against another witness in this run.",
          "The second half is the more useful. The correct answer cost the huaca its temple, because the person it was correct about was the father of the man who burned it. A transmission in which an accurate oracle can be destroyed for its accuracy is not one in which reputation tracks accuracy, and the story says both things at once."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, on the idol Catequilla",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of ascribing a capacity, property, or deed to a subject. A lost faculty is never a finding; it is an ascription made in a work, and the work is what the graph can hold."
      },
      "id": "pm:Attributing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuschreiben",
        "en": "Attributing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The working handbooks of late antique Egypt: procedures with materials, hours, purity requirements, spoken formulae and stated signs of success."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri",
      "label": {
        "de": "Griechische und demotische Zauberpapyri",
        "en": "Greek and demotic magical papyri"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Dieterich says himself where he is certain that the received text already carried its own names, and the list is short: the vowel groups for the seven planets, the point where the gods are appeased, the opening of the disk, the appearance of the sun god, the protective cry at the gates, and the adjuration that the god remain in the operator's soul. Those places are the ones on which a reconstruction of the sounds may be attempted; the rest of the barbarous names he holds to be interchangeable stock that the magicians supplied from their own apparatus, so that even a perfect reading of the papyrus would not recover what the rite said there.",
          "Going behind Mead was done and lands on a third layer rather than on the papyrus. His source is Dieterich's edition, opened here in the second edition of 1910; Dieterich's source is Wessely's first reading of Paris papyrus 574. What Mead removed, Dieterich had already set in small type as the magicians' insertions, on his own judgement and not on any mark in the manuscript, and Mead's obeli are Dieterich's typography carried into English. The distinction between the liturgy and its magical redaction is therefore an editorial reconstruction from 1903, and every node in this file that speaks of what belongs to the rite is standing on it.",
          "The name is a modern label twice over. Dieterich called the piece a liturgy in 1903; Mead objects that a liturgy has several participants while this is a rite for one person alone, and whether it is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Neither question is settled here.",
          "Two properties of the translation first used bear on the nodes that cite it, and Mead states both himself. He removes the barbarous names he judges to be later insertions by an Egyptian magical school and marks the rest with obeli, so most of the names in the ritual are not in the text read; the one long vowel chain that is printed survives because he holds it to belong to the ritual. And he renders one of the two prescribed sounds as puff rather than pop because he takes it to be connected with breath, which is a reading and not a translation.",
          "What was read: the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (Echoes from the Gnosis VI, London and Benares 1907), public domain. Nothing else of the corpus is opened, and this is a single piece out of a codex of thirty-six pages."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Papyri Graecae Magicae and Papyri Demoticae Magicae, including the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829",
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source was read for this question and does not answer it."
      },
      "id": "pm:anchoringNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sinnesbindung nicht angegeben",
        "en": "Anchoring not stated"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Not the same as an untagged node, and the difference is what makes a query of the form 'only in this state' readable at all. No placement means nobody asked; this value means somebody asked and the source is silent."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite at Eleusis, held in the month Boedromion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:GreaterMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die großen Mysterien",
        "en": "The greater mysteries"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:PrayerPresupposesWatchfulness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herzensgebet setzt Wachsamkeit und Führung voraus",
        "en": "The prayer of the heart presupposes watchfulness and guidance"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The writers of this corpus set watchfulness and the direction of a spiritual father before the prayer, and describe the way as passing from purification through illumination to perfection. The chapter divisions vary between the editions of the individual texts; the works and their titles are given so that the passages can be found in whichever edition is at hand."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:PrayerOfTheHeart"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Watchfulness"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness",
        "Philokalia IV, Gregory of Sinai, On Stillness and the Two Ways of Prayer",
        "Philokalia IV, Nikephoros the Monk, On Watchfulness and the Guarding of the Heart"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Three positions are reported side by side. Some hold that those who cast themselves down are not really but only in appearance dead. Others hold that the soul departs from the body and, after its travel abroad, returns again. The compiler holds both to be false, on the ground that it is impossible for either man or devil to restore a soul to the body it has once left, and concludes that the devil only stifles the faculties of the soul for a time and hinders their operations."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was da stillliegt",
        "en": "What it is that lies still"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No dispute node is written, and the reason is a rule of this repository rather than a judgement about the material. A dispute cites each side from its own work; here all three positions stand in one compilation, reported by one man, and two of them without an owner. What the passage establishes is that the question was open among those the compiler drew on, not who held what.",
          "The compiler's own position is reached from a doctrine and not from the material: he rejects the departure of the soul because it would be theologically impossible, not because anything he was told tells against it. That is worth recording, because the same move settles the question for him everywhere else in the chapter."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 56",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The guarding of the heart and the attention to what arises in it, treated as the groundwork on which the prayer of the heart is built."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Watchfulness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wachsamkeit",
        "en": "Watchfulness"
      },
      "source": [
        "Philokalia I, Diadochus of Photice, Gnostic Chapters",
        "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hesychasm"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of the descent: Man, changing his state to the formative sphere, gazed on the creatures of his brother, and they loved him and each gave him a share of his own ordering; having learned their essence and become a sharer in their nature, he broke through the boundary of their spheres and showed the fair form of God to downward Nature. He is then said to be above the Harmony and within the Harmony a slave."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticDescensusPerHarmoniam",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jeder Herrscher gibt einen Anteil seiner eigenen Ordnung",
        "en": "Each ruler gives a share of his own ordering"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The descent is not a fall through disobedience and is not punished; it is granted by the Father and driven by love. The vices given back at the ascent are therefore not sins acquired against the order but shares of an ordering that was handed over. That distinction is lost in every summary that calls this a fall."
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.13-15 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnanaIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt",
        "en": "Knowledge of another mind is exercised in withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The sūtra immediately following adds a limit that belongs to the claim: what supports the notion is not thereby known, being out of the practitioner's reach. The capacity is bounded in the base text and not only by its readers."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The sūtra reads pratyayasya paracittajñānam: knowledge of another mind from the presented notion, with saṃyama carried over from the sūtras before it. The state is the operator's, fixed at III.1-4, and the placement is inherited from the binding the source itself makes."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.20"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The undertaking sworn by the pierced child-killer, by the seven ranks of the archangels, that she will not contend against the Name, will never journey along the path where it is to be met with, or enter any church wherein it is commemorated, or any house or place whatsoever, and that this prayer shall be placed upon children, young men or the full grown, unto the end of the world."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianOathOfWerzelya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Eid, auf dem der geschriebene Schutz ruht",
        "en": "The oath that grounds the written protection"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The reason for a node of its own is that the roll states its own mechanism, which is uncommon. The protection is not a power the wearer acquires and not a favour asked each time; it is the enforcement of a promise extracted once from the adversary, and the written object is what puts the wearer inside the scope of that promise. A protocol that wanted to unsettle the claim would have to unsettle the oath, and there is no proceeding for that."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:QiListeningPresupposesEmptying",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Hören mit dem Qi setzt das Leeren des Herzens voraus",
        "en": "Attending with the qi presupposes the emptying of the heart"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Reopened in this run and confirmed at the wording, with one correction. The consequence stood here as a paraphrase of the dialogue and is now quotable from the edition named. What the earlier wording added and the passage does not say is that the hearing which stops at the ears and the mind does not reach the qi at all; the text says the pupil is to wait for the third hearing and that the first two are to rest where they are, which is an instruction and not a statement about what fails to arrive."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The pupil sets out three ways of proceeding and is refused each time; the master answers that he has too many plans, that adhering to them may keep him free from transgression but that this will be all the result, that he cannot in this way produce the transformation he desires, and that all it shows is the mind of a teacher. Only when the pupil says he can go no further and asks for the method is he told that the method is the fasting."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Qi"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart"
      },
      "source": "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:FaYieldsForeknowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wurf bringt die Antwort hervor",
        "en": "Casting the nuts yields the answer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same form of claim the graph holds from the Zande poison oracle, the Ifá casting and, since the Kongo file, the bark ordeal: the answer is read off an outcome the operator is said not to steer. Here the route to one of the others is documented and stated on the tradition instance, so this node may not be counted as a further independent witness for it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Fa"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Fon practitioners at Abomey and Whydah, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the naturalist J. A. Skertchly during his detention in Dahomey in 1871, published 1874; given in Fon, of which the work prints the figure names and a few terms, the account itself in English.",
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ForeknowledgeFromFa"
      },
      "source": "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 475-476",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in the state, movement is accomplished by willing the destination, with no counterpart to the effort and the duration that bodily movement requires."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IntentionReplacesMusculature",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Absicht ersetzt die Muskulatur, der Ortswechsel geschieht durch Wollen des Ortes",
        "en": "Intention replaces the musculature and the change of place happens by willing it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim carries an implication it does not state: if willing the place is what moves, the movement has no duration that depends on the distance. That implication is what makes it comparable to what the transmissions report."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#305",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second of the two stages, to which the six yogas belong."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CompletionStage",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vollendungsstufe",
        "en": "Completion stage"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the last helper blew a portion of power into the mixture, that this power is mixed with all the invisibles, all the rulers and all the aeons, and that every human being holds a portion of it; the chain is given step by step, from the first commandment through the great light and the five helpers into the mixture, which is called the world of corruption."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaMixtura",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Anteil in der Mischung",
        "en": "The portion in the mixture"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A doctrine of a portion present in everyone, stated as a transmission through named stages rather than as a general truth. It is the counterpart of the previous node: the disciples are said to have something more, and this says what everyone has."
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 8 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The counting of the sixty-year interval by means that are stated to be mnemonic rather than astronomical: thirty biennial millet-beer drinkings, each marked with a cowry laid down by the eldest; a millet-porridge figure above the sanctuary door in which a point is added each year; and a painted line of chevrons called sigi lugu, count of the Sigui, each tooth standing for twenty years."
      },
      "id": "pmp:SiguiReckoning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Auszählen des Sigui-Abstands",
        "en": "Reckoning the interval between two Sigui"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorders report that the rule is theoretical and that the practice departed from it: between the Sigui of the beginning of the century and 1931 only one drinking was held, and the cowries for the missing thirty years were laid down together in a single heap afterwards. The reckoning that is described as regular is thus reconstructed in arrears, and the article says so."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 275-277",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Refining essence into breath, breath into spirit, and spirit back into emptiness."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NeidanThreeStages",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die drei Stufen des inneren Elixiers",
        "en": "The three stages of the inner elixir"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A dating warning belongs on this node. The sequence is routinely attributed to the Cantong qi or to the Wuzhen pian, but neither carries it as a fixed numbered progression; the Wuzhen pian sets only a precedence of vital force before inner nature, and of doing before non-doing. The earliest ordered witness located for the full triad is the Yuan-period Jindan dayao."
      },
      "source": "Chen Zhixu, Jindan dayao, juan 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The ascription under which the claim is commonly reported: the tradition, the age or the authority it is credited to and does not have."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Originating",
      "id": "pm:circulatesAs",
      "label": {
        "de": "zirkuliert als",
        "en": "circulates as"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of forming, from several attested claims, one statement that no source makes: a claim about a kind of practice or a kind of effect, worded so that an outcome could contradict it."
      },
      "id": "pm:Generalizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verallgemeinern",
        "en": "Generalizing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It carries a derivation instead of an origin, and the derivation is checkable in the same way a source is. pm:generalizedFrom points at the attested nodes it was formed from, so a reader can go back to the passages and disagree with the step rather than with the conclusion alone.",
          "No instance may carry dcterms:source or pm:withinTradition. A generalization that named a work would report as transmitted what no transmission says, and that is the one confusion this layer exists to prevent.",
          "That is also the whole safeguard against the direction the project must not run in. An unpublished draft has no attested predecessors: a sentence from a method draft cannot be generalized into the graph, because there is nothing for pm:generalizedFrom to point at. The class is therefore open where pm:Positing had to be locked, and locked where pm:Positing is open.",
          "The absence of a pm:Testing pointing at a generalization is not a defect and is deliberately not enforced. It is the queue: the generalizations nobody has taken up are exactly the ones a run should reach for, and a shape that required a protocol would make the queue unwritable, as it does for pm:Positing."
        ]
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to the witch-doctors: to see what is unseen, to tell who the witches are, to see the emanations of witchcraft floating about as small lights invisible to the uninitiated, and to resist a fatigue that the source says could not otherwise be borne."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Sehen der Hexerei und das Weissagen",
        "en": "Seeing witchcraft and prophesying"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The tradition holds that the distinction between a passing and an abiding condition does not apply to what it teaches."
      },
      "id": "pm:durationDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dauerunterscheidung abgelehnt",
        "en": "Duration distinction declined"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateDuration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act by which a claim entered the record. Instances of this class are modes of entry, not claims; a claim points at one of them through pm:attestedBy."
      },
      "id": "pm:Attesting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Bezeugen",
        "en": "Attesting"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Orthogonal to pm:AwarenessContext, which describes the state the asserter was in rather than the route by which the assertion reached the record."
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:recordScale"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1832",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The English naming of the dakṣiṇācāra and vāmācāra divisions of the Śākta sect as the followers of a right hand and a left hand ritual, together with the characterisation of the second as impure, as running contrary to what is usual, and as something its adherents will not publicly avow."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wiedergabe der beiden Śākta-Zweige als rechtshändiges und linkshändiges Ritual",
        "en": "The rendering of the two Śākta branches as a right and a left hand ritual"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an ordering act because the naming is regularly credited to Blavatsky, who wrote in 1877 and in 1888. It stands in Wilson forty-five years earlier, in the volume of the Asiatic Researches for 1832, in those terms and with the moral charge already attached. Whoever first coined it, it was not she, and a record that repeats the later attribution reports a reception as an origin. See prima-materia#57.",
          "No pm:ordersClaim is given, and the reason has changed. It used to be that the division this naming orders stood nowhere in the graph as a claim of its own. Since pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods it does, in a pre-colonial text, and the naming is therefore no longer the only witness to a pair. What is still missing is the link: Wilson orders what he found in Bengali Śākta circles of his own century, and that it is the same division the Assamese purāṇa states has not been shown. Asserting the link would make a shared vocabulary into a shared claim, which is the error this file exists to record. See prima-materia#53.",
          "The same section carries a ranking the naming does not survive. Wilson translates a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava that grades the Veda lowest and ascends through the Vaiṣṇava and the Śaiva to the right hand Śākta, then sets the left hand above the right, the Siddhānta above that, and the Kaula above all. In the material being ordered, the left is therefore the higher of the two; the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses the valuation. The verse is not located in an edition of the Kulārṇava here and is not held as a claim of that work. See prima-materia#56."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "Horace Hayman Wilson",
      "source": "H. H. Wilson, Sketch of the Religious Sects of the Hindus, Asiatic Researches XVII (Calcutta 1832), the sections headed Dakshinas, or Bhaktas and Vamis, or Vamacharis",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second group of seven in this witness: the serpent, cast down into the lower world, subdues the angels there and begets six sons, himself forming the seventh, after the example of the hebdomad that surrounds the father. These are called the seven mundane demons, said always to oppose and resist the human race."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteHebdomasInferior",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die untere Hebdomas der herabgestürzten Schlange",
        "en": "The lower hebdomad of the cast-down serpent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The doubling is therefore attested twice and independently of the diagram. Where the first witness has two lists side by side in one drawing and never equates them, this one states outright that the lower seven are formed after the pattern of the upper seven and are not the same beings. See pmc:OphiteDuaeHebdomades."
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.8 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Not all who address the huaca see it. Although everyone knows in which direction it stands, few see it, because they hang back and only the priest goes forward, speaks and offers. Places where a huaca is said to have rested, called zamana, and places from which it is invoked, called cayan, are reverenced too."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AddressWithoutSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Haupt-Huaca wird angesprochen, ohne gesehen zu werden",
        "en": "The principal huaca is addressed without being seen"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "In one village a woman who kept the huaca told the people to cover their eyes when they adored it, because the divine essence cannot be seen with bodily eyes. That is the transmission stating a reason for the arrangement, and it is entered because a reason given from inside is worth more than the recorder's explanation, which is that the devil had put fear into them.",
          "The arrangement has a consequence the recorder reports with satisfaction and which is a finding whoever reports it: when the villagers were finally made to look at what they had feared, that was itself the strongest argument against it. A cult whose object is addressed without being seen is a cult that can be undone by showing the object."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II and cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where the oracle contradicts itself the failure is attributed to one of eight causes: the wrong variety of the creeper having been gathered, the breach of a taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest in which the creeper grows, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Two varieties of the creeper are distinguished by name, and poison that kills without discrimination is set aside for some months to cool and is thrown away if it does not."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BengeFailureCauses",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die acht Ursachen, denen ein Versagen des Orakels zugeschrieben wird",
        "en": "The eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The list is the reason the falsifying condition on the neighbouring node does not function as one inside the transmission. Every occurrence of the condition is assigned to a cause that leaves the oracle itself untouched, and the recorder reports that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. That sentence is the recorder's summary of an attitude and not a quoted Zande statement, and it is entered as such.",
          "This is also the passage on which the whole later argument about whether Zande thought is closed against refutation rests. None of that argument was read at the wording in this run; it stands as an issue with the works named."
        ]
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 155 and pp. 155-157",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first stage of a secret knowledge, named with a Bambara expression. The account states that the rules of the Sirius system revealed to initiates at this stage are simplified in some parts and complicated in others, in order to turn attention away from calculations that remain more secret still."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Leichte Kenntnis",
        "en": "Light knowledge"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The practitioner acts, and what comes of it is credited to the practice."
      },
      "id": "pm:ownAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eigene Trägerschaft",
        "en": "Own agency"
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AgencyHolding",
        "pm:AwarenessValue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission's warning against the man who takes on the purification without the knowledge for it. The penalty stated for him is death: they shall cut the head off his neck and give over his corpse to the vultures. The reason given is not that the rite is wasted but that it works the wrong way: through such cleansing the sickness and the death that were in the world become stronger than they were before."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BarasnumCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegen die Reinigung durch einen Unkundigen",
        "en": "Against undertaking the purification without knowing the rites"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the strongest thing of its kind the graph holds. Most transmissions say that a botched procedure fails; this one says that it makes the harm greater than if nothing had been done, and it stakes a man's life on the rule. A claim that a wrong performance actively worsens the world is decidable in a way that a claim of mere failure is not, because the two predict opposite directions of the same measurable thing."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Barasnum"
      },
      "source": "Vendidad, fargard 9.47-49, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The axis on which the relation of awareness to the ordinary sensory channel is placed, from outward waking perception through withdrawal, the edge of sleep and dreaming to a condition in which no object is apprehended."
      },
      "id": "pm:SensoryAnchoring",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sinnesbindung",
        "en": "Sensory Anchoring"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondWeekPresupposesFirst",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zweite Woche setzt die erste voraus",
        "en": "The second week presupposes the first"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The same annotation that sets the order removes any fixed duration from it: the weeks need not have seven or eight days, and one who needs longer in the first is to be given longer. The order is set, the schedule is not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:SecondWeek"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:FirstWeek"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the oracle is consulted for: to learn beforehand how a matter stands and will fall out. The answer is delivered through the channel of parables, and the account of eighteen ninety-four reports that nothing of importance is undertaken without the consultation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaForeknowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vorauswissen über den Ausgang einer Sache",
        "en": "Foreknowledge of how a matter will fall out"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The captions painted above the procession of the seven grades on the south wall of the mithraeum beneath Santa Prisca in Rome, each naming a grade in the dative and the planet whose guardianship it stands under: the father under Saturn, the sun-runner under the Sun, the Persian under Mercury, the lion under Jupiter, the soldier under Mars, the bridegroom under Venus; the raven, of whom only traces remain, under the Moon."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicTutelaePlanetarum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jeder Grad unter der Obhut eines Planeten, neben seinen Träger gemalt",
        "en": "Each grade under the guardianship of one planet, painted beside its bearer"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "And the wall does not agree with the other wall. At Ostia the Persian stands under the Moon and the raven under Mercury; here the two are exchanged, the Persian under Mercury and the raven under the Moon. The catalogue entry records the divergence in a footnote and moves on. It is not a small thing: the received table follows Ostia, drops this one, and presents the result as the Mithraic order.",
          "This is where the pairing of grade and planet actually comes from. No literary source has it: Jerome names the grades without stars, and the only ancient text that distributes planets over a sevenfold Mithraic structure, Contra Celsum VI.22, distributes them over gates and not over grades. Every modern table that seats a grade on a planet is reading a wall.",
          "Three Mithraic orderings are now in this graph and no two agree. The ladder of Contra Celsum VI.22 ascends Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, Moon, Sun. This wall descends Saturn, Sun, Mercury, Jupiter, Mars, Venus, Moon. Ostia gives a third by exchanging two of them. Whatever governs the sequence, it is not one fixed doctrine, and an argument that derives any other seven-station scheme from the Mithraic order has to say which of the three it means. See prima-materia#595.",
          "What was read: the transcription of the seven dipinti in the catalogue entry for this monument, which itself depends in part on Ferrua's record of details already lost when the modern excavation began. Not the wall, not a photograph, and not the excavation report. Three of the seven captions are restored in part, and the seventh is a reconstruction from traces."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Mithraeum of Santa Prisca, Rome, upper-layer dipinti of the south wall (CIMRM 480, after Ferrua and Vermaseren-van Essen 1965)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The teaching that the figures met in the intermediate state are appearances of the one who meets them and not independently existing beings, and that recognising them as such is what frees."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AppearancesAreSelfArising",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Erscheinungen sind Selbst-Erscheinung",
        "en": "The appearances are self-arising"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan rang snang, self-appearance. A dating warning belongs on this node: the work is traditionally ascribed to Padmasambhava in the eighth century but is a treasure text of the fourteenth, revealed by Karma Lingpa; the older ascription is not held in the scholarship. Note also the scope. The doctrine holds of all appearance in this tradition, waking included, and is therefore not a statement about the threshold state in particular."
      },
      "source": "Bar do thos grol (Karma Lingpa recension, 14th century; trans. Gyurme Dorje 2006), the intermediate state of reality",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "In eighteen eighty-five men came from Luanda with the story of a fish that begged not to be killed and promised that whoever drank the water from its mouth, or water it had lain in, would never die by fair means or from natural causes, but only by witchcraft. The water was hawked through the country and widely bought, even at San Salvador."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kinyambi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kinyambi, das Wasser gegen den gewöhnlichen Tod",
        "en": "Kinyambi, the water that was to prevent an ordinary death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corpus notes that the promise conceded almost nothing, since every death is in any case attributed to witchcraft, and that it was nonetheless a great success. That is a claim that discriminates almost nothing by construction, and the transmission that made it is the one that noticed.",
          "The outcome is the reason the node stands beside pmc:Kiyoka: when it was seen that the purchasers died like ordinary mortals, the traffic ceased. The same corpus, thirteen years earlier and in the same districts, had recorded a failure that was explained away instead. The two responses to a failed promise stand two pages apart, and neither is the rule of the transmission."
        ]
      },
      "source": "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, p. 292",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to hear what is transpiring at a great distance, ascribed to a priest of the second degree and marked in the chart by lines drawn from the ears."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideDistantHearing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hören dessen, was in großer Ferne geschieht",
        "en": "Hearing what happens at a great distance"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The formation of a concept as an act within awareness; instances of this class are concept nodes such as pmc:Pleroma or pmc:Hen."
      },
      "id": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Begriffsbildung",
        "en": "Conceptualizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ZuyuaThanAnchoring",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zuyua-Verhör findet im gewöhnlichen Wachen statt",
        "en": "The Zuyua interrogation is carried out in ordinary waking"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:outwardWaking"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:ZuyuaThan"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": "The candidate walks in, is spoken to by the first head-chief, fetches the objects demanded and hands them over, and the outcome is executed on the spot. Nothing in the chapter withdraws the senses, alters the state of either party, or places the exchange anywhere but in the public day.",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect the fixed prayer is held to obtain when it is delivered as required."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZuniPrayerEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit des Zuni-Gebets",
        "en": "The efficacy of the Zuni prayer"
      },
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), p. 615",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ZuniRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source has the condition arrive without being worked for: given, inherited, conferred at birth, or descending on the wayfarer as a gift."
      },
      "id": "pm:notByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nicht durch Anstrengung",
        "en": "Not by effort"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Where a source says this, a missing prerequisite claim is a doctrine and not an omission. The Sufi handbooks say it outright of the aḥwāl; the Inuit narrative says it of second sight in another way, by reporting the capacity as something a person has."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:StateAcquisition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of whether contact at the brow differs from contact elsewhere on the head in what the recipient reports."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BrowContactProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob der Ort der Berührung einen Unterschied macht",
        "en": "Whether the place of contact makes a difference"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Procedure without cases. The design is the same as for the palms and inherits the same controls, with one addition that matters more here: the brow is the place the recipient has most often been told about, so an effect found without blinding him to the site would be an effect of the telling.",
          "What a negative result would and would not settle. It would touch the draft's addition and leave pmc:Saktipata untouched: a descent of power that names no place cannot be refuted by showing that a named place does nothing. Keeping the two apart is why this node tests the posit and not the transmission."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The falsifying condition follows from the claim naming a site",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Recipients blinded to the site of contact report no difference between contact at the brow and contact at another site on the head beyond chance."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:BrowContactOpensTheBrow"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Shia transmission of a graded emanation and of an inner meaning disclosed by initiation, together with the encyclopaedic Neoplatonism of the Brethren of Purity."
      },
      "id": "pmt:IsmailiCosmology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ismailitische Kosmologie",
        "en": "Ismaili cosmology"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sijistānī, Kashf al-Maḥjūb; Nāṣir-i Khusraw, Jāmiʿ al-Ḥikmatayn; Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The coordinate the node is placed at. One value per node; a claim placed on two axes is two placements, and a claim placed at two values on one axis is either two claims or a dispute."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Situating",
      "id": "pm:axisValue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Achsenwert",
        "en": "axis value"
      },
      "range": "pm:AwarenessValue",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the practitioner comes back at once by turning his attention to his body, without the passage the outward journey required."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ReturnIsImmediate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rückkehr erfolgt unmittelbar durch Denken an den Körper",
        "en": "The return is immediate, by thinking of the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered separately from the movement claim because it asserts something the movement claim does not: an asymmetry between going and returning. If the outward journey also happened by willing, there would be nothing to say about the return that was not already said."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#306",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MayaXibalbaSurvivalPresupposesUnconsumedTorch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das lebende Verlassen setzt die unverbrauchte Rückgabe der Fackel voraus",
        "en": "Coming out alive presupposes returning the torch unconsumed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is reported for a named case with the condition quotable beside it, not because a rule is pronounced. Where a transmission gives the precedent instead of the rule, the precedent is what a later reader can check."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The transmission names the case and its outcome. Hun-Hunahpú and Vucub-Hunahpú burned the torch and the cigars through, and were sacrificed at once and buried at Pucbal-Chah. The consequence is not stated as a rule but exhibited as a precedent, and the later pair act on it.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:XibalbaSurvival"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:QuequmaHa"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FiveHindrances",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf Hemmnisse",
        "en": "The five hindrances"
      },
      "source": "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The art of bringing the resentment of another person's own family atua down upon them, worked upon something that has been part of them or has touched them: a portion of spittle, or what is left of their food."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Makutu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Makutu",
        "en": "Makutu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The operation runs through the same transmissibility by contact that tapu runs through, which is why the two belong in one file and not in two."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 31",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The works in which this transmission is chiefly carried, named so that a later reader can find them."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:principalCorpus",
      "label": {
        "en": "principal corpus"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A source carries a passage that was read, with its place and its edition; this names a corpus that was not read. Using dcterms:source here would let a registration claim a textual knowledge it does not have. The literature rule applies all the same: a work, never a URL."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MulabandhaYieldsInnerFire",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wurzelsiegel bringt das entfachte innere Feuer hervor",
        "en": "The root seal yields the kindled inner fire"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pairing is set at the fire and not at the reversal on purpose. The manual defines the root seal as the drawing upward of the downward wind, so a claim that the seal produces that upward movement would assert only that the practice is what it is. The heat, the lengthened flame and the waking of the power are consequences the verses state beyond the definition, and they are what a protocol could address."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Mulabandha"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:KindledInnerFire"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.66-68 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is not open to ordinary sight: the state of the sick, the whereabouts of what is lost, the presence of the dead among the living. The tradition treats this as a domain that some persons perceive and that others are ill advised to form opinions about."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitHiddenThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die verborgenen Dinge",
        "en": "Hidden things"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Relevant to prima-materia#127, which asks whether a perception of the distant, hidden or concealed is attested outside the spheres already held. The passage here attests the domain and one case of its perception; it does not attest a procedure for acquiring it, and that part is not in the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19, 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol taken from the transmission's own reasoning. The claim is not that an unqualified purification is ineffective but that it increases sickness and death in the world beyond what they were. That is a directional claim about an outcome outside the practitioner, and it separates two states of affairs that a later observer could tell apart."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BarasnumExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reinigung, die es schlimmer macht",
        "en": "The purification that makes it worse"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at procedure without cases. No series has been collected, and none is available from within the transmission: the man who would provide the case is executed before the outcome could be recorded, which is itself a feature of the protocol worth stating rather than passing over.",
          "The transmission supplies the falsifying condition without meaning to. It could have said that the wrong rite simply does not cleanse, which nothing would contradict; instead it named an effect in the opposite direction from the one intended, and thereby excluded an outcome. Where a claim of failure predicts nothing, this one predicts an increase."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A recorded series of purifications carried out by persons known to be unqualified, after which sickness and death in the affected place did not rise above the rate where the rite was performed correctly or not at all. The transmission asserts an increase, so a flat or falling rate falls due against it.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:BarasnumCaution"
      },
      "source": "Vendidad, fargard 9.47-49, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)",
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Conditions that descend on the wayfarer as a gift and pass, distinguished in the handbooks from the stations precisely by not being attainable through effort."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Ahwal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zustände",
        "en": "States"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No prerequisite claim is entered for these. The tradition holds that they cannot be worked for, which is a claim about the limits of any ordering and not an omission in this record."
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Saying, after passing what the text calls the fence of wickedness, a set speech to the ruler of each gate in turn: addressing him by name and office, naming what the speaker brings or has been purified by, asking to be let through, and closing with a request for grace."
      },
      "id": "pmp:OphiteTransitusOratio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die an jedem Tor gesprochene Rede",
        "en": "The speech said at each gate in passing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The instruction is given as something the passer is taught beforehand. Origen introduces the speeches as the teaching received after the fence of wickedness, that is as material learned in life for use at the gates, not as words spoken in the moment by whoever happens to arrive."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome claimed: that the hearer is saved from the ruler of this aeon and his persecutions, to which there is no end; that the ruler will not fight with him, having found no commandment of his own in the speaker; and that the hearer is made free and becomes whole through a freedom in which there is no blemish."
      },
      "id": "pmc:JeuLiberatioAbArchonte",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vor dem Archon dieses Äons gerettet werden",
        "en": "Being saved from the ruler of this aeon"
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission behind a diagram of circles and gates in which the soul, rising after death, says a set speech to the ruler of each gate, naming what it brings and asking to pass."
      },
      "id": "pmt:OphiteGnosis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ophitische Gnosis",
        "en": "Ophite gnosis"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Origen states that the bearers are not Christians, that they admit nobody who has not first cursed Jesus, and that they name one Euphrates as the author of their opinions; he also says the sect is very small and that he never met anyone anywhere who believed the diagram. The size claim comes from a hostile witness with a reason to make it and is recorded, not adopted.",
          "Read in the Ante-Nicene Fathers translation of Frederick Crombie. Neither the critical Greek of Koetschau nor Chadwick's translation was consulted, and no claim in this file rests on a word whose Greek was checked. The edition is named on every node for that reason.",
          "The caution that governs every node in this file. The whole of it is read out of an opponent. Origen is refuting Celsus and holds both of them in contempt; he says he obtained the diagram himself out of a love of learning, and he prints the speeches in order to show that he knows what he rejects. Every node therefore carries pm:thirdPartyAscription, and none of them may be read as a statement the bearers of this transmission made about themselves.",
          "The report is nonetheless of an unusual kind, and the difference is worth stating because it is what makes the file worth having. Origen is not summarising a doctrine: he is copying out of a document he possessed, and he says twice that he is doing so where Celsus could not or would not. What is entered here is closer to a transcribed liturgy inside a polemic than to a heresiological paraphrase.",
          "The second witness was opened afterwards and is a different kind of report. Irenaeus gives no speeches: he tells a myth, and the seven names appear in it as a chain of generations rather than as gatekeepers. Where the two accounts touch, they are entered side by side and their divergences are held as disputes, because an averaged account would report a witness neither of them gives."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.24-38; Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30",
      "source": [
        "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.24-38 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The beginner draws the qi in at the nose and closes it off, counting silently in the mind to one hundred and twenty, and then lets it out at the mouth. Neither the drawing in nor the letting out is to be audible to the practitioner himself, and always more is to be taken in than given out. With practice the count is raised by degrees, and in the long run it may reach a thousand; at a thousand, the passage says, the old grow younger and a day is given back for a day."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Biqi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Verschließen des Atems",
        "en": "Closing off the breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Correction, and the reason the edition rule exists. The earlier wording of this definition said that the retention is counted in heartbeats and gave no number. The chapter counts in the mind, not at the pulse, and it names both ends of the scale, one hundred and twenty at the start and a thousand in the long run. Neither could be checked while the citation named no edition, which is the harm SPEC §10 describes; the citation now names the one that was read. See prima-materia#45."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How long he lies is stated to depend on how near or far the place is of which he makes enquiry — an hour or two for a near one — and to have an upper bound: the time never exceeds twenty-four hours, let the place be at never so great a distance."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DurationTracksDistance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Dauer des Liegens folgt der Entfernung und endet bei einem Tag",
        "en": "The time he lies still follows the distance, and stops at a day"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A quantity that varies with a measurable quantity, and a ceiling that does not. Both halves are unusual: the first makes the claim comparable across cases, and the second makes it refutable by a single case that runs longer. Nothing else in this file states a number."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The number of the great expansion is fifty, and forty-nine of them are used. They are divided into two to image the two; one is hung between the fingers to image the three; they are counted through by fours to image the four seasons; the remainder is returned between the fingers to image the intercalary month, and because five years hold two intercalations there are two such returns before the next hanging. Four operations complete a change, eighteen changes complete a hexagram, and the eight trigrams are a small completion."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Dieshi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Abzählen der Schafgarbenstengel",
        "en": "The counting of the yarrow stalks"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The chapter as it stands does not say what the remainder means. It gives four operations and eighteen changes and nowhere says that three changes make one line, nor how a remainder is read off as a value; the string for three changes does not occur in the section at all. The rule that makes the procedure executable is later, and stands in the Song commentary rather than in the canon. This file records what the canon gives and leaves the completion to the issue that carries it, because a node that printed the Song rule as canonical would report as ancient a step the classic does not take.",
          "Two numbers stand in the same passage and only one of them is the famous one. The stalks of the two parts are eleven thousand five hundred and twenty, matching the number of the things; that is in the text. Four thousand and ninety-six is not, and belongs to a commentary."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the whole system rests on, since every claim in it arrives through a communication that may be false. The criterion given is not evidential but linguistic and moral: spirits are judged as men are judged, by their language, and goodness and benevolence are held to be the essential attributes of purified spirits."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DistinctionDesEsprits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unterscheidung guter und schlechter Geister",
        "en": "Telling a good spirit from a bad one"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "distinction des bons et des mauvais Esprits"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-264",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The light-garment sent from the last mystery and put on before the ascent. It is said to carry the glory of the name of every mystery and every emanation of the orders below: the treasury and its saviours, the seven amens, the seven voices, the five trees, the three amens, the twin saviour, the nine watchers of the three gates, the great invisible and the three thrice-powerful ones, the twelve aeons with their archons, and everything in fate and in the spheres."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaVestisNominum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Kleid, auf dem die Namen aller Ordnungen stehen",
        "en": "The garment on which the names of all orders are written"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The garment is an inventory rather than a vesture: what is listed on it is exactly the sequence that will be passed through. Whatever else the ascent is, the thing worn is a list of the names of what stands in the way."
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 10 (trans. C. Schmidt, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SituatingDujianAnchoring",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Alleinsehen wird bei abgezogener Vertiefung verortet",
        "en": "Seeing alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Dujian"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The passage names the withdrawal of the ordinary channel itself and does not leave it to be inferred: the ear does not hear, and where all look he alone sees. The practitioner is awake and at work on a patient throughout, so what is set aside is the reliance on what the senses report and not waking itself. That is the criterion this coordinate names."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Writing down the seventh prayer and wearing it, by the woman who has children to suckle and bring up, for the removal of disease of every kind from those who suck at their mothers' breasts."
      },
      "id": "pmp:EthiopianSusenyosPrayerWorn",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das niedergeschriebene und getragene Gebet des Susenyos",
        "en": "The prayer of Susenyos written down and worn"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The American transmission holding that mental states determine bodily and material conditions, and that the determination can be exercised deliberately."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NewThought",
      "label": {
        "de": "New Thought",
        "en": "New Thought"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is stated in a form that invites counting, and it is the ancestor of most of what circulates today as manifestation."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Quimby, The Quimby Manuscripts; Emma Curtis Hopkins, Scientific Christian Mental Practice; Troward, The Edinburgh Lectures on Mental Science",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Jewish transmission of a descent through palaces whose gatekeepers test the descender, and of an adjuration by which knowledge of the Law is obtained without study."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Hekhalot",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hekhalot- und Merkava-Literatur",
        "en": "Hekhalot and Merkavah literature"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "How old the practice behind these texts is, and whether there is a practice behind them at all, is the open question of the field and is not decided here. The oldest manuscripts are of the thirteenth century and later, at least half a millennium after the composition anyone claims for the material. Two positions stand against each other and neither was read at the wording. See prima-materia#495.",
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31 in the Smith and Scholem translation edited by Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension; and the opening instructions of the Sword of Moses in Gaster's translation. Not opened: the Sar Torah paragraphs that carry the numbers, Maaseh Merkavah, Hekhalot Zutarti, Shiur Qomah, Sefer ha-Razim and 3 Enoch. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#492.",
          "The paragraph numbering of this literature is Schäfer's, from an edition that could not be reached, and the translations read carry it in brackets. Where this file gives a chapter it is the chapter of the digital witness actually read, and where it gives a paragraph the paragraph is taken from that witness and not from the edition itself. That is a weaker citation than the rest of the graph uses and it is marked rather than smoothed. See prima-materia#493."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Hekhalot Rabbati; Hekhalot Zutarti; the Sar Torah material; Maaseh Merkavah; Shiur Qomah; 3 Enoch; the Sword of Moses",
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapters 26, 30 and 31, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr, against the Hebrew of the Wertheimer recension",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first of the ordeals of Xibalba. A lighted torch of pine and cigars are handed to those under trial at nightfall; they must be returned at dawn and returned whole, that is, not burned down. The houses named in the chapter beside it are the House of Cold, the House of Jaguars, the House of Bats and the House of Knives."
      },
      "id": "pmp:QuequmaHa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Probe im Finsteren Haus",
        "en": "The ordeal of the Dark House"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The condition is unusual in this collection because it is arithmetical rather than moral: what is required is that a thing that burns be given back unconsumed after a stated interval. The failure is measurable by anyone holding the object, and the interval is named."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Popol Vuh: The Sacred Book of the Ancient Quiché Maya, English version by Delia Goetz and Sylvanus G. Morley from the Spanish translation of Adrián Recinos (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1950), part II, chapter 9",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsForesight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt den Blick in das Künftige hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields the look into futurity"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideForesight"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The condition, visible in every prayer of the roll and never stated as a rule in it, that the protection is asked for a named person: each of the nine prayers of the Meux roll closes by naming the woman Aheta Mikael, and the three supplementary rolls each name their own owner in the same place."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rolle nennt die Person, für die sie geschrieben wurde",
        "en": "The roll names the person it was written for"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Read off the wording and not from a statement about it. The volume nowhere says that a roll must carry its owner's name; it prints four rolls, and all four do it in every section. What follows for any protocol is that a failure is always available for explanation as a wrong or missing name, and that this escape is built into the object rather than argued for."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I-IX, pp. 216-220; the supplementary rolls, pp. LV-LXIV",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of one form standing for another within awareness."
      },
      "id": "pm:Symbolizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Symbolisieren",
        "en": "Symbolizing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama bringt das göttliche Ohr hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama yields the divine ear"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Chinese transmission of elixirs compounded from mineral substances, with an explicit catalogue of the capacities each elixir is said to confer."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Waidan",
      "label": {
        "de": "Waidan, äußere Alchemie",
        "en": "Waidan external alchemy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Ge Hong's inner chapters list attainments in the same enumerated form as the siddhi lists and were compiled without access to them. The comparison is one of the sharpest tests the project can set itself."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian; Zhouyi cantong qi",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Dibbasota",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Ohr",
        "en": "Divine ear"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Dogon transmission of a renewal of the world celebrated once in sixty years, of the ways by which the interval is counted, and of a seed-star said to be the smallest and the heaviest of things."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Dogon",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dogon-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Dogon transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read is Griaule and Dieterlen 1950 in full, pp. 273-294 of the first printing, in the digitised page images of the journal. Nothing else of the principal corpus was opened: neither Dieu d'eau, nor Masques dogons, nor Le renard pâle, nor Les âmes des Dogon. The Sirius article was chosen first because it is the single text the outside reception rests on.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent. The reason is in the article and not in a later judgement about it: on p. 282 the recorders attach to the fifty-year orbit a footnote observing that this period lies very close to that of the companion of Sirius, and they cite a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for it. Whatever the informants said, the account that reached print was written by readers of European astronomy, and the central enquiry ran from 1946 to 1950 in a region under French administration with a mission station in it. A route to the material this graph already holds therefore exists at the point where the corpus was fixed, and it runs through the recorders. See prima-materia#102 for the same question across the rest of the registry.",
          "The registration named van Beek's re-study as part of the principal corpus. That was wrong under SPEC §14: a field re-study is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise, which makes it evidence and not testimony. It has been removed from the corpus here and stands at pm:evidenceFrom on the examination node instead.",
          "The tradition most widely reported outside itself, and reported wrongly. Registered in this graph from the start as the standing warning of the whole registry, because a body of teaching that circulated for decades as ancient African astronomy could not be found by later workers among the people it was ascribed to."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Griaule, Masques dogons; Griaule, Dieu d'eau; Griaule and Dieterlen, Le renard pâle; Dieterlen, Les âmes des Dogon; Griaule and Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius",
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-294",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AscentPresupposesTheRungs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schau der Schönheit selbst setzt den Aufstieg über die Stufen voraus",
        "en": "The vision of beauty itself presupposes the ascent by rungs"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The word rendered as rungs stands in the base text at 211c, which is why this counts as a stated ordering and not as a modern reading of a sequence of paragraphs."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The ascent is described as rightly conducted only when it proceeds as by rungs of a ladder, from one body to two, from bodies to pursuits, from pursuits to studies, and thence to beauty itself; the one who does not go this way does not arrive at the final vision.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BeautyItself"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BeautyOfOneBody"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 211c",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The late antique Platonic transmission concerned with the ascent of the soul and with the criteria by which a divine communication is told from a human one."
      },
      "id": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neuplatonische Theurgie",
        "en": "Neoplatonic theurgy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The De mysteriis citations name the edition they were read in. For Synesius, De insomniis neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened.",
          "The file records that this layer underlies the later Jewish and Islamic dream theory, so agreement among those three may not be counted as independent."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The corpus sets out a ranking of what the people hold, in the order of its binding force: witchcraft and evil spirits first, the ordeal second, prohibition third, divination fourth, omens fifth, the practitioners sixth, and the charged things last. On the first two it states that doubt is not permitted, since to scout the idea of the power to bewitch or to sneer at the ordeal is held to prove the doubter a witch himself; on the last two it states that doubt costs nothing, that a man may snub a practitioner and talk slightingly of his charms and suffer no inconvenience, and that no one believes in all the charged things but each keeps his own few and regards the rest with contempt."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhatMayBeDoubtedAndWhatMayNot",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was bezweifelt werden darf und was nicht",
        "en": "What may be doubted and what may not"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The corpus states one consequence of this ranking as an observation of the recorder: the practitioner of a village is seldom if ever engaged by the people of that village, who know too much about him and see that his charms cannot keep himself, his wife, his children or his animals in health, and who send instead for the practitioner of another village of whom they know little.",
          "The most valuable node in the file for what the project is for, because the transmission itself distinguishes the propositions that carry the system from the ones that do not. It also states the reason the ordinary man cannot check the charged things against each other: if a thief is not found out his charm was strong, and if he is caught the other man's was stronger."
        ]
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), pp. 51-54",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Bengali Vaiṣṇava transmission descending from Caitanya, which takes over the aesthetic vocabulary of Sanskrit dramaturgy and applies it to devotion, treating the relations in which a devotee stands to the deity as rasa and ranking them."
      },
      "id": "pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gauḍīya-vaiṣṇavische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇava transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run: the fifth wave of the Southern Quadrant and the third wave of the Western Quadrant of the Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu, at the wording, in the bilingual edition of two thousand and three, whose Sanskrit stands on the left page and the translation on the right. Left unread: the Eastern and Northern Quadrants entirely, and the remaining waves of the two quadrants opened. The Ujjvalanīlamaṇi and the Prīti-sandarbha were not opened; both are named in the notes of the edition read and would carry the fuller series of the expansions of love.",
          "The contact route is documented and not undecided, and it runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The rasa vocabulary of this corpus is taken over from the dramaturgical treatise: the same terms, the same distinction of foundational and transitory states, the same eight secondary rasa. An agreement between this file and traditions/sanskrit-dramaturgy.ttl therefore counts as reception and never as independent attestation. What is worth looking for is what this later station added, and it added the ranking: the dramaturgical treatise arranges its rasa by derivation, deity and colour and does not rank them."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu; Rūpa Gosvāmin, Ujjvalanīlamaṇi; Jīva Gosvāmin, Prīti-sandarbha",
      "source": "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 2.5.113-115 (trans. Haberman 2003)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The invariable rules taught to a candidate for the second, third or fourth form: he must subordinate himself to his mentor, meditate continually on his undertaking, speak little with others than his mentor, and use only his consecrated implements and utensils; he must not become angry, must not hear ribald speech, must not go into the water, and must not have sexual intercourse. A candidate who disregards any of them must do such penance as his mentor prescribes before he can proceed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionOnTheCandidatesConduct",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die acht Regeln, die einen Kandidaten binden",
        "en": "The eight rules that govern a candidate"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A warning with a remedy rather than a forfeit: the breach does not void the undertaking, it interrupts it until a penance set by another man has been done. That is a different shape from the taboos which are said to destroy an instrument outright, and the two stand in the same corpus.",
          "Two further restrictions belong to the same regime and are entered here rather than as separate nodes: the consecrated clothing, implements and utensils may be used by no one else until the dance is danced, and from the beginning of his last day in the sacred lodge the candidate must fast and take no drink until he has danced. Only a shaman can release him from any requirement of his conduct in the sacred lodge."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:WiwanyangWacipi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 71 and p. 93",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KevalaPresupposesSahita",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das alleinige Anhalten setzt das Anhalten mit Füllen und Leeren voraus",
        "en": "Retention alone presupposes retention with filling and emptying"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The instruction is to keep practising the accompanied form until the unaccompanied one succeeds. Recorded at prescribed order because the verse sets a sequence without saying what befalls one who skips it. The direction of the breath drops out at the top of this sequence, which is what makes the claim that inhalation draws and exhalation expels a claim about the lower rungs only. See prima-materia#27."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.72 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity, practice or pursuit a warning is directed at."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Cautioning",
      "id": "pm:cautionsAbout",
      "label": {
        "de": "warnt vor",
        "en": "cautions about"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Seven conditions of the person are named in which the needle is not to be used, and the first two carry their consequence with them: do not needle one greatly drunk, it makes the qi disordered; do not needle one greatly angered, it makes the qi go against its course; do not needle one greatly exhausted, nor one newly filled with food, nor one greatly hungry, nor one greatly thirsty, nor one greatly startled."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WucijinQizhuang",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sieben Zustände, in denen nicht genadelt wird",
        "en": "The seven states in which one does not needle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The consequence is stated for the first two and withheld for the remaining five, which the file keeps as it stands rather than extending the pattern. Where a later chapter of the same corpus states consequences for the whole list, that is a second and differently worded passage and it is entered separately."
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Cizhen"
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the ascetic literature of the Egyptian desert and its Byzantine continuation, in which the path is set out as an ordered ascent."
      },
      "id": "pmt:DesertMonasticism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wüstenmönchtum",
        "en": "Desert monasticism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. For every citation in this file the edition or translation the passage was read from is not recoverable, and it is left unstated rather than guessed. The chapter numbering of the Praktikos and the step division of the Ladder both vary between editions, so the numbers here stand on nothing until the passages are reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Andean transmission of huacas as powers that are addressed and answer, of camac as the making and animating that a huaca is invoked by, and of the graded specialists who speak with them, fast for them and hear confession before them."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Andean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Andine Überlieferung",
        "en": "Andean transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Left unread: chapters VIII to XIII and XV to XX of the extirpation account, which are about the causes of idolatry and the conduct of the visitation rather than about what is transmitted; the whole of the report on the Incas apart from the section on the specialists; and the rest of the chronicle of sixteen fifteen. The Quechua prose of Huarochirí, which is the one document of this transmission written in its own language, was not opened at all: neither the manuscript nor any of its three standard translations could be reached in full text, which is a property of the works and is recorded as its own issue.",
          "Read in this run, all three at the wording: the account of the extirpation of idolatry of sixteen twenty-one, chapters I to VII and XIV, in a digitised copy of the first edition; the report on the fables and rites of the Incas of about fifteen seventy-five, in the critical edition of two thousand and ten, at the folios given; and the chapter on the sorcerers of this kingdom in the autograph chronicle of sixteen fifteen, in the transcription published with the manuscript by its holding library, at the author's own page numbers.",
          "The contact route is entered as undecided and no longer as absent. The registry's absent was not wrong about the Andes before fifteen thirty-two; it was wrong about the corpus. Every word of this transmission that can be read was written down after the conquest, and two of the three recorders wrote in order to destroy what they described. The categories they carried in — sin, confession, penance, priesthood, the devil — are the categories under which the material is reported, and the confession complex entered below is exactly where that matters most. Whether a validity test for a confession is Andean, or Christian confession seen through an Andean procedure, or an Andean procedure seen through Christian confession, is not decidable from these three witnesses, and the entry says so rather than settling it by silence.",
          "The first edition of the extirpation account carries folio numbers that its digitisation does not reproduce reliably, so the places below are given by chapter and by the passage within it. A modern transcription of the same text was held alongside to disambiguate the reading of the sixteen twenty-one type; the two give the same wording at every place used here. No place rests on the transcription alone.",
          "The third recorder states his own provenance and it cuts both ways. He writes that he knows what he reports about the priesthood because he served the visitor general who destroyed the huacas of the kingdom. That makes him a witness from inside the transmission who learned much of what he reports while helping to extinguish it, which is a stronger warrant than either priest for the terminology and a weaker one for the practice."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Huarochirí manuscript; Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú; Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas; Guaman Poma de Ayala, Nueva corónica y buen gobierno; Polo de Ondegardo, Los errores y supersticiones de los indios; Cobo, Historia del Nuevo Mundo, book XIII",
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, the summary of what is worshipped",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Concealing a fault in confession is held a great fault, and the confessor makes great efforts to find it out, by different ceremonies in different places. In some, the penitent first calls on the hills around, the plains, the condors that fly, the owls, saying that he wishes to confess his faults; he says them holding a small bead of shell fixed on a thorn between two fingers of the right hand, the thorn raised; when he has finished he gives it to the confessor, who drives the thorn into his cloak and presses until the bead breaks, and looks at how many parts it broke into. If it broke into three the confession was good; if into two it was not good, and he tells him to confess his faults again. In other places a small bundle of the grass called ichu, from which the confessor's name is taken, is divided in two and straws are drawn one from each side until it is seen whether they come out even, and then the confession was good, or odd, and it was bad. In others they divine it by the blood of guinea pigs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConfessionValidityTest",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Test, der entscheidet, ob eine Beichte galt",
        "en": "The test that decides whether a confession held"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A falsifying condition stated by the transmission, applied by its own practitioners to its own act, with a stated outcome and a stated remedy: the confession is void and must be made again. It is not a test of whether the specialist is right; it is a test of whether the layman held something back, so its target is the penitent and not the instrument. That is the opposite arrangement from the one this graph holds for the Zande poison oracle, and the difference is worth more than the resemblance.",
          "The same passage records what the procedure is when it fails to persuade: in one village the penitent's hands were tied behind him and squeezed with a cord until he told the truth; a man said before the visitor that his confessor beat him with a stick and another that he was beaten with a rope. A validity test with a coercive fallback is not an oracle, and both are in the same paragraph.",
          "The three variants are three different randomising devices — a break count, a parity count and an inspection of blood — attached to one function. That the function is stable across three devices, and that a fourth device with the same parity mechanism is used for divination proper elsewhere in the same corpus, is the finding here."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the account of the confession before the ichuri",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The modern transmission of root races, planes and masters, presented as the recovery of a single ancient doctrine underlying all religions."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Theosophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Theosophie",
        "en": "Theosophy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The pedigree claim is the tradition's own central assertion and is contested; it belongs in the graph as a claim about origin and not as a fact about transmission. Much of what circulates today as the doctrine of older traditions entered general circulation here."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled; Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine; Leadbeater, The Chakras",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cases have been recorded, they show no deviation, and the claim is nonetheless uninformative: it is worded so that outcomes which differ from one another all satisfy it equally."
      },
      "id": "pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate",
      "label": {
        "de": "Behauptung unterscheidet nicht",
        "en": "Claim does not discriminate"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The distinction that decides which of the two applies is not how strong the evidence is but whether an outcome exists that the claim would have ruled out. If the claim covers a result and its opposite alike, it discriminates nothing and belongs here, however well the cases fit.",
          "The fifth state and the least obvious. It is not a milder pm:casesWithoutDeviation. There the claim said something and the cases bore it out; here the cases bear it out because the claim did not say enough to be borne out against anything. Recording such a protocol as cases without deviation would enter a success where an examination in fact found the claim empty, and a reader counting confirmations would count this one.",
          "This state exists because a case forced it. A claim that coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the threshold for risk is confirmed by the evidence and useless to whoever acts on it, because fear and anger are both strong and move that threshold in opposite directions. See prima-materia#141."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Zuni transmission of fixed prayer, memorised verbatim and held to be ineffective if altered."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ZuniRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zuni-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Zuni ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A tradition that states its own falsifying condition: the words must be exact. Whether exactness is in fact required is examinable.",
          "Read for the first time in the introduction to the ritual poetry, pages 611 to 620 of the printed report. The body of the collection, the prayers themselves, was not read. The corpus stands in the language of its speakers with a facing translation, which is what makes it admissible here at all; the recorder is an outsider and the question of ethnographic first fixation raised at prima-materia#91 is not settled by this file."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, with the Zuni texts",
      "source": "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 611-835",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity claimed for the oration of the physical art: to have the perfect knowledge of any disease, whether the same tend to death or to life, including for a sick person who is far off and named only, and for one who dissembles and hides his infirmity."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaCognitioAegritudinis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vollkommene Kenntnis, ob eine Krankheit zum Tod oder zum Leben führt",
        "en": "Perfect knowledge whether a disease tends to death or life"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), §§ 26, 30a-30b, pp. 24-25, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EzeqeelTeachesKnowledgeOfClouds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ezeqeel offenbart die Wolkenkunde",
        "en": "Ezeqeel discloses the knowledge of the clouds"
