    {
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianSpokenAsmatYieldsTurnedSpear",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die gesprochenen Namen wenden den Speer zurück",
        "en": "The spoken names turn the spear back"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The passage is printed twice in immediate succession in the edition read, word for word, which the editor does not remark on. Whether that is a repetition in the parchment or a compositor's doubling is not decidable from the volume, and it changes nothing about the claim."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EthiopianAsmatSpokenAgainstTheSpear"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianSpearTurnedBack"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 6, p. LVIII",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1925",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a thing given holds a spiritual power belonging to its giver, that this power seeks to return to its place of origin, and that the obligation to make a return gift follows from it. The claim is put forward in a work of nineteen twenty-five, on the strength of the single Māori passage above."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HauAsSpiritOfTheThing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Gabe trägt einen Geist, der ihre Erwiderung erzwingt",
        "en": "The gift carries a spirit that compels its return"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as an origination and not as a Māori claim because the generalisation is the author's. The letter speaks of one kind of valuable and of a forest rite; the obligation of the return gift as a universal law of archaic exchange begins here. That the reading of the passage was itself contested from nineteen twenty-nine onward is recorded in prima-materia#107, because those sides were not read at the wording in this run."
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "the Māori doctrine of the spirit of the gift, and beyond it a universal principle of archaic exchange",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Marcel Mauss",
      "source": "Marcel Mauss, The Gift, translated by Ian Cunnison (London: Cohen and West, 1966), pp. 8-10",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity claimed. The transmission puts it as a contrast and not as a degree: the voice of the academies sounds, and not with toil and not with weariness do they learn, but with the name of this seal. What is promised beside it is public standing of a specific kind - to seat academies, to expound what is prohibited and what is permitted, to declare the impure impure and the pure pure, to determine the months and the intercalation of years, to appoint judges and to anoint patriarchs."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TorahWithoutToil",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Erlernen des Gesetzes ohne Mühe",
        "en": "Learning the Law without toil"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Set against it in the same passage is what the ordinary student must do without it, and the transmission puts that in the students' own prayer: may that which we have read remain in our possession, may that which we have studied remain in our possession. The claim is therefore not that learning is easier but that a different faculty is being offered in place of memory."
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 30, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What Origen reports of the drawing itself: ten circles, distinct from one another and bound by one circle said to be the soul of all things and called Leviathan, whose name stands twice, at the circumference and at the centre; Behemoth placed beneath the lowest circle; a thick black line called Gehenna or Tartarus; a greater and a lesser circle inscribed Father and Son along their diameter; a barrier in the shape of a hatchet; short circles inscribed Love and Life; a rhomboid figure called the foresight of wisdom, with the nature of wisdom at the point of common section, and Knowledge and Understanding above and below it; and, in what Celsus called the square pattern, a flaming sword drawn as the diameter of a flaming circle, guarding the tree of knowledge and of life."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteDiagrammaDescriptum",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kreise des Diagramms und ihre Inschriften",
        "en": "The circles of the diagram and their inscriptions"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a description and not as a reconstruction. Origen gives the parts and almost never their positions relative to one another, and every published drawing of the diagram supplies an arrangement that this passage does not contain."
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.25, VI.33 and VI.38 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The early Chinese transmission of breath circulation, guided stretching and dietary regimen for the prolongation of life, with illustrated postures."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yangsheng",
      "label": {
        "de": "Yangsheng-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yangsheng nourishing-life transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Mawangdui and Zhangjiashan manuscripts are dated by their burial, which puts the breath material before any documented contact with Indian practice. This is the single most valuable registration in the file for the independence question."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Mawangdui manuscripts, including Daoyin tu and Quegu shiqi; the Zhangjiashan Yinshu; Huangting jing",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Crowley's transmission of a received book, of the discovery of one's own will as the central task, and of a practice curriculum with a demand for recorded results."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Thelema",
      "label": {
        "de": "Thelema",
        "en": "Thelema"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The insistence on keeping a magical diary and treating claims sceptically is a methodological demand made from inside a tradition, and belongs in the graph as such."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Crowley, Liber AL vel Legis; Crowley, Magick in Theory and Practice; Crowley, The Vision and the Voice",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The stage at which the gathered and extended breath goes downward, after which it settles and becomes firm; the sequence then turns and rises."
      },
      "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg als Stufe der Folge",
        "en": "Descent as a stage of the sequence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The inscription assigns direction to a place in a sequence, not to a phase of breathing. Descent follows gathering and extension; ascent follows sprouting and growing. The closing lines set the two directions against the two powers, the dynamism of heaven showing in what rises and that of earth in what descends. Nothing here binds a direction to inhaling or to exhaling. See prima-materia#27 and prima-materia#28."
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HermeticDissolutioAnte",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Aufstieg setzt die Auflösung des Körpers voraus",
        "en": "The ascent presupposes the dissolution of the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The treatise sets the sequence — first the body is surrendered, then the way of life to the daimon, then the senses return to their sources, and thereafter the man speeds upward through the Harmony — and never says what would follow from taking it in another order, because it does not envisage another order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HermeticDissolutioCorporis"
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.24-25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:KokabelTeachesSignsOfTheStars",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kokabel offenbart die Zeichen der Sterne",
        "en": "Kokabel discloses the signs of the stars"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with the constellations."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheStars"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Kokabel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that saying the oration while touching the pulse of a pregnant woman reveals whether she will bring forth a male or a female. It is the claim in this corpus that comes closest to being ready for a count: a binary outcome, a known base rate, a public result, and a prediction that can be fixed in writing beforehand."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaSexPredictionExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Oration das Geschlecht des ungeborenen Kindes offenbart",
        "en": "Whether the oration reveals the sex of the unborn child"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No case has been collected, and the obstacle here is not the record but the era: the claim is from a work in print since sixteen fifty-seven and nobody appears to have counted. That is a property of the attention the claim has had and not of the claim, which is why the state is procedure-without-cases.",
          "The passage that carries this claim also carries the sentence that would explain a failure away, and it explains it away in the wrong direction: the miracle proceeds not from the operator's nature but from the angels, whose office it is to reveal these things. That locates the acting elsewhere but sets no condition on the operator, so it does not absorb a wrong answer. Nothing in the passage does.",
          "What a protocol would have to guard against is not the operator's honesty but the moment of prediction. Touching the pulse puts the operator in front of the woman, and a prediction made late in a pregnancy by someone who can see her is not a prediction from the oration. The prediction has to be sealed and the point in the pregnancy fixed, or the protocol measures ordinary observation."
        ]
      },
      "pm:baseRate": "About one half. The sex ratio at birth in human populations runs a little above parity for males, so an operator predicting at random scores close to fifty in a hundred, and a series has to be long enough to distinguish a real excess from that. This is the only claim the run met whose base rate is known in advance rather than having to be estimated from the same data that test the claim.",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/ars-notoria.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A series of predictions recorded before each birth, by an operator keeping the conditions the text sets, whose proportion correct does not exceed what guessing would give on the same series. The claim is stated without degree and without exception, so a single wrong prediction refutes it as it stands; the weaker form, that the operator does better than chance, is what a series would actually settle, and it is stated here because the strong form is the one the text makes and the weak form is the one worth measuring.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-07",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaOratioYieldsSexusInfantis"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The book closes with three conditions under which a hollow is felicitous and six under which the burial is adverse. The six: a discordance of yin and yang; a going wrong of the year and the season; a small strength that plans a great thing; a reliance on present fortune and standing power; an overreaching above or a falling short below; and a strange appearance among the responses. The classic is quoted for the conclusion that a felicitous hollow with an adverse burial is the same as abandoning the corpse."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SanjiLiuxiong",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drei Glücksfälle und sechs Unglücksfälle",
        "en": "Three felicities and six adversities"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The second adversity is separately consequential, because it is the one an outside witness attacked with a count. It requires the year and the season of the burial to be right, and the memorial recorded in the dynastic history sets against that a tally of burials in the classical annals on the day the burial books call most inauspicious.",
          "The sixth adversity is the reason this node stands here and not among the ordinary cautions. The commentary printed with it enumerates what the strange appearances are, and the list is a list of ways in which nothing happens: the site is right and the family delays the burial; a quarrel prevents it; poverty and sickness together make it impossible; the master dies before he can return; the coffin stands for years and a war intervenes; the descendants do not agree; a lawsuit or a prison ends it; the days pass in neglect until the thing is abandoned. Every one of these is a reason a favourable grave produced no fortune, and every one is available after the fact.",
          "What the graph gains here is not a warning but a shape of claim. Beside the interval that does not turn a day, this file now holds the machinery that keeps that interval from ever being read as a refutation, in the same book and a few folios apart."
        ]
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Zang"
      },
      "source": "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The fragmentary hexameters that the later Platonists treated as revealed, and on which their theurgic practice was built."
      },
      "id": "pmt:ChaldeanOracles",
      "label": {
        "de": "Chaldäische Orakel",
        "en": "Chaldean Oracles"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The graph already holds the theurgy that rests on this text without holding the text."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
