      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Chaldean Oracles, transmitted in fragments through Proclus, Damascius and Psellos",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of import from the sun."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zeichen der Sonne",
        "en": "Signs of the sun"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The second entry: three feasts to the instructing and officiating priests, three chanted prayers to the named manidō, and a second shooting of the shell."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree",
      "label": {
        "de": "Einweihung in den zweiten Grad",
        "en": "Initiation into the second degree"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree, marked in the chart by the shell placed at the top of the head."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideThoughtReading",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lesen der Gedanken und Absichten anderer",
        "en": "Reading the thoughts and intentions of others"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A second question, put with a second fowl, in which the oracle is told what its first answer was and asked to confirm it by the opposite outcome. The first test is called bambata sima and the second gingo. If the first fowl died the second must survive, and if the first survived the second must die."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der bestätigende zweite Test am Giftorakel",
        "en": "The corroborative second test at the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two tests may be made at one seance where the poison suffices, and are often separated by weeks or months. The transmission names six situations in which a single test is accepted instead, among them the case where the answer came decisively and the case where a lesser oracle had already been asked; but two tests are said to be indispensable in any question concerning the relations between two persons, and especially where a legal issue is involved."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 138-140",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SelfRememberingPresupposesNonIdentification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Selbsterinnern setzt das Nicht-Identifizieren voraus",
        "en": "Self-remembering presupposes not identifying"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the step that was missing while the graph held only the diagnosis. pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite records that the state to be undone is regularly valued under names such as enthusiasm and inspiration; this node records what the same source prescribes against it, and names the exercise the diagnosis exists for."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "One who identifies with anything is unable to remember himself, and remains the slave of whatever can happen to him."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:IdentifyingTakenForItsOpposite"
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapter eight, at the passage naming identifying the chief obstacle to self-remembering (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission's own warning against the evidential test. Superior spirits, it says, have no signs by which to be recognised other than the superiority of their ideas and their language, and every spirit can imitate a material sign. It adds that spirits take the names of saints and of famous persons, and that this is not fraud where the intention is good, so that the name is no proof either."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SigneMaterielCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kein materielles Zeichen beweist, wer spricht",
        "en": "No material sign proves who is speaking"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two of the three things an outside investigator would reach for - a signature or handwriting, and a name - are ruled out by the transmission itself before anyone else can propose them. What is left is the third, the quality of the language, which is the one an outsider cannot measure."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:Evocation"
      },
      "source": "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, section 268, questions 3-6 and 23",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Spiritism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A work of the corpus has been obtained and read and its edition is settled, but no passage has yet been entered as a node."
      },
      "id": "pm:workOpened",
      "label": {
        "de": "Werk geöffnet",
        "en": "Work opened"
      },
      "type": "pm:CoverageState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Australian transmission of an ancestral order laid into country, held in song and in the routes the song follows."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AustralianDreaming",
      "label": {
        "de": "Australische Aboriginal-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Australian Aboriginal transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered and deliberately left at corpus named. There is no corpus in the project's sense, much of the material is restricted by rules that are still enforced, and a public-domain dedication of restricted knowledge would be a taking and not an opening. The gate here protects ownership, not authority, and the project's criterion does not apply to it unchanged."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteAbsent"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "No transmitted written corpus; the ethnographic record stands in its place and much of it is restricted",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A pair of methods of worship announced by the text as the orthodox and the heterodox, and closed by it as the right and left hand methods of worshipping the goddess. The text defines both terms itself. Worship performed strictly by the prescribed rules, with the five sacrifices that repay the debts to sages, gods, ancestors, men and the elements, is called the state of dakṣiṇa, and it is so called because the worshipper becomes humble towards gods and ancestors and the goddess is favourably disposed towards him. Vāmā is the goddess who, when worshipped, takes the share of the sacrifice for herself before all other gods, and one who worships in that method should himself be a vāma, whether or not he performs the five sacrifices. Which method applies is often decided by the deity rather than by the worshipper: Bhairava, who says he assumed his form for the sake of meat, wine and intercourse at will, is to be worshipped in the left hand method always; the left images of Sūrya and Gaṇeśa likewise; and the text keeps the five great brahmanical sacrifices standing alongside."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KalikapuranaLeftAndRightMethods",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die rechts- und linkshändige Methode, wie das Purāṇa sie angibt",
        "en": "The right and left hand methods as the purāṇa states them"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chapter numbering is not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running heads the scan does not carry reliably. Work, translator, part and verse numbers are named, and the passages sit in the chapters on the worship of Tripurā; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different chapter count. Same limitation as at pmc:AcaraLadder. See prima-materia#45.",
          "It stands beside two other divisions already held here and agrees with neither. pmc:AcaraLadder gives seven ascending steps with the left above the right; pmc:BhavaClassification gives three dispositions that decide what a person is competent for. This purāṇa gives two methods assigned by the object of worship. Three texts, three different shapes, and the only element common to all three is that none of them is the pair as the West received it.",
          "This is the passage the project had been unable to produce, and it changes the state of the question rather than settling it. A pre-colonial Indian text does announce a two-way division in these terms, so the sharpest form of the claim that the pair is an entirely Victorian imposition does not hold. What the text does not supply is the thing the Western pair carries: there is no moral polarity between the two, no camp a person joins, and no criterion of union against self-deification. The division sorts deities and rites, not practitioners."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the announcement of the orthodox and heterodox (right and left hand) methods following the yonimudrā verses (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
        "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the closing statement of the Tripurā-kavaca chapter that the left hand and right hand methods of worshipping the goddess have now been stated (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
        "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on the Bhairava image (204-205a) and on the left images of Sūrya and Gaṇeśa (212-218) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the transmission's own rule of verification. Two passages by the same author, in two works, stand against each other in the way that matters here. One states a control and states it well: concordance of spontaneous teaching, from many mediums unknown to one another, in different places, restricted to fundamental principles, with the express refusal to count a single medium or a single centre. The other forbids the question put in order to test, and settles both outcomes in advance - the spirits addressed do not answer and withdraw, and a spirit that does answer such a question is thereby classed as imperfect."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SpiritismControlExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Kontrolle, die sich nicht anwenden lässt",
        "en": "The control that cannot be applied"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The state stood at procedure without cases until prima-materia#499 was decided, and understated the protocol: pm:CasesRequireCounterSearchShape grouped the verdict on a claim's wording with the two states that report cases and demanded a counter-search that would have had nothing to search. The shape was corrected rather than the state faked, and the state is now the one the reading reached.",
          "The two rules do not contradict each other on their face and the finding is not that they do. The finding is what they leave: a criterion that can be satisfied by agreement and cannot be strained by a question. Agreement among mediums who share a doctrine, a vocabulary and an expectation is the outcome to be explained, not the evidence that explains it, and the one operation that would tell a real concordance from a shared expectation is the one the transmission rules out."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, protocol formed from two passages of the same author read at the wording; no case series has been collected and no external examination has been read",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A passage in this transmission in which a concordance of independent mediums is reported and then set aside as insufficient on some ground other than the moral quality of the communication; or a recorded case in which a test question was put, answered, and the answer accepted as evidence rather than as a mark of an imperfect spirit. Either would show that the control can be strained and that the exclusion of the test question is not doing the work described here.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-04",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:ControleUniversel"
      },
      "pm:undividedOutcomes": "A spirit that withdraws when tested and a spirit that was never present produce the same observation; a spirit that answers a test question is by that answer classed as imperfect. Agreement among mediums and agreement produced by a shared doctrine are likewise not separated, since concordance is admitted as the control and no operation is allowed that would strain it.",
      "source": [
        "Allan Kardec, L'Évangile selon le spiritisme, introduction, section II, sub-heading Contrôle universel de l'enseignement des Esprits",
        "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXVI, section 288, item 3"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:StationsPresupposeTheOneBelow",
      "label": {
        "de": "Eine Station setzt die darunterliegende voraus",
        "en": "A station presupposes the station below it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbooks set turning back as the first station and hold that the one below must be kept before the one above is reached. The enumeration and the count of the stations differ between the handbooks; the precedence of turning back does not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Maqamat"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Tawba"
      },
      "source": [
        "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
        "al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Cuban transmission of the orichas, carrying the Yoruba divinatory corpus in a shortened form alongside Catholic imagery."
      },
      "id": "pmt:LucumiSanteria",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lucumí",
        "en": "Lucumí"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Its relation to Ifá is a documented case of a corpus surviving a forced transfer with measurable loss, which is examinable rather than merely regrettable."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Cabrera, El Monte; the published libretas",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "It is forbidden, at the felling, for anyone to call another by his name, for the man so called will die and his spirit will pass into the tree and preside over the figure. The corpus states the term and the record together: the named one then dies, certainly within ten days, and they say that the named one never fails to die. It states in the same place that they repudiate all idea of his being poisoned or of his death being hurried on in any material way by the practitioner, who they say may be miles away."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheNamedManDiesWithinTenDays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Gerufene stirbt binnen zehn Tagen",
        "en": "The man whose name is called dies within ten days"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The claim of a perfect record is the transmission's own and is entered as such. Whether any case of a named man surviving was ever recorded is not answered by this work; the same work states that the caller who names a man carelessly answers with his life to the dead man's relations, which is a reason why the naming is not done carelessly and also a reason why a failure would be inconvenient to report.",
          "The most examinable claim in the file, and the reason it is examinable is that the transmission itself supplies the three parts: an outcome, a term of ten days, and an explicit denial of the obvious naturalistic explanation. Nothing else read in this run puts a number of days on anything."
        ]
      },
      "source": "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), p. 93",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaRepetitioYieldsGradatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die wiederholte Oration bringt Beredsamkeit nach Maß hervor",
        "en": "The repeated oration yields eloquence by measure"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioRepetita"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaGradatioFacundiae"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Freedom from the disturbance of the passions, called the flower of ascetic labour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Apatheia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Apatheia",
        "en": "Apatheia"
      },
      "source": "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The lineage of Gorakhnāth, teaching a body perfected by practice and a state in which mind is said to be absent rather than concentrated."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Nath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Nāth-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Nāth transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Siddha-Siddhānta-Paddhati; Gorakṣaśataka; Amanaska",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome the undertaking names. On the seventh day the traveller rises, and the transmission compares it to rising from a pleasant sleep. He asks first for food and drink, and after that for a writer who is wise and learned."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WirazReturn",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rückkehr des Reisenden am siebten Tag",
        "en": "The return of the traveller on the seventh day"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The order of the two requests is worth keeping: the body first, the record second. The writing down is fixed at the moment of return and not at the end of the vision, which makes the report a part of the procedure rather than a later literary act."
      },
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 3.2-3 and 3.15-26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a person whose hand is poisonous kills a turtle within fifty days by pressing on the vessel it is held under, while a person whose hand is sweet leaves it living as before. The claim is examinable in the form the source gives it: the term is fixed, the arrangement is described, and the two outcomes are stated as different."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PoisonousHandTurtleExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Probe an der Schildkröte die giftige Hand unterscheidet",
        "en": "Whether the pressing of the turtle discriminates the poisonous hand"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A protocol that ever ran would also have to say what the corpus does not: whether the term begins at the first pressing or at the last, and how often the pressing is repeated within the fifty days. The passage gives neither, and a run that fixed them would be fixing them, not reading them.",
          "No case has been collected, and the obstacle is not the scarcity of turtles. It is that the source does not say what it is about the pressing that is supposed to act, so the arrangement it describes has no condition under which the pressing is present and the poison absent. A run of the procedure would establish that a turtle held under a pressed vessel for fifty days dies or does not; whether the hand made the difference needs a second arm the source does not describe, and devising one is the work this state is waiting on.",
          "The state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised, and the difference is the whole reason this node exists. The tradition devised the procedure; nobody has carried it. Recording that as though nobody had thought of a test would report the reach of this project's instruments as a property of the corpus.",
          "What the claim does not do is the thing most claims of this kind do. It does not say that the poisonous hand harms only where the intent is wrong, or only in a person unprepared, or only where the practitioner is not seen. There is no absorbing clause in the sentence, and that absence is what makes the fifty days mean anything."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/chinese-medical-canon.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A person named as having the poisonous hand who presses as described and whose turtle is alive and unchanged after fifty days; or a person named as having the sweet hand whose turtle dies within the term. The source states the outcomes as a pair and attaches no clause under which either could still be counted as confirming, so a case on either side falls against the claim.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-09",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:Shoudu"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seal in which the tongue is turned back and set above the palate while the gaze rests between the brows; the manual describes a months-long preparation of the tongue before the position can be held."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Khecari",
      "label": {
        "de": "Khecarī",
        "en": "Khecarī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.32-33 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The requirement that the songs be known in a most exact manner, down to the syllable, no important mistake being tolerated."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoSongExactness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Genauigkeit der gesungenen Worte",
        "en": "Exactness of the sung words"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The generation of inner heat, presented in three components: the channels, the syllables, and the heat meditation itself."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Tummo",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tummo",
        "en": "Tummo"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tamil transmission of bodily perfection, rejuvenation regimens and a deliberately obscure poetic idiom that resists the commentary it invites."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TamilSiddhar",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tamilische Siddhar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Tamil Siddhar transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Tirumūlar, Tirumandiram; the Bogar and Agastyar corpora; the kāya-kalpa literature",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to certain persons of coming to life again after death and returning to their place among people, held to belong to those over whom particular words were spoken at their birth."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederaufleben nach dem Tod",
        "en": "Coming to life again after death"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The narrative gives the being a name in a phonetic transcription that the digitised text renders unreliably; the spelling is therefore not carried into this node, and the term is to be taken from a clean copy of the page before it is asserted anywhere. The claim itself does not depend on the spelling."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The same chapter states four conditions, and they are the ones the Changes leave unsaid. There is a way in consulting: who is not gathered does not consult; who does not doubt does not consult; who is not within the rule does not consult; and who will not act on the prognostication had better not have consulted. Before the casting a charge is spoken twice over, borrowing the Supreme Mystery and putting the matter in doubt before the spirits."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FanshiYoudao",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vier Bedingungen, unter denen nicht befragt wird",
        "en": "The four conditions under which one does not consult"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The fourth condition is the one that does work a graph can use. It makes the consultation pointless where the enquirer will not follow the answer, which is a statement about the enquirer and not about the world; and it thereby rules out the cheapest test of a divination, which is to consult and then do the opposite. Together with the prohibition on asking twice in the Changes, this transmission has now been found to close both of the two obvious tests, in two different works, by two different means.",
          "The second condition cuts the other way and is worth keeping separate: who does not doubt does not consult. That excludes the control case in which a consultation is made about a question whose answer is already known, and a control case is what an examination of this practice would need first."
        ]
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:XuanShu"
      },
      "source": "Yang Xiong, Taixuanjing, Xuan shu, the eleventh, with the commentary of Fan Wang, juan 8, Sibu congkan facsimile",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseDivination"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to know in advance that something remarkable is about to happen, reported in the narrative as a standing property of a person rather than as the outcome of an exercise."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zweites Gesicht",
        "en": "Second sight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The English term is the translator's. What the node carries is the claim reported under it and not the term's own history in Scottish and Scandinavian usage; whether the Inuktitut word behind it has that range is not decidable from the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The penalty the handbook states for a user who does not keep the preparation. If he will not act accordingly, the angels of wrath, ire, fury and rage will come near him to minister to him, and they will lord over him, and strangle him, and plague him all over. Four leaders are named for the four."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HarbaDeMosheCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was den trifft, der sich nicht daran hält",
        "en": "What comes to the man who does not act accordingly"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The harm named is bodily and immediate and falls on the practitioner himself, which distinguishes this caution from the Hawaiian case in traditions/hawaiian.ttl, where the ill-luck of a fault falls on the party the chant honours. Where the sufferer is the one who could report the fault, the tradition can in principle be contradicted from inside."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:HarbaDeMoshe"
      },
      "source": "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The being named in the seventh prayer of the Meux roll as the one who came in the matter of the first child and killed him, and who after being pierced swears never again to approach where the Name is met with."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianWerzelya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Werzelja",
        "en": "Werzelya"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The edition read renders the name as Ursula in the introduction and prints Werzewelya in the translation. The identifier keeps the transliterated form of the roll and not the editor's substitution, which is a Latin name for a being the roll does not call by one."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Werzewelya",
      "pm:nameRole": "the killer of the child at the breast, bound by her own oath",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Ursula"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The gathering and preparation of plant material for pharmacological or operative use."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RootCutting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wurzelschneiden",
        "en": "Root-cutting"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The count as the witness gives it. Seven speeches stand in the passage: one to a one-formed king, from whom the beginnings of the ogdoad are said to derive, and then in order to Ialdabaoth, Iao, Sabaoth, Astaphaeus, Aloaeus and Horaeus. Six of the seven names of the hebdomad therefore have a speech, and Adonaeus has none."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteSeptemOrationes",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sieben Reden, sechs davon an einen benannten Herrscher",
        "en": "Seven speeches, six of them to a named ruler"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The count is worth entering because it is regularly given otherwise. The secondary literature speaks of eight speeches to seven named archons; the translation read carries seven speeches and six names. Whether the difference lies in the Greek, in the numbering of the chapters, or in the counting is unchecked here, because neither Koetschau nor Chadwick nor the study making that claim was opened. See prima-materia#596.",
          "The gap falls where the numbering also breaks. Gates one, two and three are named, the fifth realm is named, Ialdabaoth is first and seventh, and the fourth and sixth are never mentioned. Whether the missing speech is a loss in transmission, a diagram Origen copied incompletely, or a system that never had one is not decidable from this witness."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The procedure the chapter gives for deciding whether a person has the poisonous hand. A turtle is placed under a vessel and the person presses on the vessel from above. If his hand is poisonous, the turtle is dead in fifty days; if his hand is sweet, it lives on as before."
      },
      "id": "pmp:AnguiShi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Probe an der Schildkröte",
        "en": "The pressing of the turtle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered as a practice in its own right because of what it is: a discrimination procedure, stated by the tradition, over a claim about a person, with a fixed term and two outcomes that are not the same. Nothing else read in this corpus in this run has that shape, and the reason for opening the corpus from the testability side was the expectation of finding it."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source says what fails to happen without the prior step: that the later step is not possible, or does not arise, or turns harmful."
      },
      "id": "pm:statedNecessity",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausdrücklich gesetzte Notwendigkeit",
        "en": "Stated necessity"
      },
      "type": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect claimed for the procedure. The witness reports that the revels and the sacrifices were held to be very acceptable to the idols and remedial for freeing them from the ills indicated as to come."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MayaAvertingOfForetoldIlls",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befreiung von den angekündigten Übeln",
        "en": "Being freed from the ills indicated as to come"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama ist abgezogene Vertiefung",
        "en": "Saṃyama is withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Numbering. The three members stand at III.1-3 and their combination at III.4 in the received division; the practice node in this file cites III.1-3 alone. See prima-materia#456."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The three members are defined in sequence as the binding of the mind to one place, an unbroken flow of attention to that same place, and the same again with the object alone shining forth; the fourth sūtra names the three taken together on one object as saṃyama. That is the criterion this value states — awake, the senses off their objects, attention held on a single one — given in the base text rather than inferred from the capacities. The withdrawal of the senses is a separate limb preceding the three."
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The report adds, of this class and of no other in its list of specialists, that these were the least esteemed, because they always lied."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheFireReaders",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Feuerleser galten am wenigsten, weil sie immer logen",
        "en": "The fire-readers were least esteemed because they always lied"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It bears directly on the claim, open elsewhere in this project, that the old traditions test origin and fruits and never a rate of hits. Here a transmission is reported as having formed a standing comparative judgement about a whole class of practitioners on the ground that they were always wrong, which is an aggregation over cases however loosely it was arrived at. Whether the judgement was reached by counting or was itself a piece of received esteem is not decidable from the one sentence, and no examination node is written on it.",
          "The most valuable sentence found in this run. It is a ranking of divinatory techniques by reliability, made inside the transmission, with the reason given as repeated failure rather than as impurity, wrong descent or broken taboo. Every other failure in this file is attributed to something about the person; this one is attributed to the method."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:FireReading"
      },
      "source": "Cristóbal de Molina, Relación de las fábulas y ritos de los incas, ed. Paloma Jiménez del Campo, transcr. Paloma Cuenca Muñoz (Madrid: Iberoamericana / Frankfurt: Vervuert, 2010), fol. 6v, on the virapiricoc",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AristeasJourney",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben",
        "en": "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SoulJourney"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": [
        "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GraecoRoman"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise bringt das Wissen um den fernen Ort hervor",
        "en": "The journey yields the knowledge of the distant place"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DistantSight"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-57",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The chapter names it the great essential of the whole art: whoever has obtained the embryonic breathing can breathe without using nose or mouth, as though he were within the womb, and then the way is complete."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Taixi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Embryonalatmung",
        "en": "Embryonic breathing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The definition is stated as a threshold and not as a degree, which is unusual in this collection: not better breathing but breathing without the two openings. It is also the point at which the chapter stops being examinable from outside, because the sign it gives for the lower stage, the down that does not stir, would be satisfied trivially by someone who had reached this one."
      },
      "source": "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What is done while the traveller is gone. The dasturs give the seven sisters an undertaking in a number: they will deliver him back safe and sound in seven days. The body remains on the carpet; the dasturs, the priests and the seven sisters keep watch around it for seven days and nights, with fire and fumigation, reciting the Avesta and the Zand, going through the Nasks and chanting the Gathas, and they keep watch in the dark."
      },
      "id": "pmp:WirazWatch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die siebentägige Wache über dem Körper",
        "en": "The seven days' watch over the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the part of the procedure that an outside observer could check, and it is stated in a form that could fail: a named man, a named place, a stated number of days, and a promise made to named women that he will be given back alive."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 2.18 and 2.31-33",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Zoroastrian"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol addressing the claim that Dogon transmission carries knowledge of an invisible companion of Sirius. It asks whether the star names and the doctrine can be found among Dogon outside the circle of the original informants, and whether any account of them leads anywhere but back to the recording work."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DogonSiriusExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Steht die Kenntnis des Begleiters in der Überlieferung?",
        "en": "Is the knowledge of the companion found in the transmission?"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386.",
          "The counter-testimony has not been read at the wording in this run. A reply appeared in the same journal in the following volume, and a further exchange in the two years after; the deviation recorded here therefore rests on one side of a controversy that has two. See prima-materia#252. Under SPEC §13 this state is a one-sided dispute waiting for its second side, not a refutation, and the state below is set on the cases and not on the conclusion.",
          "The one trace that remains is itself the finding. Informants in that village say that in former times people knew of a star appearing in the year of the ceremony, and further enquiry leads back to the recorder. What looks like independent survival is feedback from the published account into the field it describes, and any later confirmation must be tested against that path before it counts.",
          "The re-study reports the ethnographic facts as straightforward. Dogon know Sirius as a star and call it the hunter's star; knowledge of the stars matters neither in daily life nor in ritual, the sun and the phases of the moon being what reckoning runs on. No Dogon outside the circle of the original informants had heard the star names the account turns on, and no one, not even inside that circle, had heard or understood that Sirius was double. The scheduling of the ceremony is done in several ways in the village the account draws on, none of them astronomical.",
          "The re-study's explanation of how the account arose is not adopted here as a finding, only recorded: that an informant spoke of stars of different generations, meaning two adjacent visible stars held as father and son to Sirius as grandfather, and that the recorder, who had read astronomy in Paris, made a double and triple system of it. That is a reconstruction of an intention and cannot be examined; the absence of the doctrine in the field can."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "Walter E. A. van Beek, Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule, Current Anthropology 32/2 (1991), pp. 149-150 with its footnote 10, and pp. 156-157",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Walter E. A. van Beek, field re-study among the Dogon of Sanga and Yugo Doguru, reported 1991",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A report of the star names or of the doctrine of an invisible companion from Dogon speakers outside the original circle of informants, where the chain of the report can be shown not to run back through the published account; or a fixation of the doctrine in any text or record datable before nineteen thirty-one. Either would return the claim to contested and undo the deviation recorded here.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-01",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rendering of an invocation in which neither a mistake nor a pause occurs. The tradition sets the two on the same footing: hesitating is treated as failing, not as taking care."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MaoriExactRecitation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wiederholung ohne Fehler und ohne Zögern",
        "en": "Repetition without error or hesitation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Both recorders report that a ceremony or a medicine is undone by who is present. The first states that no ceremony will be efficacious if a woman is present during her menstrual flow or if the influences that surround her at that time are present, that such influences remain about a tipi she has occupied until it is taken down and set up again, and that the conductor of the Buffalo ceremony walked the lodge scanning the women to find such a one and the men to find an unworthy one, and would have ordered either to withdraw. The second states that the presence of the wrong kind of person in the lodge was believed to affect the efficacy of a medicine, and that if it were exposed to such influence for any considerable time its power would be entirely destroyed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:CautionAgainstTheWrongPresence",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die falsche Anwesenheit zerstört die Kraft der Sache",
        "en": "The wrong presence destroys the power of the thing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The caution is entered against the imparted potency rather than against any single rite, because that is the thing both sources say is destroyed: the first speaks of a ceremony that will not be efficacious, the second of a medicine whose power is entirely destroyed, and what the two have in common is the potency the rite is supposed to put to work.",
          "The measure the first source prescribes is worth recording exactly, because it is physical and datable: the tipi in which the young woman had lived alone was taken down by her mother and immediately set up again on the same spot, and that act is what made it fit for the ceremony.",
          "This is the failure condition that does the most work in the file, because it is prior to every other: it can be invoked after the fact against any rite that did not take, and it needs no evidence beyond the assertion that somebody was there. Entered as a caution and not as a mere doctrine because both sources give it in the imperative, with an action to be taken."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:Sicun"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried.",
      "source": [
        "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 252",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 143 and p. 145 and p. 92"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Brazilian transmission of the orixás, with an initiation that seats a spirit in the head of the initiate."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Candomble",
      "label": {
        "de": "Candomblé",
        "en": "Candomblé"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Bastide, Le candomblé de Bahia; Verger, Notes sur le culte des Orisa et Vodun",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A claim held in the graph solely so that a protocol has something to address. It witnesses no transmission, claims no source, and belongs to no tradition."
      },
      "id": "pm:Positing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Setzen",
        "en": "Positing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The class exists because a protocol needs an object and because the claims most worth examining are often the ones no received work carries. It is deliberately narrow: an instance may exist only where a pm:Testing points at it, enforced by pm:PositingRequiresExaminationShape. Without that restriction the class would become the channel through which an unpublished draft enters the record after all, and the direction the project runs in would be reversed."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Śaiva and Śākta tantric corpus and its Kashmirian exegesis."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SaivaSakta",
      "label": {
        "de": "Śaiva- und Śākta-Tantra",
        "en": "Śaiva and Śākta tantra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A caution governs everything read out of this tradition. What is old is a set of subtle centres carrying seed syllables, and the older tantras vary in how many they count. The fixed system of seven centres with spectral colours and assigned psychological qualities belongs to the twentieth century and to no tantra; it is held in this file as pmc:SevenCentreQualityMapping so that it is available as what it is, and it must not be read back into the material it was built on.",
          "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Vijñānabhairava citations name the numbering they were read in. For the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, the Tantrāloka, the Tantrasāra and the Mālinīvijayottaratantra the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those passages are to be reopened, and the chapter divisions of the Kashmirian exegesis are edition-dependent."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Pūrṇānanda, Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa (1577)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:StillnessPresupposesRepentance",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stille und Liebe setzen Buße und den Kampf gegen die Leidenschaften voraus",
        "en": "Stillness and love presuppose repentance and the labour against the passions"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The work is emphatic that neither stillness nor the love of God is reached without the prior repentance and the fight with the passions. It does not, however, state for every rung that the next is unreachable without it, and the image of the ladder is didactic before it is ontological, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:LadderOfThirtySteps"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Praktike"
      },
      "source": "John Climacus, The Ladder of Divine Ascent, steps 1-30",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:DesertMonasticism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account of the extraction: when the time of the number of Melchisedec, the great receiver of the light, came, he would come into the midst of the aeons and of all the rulers bound in the sphere and in fate, and carry away the purification of the light from them; he set in motion the hastener who is over them and made them turn their circles swiftly, and the hastener carried away the power that was in them, and the breath of their mouth, and the tears of their eyes, and the sweat of their bodies."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PistisSophiaMelchisedecPurgatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Licht wird den Herrschern zu einer bestimmten Zahl entzogen",
        "en": "The light is carried away from the rulers at an appointed number"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The procedure is stated as recurring and as timed by a number, not as a single event, and the mechanism is acceleration: the circles are made to turn faster and the powers come off. Whatever else it is, it is described as an extraction with a schedule, which is rarer in this corpus than the extraction itself.",
          "The receivers of the sun and of the moon are given the same office at a lower level: when they look up and see the configurations of the paths of the aeons, of fate and of the sphere, they take the light-power from them, make it ready and hold it until they hand it to the receivers of Melchisedec. The passage therefore has the light travelling upward through two named stations, and it is the closest thing in this witness to a machine."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 25 (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1921)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PistisSophia"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A leader among the Watchers, named in the Book of the Watchers as the one who disclosed metallurgy, weaponry and adornment."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Azazel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel",
        "en": "Azazel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Azâzêl",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Asael"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorders state once and for all that what they print represents one stage of the revelations granted to initiates of high standing who were not themselves responsible for the calculations concerning this part of the sky, and that the stage is deliberately distorted in both directions."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LightKnowledgeCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das veröffentlichte System ist eine Enthüllungsstufe, nicht das System",
        "en": "The published system is a stage of revelation, not the system"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A caution that cuts against the account carrying it, which is why it is held as a node. It makes the text unfalsifiable in the direction its later readers wanted: any discrepancy can be assigned to the withheld stage. It also means the article cannot be cited as the Dogon system without citing this sentence, and the reception has cited it without."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:LightKnowledge"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 274",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NoaidiJourneyIsDisplaced",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Reise ist ein verlagerter Ort",
        "en": "The journey is a displaced locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:displacedLocus"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:NoaidiJourney"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The practice is named for it: the journey is made while the body lies still, and a token is brought back from where the body was not. The placement records the account the tradition gives and not a verdict on it — the same compilation carries three positions on what actually lies there, and its author rejects the departure of the soul on doctrinal grounds rather than from anything he was told. See pmc:WhatLiesStillWhileHeTravels."
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, pp. 55-56",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:UncreatedLightCedesAgency",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das ungeschaffene Licht tritt die Trägerschaft ab",
        "en": "The uncreated light cedes agency"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:cededAgency"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:UncreatedLight"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The file's own note states the criterion: this is a claim about what shines through the practitioner, not about an emanation the practitioner produces or directs. The defence turns on the light being uncreated, which is what puts the acting side outside the practitioner."
      },
      "source": "Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts (trans. Gendle 1983)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The reading of weather and import from cloud formations."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KnowledgeOfClouds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wolkenkunde",
        "en": "Knowledge of the clouds"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteAdonaeus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Adonaeus, in der Liste genannt und in den Reden abwesend",
        "en": "Adonaeus, named in the list and absent from the speeches"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "He is the only one of the seven for whom the witness carries no speech. What is known of him here is his name and its derivation, and nothing else; the role recorded above is what the passage gives and is deliberately empty of office."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Adonaeus",
      "pm:nameRole": "one of the seven names of the hebdomad, taken from the Hebrew scriptures, where the text is Adonai; no office and no gate are given him",
      "skos:altLabel": {
        "@language": "de",
        "@value": "Adonaios"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Palm nuts are held in one hand and thrown into the other, one or two being dropped on purpose during the throw; if one nut is left a single mark is made and if two are left a double mark. Sixteen principal figures are called mothers and are set down with their marks and a symbol apiece; further figures called children are formed by taking the right side of one mother and the left side of another. The first and the last of the mothers are held to be the most powerful, and their children the most important."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Fa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fa, der Wurf der sechzehn Palmnüsse",
        "en": "Fa, the casting of the sixteen palm nuts"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The figure names are given by both witnesses in nearly the same forms, from the first, called the mother of all, to the sixteenth, called the father of all. They are the names of the Ifá figures, which is one half of the reason the contact route on this tradition is entered as documented.",
          "The second witness, who says he was himself taught the craft, gives the same casting with a differently proportioned board and puts the marks the other way round, adding of his own accord that the contrary may be the case. Two travellers who did not work from one another thus agree on the procedure and disagree on the reading rule, and the file keeps both."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Fon practitioners at Abomey and Whydah, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the naturalist J. A. Skertchly during his detention in Dahomey in 1871, published 1874; given in Fon, of which the work prints the figure names and a few terms, the account itself in English.",
      "source": "J. A. Skertchly, Dahomey as it is (London: Chapman and Hall, 1874), pp. 474-476",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:JeuCognitioYieldsLiberatio",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Erkennen des Wortes bringt die Freiheit vom Archon hervor",
        "en": "Knowing the word yields freedom from the ruler"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The mechanism given is not a passage and not a password. The ruler does not fight because he finds nothing of his own in the one he faces: what decides is a constitution and not a credential. That is the same reading the ophite speeches invite and never state, and here it is stated."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:JeuVerbumCognoscere"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:JeuLiberatioAbArchonte"
      },
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapter 2 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:BooksOfJeu"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MedicineEatingYieldsSeeing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Essen der Medizinen bringt das Sehen hervor",
        "en": "Eating the medicines yields the seeing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is not circular in the sense SPEC §11 forbids: the medicines are vegetable preparations named by their plants and the capacity is a perception ascribed to the eater, so the practice is not the capacity under another name."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:WitchDoctorMedicineEating"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SeeingWitchcraft"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 88-89",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity to bring back an object that has been lost, named in the narrative for iron and steel knives, which were rare at the time and often lost."
      },
      "id": "pmc:InuitRecoveryOfLostThings",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wiederfinden verlorener Dinge",
        "en": "The recovery of lost things"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect a chantway is held to obtain, which the witness says an error in a song may destroy."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NavajoCeremonyEfficacy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wirksamkeit der Zeremonie",
        "en": "The efficacy of the ceremony"
      },
      "source": "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 41, p. 24",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NavajoCeremonial"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiIsExercisedInWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die übernatürlichen Kräfte werden in abgezogener Vertiefung ausgeübt",
        "en": "The supernormal powers are exercised in withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The handbook assigns the powers to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption their natural plane. The absorption is one of the four described as awake and turned from the senses to a single object, which is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the exercise, not of the training that leads to it."
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A scale that reports how firmly a source sets what it says. Its values are read off the wording of the source: they are neither a judgement about the subject matter nor a judgement about the source's reliability."
      },
      "id": "pm:sourceModalityScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Quellenmodalitätsskala",
        "en": "Source modality scale"
      },
      "type": "pm:ScaleFamily"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:LegalActionPresupposesPoisonOracle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rechtsverfolgung setzt das Giftorakel voraus",
        "en": "Legal action presupposes the poison oracle"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is stated flatly and is not a matter of degree: the action cannot be brought. The passage dates from the period of the monograph, in which the declarations of the oracle had already ceased to be recognized as evidence in the courts the administration had set up, so the necessity is the transmission's own and not the colonial law's."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Legal action cannot be taken without a decision of the poison oracle."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:BengeValidVerdictRule"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 164",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Anishinaabe medicine society, with graded degrees and a mnemonic record incised on birchbark."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Midewiwin",
      "label": {
        "de": "Midewiwin",
        "en": "Midewiwin"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in the report of nineteen ninety-one for the first time, pages 143 to about 180 of the printed text: the account of the society, of the initiation, of the four degrees and of the pictographic charts. The song scrolls, the medicine formulas and the extensive appended vocabularies were not read. The recorder worked among the Ojibwa in eighteen eighty-seven to eighteen eighty-nine; his chief informants are named in the report, the principal one a second-degree priest of Mille Lacs first initiated about eighteen thirty.",
          "The birchbark scrolls are a written transmission by their own means, which puts this tradition closer to the project's source rule than most of the registry it came from.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and not as absent. What is disputed is the age of the society itself, not a route to any tradition this graph holds: one side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, the other points to a dated scroll fragment from before any documented European contact in the region. The dispute stands as its own node below, which is what pm:contactRouteDisputed requires before an agreement may be weighed at all. Note that the axis of the dispute and the axis a convergence asks about are not the same axis; see prima-materia#101."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The birchbark scroll record; Hoffman, The Midewiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa",
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 143-300",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HekhalotDescentPresupposesFitness",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Bestehen am sechsten Palast setzt die Eignung zum Abstieg voraus",
        "en": "Passing the sixth palace presupposes fitness to descend"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered at stated necessity because the consequence is quotable and because the passage sets no lesser outcome beside it: no second attempt, no purification that would restore the failed descender, no return."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The transmission states the consequence in a sequence and states it as violence, not as exclusion. The doorkeepers run after him and stone him; they name him of the seed of them that kissed the calf and unfit to see the King and his throne in his beauty; a voice from the seventh heaven confirms that they have said well; and he does not move from the spot before they place on him a thousand thousands of pieces of iron.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:YeridatMerkavah"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HekhalotFitness"
      },
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Chinese brass mirror with pendants, used as a placing for spirits and the one object that cannot be dispensed with. Where there is no costume the shaman can perform with the mirror alone; where there is no mirror or a substitute for it, no performance is possible. The word is the same across all Tungus dialects and Manchu and stands beside the Mongol, Buriat and Dahur forms."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusToli",
      "label": {
        "de": "Toli, der Bronzespiegel",
        "en": "Toli, the brass mirror"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "In the same passage he lists what stands beside it: a headdress with five images of Buddha among the Tungus of Manchuria, sometimes replaced by ornaments of Chinese style; the swastika and the Christian cross used by all shamans; the boa constrictor symbolised in the costume although unknown in the region; and Manchu shamans with weapons familiar to Buddhism and pictures reproducing the costume of Buddhist priests.",
          "The mirror carries the contact route on its face. The recorder states that it is widely used in Lamaism as one of the indispensable components of the altar, and that mirrors are either found in the earth, or received from predecessor shamans, or bought from the Mongols, who receive them from China and Tibet. The animals on the back are interpreted by each group according to its own imagination."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 D, p. 272, and chap. XXIII, sect. 99, p. 278",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InuitSpiritCallingAnchoringIsNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Geisterrufen gibt keine Sinnesbindung an",
        "en": "The spirit calling states no sensory anchoring"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:anchoringNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:InuitSpiritCalling"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The portion read gives the occasions on which the session is held and the outcomes claimed for it, and not the procedure; the node's own note already records that nothing was found there about posture, words or darkness. A session held in the household on the occasion that requires it is not thereby placed on this axis, and the silence is worth recording because the later chapters of the same volume are known to describe what this portion does not."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 24, 28",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "It is a bad omen for a cock to crow in the middle of the night, and the bird is immediately killed in order that the omen may be fulfilled at its own expense."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheOmenDischargedOnTheBird",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Hahn wird getötet, damit das Vorzeichen an ihm selbst eintritt",
        "en": "The cock is killed so that the omen falls on itself"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A procedure for discharging a prediction on a substitute, and thereby the cleanest case in this file of a claim that cannot come out wrong: the bad thing foretold has happened, to the bird. The graph holds nothing else of this shape.",
          "The witness draws his own conclusion two pages later, that to these people there is no such thing as an accident. That is his sentence and not the transmission's, and it is recorded here rather than entered as a claim."
        ]
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 96 and p. 98",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Askew Codex, in which the risen Jesus puts on a garment of light inscribed with the names of every order below, ascends through firmament, sphere, fate and the twelve aeons, and afterwards explains to his disciples what he did to the rulers on the way."
      },
      "id": "pmt:PistisSophia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pistis Sophia",
        "en": "Pistis Sophia"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Route of contact: documented and close. The work cites the two Books of Jeu by name as writings of its own circle, so nothing entered here may be counted as an independent witness beside traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl. Both belong, on Schmidt's account, to the same Egyptian group that the heresiologists split into Ophites, Sethians, Cainites and Archontics — which is the same reason nothing here counts independently beside traditions/ophite.ttl either.",
          "The work names itself otherwise. Schmidt shows that the title Pistis Sophia is a convenience: the manuscript's own subscription reads a part of the books of the Saviour, and the figure of the Pistis Sophia does not appear in the second half at all. The name is kept here because it is the one under which the work is found, and this note is the correction.",
          "Two editions in one file, and the edition therefore stands on every node rather than in this header. Their transliterations differ where it matters most for a later comparison: Schmidt writes Jeu, Mead writes Yew, for the same figure who is also the Jeu of traditions/books-of-jeu.ttl. Anyone tracing this name through the literature meets all three spellings before meeting a disagreement of substance.",
          "What was read: chapters 1 to 18, in the German translation of Carl Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I (GCS 13, Leipzig 1905), the volume that also carries the Books of Jeu; and chapter 25 separately, in the English of G. R. S. Mead (London 1921), because the first volume was read only as far as chapter 18. Chapters 19 to 24, everything from 26 onwards, the repentances of the Sophia and the whole of the fourth book with its rites are unread."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Pistis Sophia in the Askew Codex, British Museum MS Add. 5114; ed. and trans. Carl Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I, GCS 13 (Leipzig 1905)",
      "source": "Pistis Sophia 1-18 (trans. C. Schmidt, Koptisch-gnostische Schriften I, GCS 13, Leipzig 1905)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Burmese transmission of an accomplished practitioner who prolongs life by alchemy, diagram and mantra in order to await a future buddha."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BurmeseWeikza",
      "label": {
        "de": "Burmesische Weikza-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Burmese weikza transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A living tradition whose claims are made about named contemporaries, which is rare and worth more to an examination than any ancient ascription."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published weikza and dhat-lonn literature in Burmese",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account gives the period of the seed-star's orbit as some fifty years and the interval between two Sigui as sixty, and states in a footnote that there is here a contradiction which has not yet been resolved. It then offers, as a question and not as a finding, that the last ceremony may have been brought forward and that the profane may be kept in the belief that the official period is sixty years."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FiftyAgainstSixty",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fünfzig gegen sechzig: Bahnperiode und Zeremonienabstand",
        "en": "Fifty years against sixty: the period of the orbit and the interval of the ceremony"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered as a dispute although both sides stand in the same work, because the work states the disagreement itself rather than smoothing it. The dispute is decisive for everything the outside reception built on the passage: fifty years is the figure that matches the companion of Sirius, sixty is the figure the ceremony actually runs on, and only the second is attested by anything a later worker could count.",
          "The same footnote that reports the fifty-year period cites a French astronomical periodical of 1931 for the closeness of that period to the companion of Sirius. Whether the fifty came from the informants or from the reading is the question this graph cannot decide from the article alone; see prima-materia#250."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit"
      },
      "source": [
        "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 282 with its first footnote",
        "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 283 with its fifth footnote"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The passage through the palaces, called a descent although the traveller goes up. The transmission does not explain the word anywhere in the passages read."
      },
      "id": "pmp:YeridatMerkavah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg zur Merkava",
        "en": "The descent to the Merkavah"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The term is kept in the tradition's own form and not translated as ascent, because a rendering that corrected the direction would remove the only trace of whatever the word once meant. That it is nowhere explained is itself worth recording: a technical term that its own literature never glosses has usually come from somewhere else."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "yordei merkavah"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "yored merkavah"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Hekhalot Rabbati, chapter 26, trans. Morton Smith, corrected by Gershom Scholem, ed. Don Karr",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The potency of a human being and the emitted potency of a god. One is imparted to each person at birth and stays with him until death, enabling him to do what the beasts cannot and giving courage and fortitude; it may be pleased or displeased with its possessor and may be operative or inoperative at its own pleasure, may be invoked by ceremony or prayer, and can be imparted to no other person or thing. Most gods can emit their potencies, and an emitted potency can be put into a material thing by a shaman with a proper ceremony correctly performed. A potency so imparted is operative only when it is wrapped and hidden; wrapping, material and potency together make a wasicun, which the corpus renders as fetish."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Sicun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sicun, verliehene Potenz",
        "en": "Sicun, imparted potency"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A man may choose further potencies during his life, and the corpus says an evil man cannot secure a good one but may secure an evil one. The shaman who prepares a fetish devises a formula that must be repeated to invoke its powers."
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 87-88 and p. 158",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteIao",
      "label": {
        "de": "Iao, der bei Nacht scheint",
        "en": "Iao, who shines by night"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No gate number is given for him, only the ordinal second. Origen says elsewhere that the name comes from the Hebrew scriptures, where it is Iao or Jah."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Iao",
      "pm:nameRole": "second, who shines by night, ruler of the secret mysteries of son and father, first prince of death and portion of the innocent",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state of the contact question between this tradition and those the graph already holds."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Tradition",
      "id": "pm:contactRoute",
      "label": {
        "en": "contact route"
      },
      "range": "pm:ContactRoute",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim of the fourth section of the same roll: that the names shall enable a man to guard himself against evil men, liars, calumniators, men of wrath, the hand of him that would attack him, and the feet of those who would rush upon him by means of magical powers; and, in the petition that follows, that those who would oppose him in the time of judgment shall not do so."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianAsmatGuardAgainstMen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Namen schützen vor Menschen",
        "en": "The names guard against men"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Set beside the outcome of the sixth section because the two are of different kinds. Guarding oneself against liars has no moment at which it can be seen not to have happened; a spear turned back has one."
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 4, p. LVII",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of the Indian tantric completion-stage practices in Tibet, of which the six yogas of Nāropa are the best documented cycle."
      },
      "id": "pmt:TibetanTantric",
      "label": {
        "de": "Tibetisch-tantrische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Tibetan tantric tradition"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Tsongkhapa and Bar do thos grol citations name the translation they were read in. For Patrul Rinpoche's Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung neither edition nor translation is recorded, and the citation names no chapter; it is left unstated rather than guessed and the passage is to be reopened."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A modern work bearing on the claim under examination, given as a bibliographic reference or a work identifier."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:evidenceFrom",
      "label": {
        "de": "Evidenz aus",
        "en": "evidence from"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Deliberately not dcterms:source. A modern study is never a witness to a tradition and must never appear as the source of one of its claims; it bears on a claim only as evidence inside an examination. Keeping the two predicates apart is what allows research to enter the record at all without loosening the rule that sources are received works."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A search for opposing or non-replicating work was carried and returned nothing. This is a statement about the search, and the note must say what was searched."
      },
      "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundNothing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Gegensuche ohne Fund",
        "en": "Counter-search found nothing"
      },
      "type": "pm:CounterSearchState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The emergence of a form out of a prior undifferentiated ground."
      },
      "id": "pm:Manifesting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Manifestieren",
        "en": "Manifesting"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Female shamans do not perform at all during pregnancy and in the first weeks after the delivery, nor during the few days of menstruation each month. The consequence stated is practical and not ritual: she cannot help her clansmen at any moment when she might be needed, and this is given as one reason for a preference for male candidates."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusFemaleShamanSuspension",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wann eine Schamanin nicht auftritt",
        "en": "When a female shaman does not perform"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The same passage carries a second, separate reason, given as the assertion of the Tungus themselves: that females are physically less resistant and therefore cannot carry out very long and difficult performances. The two are reported side by side and are not the same kind of claim.",
          "The suspension reappears as the one excuse the office admits. A shaman who is not very old and physically weak may be excused from attending only if she is a female and pregnant or otherwise under the prohibition; without such an excuse he may lose his position."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, pp. 349-350, and chap. XXX, sect. 140, p. 380",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two cases are reported together. A man of a Lapp village whose reindeer were dying prayed to his idol, strewed fir boughs about it, offered the skins, horns and skulls of reindeer, and prostrated himself with his whole family beseeching it to testify by some sign that it was the true god; after a whole day of prayers and devotions, finding no sign given, he set fire to the wood he had brought and burned the idol down, and asked the neighbours who sought to kill him why they would not let the god avenge himself. And a man whose mother was grievously sick sought remedy from the drum; his mother died notwithstanding, whereupon he cut his drum in pieces, alleging that he saw no use of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AbandonmentAfterFailure",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zwei Praktiker, die das Instrument nach dem Versagen aufgaben",
        "en": "Two practitioners who gave up the instrument after it failed"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Both cases have the shape a protocol has: a question, a stated period, an outcome that could have fallen either way, and an act that follows from the outcome. In the first the period is a whole day and the demanded sign is named in advance. Neither man explains the failure away, and that is the whole point of entering them: it is the counter-case to a transmission that meets every disappointment with a named excuse.",
          "The provenance is the reason no more is made of them. Both stand in the chapter on the reception of Christianity, both are credited to a named priest of the mission, and both end with the man becoming a Christian or being praised as one. A missionary reporting that heathens destroyed their own idols after they failed is reporting the thing he most wanted to find. The cases are entered as claims standing in the work, and what they are evidence of is a separate question from what they say."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. VIII, Of the Religion of the Laplanders, p. 33",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The undoing of a binding laid by another, held in the text as a distinct art from the laying of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LoosingOfSpells",
      "label": {
        "de": "Lösen von Beschwörungen",
        "en": "Loosing of spells"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A negative finding, held as a node so that it is not lost: the old traditions test a claimed movement by its origin and its fruits, and none of those examined here tests a claimed capacity by counting hits against the rate obtainable by guessing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NoQuantitativeVerification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Keine Zählung gegen den Zufall in den alten Quellen",
        "en": "No count against chance in the old sources"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Any protocol that counts outcomes against a base rate is a modern addition and is to be recorded as one, not as the recovery of an ancient method."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:TestingOfThoughts"
      },
      "source": [
        "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 313-336",
        "John Cassian, Collationes I.20-22"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:WatchfulnessIsUnbound",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Wachsamkeit ist an keinen Zustand gebunden",
        "en": "Watchfulness is bound to no state"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:anchoringUnbound"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Watchfulness"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The guarding of the heart is set as an unbroken attention to what arises in it, and its whole point is that it does not stop when the occasion changes. A watchfulness held only in a session would not be the thing the literature asks for."
      },
      "source": "Philokalia I, Hesychios of Sinai, On Watchfulness and Holiness",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The finding that the passage nowhere assigns a planet to a gate or to a named ruler. The rulers are counted by gates and realms; the only celestial body named in the whole of the diagram material is Phaenon, and it is put in sympathy with the lion-like ruler of the other heptad."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteNullaOrdinatioPlanetaria",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Zeuge ordnet den Toren keine Planeten zu",
        "en": "The witness assigns no planets to the gates"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The planetary ladder does stand in the same book, and it is expressly other material. At VI.22 Celsus reports it from the mysteries of Mithras, with seven gates of metals assigned to Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, the Moon and the Sun, and Origen treats the quotation as an intrusion from a foreign cult. Celsus himself adds that the order is explained by musical reasons rather than by the planets. Reading the ophite gates through that ladder joins two things the text keeps apart, and it changes the order into the bargain: the Mithraic sequence is not the sequence of the gates.",
          "This is a statement about one witness and not about the transmission. The second witness does make the identification, and it makes it in a form nobody reports: Irenaeus has the holy hebdomad be the seven stars they call planets, collectively, and never says which name belongs to which star. See pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae. What is unattested in both witnesses is therefore not the planetary reading as such but the table that pairs each name with one planet. See prima-materia#595."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 and VI.30-32 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The shaman cannot do two things, the hunting and a regular carrying out of the performances, and therefore needs the support of the junior members of his clan. It is given as one reason why father and son are not both shamans and why the office commonly skips a generation."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusShamanCannotHunt",
      "label": {
        "de": "Amt und Jagd schließen einander aus",
        "en": "The office and the hunt exclude each other"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "An economic condition stated as such, and one of the few places where a transmission in this graph says what its own office costs the household that carries it."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 127, p. 345, and chap. XXX, sect. 140, pp. 378-381",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the bounded self is dissolved into, or united with, something that exceeds it, and treats the persistence of the separate self as what is to be given up."
      },
      "id": "pm:unitiveTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vereinigendes Ziel",
        "en": "Unitive telos"
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The outcome the same chapter attaches to that conduct: form and spirit together, the heaven-given years carried to their end, a hundred years passed through and then departure. Against it stands the stated outcome of the counter-case, decline at half a hundred."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vollständig gelebten hundert Jahre",
        "en": "The hundred years fully lived out"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a capacity claim and not as a piece of cosmology because the chapter states both arms and states them in the same terms. It is nonetheless not a claim any protocol reaches from here: the antecedent is a manner of living over a lifetime and the corpus supplies no way of establishing that it was kept."
      },
      "source": "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the five primary forms of devotional love stand in a single ascending order: peacefulness, respectful service, companionship, parental affection, amorous love, with the first counted lowest and the last highest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FivefoldPrimaryRasaRanking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die fünf primären Hingabeverhältnisse, nach Rang",
        "en": "The five primary devotional relations, ranked"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The ranking is asserted in the verse itself and not by a commentator. The operative words are yathā-pūrvam anuttamāḥ, rendered in the bilingual edition of two thousand and three as a hierarchical manner in which the first is considered the lowest, and in the independent translation published by a different lineage as an order of excellence running from first to last. Two translations from different hands and different schools agree on the direction, which is worth recording because the compound admits a second parsing that would reverse it.",
          "What the verse does not do is give the criterion. It states the order and moves on. Any statement of the form that the ranking follows increasing intensity, density or nearness of affection is drawn from elsewhere in the corpus and must be cited from there, not from this verse. The nearest thing the text supplies is the graded series inside a single relation, held separately below."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Rūpa Gosvāmin, Bhaktirasāmṛtasindhu 2.5.115 (trans. Haberman 2003)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GaudiyaVaisnava"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Perception of what is distant, hidden or obstructed."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DivyaCaksus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Göttliches Auge",
        "en": "Divine eye"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SuccessPresupposesNadiPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erfolg der Praxis setzt die Reinigung der Kanäle voraus",
        "en": "Success in the practice presupposes purification of the channels"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The text directs the breath practice at clearing the impurity of the central channel and gives the outward signs by which accomplished purification is known. It makes success depend on the purification without stating in these verses what befalls one who proceeds without it, which is why the strength here is order and not necessity."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:NadiPurification"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.19-20 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.6 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DescentPresupposesDepth",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Abstieg setzt die Tiefe voraus, die den Atem sammelt",
        "en": "The descent presupposes the depth that gathers the breath"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded at stated necessity because a consequence is quotable, which is unusual: most sequences in this collection state an order and leave the consequence unsaid. The strength rests on the closing line, not on the chain of conditionals, which on its own would carry only a prescribed order."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The inscription closes by setting the two outcomes against each other: to go with the sequence is to live, to go against it is to die. The consequence is stated for the order as a whole, and every stage is joined to the one before it by the same conditional form.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:XingqiDescent"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:XingqiDepth"
      },
      "source": "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Daoist"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteMichael",
      "label": {
        "de": "Michael, der Löwengestaltige",
        "en": "Michael the lion-like"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Michael",
      "pm:nameRole": "first of the seven ruling demons of the diagram, lion-like; honoured in scripture as the angel of the Creator",
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen",
        "en": "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Lower Congo and Loango transmission of composed objects charged to act, whose power is held to be renewed by sacrifice and destroyed by the breaking of a stated prohibition, and of a poison ordeal by which an accusation of witchcraft is decided at an outcome the operator is not supposed to control."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KongoNkisi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kongolesische Nkisi-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Kongo nkisi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance. What the reading found is that the entrance was mistaken for this transmission and the correction is the chief finding of the run: the contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and it is documented from inside the corpus and not from the scholarship. The word the transmission uses for a charged object is the word the old Catholic mission chose for holy, and the mission called its own churches nzo ankisi; the Kongo name for the devil is stated to have stood for four hundred years; the image of the Virgin in the church at San Salvador is addressed for a child like any other charged thing, and the crosses, discs and pictures given out by the priests are called by the same word as an amulet. A transmission whose central term was in liturgical use for four centuries cannot be counted as a witness without a route.",
          "One work could not be read where it should have been. The digitised text of Bentley 1900 volume one that was reachable breaks between printed page two hundred and thirty-seven and printed page two hundred and seventy-four, and the chapter on the charged objects themselves lies inside that gap, as does the passage in which a retiring witch-doctor is said to have put a number on her own false denunciations. The gap belongs to the copy and not to the work, so the pages are owed and stand as an issue, not as a property of the work.",
          "Read in this run at the wording: in Weeks 1914 the two chapters on magic and on charms and their prohibitions, printed pages two hundred and sixteen to two hundred and fifty-one, and the chapter on divination and the ordeal, printed pages two hundred and sixty-one to two hundred and sixty-five; in Weeks, Folk-Lore volume twenty, printed pages fifty-one to fifty-eight; in Bentley 1900 volume one, printed pages two hundred and thirty-six to two hundred and thirty-seven and two hundred and seventy-four to two hundred and ninety-three; in Dennett 1906, printed pages twenty-four to twenty-five, seventy-three to seventy-seven, eighty-three, eighty-six to ninety-three and one hundred and forty-seven to one hundred and forty-eight. The rest of each work was searched by term for the owed kinds and was not read continuously; that is a decision and not an exhaustion.",
          "The corpus is widened at the instance and the coined identifier is kept. The registration named the Laman notebooks in Kikongo and Laman, The Kongo; neither was opened. The four volumes are in any case a derivative rather than a source, since the notebooks were sorted, cut and fused in making them, and what a later run must obtain is the individual notebook with its author and district. What was read instead is four public-domain works of the years eighteen ninety-five to nineteen fourteen, three of them by men resident in the country for decades.",
          "The recorder problem is stated here rather than left in the sources. All four works are by outsiders: two Baptist missionaries at San Salvador and in the Ngombe Lutete district, and a trader on the Loango coast. Two consequences run in opposite directions and both are kept. The missionaries report the transmission as an evil to be ended, and every judgement of theirs is to be weighed separately from their observations. But their reports of fraud, of doubt among the people, and of promises that failed are reports against the interest of the thing they were describing and not for it, and they are the part of the corpus this graph can least afford to lose.",
          "Two speech communities stand in one file and the file says so rather than smoothing it. Weeks and Bentley record Kikongo-speaking Bakongo of the Lower Congo around San Salvador, Wathen and Ngombe Lutete; Dennett records Vili-speaking Bavili of Loango, whose orthography differs and who write nkici where the others write nkisi or nkixi. Dennett states that the overlord of Maluango was the ruler of Kongo, so the two stand in one documented sphere; whether the Loango transmission is to be registered as its own is a decision that has not been taken and is held as an issue."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo; Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vols. XIX-XX; Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo, and the Appendix to the Dictionary and Grammar of the Kongo Language; Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind, and Notes on the Folklore of the Fjort; the Laman notebooks in Kikongo, with Laman, The Kongo, as the derivative made from them",
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 230-231",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Vomiting acquits and retention condemns, with a confirming step: should the accused vomit three times he is given a fourth dose, and if he vomits that he is held to have proved beyond doubt that he is not a witch. He does not clear himself if he fails to vomit, or if there are signs of blood or of green matter in what he brings up, or if he has bad diarrhoea; in that case he is brought out of the hut and killed and the body left on the hill."
      },
      "id": "pmc:NkasaVerdictRule",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Urteilsregel des Ordals",
        "en": "The verdict rule of the ordeal"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A fourth dose after three successful vomits is a repetition of the trial by the transmission itself, and the graph holds only one other case of a prescribed repetition, the second consultation of the Zande poison oracle. The difference is that the Zande control must reverse the first answer and this one must repeat it.",
          "The second witness records that the rule is not the same everywhere, and the variants are the finding. In some districts the vomit is examined and guilt inferred from something in it; in others the man who has vomited must run down into the valley and up the next hill, which he is usually in no condition to do, so that the vomiting has proved nothing. The same witness states that in some districts the ordeal-giver loses his fee if the accused vomits and may be punished, and that he is sometimes bribed to prepare a mild dose that will ensure one."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 263",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 276-277"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Vaiṣṇava tantric transmission, in which the practitioner's body is dissolved and rebuilt by placement before any worship may proceed."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Pancaratra",
      "label": {
        "de": "Pāñcarātra",
        "en": "Pāñcarātra"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The body-construction sequence is a prerequisite chain of unusual explicitness and belongs in the graph for that reason before any doctrine of the school does."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Jayākhya Saṃhitā; Lakṣmī Tantra; Sātvata Saṃhitā",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Whoever is to enter one of the greater offices fasts: a month, in other places six, in others a year, eating neither salt nor chilli, not sleeping with his wife, not washing and not combing. In some places he must not bring his hands to his body, and one who kept it said that in the time of his fast he scratched himself with a little stick."
      },
      "id": "pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fasten vor dem Antritt eines höheren Amtes",
        "en": "The fast before entering a major office"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. III",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Four statements from inside the corpus, all of them by missionaries reporting their own trade. The word chosen by the old Catholic missionaries for holy was nkisi, and the word they chose for church was nzo ankisi, which is the ordinary word for a grave and means the house of the charged things, so that they spoke of the church of Rome as the Roman grave. The Kongo name for the devil is stated to have stood for four hundred years. The image of the Virgin in the Catholic church at San Salvador is one of the places a couple goes for a child, the church itself being called the house of the charged things. And the crosses, metal discs and small pictures given by the priests to be worn are called by the same word as an ordinary protective amulet."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TheMissionEnteredTheNkisiCategory",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Mission trat in die Kategorie des geladenen Dings ein",
        "en": "The mission entered the category of the charged thing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It also fixes the date. A term in liturgical use since the end of the fifteenth century cannot be treated as untouched in a corpus written down between eighteen eighty-seven and nineteen fourteen, and any agreement between this transmission and the Christian material this graph holds is reception until somebody shows otherwise.",
          "This is the node that carries the contact route of the whole file, and it is entered as a node rather than as a note on the tradition because a note would not survive a query. It shows the route in the direction that matters here: not that the mission borrowed a word, which would be a fact about the mission, but that the objects of the mission were classified by the transmission under its own category, which is a fact about the transmission."
        ]
      },
      "source": [
        "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 240",
        "W. Holman Bentley, Pioneering on the Congo (London: Religious Tract Society, 1900), vol. I, pp. 236-237 and p. 281"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Tamil devotional transmission of the twelve Ālvār, in which the relation to the deity is sung in the first person and the states it passes through are named."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Alvar",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ālvār-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Ālvār transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earliest of the devotional corpora and the one on which the later theory of devotional states rests. Worth registering ahead of the others for the same reason the Gauḍīya file was opened: the states are named, not only described."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham, in particular the Tiruvāymoḻi of Nammāḻvār",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the attempt to fail the claim has been carried."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:examinationState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Prüfstand",
        "en": "examination state"
      },
      "range": "pm:ExaminationState",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Giftorakel bringt die Benennung des Hexers hervor",
        "en": "The poison oracle yields the naming of the witch"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:BengeConsultation"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:IdentificationOfTheWitch"
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), pp. 35-36 and pp. 141-143",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity ascribed to a priest of the fourth degree to call forth the shadow, that is the soul, and to retain it within his grasp at pleasure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MideSoulSeizing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Herausrufen des Schattens und sein Festhalten",
        "en": "Calling forth the shadow and holding it"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 170",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The path claims that the creature's proper movement is to hold itself as itself against a dissolution that would end it, while denying that the movement is towards difference or towards becoming an absolute; what is sought is the creature's own being and not its distinction from others."
      },
      "id": "pm:individuatingTelos",
      "label": {
        "de": "Individuierendes Ziel",
        "en": "Individuating telos"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Between the two older values and reducible to neither. It refuses dissolution, so it is not unitive; and it refuses the striving after difference, so it is not separative. The distinction is fine and load-bearing: a scheme that reads every refusal of dissolution as self-deification cannot state it, and that misreading is common enough to be worth a term."
      },
      "type": "pm:TelicOrientation"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSightIsNotByEffort",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das zweite Gesicht wird nicht durch Anstrengung erlangt",
        "en": "Second sight is not attained by effort"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:notByEffort"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The narrative reports the capacity as a standing property of a person and expressly not as the outcome of an exercise. No training is named for it anywhere in the portion read."
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The part given over to the life of Christ up to Palm Sunday, and to the following of it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SecondWeek",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zweite Woche",
        "en": "The second week"
      },
      "source": "Ignatius of Loyola, Spiritual Exercises 4",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A Kurdish transmission carried chiefly in hymn and recitation, teaching successive returns of the soul."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Yazidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jesidische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Yazidi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The two books circulated as its scripture are held by most scholarship to be forgeries of the early twentieth century, which makes the corpus question itself the first thing to decide."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Qewls (hymns) in the published collections; the disputed Mišefa Reš and Kitêba Cilwe",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The source holds that the distinction does not apply to its path: nothing is kept and nothing is broken, because it denies that anything needs to be renounced, purified or transformed."
      },
      "id": "pm:normDistinctionDeclined",
      "label": {
        "de": "Abgelehnte Normunterscheidung",
        "en": "Norm distinction declined"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The value exists because a path that declines the axis is not the same as one whose position on it is unknown, and a scale without it would force the first to be recorded as the second. It is asserted only where a source says so."
      },
      "type": "pm:MethodOrientation"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The restriction stated in the opening prayer: that these are mysteries which a father alone may hand on, and that only to a son, an initiate worthy of the craft; the rite is for one person alone, who is to raise himself."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein Vater darf es weitergeben, und nur an einen Sohn",
        "en": "A father may hand it on, and only to a son"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It sits oddly beside the rite itself, which is expressly solitary and needs no officiant. What is withheld is therefore the text and not the performance, and a text that has been in print since nineteen hundred and seven is no longer withheld by it.",
          "The same gate the project's criterion is aimed at, in a third transmission after the Ars Notoria and the ophite requirement of a curse before admission. The restriction here is narrower than either: not an order, not a school, but one person to one person."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, the father's prayer (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The number of mastered spirits is the measure of the shaman's power. A beginner has at least one, usually a complex one, with whose help he masters others; among the Tungus of Manchuria beginners are stated to have five or six, and by the end of a good career all spirits are mastered directly or through other spirits. The number is expected to increase with the years practised."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusListOfSpirits",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Schamane braucht eine Liste von Geistern",
        "en": "The shaman must have a list of spirits"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is quantified and the quantity is watched by other people, which is unusual in this graph. It also has a stated downward direction: a shaman who fails to master enough spirits is called a small shaman, not a real one, and when the practice is given up for lack of new spirits the person is said to be no more a shaman."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXII, sect. 95 B, pp. 271-272",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantTouch",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Grad bringt das Fernberühren hervor",
        "en": "The second degree yields touching at a distance"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MideInitiationSecondDegree"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MideDistantTouch"
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), p. 168",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Cases have been recorded and show no deviation from the stated base rate."
      },
      "id": "pm:casesWithoutDeviation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Fälle ohne Abweichung",
        "en": "Cases without deviation"
      },
      "type": "pm:ExaminationState"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the second of the six adversities of the burial book, that a going wrong of the year and the season makes the burial adverse however favourable the ground. A memorial of the seventh century, written on an imperial commission to revise the divinatory literature, sets against it a count: the burial books call one day of the sexagenary cycle the most inauspicious of all for a burial, and a check of the classical annals turns up more than twenty burials on that day."
      },
      "id": "pmp:BurialDayExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Tag und Jahreszeit der Bestattung etwas entscheiden",
        "en": "Whether the day and season of a burial decide anything"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The count is treated as evidence and not as testimony, and the reason is the functional line SPEC §14 draws rather than the date. It is a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise: the annals might have shown no burial on that day, and then the memorial would have had nothing. That it was carried in the seventh century does not make it a witness to a transmission; it makes it an early instance of the only kind of check this doctrine admits.",
          "The state stays at procedure-without-cases all the same, and the distinction is the one this protocol exists to hold. What the count establishes is that the classical corpus, which the doctrine claims as its warrant, buried on the forbidden day more than twenty times. That is a case against the antiquity of the rule and not against its effect: the memorial does not say what became of those twenty houses. Setting the state to cases-with-deviation here would report the reach of a philological check as a result about the world.",
          "Two things a later run would have to establish before the count could be used at all, and neither was done here. Which day of the cycle the memorial names, and whether the burial books it checked are the recension of the burial book this graph holds — it speaks of a hundred and twenty houses of the art, and the text in the graph is a redaction of the fourteenth century. Until both are settled the evidence points at the doctrine and not yet at this node."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": "Searched for the defending side within the works opened, and it is there. The burial book itself supplies the sixth adversity, whose commentary lists the delays, quarrels, poverty, wars, lawsuits and neglect through which a burial fails to happen, so that a favourable grave without a fortune is always attributable; and the commentary cites two cases the other way, a reader of graves who foretold the extinction of a general's clan and a duke of the annals who moved against a prognostication and died in the fifth month. Neither was traced to the works it names. Not searched: whether anyone answered the memorial itself in writing, and whether the later burial literature took up its count.",
      "pm:evidenceFrom": "Jiu Tangshu 79, the memorial of Lü Cai on the burial books, juan 79, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text",
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Lü Cai, in the memorial on the burial books recorded in Jiu Tangshu 79, seventh century; recorded here from the passage in traditions/chinese-divination.ttl",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": "A comparison of what followed burials made on the day the books forbid with what followed burials made on days they allow, over a set of burials fixed before the outcomes are known. The doctrine claims a difference; no difference falls against it, and a difference in the wrong direction falls against it twice.",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-09-09",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:SanjiLiuxiong"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Repentance, placed first among the stations in the handbook enumeration."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Tawba",
      "label": {
        "de": "Umkehr",
        "en": "Turning back"
      },
      "source": "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya, chapter on the stations",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Sufism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of messages ascribed to a command or council of non-terrestrial beings charged with the oversight of the earth, received after the first by many transmitters who do not share a lineage."
      },
      "id": "pmt:AshtarTransmission",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ashtar-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Ashtar transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The motif of a federation or confederation of worlds enters general circulation here and is afterwards found in corpora that never name Ashtar. That spread is a reception history and belongs in the graph as one, before anybody reads it as agreement between independent witnesses."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Van Tassel, the Ministry of Universal Wisdom publications; the published Ashtar message collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act the whole transmission turns on. The priest sits on the ground, extends the left hand towards the huaca, names it, and makes with the lips the sucking sound that is what the word properly means; he says that he comes and brings what its children and creatures offer it, asks it to receive them and not be angry and to give life and health and good fields, pours chicha before it and sometimes over it, smears it with the blood of guinea pigs or llamas, and burns or blows the rest of the offerings."
      },
      "id": "pmp:MocharTheHuaca",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mochar: der Akt der Anrede",
        "en": "Mochar: the act of address"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The recorder states expressly that to have mochado a huaca does not mean to have seen it or knelt before it, because the adoration does not consist in that. It consists in having given the offering to the priest at the appointed place and joined the invocation with the left hand raised and open. That correction is his, and it is the most useful sentence in the chapter: it says that the act is participation and not sight, which is what makes the next node possible."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Andean"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Sámi transmission of a drum read by the travel of a ring across painted figures, of an office entered through a threefold seizure, and of a journey to a distant place undertaken while the body lies as if dead and from which a token is brought back."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SamiNoaidi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Samische Noaidi-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Sámi noaidi transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Read in this run, both at the wording: the compilation of sixteen seventy-four in its Oxford English edition, chapter VIII on the religion and chapter XI on the magical ceremonies; and the account of Danish Lapland of seventeen sixty-seven in the English translation printed in a collection of eighteen hundred and eight, its chapter on the magic arts. Left unread: the remaining thirty-three chapters of the first and the remaining twenty-two of the second, and the treatise on the pagan religion of the Finns and Lapps printed with the second.",
          "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as undecided, and the ground is inside the practice rather than around it. By seventeen sixty-seven a named practitioner marked himself and his drum with the sign of the cross, said the Lord's Prayer over it and invoked God the Father, the Son and an angel before striking it. That is a route to the Christian material this graph already holds, visible in the operation itself and not inferred from proximity. It says nothing about the route to the Siberian material or to the Norse, which is the question the registry entry meant and which is still not carried out; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers, so it holds the stronger. See prima-materia#101.",
          "The recorder of seventeen sixty-seven applies source criticism to his own material, and it is entered here because it is rare and because it bears on how the rest of this file is to be read. Of the confessions of witchcraft in the Norwegian trials he says that it is probable they were extorted, partly by violence and fear, and that it appears from the law books that some were put to the torture before they would confess. He then separates that material from what he goes on to report of the Sámi themselves. The separation is his and it is the reason the trial material is not in this file.",
          "The two are of different kinds and that is why both were opened. The first was written by a professor at Uppsala who never went to Lapland, on the commission of the Chancellor of Sweden, out of the written reports of parish priests whom he names in the text — so what it carries is at one remove and its author says so. The second was written by a missionary who lived among the people he describes and who reports named practitioners in their own words. The first is a compilation with a stated method; the second is first-hand and confessional. Where the two agree, the agreement is worth something; where the first stands alone, it stands on reports it names but this graph has not seen."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Schefferus, Lapponia, in the English edition The History of Lapland; Leem, Beskrivelse over Finmarkens Lapper; the eighteenth-century missionary manuscripts of Skanke and Randulf; Jessen, De religione paganorum Fennorum et Lapponum",
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 45-47",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The leader of the Watchers who binds the others by oath on Mount Hermon and is named as the teacher of spells and root-cutting."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Shemihazah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schemichasa",
        "en": "Shemihazah"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:nameForm": "Semjâzâ",
      "pm:nameRole": "watcher",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Amezarak"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Semjaza"
        }
      ],
      "source": "1 Enoch 6:3, 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Naming",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that in the present age success is attained by Kaula worship alone, and that the mantra received from a Kaula brings knowledge of Brahman, where the mantra received from one of the lower dispositions reproduces that disposition in the receiver."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KaulaAboveTheOtherWays",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die kaulische Verehrung als der in diesem Zeitalter tragende Weg",
        "en": "Kaula worship as the way that succeeds in this age"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Kaula is set above the other ways here, not beside them. That is the same order Wilson translates from a passage he ascribes to the Kulārṇava, in which the left hand stands above the right and Kaula above all — an order the Western pair of names keeps while reversing its valuation. Two witnesses, one of them located by chapter and verse in a named translation and one not; the second is still to be found. See prima-materia#56."
      },
      "source": [
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra XIV.203 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The seat, treated in the first chapter as the first limb of the practice."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Asana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sitzhaltung",
        "en": "Posture"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The identification the second witness makes: that the holy hebdomad is the seven stars which they call planets. It is made of the group as a whole and is never broken down; no name in the list is paired with a star."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OphiteHebdomasPlanetae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die heilige Hebdomas sind die sieben Sterne, die sie Planeten nennen",
        "en": "The holy hebdomad is the seven stars they call planets"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the exact state of the evidence that the received tables overwrite in both directions. Whoever says the ophites had no planetary doctrine has not read this sentence; whoever prints a column pairing Ialdabaoth with Saturn and Horaios with the Moon has supplied what the sentence withholds. A collective identification without a distribution is a third thing, and it is the thing the witnesses give."
      },
      "source": "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.9 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A scale that reports how far the work of recording, searching and examining has been carried. Its values describe what has been done to the subject matter, never the subject matter itself."
      },
      "id": "pm:recordScale",
      "label": {
        "de": "Aufzeichnungsskala",
        "en": "Record scale"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The cardinal error this family exists to prevent is the collapse of a state that reports absent work into one that reports a finding: a claim for which no procedure was devised and a claim examined and unsupported are different findings and must never read alike."
      },
      "type": "pm:ScaleFamily"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:MayaIxRemedyYieldsAverting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Abwehr befreit von den geweissagten Übeln",
        "en": "The conjuring frees from the ills foretold"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered with its own weakness stated, because the weakness is the finding. The witness names no sign by which the freeing would be recognised, no interval within which the ills would otherwise have come, and no consequence of omitting the procedure. The ills themselves are drawn wide enough to include drought and political discord together. Nothing in the passage marks out an outcome the claim would exclude, and the harvest rule keeps signs of success apart from conditions of failure exactly because a transmission can be rich in the first and empty of the second. This one is."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:MayaIxYearRemedy"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:MayaAvertingOfForetoldIlls"
      },
      "source": "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXXVII, pp. 65-66",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:MayaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Jain transmission of restraint, austerity and the shedding of karmic matter, with its own catalogue of powers said to arise along the way."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Jain",
      "label": {
        "de": "Jainistische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Jain transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A second capacity list formulated in the same region and period as the yogic one and not derived from it. Within the Indian sphere this is the closest thing to a control case."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ācārāṅga Sūtra; Umāsvāti, Tattvārthasūtra; Hemacandra, Yogaśāstra",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Saṃyama auf die Vorstellung bringt Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins hervor",
        "en": "Saṃyama on the presented idea yields knowledge of another mind"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Samyama"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:ParacittaJnana"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.19",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotraIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das göttliche Ohr wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt",
        "en": "The divine ear is exercised in withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:DivyaSrotra"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The sūtra has the divine ear follow from saṃyama on the relation between the organ of hearing and the ether. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."
      },
      "source": [
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A closed transmission teaching the transfer of the soul into a new body at death, with an internal literature restricted to the initiated."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Druze",
      "label": {
        "de": "Drusische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Druze transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A case where the gate the project exists to remove is still shut and still enforced. Registration says so rather than pretending the corpus is available."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The state reported after the seven: stripped of all the energisings of the harmony and clothed in his proper power, the ascending one comes to the nature belonging to the Eighth and there hymns the Father with those that are; they welcome him, he is made like to them, and he further hears the powers above the eighth nature singing to God in a language of their own."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticOgdoas",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die achte Natur, und was über ihr liegt",
        "en": "The eighth nature, and what lies above it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two stages and not one, and the second is only heard. The passage marks a boundary the ascent does not cross by the same means: what lies above the Eighth is audible before it is entered, and entering it is described in the next sentence as a surrender of self rather than a further passage."
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The first object of the ascent, from which the climb begins."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BeautyOfOneBody",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Schönheit eines Körpers",
        "en": "The beauty of one body"
      },
      "source": "Plato, Symposium 210a-211c",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Platonic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Strictly the rubbing-board requires the same corroborative second test as the poison and termites oracles, with the opposite outcome for the verdict to be valid. In fact the second test is very seldom made, and important questions are taken to the poison oracle instead."
      },
      "id": "pmc:RubbingBoardSecondTestSeldomMade",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der zweite Test am Reibbrett wird selten gemacht",
        "en": "The second test at the rubbing-board is seldom made"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The passage is a self-warning of the transmission against a rule it lays down, and it is the sharpest one this file carries: the control procedure exists for the instrument that is used most often and is precisely the one it is not applied to. The recorder adds the inference that the Azande must be aware that the second test always confirms the first. That is his conclusion and not a reported Zande statement, and it is not entered as a claim of the transmission."
      },
      "source": "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 171",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:AzandeOracle"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:AzazelTeachesMetalworking",
      "label": {
        "de": "Azazel offenbart Metallverarbeitung",
        "en": "Azazel discloses metalworking"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Metalworking"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Azazel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:1 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteOratioYieldsTransitus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Rede am Tor erwirkt den Durchgang",
        "en": "The speech at the gate obtains the passage"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "No condition of failure is stated anywhere in the passage. The witness gives what is to be said and never what happens to one who says it without having what the words claim, nor any sign by which the passer would know he had been refused. The claim is therefore not examinable from this source, and that is a property of the source and not of the claim."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:OphiteTransitusOratio"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.31 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that what is called sleep paralysis is the ordinary switching off of motor control that happens as sleep is entered, and therefore a regular part of the architecture of sleep rather than a disorder."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ParalysisIsTheRegularShutdown",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Lähmung ist die reguläre Abschaltung der Motorik am Schlafübergang",
        "en": "The paralysis is the regular shutdown of movement at the threshold of sleep"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The weaker of the two claims in this file and separated from the other on purpose. This one places the event and calls it regular; pmc:ParalysisPrecedesItsNotice adds that it is universal and that only its noticing is rare. The two are examined apart because they fail apart, and one of them does."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#137",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Steiner's transmission of a schooling of perception said to open supersensible cognition, presented as a discipline with stated stages and moral prerequisites."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Anthroposophy",
      "label": {
        "de": "Anthroposophie",
        "en": "Anthroposophy"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The prerequisites are stated as conditions without which the exercises are held to be harmful, which makes them a prerequisite chain of the strong kind rather than an exhortation."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Steiner, Wie erlangt man Erkenntnisse der höheren Welten; Steiner, Die Geheimwissenschaft im Umriss",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CrookedLimbsIfTheOfficeIsRefused",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das verweigerte Amt wird am Leib bezahlt",
        "en": "The office refused is paid for in the body"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at stated necessity, and the consequence is quoted rather than paraphrased because it is the sharpest one in the file. The case is a single one, among the Khingan Tungus: a girl of fifteen entered by a clan spirit, taught by an old shaman to distinguish the questions asked by the assistants, a shaman at the time of the report for some twenty-five years.",
          "One case is a case and not a rate. What makes it worth a node is that the transmission states a specific bodily outcome for the refusal, which is the shape a condition of failure has, and not a general misfortune."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "If she had not become a shaman, her arms or legs or neck, or back would have been crooked for life."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusCandidateCondition"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits"
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, p. 349",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Two statements from two witnesses who did not work from one another. The first says that among the eastern Ewe tribes divination is always practised after the manner of Ife, the Yoruba god of divination, and elsewhere that those tribes have their notion of the passage of souls from their Yoruba-speaking neighbours. The second identifies the Fon practitioner of the oracle with the babalawo of the Egba, and reports that the figure names are the same."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FaCameFromTheYoruba",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Orakel soll von den Yoruba stammen",
        "en": "The oracle is said to have come from the Yoruba"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the node that carries the contact route of the file. It has a consequence for counting and not only for history: the graph already holds the Ifá casting in traditions/ifa.ttl, and an agreement between the two is the same statement twice, not two witnesses. Whether the chain runs further back, to the sand divination of the Arabic-speaking world, is asserted by one of the witnesses in a passage that lies in a volume this run could not reach, and is held as an issue."
      },
      "source": [
        "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), p. 97",
        "Richard F. Burton, A Mission to Gelele, King of Dahome (2nd ed., London: Tinsley Brothers, 1864), vol. II, p. 143"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A retention in which the mouth is held closed and the breath is drawn through both nostrils so that it grazes the throat and sounds as it passes."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Ujjayi",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ujjāyī",
        "en": "Ujjāyī"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.51-53 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ShamsielTeachesSignsOfTheSun",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schamsiel offenbart die Zeichen der Sonne",
        "en": "Shamsiel discloses the signs of the sun"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfTheSun"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Shamsiel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How firmly the source sets the prerequisite."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Presupposing",
      "id": "pm:prerequisiteStrength",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stärke der Voraussetzung",
        "en": "prerequisite strength"
      },
      "range": "pm:PrerequisiteStrength",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An address to the spirits of dead ancestors, invoked in genealogical order and closing in a petition that the invoked power give the address its effect. The witness insists that the ordinary rendering as charm is wrong and that invocation comes nearer."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Karakia",
      "label": {
        "de": "Karakia",
        "en": "Karakia"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The genealogies are said to be preserved because they form the ground-work of the invocation, so that the descent recited and the efficacy claimed are the same fact for this tradition rather than two."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), pp. 10-11, and the vocabulary entry karakia, p. 108",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Maori"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about the age of the society. One side reads it as a nativistic formation of the contact period, assembled from older elements in response to epidemic, trade and mission. The other points to a birchbark scroll fragment from an archaeological context, radiocarbon dated to about the middle of the sixteenth century, which is earlier than any documented European contact with the central Algonkians."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MidewiwinAgeDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob die Gesellschaft dem europäischen Kontakt vorausgeht",
        "en": "Whether the society precedes European contact"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a node because everything else in this file depends on it. If the society is a formation of the contact period, its agreement with anything the graph already holds has to be weighed differently, and the value at pm:contactRoute changes with the outcome.",
          "Only one side was read at the wording in this run. The dating, the laboratory reference and the argument against the nativistic reading were read in the note of nineteen eighty-one; the nativistic reading itself was read only as that note quotes it, and the paper of nineteen sixty-two that states it in full was not obtained. See prima-materia#118."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmt:Midewiwin"
      },
      "source": [
        "Harold Hickerson, The Chippewa and Their Neighbors: A Study in Ethnohistory (New York: Holt, Rinehart and Winston, 1970), p. 63, as quoted in Kidd 1981, p. 42",
        "Kenneth E. Kidd, A Radiocarbon Date on a Midewiwin Scroll from Burntside Lake, Ontario, Ontario Archaeology 35 (1981), pp. 41-43"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CizhenPresupposesJingshen",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Nadelung setzt voraus, dass der Geist da ist",
        "en": "Needling presupposes that the spirit is present"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Two passages state it and both state a consequence rather than a degree. Where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured, and the body worn out and the blood exhausted the treatment establishes nothing. Where the five, essence, spirit, hun, po and the intent, are damaged, the needle cannot treat him at all."
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:Cizhen"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Jingshen"
      },
      "source": [
        "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The division of the day the orations follow: once in the morning, once about the third hour, once about the ninth hour and once at evening; where a great work presses, twice in the morning and twice about the ninth hour."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaHorae",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Tagesstunden und die Zahl der Wiederholungen",
        "en": "The hours of the day and the count of repetitions"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 13-14, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The corpus names three limits on its own means. The Moon fixes the time for the more important undertakings of mankind but is indifferent to ceremonies and cannot be influenced by them, and her potency can be imparted to nothing. The Wind cannot be influenced by sacrifice or ceremony and his potency can be imparted to nothing. And the Sun's potency abides in fire and can be imparted to no other thing, so that since fire cannot be clothed in wrappings, a fetish having the potency of the Sun cannot be prepared at all; a second informant puts the same thing as a refusal rather than an impossibility, saying that no one ever had the potency of the Sun for his own, for the Sun will not be that for anyone."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WhatCeremonyCannotReach",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Götter, die keine Zeremonie beeinflussen kann",
        "en": "The gods that ceremony cannot influence"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A fourth is recorded beside them without being one of them: a fetish carrying the potency of the Great Spirit is said to be able to prevail in all things and to be not permissible to mankind. That is a prohibition and not a limit of the means, and it is not entered as one.",
          "Statements of what does not work are scarcer in this graph than statements of what does, and they are worth more per line, because a transmission that names the boundary of its own instrument is making a claim that its own practice could refute. Here there are three of them in a corpus that elsewhere ascribes very wide powers to a shaman."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 82 and p. 83 and p. 88 and p. 158",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1950",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The recorders state that the material was put in order in the sense that the sayings of the four principal informants were fused into one and the same exposition. What the printed system presents as a single doctrine is therefore a composite, and no sentence in it can be assigned to a named speaker."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FusionOfTheFourInformants",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vier Gewährsleute zu einer Darstellung verschmolzen",
        "en": "Four informants fused into one account"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an ordering act because the recorders describe it as one in the same breath in which they claim to give the raw documents. The two claims are in tension on the same page: material that has been fused across four speakers is no longer raw, and the fusion is the step at which a disagreement between informants would have become invisible.",
          "One informant is described as having supplied the system in its entirety and the others only its essential parts, and one of them obtained a supplement between the visits of the enquirers, from people elsewhere. The account is thus composite in a second sense: part of it was gathered by an informant on the enquirers' behalf between their stays."
        ]
      },
      "pm:compiledBy": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen",
      "pm:ordersClaim": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:EmmeYaTolo"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoTolo"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoToloAsAxis"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 273-274",
      "type": "pm:Systematizing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The record the priests keep on birchbark, incised in fine lines, giving pictorially the ground plan of the degrees to which the owner is entitled."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BirchBarkRecord",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Birkenrindenkarte",
        "en": "The birchbark chart"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Two things follow from this and both bear on how the rest of the file is to be read. The society keeps a written record by its own means, which is unusual among the transmissions this graph registers as lacking a corpus. And the capacities of the degrees are stated as readings of marks on such a chart, so what the report carries is an informant's exegesis of a pictograph and not a free verbal account; that is a different kind of witness, and arguably a firmer one, because the marks were there before the question was asked."
      },
      "source": "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-165",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Midewiwin"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The three surrenders that precede the ascent: the body is given to the work of change and the form one had vanishes; the way of life, void of its energy, is given to the daimon; and the body's senses pass back into their sources, becoming separate and rising again as energies, while passion and desire withdraw to the nature that is void of reason."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HermeticDissolutioCorporis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Was vor den Zonen abgegeben wird",
        "en": "What is given up before the zones"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The way of life goes to a daimon before the zones are reached, and the treatise does not say which daimon or whether it is the avenging one of I.23. The silence is left standing here rather than filled."
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.24 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The passage between waking and sleeping in either direction, in which the sensory channel is partly detached and the body may already be immobile while awareness continues."
      },
      "id": "pm:sleepThreshold",
      "label": {
        "de": "Schlafschwelle",
        "en": "Sleep threshold"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Set this where the source describes the condition rather than the clock: sleep departing and waking beginning, an inability to move, a luminosity at the moment of falling asleep. The holdings already carry the finding that this state is well attested as a place of reception and not as a place where a faculty is exercised; see convergences/threshold-of-sleep.ttl."
      },
      "type": [
        "pm:AwarenessValue",
        "pm:SensoryAnchoring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Wurf bringt das Vorauswissen hervor",
        "en": "The casting yields foreknowledge"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:IfaCasting"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:IfaForeknowledge"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where too many of those present are not susceptible, the audience reacts too slowly and the performance may fail. It may still be carried out formally, as a sequence of well known actions, without attaining its aim. The presence of a single person who remains outside may suffice to disturb the rest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PerformanceFailsWithoutASusceptibleAudience",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorstellung misslingt, wenn das Publikum nicht folgen kann",
        "en": "The performance fails if the audience cannot follow"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "He notes in the same section that a performance staged outside its milieu is psychologically impossible to bring off, because there is no audience cooperating and no purpose in shamanizing, and describes such a staging before an ethnographical society as a farce. That is a warning by the recorder against the conditions under which his own discipline usually saw the thing it described.",
          "The condition of failure is located outside the performer, which is rare. The recorder adds that public opinion is nonetheless inclined to see the cause in the shaman, and that this is one of the reasons shamans resort to tricks in order to gain an indifferent audience. Both of those are his reading; the failure and its stated cause are reported."
        ]
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVII, sect. 122, pp. 332-333",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An operative handbook whose opening instructions state the preparation in numbers. The user must free himself three days beforehand from accidental pollution and from everything unclean; eat and drink once every evening; take his bread from a pure man or wash his hands first; drink only water; bathe once on the first day and no more; pray three times daily; and in the first three hours of the morning mention the hundred sacred names and the mighty ones, whose sum is given as three hundred and four. He is not to approach a woman during the preparation."
      },
      "id": "pmp:HarbaDeMoshe",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schwert des Mose",
        "en": "The Sword of Moses"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Three days, once a day, water only, one bath, three prayers, a hundred names summing to three hundred and four. A preparation given entirely in counts is checkable in a way a preparation given in dispositions is not, and this one has no clause about the state of mind of the user at all."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Harba de-Moshe"
        },
        {
          "@language": "en",
          "@value": "Sword of Moses"
        }
      ],
      "source": "The Sword of Moses, ed. and trans. Moses Gaster (London: David Nutt, 1896), the opening instructions",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hekhalot"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The training of awareness within the dream, set out in the treatise as a course of exercises in apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears."
      },
      "id": "pmp:DreamYoga",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumyoga",
        "en": "Dream yoga"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Tibetan rmi lam, transliterated milam. Older witnesses stand behind the fifteenth-century treatise: the dream-yoga manuals in the collected works of Gampopa (1079-1153) and the root verses ascribed to Tilopa. That ascription is traditional and only partly secured philologically. Note also that the list of the six is unstable between witnesses, and that the name six yogas is a western coinage for what the tradition calls six dharmas."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement between the two recorders about the extent and the naming of the Zande set of oracles. The monograph gives the generic term as soroka and names four principal oracles: the poison, the rubbing-board, the termites and a three-stick oracle called mapingo. The earlier Belgian ethnography gives the generic term as lora, lists eight procedures, and assigns the pile-of-sticks oracle to the Abarambo, Mangbetu and Bakango and to Azande of those origins rather than to the transmission at large. It also describes the termites procedure differently, as a plaited grass braid laid in a small pit and read from whether the termites cut it through or gnaw only its underside."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ZandeOracleSetDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Welche Orakel die Überlieferung als die ihren zählt",
        "en": "Which oracles the transmission counts as its own"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The disagreement matters beyond terminology. Which procedures belong to the transmission and which were taken over from neighbours is exactly the question a contact route turns on, and the two recorders answer it differently for the same set. Neither was read against the other in a modern study in this run.",
          "The two accounts also differ on who receives the poison. The Belgian ethnography, written sixteen years earlier and on the other side of the border, gives the ordeal as administered to a suspected person or to a fowl, in the present tense and without qualification. The monograph gives administration to human beings as a thing of the past and very rare even then. Whether that is a change over sixteen years, a difference between the two administrations, or a difference of observation is not decidable from the two passages."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:OracleRankOrder"
      },
      "source": [
        "A. de Calonne-Beaufaict, Azande: introduction à une ethnographie générale des bassins de l'Ubangi-Uele et de l'Aruwimi (Bruxelles: Maurice Lamertin, 1921), chapter V, Procédés divinatoires (mantique), pp. 183-184",
        "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), appendix I, p. 228, and p. 164"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The exchange at the first threshold: the operator sees the gods gazing intently at him and bearing down upon him, lays his right finger on his lips and says Silence three times, calling silence the symbol of the living god beyond decay and asking it to protect him, then hisses long and puffs; whereupon the gods are said to gaze benignly and to bear down no longer, going about their proper order of doings."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvSilentiumAvertit",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Schweigenswort wendet die Götter ab",
        "en": "The word of silence turns the gods aside"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A formula with an announced effect on a hostile power, and the effect is not passage but the withdrawal of pressure. The gods are not opened, moved or persuaded: they return to their own business. Beside the ophite gates, where a speech obtains a passage, this is a smaller and more precise claim."
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.475-829, first utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The act of apprehending form within an awareness context."
      },
      "id": "pm:Perceiving",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wahrnehmen",
        "en": "Perceiving"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Finnish and Karelian transmission of the tietäjä, in which power over a thing follows from knowing and reciting its origin."
      },
      "id": "pmt:FinnicIncantation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Finnische Zaubersprüche",
        "en": "Finnic incantation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Kalevala is a nineteenth-century compilation and belongs in the record as an ordering act, not as the corpus. The corpus is the collected incantations."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Suomen Kansan Vanhat Runot; the loitsurunot collections",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BaraqelTeachesSignsOfLightning",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel offenbart die Zeichen der Blitze",
        "en": "Baraqel discloses the signs of the lightning flashes"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with astrology."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:SignsOfLightning"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Baraqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An operative procedure enacted within a tradition."
      },
      "id": "pm:Practicing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Praktizieren",
        "en": "Practicing"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt",
        "en": "How high the threshold of absorption stands"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:FourAbsorptions"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBriefFrist",
      "label": {
        "de": "An welchem Freitag des Mondes der Brief angehängt wird",
        "en": "Which Friday of the moon the letter is hung on"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Kept as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would require the intervening American printings, which were not opened; that the translation is called literal on its own title page is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either side.",
          "The eighteen twenty printing sets the hanging on at the last Friday in the old light, that is at the end of the waning moon. The eighteen fifty translation sets it at the first Friday in the decreasing moon, which is the other end of the same quarter. The two cannot both be followed, and the difference falls on the only part of the prescription that could be missed."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission in the Bruce Codex in which the living Jesus sets out the emanation of the treasuries of the light, each rank with its name, its character, its three watchers at the gate and its twelve emanations, and in which the knowing of a word rather than the saying of one is what is said to free the hearer from the ruler of this aeon."
      },
      "id": "pmt:BooksOfJeu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bücher des Jeu",
        "en": "The Books of Jeu"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A limit of the medium, not of the text, and it decides what this file can hold. The names are in the diagrams and are not transliterated into the translation: what the reader sees as an ellipsis is the operative part. Every claim about a vowel chain in this corpus is therefore, from this witness, a claim about something that was reproduced as a picture and not as text. The strings themselves need the plates of MacDermot or of Schmidt.",
          "Three decisions before the first node. Route of contact: documented and dense — the corpus is cited by name in the Pistis Sophia and stands in the same Coptic-Egyptian transmission as the ophite and hermetic material this graph already holds, so nothing here counts as an independent witness against them. Edition: MacDermot's English, read in the excerpt named on each node, with the caution above. Number of citable passages: few, and knowingly so.",
          "What was read and what was not. Chapters 1 to 10 of the first book in the excerpt of the Gnostic Society Library, MacDermot's translation. The excerpt itself states that chapters 11 to 34 proceed in the same fashion and prints them only as diagrams, and it ends before the second book. The baptisms of water, fire and the holy spirit, the anointing, and the ascent through the twelve aeons with seal and cipher are therefore not entered here and are not claimed. See prima-materia#602."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Books of Jeu and the Untitled Text in the Bruce Codex, ed. Carl Schmidt, trans. Violet MacDermot, Nag Hammadi Studies 13; Carl Schmidt, Gnostische Schriften in koptischer Sprache aus dem Codex Brucianus (1892)",
      "source": "The First Book of Jeu, chapters 1-10 (trans. V. MacDermot), in the excerpt published by the Gnostic Society Library",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeartIsWithdrawnAbsorption",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Fasten des Herzens liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung",
        "en": "The fasting of the heart is placed at withdrawn absorption"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:withdrawnAbsorption"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The three steps are a graded withdrawal from the sensory channel and are stated as such: not to wait for the hearing of the ears, then not even for the hearing of the mind. The practitioner is awake throughout; what is withdrawn is the reliance on what the ears report. That is the criterion this coordinate names."
      },
      "source": "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused",
      "label": {
        "de": "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert",
        "en": "Display of powers refused"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": [
        {
          "id": "pmc:Cetopariyanana"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbacakkhu"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Dibbasota"
        },
        {
          "id": "pmc:Manomayakaya"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)",
        "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:OphiteMichaelDisputatus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob Michael ein Herrscher des Diagramms ist oder ein Name der gefallenen Schlange",
        "en": "Whether Michael is a ruler of the diagram or a name of the fallen serpent"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Origen's diagram gives Michael as the first of the seven ruling demons, lion-like, and adds that scripture honours him as the angel of the Creator. Irenaeus says the serpent that was cast down bears two names, Michael and Samael. The same name therefore stands once at the head of the ruling seven and once on the adversary they were formed against.",
          "The disagreement is worth keeping because it falls on the name and not on a concept. A name is a harder identity than a resemblance, and two transmissions putting one name in two incompatible places is a finding about the transmissions rather than about the bearer."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "pm:disputedClaim": {
        "id": "pmc:OphiteMichael"
      },
      "source": [
        "Irenaeus, Adversus Haereses I.30.9 (trans. A. Roberts and W. Rambaut, Ante-Nicene Fathers I, Buffalo 1885)",
        "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.30 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV, Edinburgh 1885)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Disputing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:OphiteGnosis"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission founded on Mani's own account of a companion who appeared to him and disclosed what he taught."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Manichaean",
      "label": {
        "de": "Manichäische Überlieferung",
        "en": "Manichaean transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The Cologne Mani Codex carries a first-person account of the syzygos, which is rare for late antiquity and rarer still with a name attached."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Kephalaia; Cologne Mani Codex; the Coptic psalms and the Turfan fragments",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A concept that participates in the observed correspondence."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Converging",
      "id": "pm:convergingConcept",
      "label": {
        "de": "konvergierender Begriff",
        "en": "converging concept"
      },
      "range": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The frame in which both accounts reach us: Celsus adduces the Persian mysteries in order to say that whoever examines the Christian mysteries alongside them, and unveils the rites of the Christians, will see the difference; Origen answers that the comparison was chosen for polemical use, that Eleusis, Aegina, Egypt, Cappadocia, Thrace and Rome would have served as well, and that Celsus never says which sect uses the diagram he describes."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicComparatioImposita",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Vergleich der beiden Diagramme wird vom Zeugen selbst verlangt",
        "en": "The comparison of the two diagrams is demanded by the witness"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as a node because it decides how much the two accounts can be worth to each other. They are not two independent reports that happen to agree; they are one author's pairing, preserved by a second author who thought the pairing tendentious. Any correspondence between them is therefore at most a second occurrence in one channel, never a second witness — the same rule that keeps the star-formula of PGM IV apart from the ophite speeches.",
          "Origen's complaint carries a fact worth keeping: Celsus, who names sects wherever he can, does not name the one whose diagram he has. The ophite attribution of that diagram is not in the witness."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The rite performed as vāma: the worshipper takes the position of one whose goddess claims the share of the offering before all other gods, and performs the worship in that method whether or not he also performs the five sacrifices."
      },
      "id": "pmp:VamaWorship",
      "label": {
        "de": "Verehrung in der heterodoxen Methode",
        "en": "Worship in the heterodox method"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses defining vāmā and prescribing that the worshipper be a vāma while worshipping in that method (130-131) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The examination of an inner movement for its origin, distinguished threefold as angelic, human or demonic."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DiscernmentOfOrigins",
      "label": {
        "de": "Unterscheidung der Herkunft",
        "en": "Discernment of origins"
      },
      "source": [
        "Evagrius Ponticus, On Thoughts 8",
        "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 80"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ChristianAsceticDiscernment"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A medicine man who has dreamed of the stones sends them long distances to obtain information. After a time they return and give it to him; he alone can understand what they say and repeats their message to the man who asked the question. During a treatment of the sick in a darkened lodge the stones are said to fly through the air and sometimes to strike those who have refused to believe in them."
      },
      "id": "pmp:TunkanSending",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Aussenden der heiligen Steine",
        "en": "Sending the sacred stones"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The men who furnished this were named and were distinguished by the recorder according to their relation to the stones: those who had dreamed of them and used them, those who owned one and believed themselves helped by it, one who owned one and could not command it, one who had sung at another man's demonstrations, and those who had only witnessed them. That classification is the recorder's and is unusually careful for the material, which is why it is repeated here."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by named Teton men who had themselves danced or doctored, among them Red Weasel, Chased-by-Bears, Brave Buffalo, Goose, Charging Thunder and Two Shields; recorded by Frances Densmore for the Bureau of American Ethnology at Standing Rock, 1911 to 1914, with Robert P. Higheagle interpreting; given in Lakota, and the bulletin carries the Lakota of the song texts but prints the spoken accounts in English only.",
      "source": "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), pp. 205-207",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that those at whose birth such words were uttered can, if they die, come to life again and return among people."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die bei der Geburt gesprochenen Worte sollen das Wiederaufleben ermöglichen",
        "en": "Words spoken at birth are said to make the return after death possible"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The interval between the act and its effect is a whole life, which puts the claim beyond any protocol that could be run on it and is worth recording as a property of the claim rather than as a defect of the record."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:InuitWordsSpokenAtBirth"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 28",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A hoe handle with cassava cores tied to it, a stick daubed with colours, an old basket hung in a fruit tree: things set where property is to be protected. The corpus states that these are not themselves charms but tokens or warnings, put up to inform a thief that a curse lies on the thing and what complaint he will get if he risks it, and it gives the reason in the form of a rule of fairness, that it is not considered fair to curse the stealing of an article without indicating it in some way."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Kandu",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kandu, das Zeichen, das den Fluch ankündigt",
        "en": "Kandu, the token that announces a curse"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A stated rule of fairness inside an operative system is rare in this graph and is the reason for the node. It also has a consequence for what the transmission can claim: a curse that must be announced in advance is one whose effects the announcement itself could produce, and the corpus records elsewhere the case of a man who could not eat what his inherited prohibition forbade only so long as he feared it."
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 239",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus",
        "en": "Concentration presupposes virtue"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Samadhi"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Sila"
      },
      "source": [
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TheopemptosLocusIsNotStated",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum gibt keinen Ort an",
        "en": "The god-sent dream states no locus"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:locusNotStated"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The passage was read for this question. It says where the communication comes from and says nothing about where the sleeper's awareness is while it arrives; nothing in it has the sleeper go anywhere. Recorded so that a later query can tell a source that was asked from a node nobody has reached yet."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A seat defined by sitting evenly with the body straight; the straightness is part of what the posture is, not a separate instruction."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gerade Rumpf als Merkmal einer Sitzhaltung",
        "en": "The straight trunk as the mark of a seat"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.21 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1981/1984",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a being becomes harvestable on one of two opposed paths at unequal thresholds — a slight majority of service to others on the one, an approach to totality of service to self on the other, with a region of indifference between them — and that the negative path is nevertheless abandoned at the density of unity, because it rests on an omission and what is not cannot endure."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PolarityHarvestThresholds",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die zwei Schwellen der Polarität und der Zusammenbruch der negativen",
        "en": "The two thresholds of polarity and the collapse of the negative one"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held as an origination rather than as the teaching of a tradition. The material was produced between 1981 and 1984 by three named people in a stated procedure, and it presents itself as received rather than composed. That presentation is what pm:circulatesAs records; the node makes no claim about the source of the material, only about where the text begins.",
          "One caution for anyone opening the passages. The published wording of the omission argument names the wrong polarity at its first occurrence, and the correction is made in the exchange itself; a reader who quotes the sentence as printed quotes a slip. And the passage about abandoning the negative polarity in the early sixth density stands at 36.12, not at 78.25 as an earlier note in prima-materia#62 had it. 78.25 carries the different statement that the two paths must take in each other.",
          "The two halves of the claim do not sit easily together, and the record keeps them apart rather than choosing. Read from the thresholds alone the system is a dualism with two valid outcomes; read from the argument about the omission it is a monism in which one outcome is provisional. Whoever cites only the thresholds reports a symmetry the same text withdraws. See prima-materia#69."
        ]
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A cosmology of great antiquity, received from a source outside the authors, rather than a text composed between 1981 and 1984.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "Carla Rueckert as instrument, with Don Elkins as questioner and Jim McCarty as scribe (L/L Research)",
      "source": [
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 36.12 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 78.25 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 85.11 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)",
        "The Law of One (The Ra Material), sessions 17.32 and 17.33 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the opening of the brow centre in another person is brought about by contact applied at that point."
      },
      "id": "pmc:BrowContactOpensTheBrow",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Brauenöffnung eines anderen erfolgt durch Kontakt zwischen dessen Brauen",
        "en": "The brow of another is opened by contact between the brows"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the sharpest of the three, and the reason is what the transmission does not say. The śaiva material holds pmc:Saktipata, the descent of power from teacher to disciple, and it makes that descent the condition of everything that follows — but it names no place on the body where it enters. The draft supplies one. The addition is entirely the draft's, and it is the addition that can be checked."
      },
      "pm:positedIn": "prima-materia#315",
      "type": "pm:Positing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A figure named in the Book of Parables as the one who disclosed the distinction of bitter and sweet and the art of writing."
      },
      "id": "pmc:Penemue",
      "label": {
        "de": "Penemue",
        "en": "Penemue"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 69:8 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HermeticVitaPiaYieldsAscensus",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das fromme Leben bringt den Aufstieg und die Einung hervor",
        "en": "The pious life yields the ascent and the union"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is stated as the good end for those who have gained gnosis, and the treatise names both what precedes it and what happens to those who do not. That is more than the ophite witness gives, and it is still not a falsifying condition: nothing here could be observed to fail, because the whole of it is placed after death."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:HermeticVitaPia"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:HermeticFinisBonus"
      },
      "source": "Corpus Hermeticum I.22 and I.26 (trans. G. R. S. Mead)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hermetic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The nineteenth century transmission of extended texts produced by a writer who reports not composing them, and issued as scripture rather than as message."
      },
      "id": "pmt:SpiritualistAutomaticWriting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Spiritistisches automatisches Schreiben",
        "en": "Spiritualist automatic writing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The earliest layer of the block and the template for all of it: a named person, a dated production, a printed volume, and the claim that the author is elsewhere."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Newbrough, Oahspe; Davis, The Principles of Nature, Her Divine Revelations, and a Voice to Mankind",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Korean transmission of the mudang, whose office is entered through a sickness resolved only by accepting it, and whose ritual is a negotiation carried out in the voice of the dead."
      },
      "id": "pmt:KoreanMuism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Koreanischer Musok",
        "en": "Korean muism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Chiefly oral. The corpus is a collection of transcribed ritual songs, which the project has no attestation mode for yet."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteUndecided"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The published muga song transcriptions; Akiba and Akamatsu's collected materials",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Among the tribes of this worship a native who kills a python of the sacred kind, even by accident, is by custom liable to be burned alive, and a European to be beheaded. A child who touches or is touched by one of the pythons must be kept a whole year in the seminaries of the god at the expense of its parents and taught the songs and dances of the worship; adults, and particularly women, were formerly liable in the same way, and the witness states that the scandals this produced and the decline of priestly power have let the practice fall into disuse."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ThePythonProhibition",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Töten einer Python, auch aus Versehen",
        "en": "Killing a python, even by accident"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A named case is printed with it, from a French cleric on the coast: a young enslaved woman crushed a python under a door, the priests demanded her, her owner declined to pay the composition, and she and five men were shut in the hut. Four of the men had bribed the escort and were brought through the crowd unharmed with her; the fifth, who had not paid or could not, was beaten until he escaped into a cask in the lagoon. What the transmission prescribes and what it enforces are two different things, and the difference is measured here in bribes.",
          "The penalty is the finding, because the source records it in the act of becoming a form. Fifty years before the writing a native was invariably burned to death; at the time of writing the appearance of the sentence is preserved and the culprit escapes with his life. A hut of dried grass is built, the man is thrust inside unbound and the hut is fired, so that he breaks out long before the flames reach him, and the worshippers waiting outside beat him with clubs until he has purified himself by reaching running water."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmc:DanhGbi"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Ewe and Fon informants at Whydah, unnamed, with one case taken from the published account of the Abbé Bouche and one from Des Marchais of 1731, both named by the recorder as he uses them; recorded by A. B. Ellis, colonial officer on the West African coast, published 1890; given in Ewe and Fon, of which the work prints the terms, the accounts in English.",
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Ewe-speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1890), pp. 57-59",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Vodun"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A communication that appears in the form of one of the messengers of the Winged God is held to have been granted by him, and the one to whom it speaks must thereafter become heyoka and speak and act contrarily, or as a buffoon, for the rest of his life. A candidate to dance the Sun dance who receives such a communication must act as a clown in the dance lodge, appear to enjoy the tortures and make sport of his fellow dancers. Seeing the substance of the Winged God himself has the same effect; seeing the god Heyoka, who may appear in the form of a bent man, does not."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HeyokaFromTheWingedGod",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Mitteilung, die den Empfänger zum widernatürlichen Handeln verpflichtet",
        "en": "The communication that obliges the receiver to act contrarily"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "An obligation imposed by the outcome of a procedure and binding for life, entered because it is the sharpest cost this transmission attaches to a communication. The heyoka must also set the door of his tipi so that it does not face the centre of the camp circle.",
          "The list of the messengers is given twice in the same paper and the two lists do not agree. The first names the dog, the shore lark, the swallow, the night hawk, the frog, the lizard and the dragon fly; the second names the dog, the swallow, the snowbird, the night hawk, the lizard, the frog and the dragon fly. Whether the shore lark and the snowbird are one bird under two English names, or whether one of the two places is a slip, is not decidable from the paper and is held as an issue rather than smoothed away here."
        ]
      },
      "source": "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 68 and pp. 83-84",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus",
        "en": "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:Iddhividha"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmp:FourteenfoldMastery"
      },
      "source": "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Theravada"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The preparatory rite at Agrai, held in the month Anthesterion."
      },
      "id": "pmc:LesserMysteries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die kleinen Mysterien",
        "en": "The lesser mysteries"
      },
      "source": "Athenian decree concerning Eleusis, IG I³ 6, face C",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EleusinianMysteries"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The solemn ritual and its exegesis, in which efficacy rests on stated equivalences between the act, the body and the world."
      },
      "id": "pmt:VedicRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vedische Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Vedic ritual transmission"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa; Aitareya Brāhmaṇa; the Śrautasūtras",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A protocol on the claim that a dead man over whom the words were spoken can come back among the living, held here to record that the one reported failure was assigned to a broken prohibition and that no condition for failing the claim itself is available."
      },
      "id": "pmp:InuitReturnFailureExamination",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob das Wiederaufleben in der berichteten Fassung überhaupt scheitern kann",
        "en": "Whether the return of the dead can fail at all as the tradition reports it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "No falsifying condition is given, and this is the permitted omission: the state says outright that none has been devised. Stating one here would misreport the situation, because the obstacle is not that nobody has looked but that the claim as transmitted has no failing case by construction. Whether a condition can be recovered by fixing the interval and the announcement in advance is an open question and belongs to whoever takes it up.",
          "The asymmetry with the protocol above is worth naming. Both claims come from the same narrator on adjacent pages; one is universal and therefore refutable by a single case, the other is hedged by a clause that absorbs every case. The difference is in the form of the claim and not in the strength of the testimony behind it.",
          "The passage is unusual in reporting a failure at all. What it does with the failure is the finding: the attempt is said to have miscarried because a third person had sewn new caribou skin where that is forbidden, so the practitioners and the claim are both left untouched. Success shows the power, failure shows the breach, and no outcome is left over that could count against the claim."
        ]
      },
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:noProcedureDevised"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "prima-materia",
      "pm:protocolUpdated": "2026-08-31",
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:WordsAtBirthYieldReturnAfterDeath"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:EthiopianWornPrayerYieldsProtectionOfTheSuckling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das getragene Gebet bewahrt den Säugling",
        "en": "The worn prayer preserves the suckling child"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The list of harms is long and the wording is a petition rather than a guarantee: it says preserve and protect, and it names the woman it is for. What makes the claim examinable at all is not the list but the term beside it, and what makes it hard is that infant death from any of the named causes can be assigned to a lapse in the wearing."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:EthiopianSusenyosPrayerWorn"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling"
      },
      "source": "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The treatment of the state after release under four theories that are all refused: that the released one exists after death, that he does not exist, that he both exists and does not, and that he neither exists nor does not; with the parallel refusal of the corresponding four theories about the limits of the world."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FourfoldNegationOfTheEndState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die vierfache Verneinung des Endzustands",
        "en": "The fourfold negation of the end state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "For the telic axis in prima-materia#52 this is the plain form of pm:selfBoundaryDeclined, and it is worth keeping distinct from the case in traditions/advaita-vedanta.ttl, where the question is not refused but assigned to a level that is itself superseded. The two look alike from a distance and are not the same move. No orientation is asserted on this node yet; the placement needs the verses on the self read as well. See prima-materia#63.",
          "The point of holding this is that it is not a third answer. The four alternatives are laid out in order to be declined together, and declining the fourth is what keeps the move from collapsing into the third: a position that said only neither would be the fourth alternative rather than the refusal of it.",
          "The same source guards against the reading that is nearest to hand. Reality is said to be neither existent nor non-existent, and the dialectic is described as negating not only every affirmation about it but every negation as well; it is expressly not the claim that the absolute is a non-entity, only that it is inaccessible to thought. A record that files this as nihilism reports the objection and not the position."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā XXV, at the four theories concerning the state after release and the four concerning the limits of the world (trans. Th. Stcherbatsky, The Conception of Buddhist Nirvāṇa, Leningrad 1927)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Madhyamaka"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The account Porphyry takes from Eubulus, who wrote a history of Mithraism in many books: that those who take part in the rites are called lions, the women lionesses or hyenas, the servants crows, and the fathers eagles and hawks; that the one invested with the character of the lion takes on the various forms of living creatures; and that the animal names signify the passage of human souls through various bodies, which the mysteries hold in common with the doctrine of the three grades of magi."
      },
      "id": "pmc:MithraicNominaAnimalium",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein anderer Satz von Gradnamen, und Frauen darunter",
        "en": "A different set of grade names, and women among them"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The reason given for the names is the transmigration of souls, and it is given as the cult's own reason rather than the reporter's. That makes the animal names a doctrine and not a taxonomy of ranks — an entirely different thing from a ladder of degrees, and one more reason not to merge this list with the other.",
          "This list is not Jerome's list. It has four ranks where his has seven, it names the fathers eagles and hawks, and it includes women — which the received account of this cult denies. Either the witness is describing a different stage of the cult, or a local form, or he is wrong; nothing in the text decides, and the graph does not decide either."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Porphyry, De abstinentia IV.16, reporting Eubulus (trans. A. S. Geden, Select Passages Illustrating Mithraism, London 1925)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Mithraic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A route has been proposed and is contested in the scholarship. Agreement counts as neither reception nor independent attestation until the dispute is entered as its own node."
      },
      "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kontaktweg strittig",
        "en": "Contact route disputed"
      },
      "type": "pm:ContactRoute"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:TheopemptosIsAtTheSleepThreshold",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der gottgesandte Traum liegt an der Schlafschwelle",
        "en": "The god-sent dream is placed at the sleep threshold"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:sleepThreshold"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmc:Theopemptos"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The account describes the condition and not the hour: sleep departing and waking beginning, or a condition between the two, with a voice, a light and an inability to move the eyes. Partial detachment of the sensory channel with the body already immobile is the criterion this coordinate names."
      },
      "source": "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made within a tradition, that a named practice produces a named capacity."
      },
      "id": "pm:Yielding",
      "label": {
        "de": "Hervorbringen",
        "en": "Yielding"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Held as a node and not as an edge for the same reason pm:Attributing is: only a node can carry its own source, its own mode of attestation, and a protocol that tries to fail it. A bare edge would make every such claim look alike, whatever it rests on, and would leave nothing for pm:Testing to point at. The question of which capacities a practice actually develops is answerable only where the pairing itself is addressable."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The clause appended to the rite: this immortalization takes place three times in the year, and if anyone should wish, after the instruction has been given, to disregard it, it will no longer come about for him."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dreimal im Jahr, und verwirkt für den, der die Anweisung überhört",
        "en": "Three times a year, and forfeited by whoever disregards the instruction"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "It stands outside the liturgy proper, in the instruction for magical use that follows it in the papyrus, which is why a reading that stops at the end of the liturgy reports the rite as carrying no condition of failure. The same appendix later has the god himself change the frequency: no longer three times a year but once a month at the full moon, and with the ointment thrown into a river rather than used. A rule that the text alters within itself is a rule that somebody was following.",
          "The one stated condition of failure in this piece, and it is not a sign to be watched for but a forfeiture. It does not say that the operator will see something different when it fails; it says that the thing will not happen at all, and it names the cause — disregard of the instruction after receiving it. That is checkable in the weak sense that the tradition can be held to it, and it is more than the announced sights offer, since none of them can fail to arrive in a way the text acknowledges.",
          "Two further constraints stand with it and are not conditions of success: for a second person to hear what is said, that person breathes with the operator for seven days and abstains from animal food and the bath; and the operator, when the god answers, is drained in soul and not in himself, while remembering unerringly what was said even if the oracle ran to ten thousand lines."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus"
      },
      "source": "PGM IV.723-834, the appended instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission the claim was taken over from. The work and the place stay in dcterms:source; this names the transmission, so that the depth of a chain can be counted."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Reworking",
      "id": "pm:receivedFrom",
      "label": {
        "de": "übernommen von",
        "en": "received from"
      },
      "range": "pm:Tradition",
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The course the seed-star is said to run around Sirius: perpendicular to the horizon, of a period of some fifty years counted double to a hundred because Sigui are paired as twins, with the star turning on itself once a year. Sirius is said to brighten when the seed-star is nearest and the seed-star to scintillate so that an observer believes he sees several stars when it is farthest."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PoToloOrbit",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Bahn des Pô tolo um Sirius",
        "en": "The course of pô tolo around Sirius"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The article calls the same course the figure of circumcision and excision: the near and far points of the orbit are the foreskin and the knife. The astronomical reading and the ritual reading are the same drawing, and the account gives no priority to either."
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 281-283",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim was taken down from a speaker in a transmission that commits nothing to writing, and the record of that taking down is the first fixation there is. The work a reader obtains is the field report; there is no earlier text behind it."
      },
      "id": "pm:fieldAttestation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Feldzeugnis",
        "en": "Field attestation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Distinct from pm:mediatedAttestation, and the difference is the whole reason for a separate mode. There the work exists and could not be obtained, so pm:readVia names whose reading stands between the claim and this graph. Here no such work exists: the transmission is oral, and the report is not an intermediary but the first witness. Fusing the two would enter every oral transmission as second-hand material about a text nobody can name.",
          "Every claim carrying this mode must state the gathering with pm:fieldRecord, enforced by pm:FieldAttestationShape. Without it a field claim would be a citation of a book with no way to see how far it stands from the speaker.",
          "It is not the mode for modern research. An ethnography enters here where it takes down testimony; where it runs a procedure with an outcome that could have fallen out otherwise, it is evidence and enters at pm:evidenceFrom on a pm:Testing node. A corpus that does both goes in twice, under different predicates. The line runs by function and not by the discipline of the writer, as SPEC §14 has it.",
          "Restricted knowledge is a separate question and this mode does not settle it. Where a transmission holds material back as its own property rather than as a gate of authority, the criterion of SPEC §0 does not apply unchanged and a CC0 release would be an appropriation. The outcome may be that such a transmission stays at pm:corpusNamed permanently, and that is a finding and not an omission.",
          "The mode exists because its absence had a direction. SPEC §10 asks for a received work with a passage, and the transmissions that carry no text are precisely those with no documented route to what this graph already holds — the only ones that can ever bear pm:independentAttestation. Leaving them at pm:corpusNamed for want of an admissible mode would have made the record report that only scribal cultures transmitted this knowledge, and every convergence claim of the project a claim about Eurasia. See prima-materia#91."
        ]
      },
      "type": "pm:Attesting"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim, made within a tradition, that one step of a path presupposes another: that a practice, state or capacity is not reached except by way of something else."
      },
      "id": "pm:Presupposing",
      "label": {
        "de": "Voraussetzen",
        "en": "Presupposing"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A node and not an edge, for the same reason as pm:Attributing and pm:Yielding. A prerequisite claim differs enormously in what carries it, and the difference is not visible on an edge. It matters whether a text sets an order or a reader inferred one from the order of the chapters."
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": "owl:Class"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An early twentieth century system of three selves and directed vital force, presented as the secret teaching of Hawaiian priests."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Huna",
      "label": {
        "de": "Huna",
        "en": "Huna"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Constructed by an outsider from theosophical and New Thought material and rejected by Hawaiian scholars as a representation of anything Hawaiian. Registered as the transmission it actually is, so that the ascription can be entered as false without the material vanishing from the record."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The power said to follow the fulfilled dream of a sacred stone, given by the recorder in three parts: an ability to cure sickness, to predict future events, and to tell the location of objects beyond the range of the possessor's natural vision."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TunkanDistantKnowledge",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wissen um das, was jenseits des Blicks liegt, durch die Steine",
        "en": "Knowledge of what lies beyond sight, through the stones"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "The first case has a sceptic, a stake and a witness, which is as close to a set procedure as anything in this file; the second has a locatable object and a named place. Neither is evidence in the sense of SPEC §14 — no procedure was devised and no outcome was recorded that could have gone otherwise for a third party — and both are entered as what they are, first-person reports taken down at the time.",
          "The third part is the one that matters to this graph, because it names a fact of the world that somebody else can check. Two cases are printed with it, both told in the first person by the man who performed them. In one, a fur trader who had said that all the medicine men did was sleight of hand offered ten articles if a buffalo were called to the spot where they stood; the stones were sent, the trader watched through field glasses, and a buffalo came near enough to be shot from where they stood. In the other, a white man dropped a rifle from a ferry into the river near the present site of Pierre in South Dakota and made no effort to recover it; the teller took the stones out on the water, dove where they told him to, found the rifle on the bed of the river, and afterwards returned it to its owner."
        ]
      },
      "source": "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 205 and p. 210",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:LakotaRitual"
    },
    {
      "dcterms:date": "1916",
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."
      },
      "id": "pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung",
        "en": "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Placed on the telic axis as pm:individuatingTelos. The placement is a reading and rests on the two refusals the passage makes in the same breath: dissolution is called the death of the creature, and the striving is said not to be after difference. Stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted.",
          "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis.",
          "The attribution is why this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material.",
          "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this for a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:circulatesAs": "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916.",
      "pm:originatedBy": "C. G. Jung",
      "pm:telicOrientation": {
        "id": "pm:individuatingTelos"
      },
      "source": "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)",
      "type": "pm:Originating"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How a path stands to the norm of the order it is transmitted in: whether its operative means keep that norm, deliberately break it, or decline the distinction. An instance of this class is a value on that axis and never a path."
      },
      "id": "pm:MethodOrientation",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ausrichtung der Methode",
        "en": "Method Orientation"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Held apart from pm:TelicOrientation deliberately, and that separation is the whole reason both classes exist. The two vary independently: a path can take the forbidden as its vehicle and still aim at dissolution into what exceeds the self. A scheme with one axis cannot state that case, and the terms that carry one axis — right-hand path, left-hand path — therefore report a transgressive means as though it were a separative goal. See prima-materia#52.",
          "Three cases, and they must not read alike. Absence of pm:methodOrientation on a node means nobody has put the question to the source. pm:methodNotStated means somebody has, and the source does not answer it. pm:normDistinctionDeclined means the source answers by rejecting the distinction, which is a positive claim and carries the same burden as any other. None of the three is the middle value; there is no middle value."
        ]
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:subjectAxis"
      },
      "subClassOf": "pm:Process",
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The modern transmission of a journey undertaken to a drumbeat and taught in workshops, presented as a technique abstracted from many indigenous traditions at once."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Neoshamanism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Neoschamanismus",
        "en": "Neoshamanism"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The abstraction claim is the point of interest: it asserts that a core method survives the removal of every particular tradition. The graph can hold that assertion beside the traditions it claims to abstract from, which is exactly what it was built to do."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Harner, The Way of the Shaman",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The named author with whom the claim begins."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Originating",
      "id": "pm:originatedBy",
      "label": {
        "de": "aufgestellt von",
        "en": "originated by"
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The scribal craft of the āšipu: a trained office with its own curriculum, treating affliction as an assault to be countered by named procedure."
      },
      "id": "pmt:MesopotamianExorcism",
      "label": {
        "de": "Mesopotamische Beschwörungskunst",
        "en": "Mesopotamian exorcistic craft"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The oldest procedural corpus in writing anywhere, and older than every tradition the graph currently holds. Whatever it shares with the Greek and Jewish material is therefore a question of derivation running one way only."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Maqlû; Šurpu; the exorcist's manual KAR 44; Ludlul bēl nēmeqi; Enūma Anu Enlil",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The differing outcomes that the claim treats alike, named so that the charge of saying nothing is itself checkable."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Testing",
      "id": "pm:undividedOutcomes",
      "label": {
        "de": "nicht geschiedene Ausgänge",
        "en": "undivided outcomes"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Required wherever the state is pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate, enforced by pm:UndividedOutcomesRequiredShape. The burden falls on the examiner for the same reason it does at pm:independenceGround: a verdict that a claim says nothing is easy to reach and hard to contest, and one that names no pair of outcomes it fails to separate is an opinion about the claim rather than a finding about it."
      },
      "type": "owl:DatatypeProperty"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Where the acquitted person is obnoxious to the people and they are set on killing him, the corpus states that he is put to several further tests although he vomited properly: twigs of six different trees are thrown at him in quick succession and he must name each tree at once, then he must name the ants running on the ground before him, then the butterflies and the birds as they pass, and should he fail in any single trial he is pronounced a witch and pays with his life. The recorder adds the reason the tests work as they do, that the effect of the ordeal is to daze the person who has taken it and to deaden his wits."
      },
      "id": "pmc:FurtherTestsAfterAnAcquittal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Proben nach einem unerwünschten Freispruch",
        "en": "The tests that follow an acquittal nobody wanted"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because it is the mechanism by which a procedure said to be beyond the operator's control is brought back under it, and because the corpus states the mechanism itself: the tests are set after the verdict is known and are chosen so that a dazed man will fail them."
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), pp. 263-264",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "In the Manchu clan history recorded by the author, the kite spirit is said to be able to eat the whole liver of an ox. After eating, the shaman must leave his assistant; if he falls down without being able to keep on his legs, the sacrifice is considered as rejected by the spirit."
      },
      "id": "pmc:TungusSacrificeRejected",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Zeichen des zurückgewiesenen Opfers",
        "en": "The sign that a sacrifice was refused"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A condition of failure with a physical criterion, stated in advance, decidable on the spot by everyone present, and not in the performer's gift. Very little in this graph has that shape, and it is the reason the passage is entered although it stands inside a narrative."
      },
      "source": "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, Case 2, p. 347",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SiberianShamanism"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Stoic doctrine of a fiery breath that pervades matter and holds it together."
      },
      "id": "pmt:StoicPhysics",
      "label": {
        "de": "Stoische Physik",
        "en": "Stoic physics"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The edition of Galen the fragment was read in is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed. The section numbering of De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis differs between the older editions and the critical one, so V.3.8 locates the passage only in the latter."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "source": "Chrysippus, in Galen, De placitis Hippocratis et Platonis V.3.8",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Retention held at will without the filling and the emptying that ordinarily frame it. The manual calls it the accomplished form of the practice and says nothing in the three worlds is beyond one who holds it."
      },
      "id": "pmc:KevalaKumbhaka",
      "label": {
        "de": "Alleiniges Anhalten",
        "en": "Retention alone"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.71-74 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The narrator states of her husband Padloq, whom she calls by a name she glosses as the one who keeps her alive, that he has the gift of second sight and always knows when anything remarkable is going to happen. The case she gives is a dream, on a journey from Iglulik to Tununeq, that a friend of his had been eaten by his nearest kin; on that journey they met the woman who had eaten her own family."
      },
      "id": "pmc:HusbandHasSecondSight",
      "label": {
        "de": "Dem Mann der Erzählerin wird das zweite Gesicht zugeschrieben",
        "en": "The narrator's husband is said to have the gift of second sight"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The claim is universal in form, which is what makes it examinable: he always knows. One confirming case is related and no failing case is; the case is retrospective and was told after the meeting it is said to have foretold. The protocol stands at examinations/inuit-second-sight.ttl."
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:InuitSecondSight"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": "Padloq, the narrator Takornâq's husband",
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:thirdPartyAscription"
      },
      "source": "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), p. 29",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:InuitAngakkuq"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A preliminary in which the channels are cleared by visualisation before the heat practice proper; the tradition's own form of the notion that a blockage is a place the energy does not reach."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ChannelPurification",
      "label": {
        "de": "Reinigung der Kanäle",
        "en": "Purification of the channels"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The whole is contemplated in the form of the path of worlds and the other paths, in sequence, in its coarse, subtle and supreme condition, until the mind dissolves; and the same method carried to the limits of the universe is said to bring the great awakening."
      },
      "id": "pmc:WorldPathTraversal",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Durchquerung des Weltenpfads",
        "en": "The traversal of the path of worlds"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "This is the old form of the claim that attention placed in a centre opens an assigned layer. The layers here are worlds, bhuvana, and they belong to a cosmology written into the body, not to a list of psychological qualities. The distance between the two is the whole point of the mapping node above. See prima-materia#39."
      },
      "source": [
        "Vijñānabhairava 56 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 57 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SaivaSakta"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, of divination by counting seeds, and of an examination in a figurative language by which the right to rule was said to be decided."
      },
      "id": "pmt:MayaRitual",
      "label": {
        "de": "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung",
        "en": "Maya ritual transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A witness of this corpus is therefore a witness for the period of its fixation and not for the period it describes. The strongest case is Landa: the autograph of 1566 is lost, what survives is an anonymous copy, and the thesis that the surviving text is a compilation of the seventeenth or eighteenth century out of Landa's papers is in the literature and not answered here. See prima-materia#461.",
          "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the Chumayel, chapter IX, in the Roys translation; Landa in the Gates translation, sections XXVII, XXXVII and XL; the Popol Vuh in the Goetz and Morley version of the Recinos translation, part I chapter 2 and part II chapters 7 to 9. What was not opened: the Ritual of the Bacabs, the Dresden Codex, the Tizimin and Mani books, and Landa section XXVI. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#458.",
          "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and that correction is the finding of this run rather than a formality. Absent asks whether a route was available in the period in which the corpus was fixed, and every work read here was fixed after 1524: Landa's account survives only in a later copy, the Popol Vuh only in the hand of a Dominican of 1701 to 1703, the Chumayel in a compilation signed at Chumayel in 1782. That the Chilam Balam books received European material is not a suspicion but a philological result, demonstrated for the almanac and zodiac material of the Kaua book against named Spanish printed sources. Against that stands an argument for continuity in the incantation genres, which was not decided here. See prima-materia#459 and prima-materia#460."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDisputed"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Popol Vuh in the Ximenez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs; Landa, Relacion de las cosas de Yucatan, as the account of an outside observer",
      "source": "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The sixteen figures the casting can produce, named mothers in the account of eighteen ninety-four. A figure formed by taking one column from one mother and one column from another is named a child."
      },
      "id": "pmc:IfaSixteenMothers",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die sechzehn Figuren, Mütter genannt",
        "en": "The sixteen figures called mothers"
      },
      "source": "A. B. Ellis, The Yoruba-Speaking Peoples of the Slave Coast of West Africa (London: Chapman and Hall, 1894), chapter II, the section on Ifa",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Ifa"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The effect claimed for the vitriol, stated for hollow teeth and left open by the author for teeth that are not hollow."
      },
      "id": "pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZahnschmerzStillung",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Vertreiben der allerheftigsten Zahnschmerzen",
        "en": "The driving out of the severest toothache"
      },
      "source": [
        "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The Bengali transmission that takes the devotional relation as a bodily practice, holding that the states named by the aesthetic theory are to be produced rather than contemplated."
      },
      "id": "pmt:VaisnavaSahajiya",
      "label": {
        "de": "Vaiṣṇava-Sahajiyā-Überlieferung",
        "en": "Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā transmission"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Registered because it is the one station of this sphere that turns a doctrine of states into a procedure, which is what SPEC §14 puts first. Its corpus is small, partly clandestine, and the attribution of several works is contested; that is to be settled before the file is opened, not after."
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "The Vaiṣṇava Sahajiyā padāvalī; Ānanda-bhairava; Vivarta-vilāsa attributed to Ākiñcana-dāsa",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The general and the tantra-specific preliminaries, placed before the training in either stage."
      },
      "id": "pmp:Preliminaries",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Vorbereitungen",
        "en": "The preliminaries"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A dating warning belongs here. The preliminaries are set before the training in the fifteenth-century treatise, but the sharpest codification of them as an obligatory course is nineteenth-century, in Patrul Rinpoche's manual. Their presence in the older text does not license reading the later regime back into it."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": [
        "Patrul Rinpoche, Kun bzang bla ma'i zhal lung (19th century)",
        "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:TibetanTantric"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The transmission of Gurdjieff's teaching: a claim that ordinary waking is a form of sleep, and a work on attention conducted in the middle of ordinary life."
      },
      "id": "pmt:FourthWay",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Vierte Weg",
        "en": "The Fourth Way"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Edition finding. The passages in traditions/fourth-way.ttl were read in the Internet Archive full-text scan of the one-volume English text, which carries no imprint and no reliable pagination. Work and chapter are named, which meets findability; the edition is left unstated rather than guessed, as in traditions/saiva-sakta.ttl. The five origination nodes drawn from the same work cite London 1950 and were entered before this rule was applied to them; the discrepancy stands until somebody opens a copy with an imprint.",
          "Only Ouspensky has been opened. Beelzebub's Tales stands in the principal corpus and is unread, so the state below claims places entered and not a harvested corpus.",
          "The graph already holds the Fourth Way anthropology as an origination, because material from it circulates under ancient ascription. Registering the transmission does not touch that finding: the tradition transmits, and the pedigree remains unsupported."
        ]
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:placesEntered"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous; Gurdjieff, Beelzebub's Tales to His Grandson",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The medieval and early modern Jewish transmission of the sefirot as the structure of emanation, and of the human act as bearing on it."
      },
      "id": "pmt:Kabbalah",
      "label": {
        "de": "Kabbala",
        "en": "Kabbalah"
      },
      "pm:contactRoute": {
        "id": "pm:contactRouteDocumented"
      },
      "pm:coverageState": {
        "id": "pm:corpusNamed"
      },
      "pm:principalCorpus": "Sefer ha-Bahir; Zohar; Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim; Vital, Ez Ḥayyim and Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim",
      "type": "pm:Tradition"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the divided attention of self-remembering is the third state of consciousness itself, that it occurs in ordinary life only as rare flashes, and that it can be made more or less lasting by deliberate training."
      },
      "id": "pmc:SelfRememberingYieldsTheThirdState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Selbsterinnern bringt den dritten Zustand hervor",
        "en": "Self-remembering brings about the third state"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "A second capacity is claimed in the same passage and is stated separately here because it is the one that could be counted: that memory retains only those moments in which a person remembered himself, and that the poverty of ordinary recollection follows from that. The recorder tests it against his own earliest memories and reports that they are moments of self-remembering. That is one case, reported by the person who formed the claim."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:SelfRemembering"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:FourStatesOfConsciousness"
      },
      "source": "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching, chapters seven and eight, at the definition of the third state and at the account of memory and self-remembering (one-volume English text as scanned in the Internet Archive full-text mirror; edition left unstated)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:FourthWay"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "Pronouncing the oration on any day on which the speaker is not hindered by a criminal sin, with confession of heart and mouth preceding and early in the morning: three times if he desires to be made eloquent, once if an evil thing troubles him or he is involved in some great business, twice for the greater measure."
      },
      "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioRepetita",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die ein-, zwei- oder dreimal wiederholte Oration",
        "en": "The oration repeated once, twice or three times"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A subtle vehicle carried by the soul, whose purity is held to decide whether what appears in a dream is clear and true or clouded."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PneumaticVehicle",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das pneumatische Seelenfahrzeug",
        "en": "The pneumatic vehicle of the soul"
      },
      "source": "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:ConsultationPresupposesQualifyingWood",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Befragung setzt sonnenläufiges Holz voraus",
        "en": "Consulting the drum presupposes wood that turns with the sun"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drum must be of such wood, and does not say what follows from using a drum that is not. The strength is the strength the text gives, not the strength the requirement sounds like.",
          "This is the condition the graph nearly lost. It was read at the wording in the same run as the rest of this file but reached only a local working copy, not the branch, and was recovered only because an uncommitted file was noticed. Recorded as a process finding in prima-materia#351."
        ]
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmp:RunicDrumConsultation"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:prescribedOrder"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun"
      },
      "source": "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:SamiNoaidi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "How far the corpus of a tradition has been carried into the graph. The scale exists so that a transmission nobody has opened and a transmission whose corpus is genuinely thin never read alike."
      },
      "id": "pm:CoverageState",
      "label": {
        "de": "Erschließungsstand",
        "en": "Coverage state"
      },
      "pm:scaleFamily": {
        "id": "pm:recordScale"
      },
      "type": [
        "owl:Class",
        "pm:Scale"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:SitaliYieldsCooling",
      "label": {
        "de": "Die Einatmung über die Zunge bringt Kühlung hervor",
        "en": "Inhalation over the tongue yields cooling"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Recorded because the opening through which the breath is taken is not incidental here but is what the practice consists in. Two of the eight retentions the manual lists take the breath in through the mouth and give it out through the nose; two others require the mouth held shut. The manual therefore assigns the opening per practice and does not set nasal breathing as a general rule, still less mark mouth inhalation as an error. See prima-materia#26."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:Sitali"
      },
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:CoolingAndQuenching"
      },
      "source": "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.57-58 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "A warning that the breath is to be mastered by degrees as a lion, an elephant or a tiger is mastered, and that it destroys the one who proceeds otherwise; performed wrongly, the practice is said to generate the very ailments it removes when performed rightly."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ForcedBreathCaution",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ein erzwungener statt allmählich gezähmter Atem tötet den Übenden",
        "en": "Breath forced rather than tamed by degrees kills the practitioner"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The named consequences are hiccough, laboured breathing, cough, and pain in head, ears and eyes. This is the strongest thing the manual says about the manner of breathing, and it concerns pace and force, not the opening the breath passes through. See prima-materia#26."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:HathaPranayama"
      },
      "source": [
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.15 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.16-17 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)"
      ],
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Hathayoga"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:NkasaYieldsDetectionOfTheNdoki",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Ordal bringt das Aufdecken des ndoki hervor",
        "en": "The ordeal yields the detection of the witch"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "Entered because this is the form of claim the graph already holds from another African transmission, at pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch in traditions/azande.ttl, and because the two differ in a way that matters: there the poison is given to fowls and the question is put twice in opposite form, here the accused drinks it himself and no reversal is prescribed. Whether the two may be counted as separate witnesses is not decided here and is held as an issue; the contact route of this transmission is entered as documented on other grounds, and that value governs."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:fieldAttestation"
      },
      "pm:byPractice": {
        "id": "pmp:BauKiaNkasa"
      },
      "pm:fieldRecord": "Spoken by Bakongo of the Lower Congo, unnamed in this passage; recorded by the Baptist missionary John H. Weeks in the districts of San Salvador and Ngombe Lutete and published in 1909; given in Kikongo, printed in English with the Kikongo terms.",
      "pm:yieldsCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:DetectionOfTheNdoki"
      },
      "source": "John H. Weeks, Notes on Some Customs of the Lower Congo People, in Folk-Lore, vol. XX (London: The Folk-Lore Society, 1909), p. 51",
      "type": "pm:Yielding",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:KongoNkisi"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "An examination of the claim that the position of a mental or emotional state on a numbered scale can be established by testing the strength of a subject's muscle, as the scale's author reports having done over some quarter of a million times."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ConsciousnessCalibrationProtocol",
      "label": {
        "de": "Ob sich eine Bewusstseinsstufe am Muskeltest ablesen lässt",
        "en": "Whether a level of consciousness can be read off a muscle test"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": [
          "A second reason for caution about the direction of the finding. The source itself names conditions under which its procedure is said to fail: synthetic clothing on the subject, metal jewellery, the voice of the tester. A procedure whose stated interferences are all channels through which an expectation could travel is one whose null result would be expected on ordinary grounds, and whose positive result would need to exclude them. That observation belongs in the protocol and not in a verdict.",
          "The counter-search is owed and has not been carried out. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists, some of it from within the field, and a rejection that has read only one side is by SPEC §13 a one-sided dispute and not a refutation. Until it is read, the state of this protocol stays where it is.",
          "What the available evidence covers. Three controlled studies bear on the muscle test as a diagnostic instrument, all with blinding, and none of them supports it. A test-retest study on wasp-venom allergy with four examiners, seven patients and twenty bottles each reports an overall kappa of 0.03 with sensitivity around forty per cent and specificity around sixty; a dental double-blind study on materials testing reports the test unreliable; a double-blind randomised study on therapy localisation reports the same. The first states plainly that as a diagnostic tool the method is no more useful than guessing.",
          "What the available evidence does not cover, and this is the point. Every one of those studies asks whether the muscle test can identify a substance or a bodily site. None asks whether it can place a mental state on a numbered scale. The evidence therefore strikes the presupposition, that the test yields answers better than chance, and not the claim itself. No blinded replication of the calibrations is known, and the claim is unexamined in the strict sense even though its foundation is not.",
          "Why this claim and not another. Almost every claim in this graph is about an inner state for which no procedure exists, and pm:noProcedureDevised is accordingly the commonest state in the holding. This one names its own procedure, the procedure requires no instrument, and blinding it is cheap. Among the claims the graph carries it is the most nearly testable, and that is the reason it was opened."
        ]
      },
      "pm:counterSearch": {
        "id": "pm:counterSearchNotCarried"
      },
      "pm:counterSearchNote": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Not carried, and the debt is stated in the notes above. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists and has not been read; until it is, the negative reading rests on one side."
      },
      "pm:evidenceFrom": [
        "H.-J. Staehle, M. J. Koch and T. Pioch, Double-blind Study on Materials Testing with Applied Kinesiology, Journal of Dental Research 84 (2005), no. 11, doi:10.1177/154405910508401119",
        "R. Lüdtke, B. Kunz, N. Seeber and J. Ring, Test-retest-reliability and validity of the Kinesiology muscle test, Complementary Therapies in Medicine 9 (2001), pp. 141-145",
        "S. A. Schwartz and others, A Double-Blind, Randomized Study to Assess the Validity of Applied Kinesiology as a Diagnostic Tool and as a Nonlocal Proximity Effect, Explore 10 (2014), no. 2"
      ],
      "pm:examinationState": {
        "id": "pm:procedureWithoutCases"
      },
      "pm:examinedBy": "Not devised here. The procedure is the one the source prescribes; the falsifying condition is the ordinary requirement of inter-rater and test-retest agreement, and nobody has yet applied it to the calibrations themselves",
      "pm:falsifiedBy": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Blinded testers, given the same states without knowing which, do not agree on their placement above chance; or the same tester, blinded to his earlier results, does not reproduce his own placements."
      },
      "pm:protocolUpdated": {
        "@value": "2026-09-02",
        "type": "xsd:date"
      },
      "pm:tests": {
        "id": "pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale"
      },
      "type": "pm:Testing"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "What the disagreement is about."
      },
      "domain": "pm:Disputing",
      "id": "pm:disputedClaim",
      "label": {
        "de": "strittiger Gegenstand",
        "en": "disputed claim"
      },
      "type": "owl:ObjectProperty"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:BaraqelTeachesAstrology",
      "label": {
        "de": "Baraqel offenbart die Astrologie",
        "en": "Baraqel discloses astrology"
      },
      "pm:ascribedCapacity": {
        "id": "pmc:Astrology"
      },
      "pm:ascribedTo": {
        "id": "pmc:Baraqel"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)",
      "type": "pm:Attributing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Enochic"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:DreamYogaIsPerformedInDreaming",
      "label": {
        "de": "Traumyoga wird im Traum ausgeführt",
        "en": "Dream yoga is performed within the dream"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:compilerInference"
      },
      "pm:axisValue": {
        "id": "pm:dreamingWithin"
      },
      "pm:situatedNode": {
        "id": "pmp:DreamYoga"
      },
      "pm:situationGround": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The exercises are set out as apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears within the dream, which places the performance inside the state and not at its edge. That the practitioner knows he dreams is what the training is for and is not a separate coordinate."
      },
      "source": "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state",
      "type": "pm:Situating"
    },
    {
      "id": "pmc:HawaiianWelfarePresupposesExactChanting",
      "label": {
        "de": "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Familie setzt den fehlerlosen Gesang voraus",
        "en": "The good fortune of the honoured family presupposes a chant without fault"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The transfer of the harm is what distinguishes this witness from the others the graph holds on the same rule. Where the Maori address is simply said to lose its efficacy and the Navajo ceremony is broken off, here the reciter is not the one who suffers. That makes the rule harder to test from inside the tradition, because the person who could report the fault and the person who bears the consequence are different people."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:consequenceOfSkipping": "A breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word was a sign of ill-luck to the person or family thus honoured. The harm is named, it is transferred, and it falls on the party who did not commit the fault.",
      "pm:dependentStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare"
      },
      "pm:prerequisiteStrength": {
        "id": "pm:statedNecessity"
      },
      "pm:priorStep": {
        "id": "pmc:HawaiianChantExactness"
      },
      "source": "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35",
      "type": "pm:Presupposing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:HawaiianRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The capacity the consultation is undertaken for: to be told whatever the questioners wish to know, with no restriction of subject stated in the passage."
      },
      "id": "pmc:OloliuhquiAnswer",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wonach das Orakel befragt wird",
        "en": "What the oracle is asked for"
      },
      "source": "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulo VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:NahuaRitual"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The warning that no man impeded or corrupted with any crime ought to presume to say the oration; on a day on which he is so impeded he may only call it to mind in his heart."
      },
      "id": "pmc:ArsNotoriaCrimenKautel",
      "label": {
        "de": "Wer durch ein Verbrechen behindert ist, darf sie nicht sprechen",
        "en": "No one impeded by crime may presume to say it"
      },
      "note": {
        "en": "The caution is the closest thing in this witness to a condition of failure, and it is worth naming what it does not do: it names who may not say the words, not what happens to one who says them anyway. The distinction decides whether the condition is examinable or absorbing, and the text leaves it on the safer side of that line."
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "pm:cautionsAbout": {
        "id": "pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio"
      },
      "source": "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson",
      "type": "pm:Cautioning",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:ArsNotoria"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The claim that the orbit of the seed-star lies at the centre of the world, that without its movement no heavenly body could hold its place, and that it orders the position of Sirius in particular, which is called the most disordered of the stars."
      },
      "id": "pmc:PoToloAsAxis",
      "label": {
        "de": "Der Same-Stern als Achse der Welt",
        "en": "The seed-star as the axis of the world"
      },
      "source": "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), p. 287",
      "type": "pm:Conceptualizing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:Dogon"
    },
    {
      "definition": {
        "en": "The performance of the eight limbs, taken together as one course of practice."
      },
      "id": "pmp:PracticeOfTheLimbs",
      "label": {
        "de": "Übung der Glieder",
        "en": "Practice of the limbs"
      },
      "pm:attestedBy": {
        "id": "pm:textualAttestation"
      },
      "source": "Patañjali, Yogasūtra II.28",
      "type": "pm:Practicing",
      "withinTradition": "pmt:PatanjalaYoga"
    }
  ]
}